WEBVTT

00:00.031 --> 00:01.695
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

00:01.836 --> 00:03.540
I have a long roller from Skills.

00:03.800 --> 00:10.678
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

00:11.039 --> 00:16.352
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

00:16.332 --> 00:19.536
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

00:19.876 --> 00:28.726
So if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

00:29.047 --> 00:35.474
Now always if we want more pressure we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

00:36.175 --> 00:42.542
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

00:42.522 --> 00:44.846
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

00:44.886 --> 00:46.288
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

00:46.628 --> 00:55.021
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

00:55.402 --> 01:07.180
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

01:07.430 --> 01:09.192
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

01:09.493 --> 01:12.917
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

01:12.997 --> 01:21.208
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

01:21.268 --> 01:26.795
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

01:26.875 --> 01:30.019
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

01:30.059 --> 01:37.369
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

01:37.349 --> 01:43.658
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

01:44.380 --> 01:47.364
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

01:47.404 --> 01:53.053
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

01:53.513 --> 02:00.083
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

02:00.063 --> 02:02.949
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

02:03.149 --> 02:04.872
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

02:05.193 --> 02:19.000
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

02:20.735 --> 02:27.545
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

02:27.746 --> 02:31.331
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

02:31.611 --> 02:34.876
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

02:35.437 --> 02:38.121
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

02:38.141 --> 02:39.824
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

02:40.124 --> 02:44.110
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

02:44.090 --> 02:47.056
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

02:47.617 --> 02:53.528
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day.

02:53.849 --> 02:55.372
We're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

02:55.713 --> 02:58.538
We got to really start to loosen that up.

02:59.918 --> 03:04.570
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

03:04.831 --> 03:08.340
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

03:08.360 --> 03:13.553
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

03:13.914 --> 03:15.598
Everything else is extra credit.

03:18.683 --> 03:22.429
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

03:22.710 --> 03:25.274
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

03:25.355 --> 03:29.321
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

03:29.822 --> 03:36.574
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running and vice versa on that other side.

03:36.554 --> 03:40.419
I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

03:40.459 --> 03:45.385
I want to stay on the ball of the foot and I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

03:45.726 --> 03:47.168
I'm not going too fast yet.

03:47.468 --> 03:49.871
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

03:49.891 --> 03:54.437
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

03:54.457 --> 04:00.064
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral.

04:00.044 --> 04:16.568
over with the left over with the left hand and then i'm just gonna speed that up i'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds

04:16.548 --> 04:20.993
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

04:21.314 --> 04:23.136
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

04:23.196 --> 04:32.447
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't wanna go to the point where we have to round our back.

04:32.968 --> 04:40.497
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

04:44.138 --> 04:48.505
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

04:48.725 --> 04:54.034
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

04:54.335 --> 05:00.885
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

05:01.306 --> 05:09.319
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

05:09.299 --> 05:31.155
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

05:32.080 --> 05:35.708
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

05:36.129 --> 05:38.515
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

05:38.575 --> 05:40.579
One, two, three.

05:40.639 --> 05:42.925
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

05:43.245 --> 05:45.270
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

05:45.310 --> 05:45.911
That's okay.

05:46.232 --> 05:47.816
Let's just get as low as we can there.

05:48.317 --> 05:49.279
One, two, three.

05:49.259 --> 05:50.983
three and back up.

05:51.023 --> 05:53.028
So three there, three there.

05:53.368 --> 05:59.663
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

05:59.763 --> 06:03.231
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

06:03.271 --> 06:07.200
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor

06:07.180 --> 06:12.350
Keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

06:12.670 --> 06:19.603
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

06:20.064 --> 06:23.130
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom.

06:23.110 --> 06:44.747
and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

06:49.705 --> 06:56.233
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

06:56.253 --> 06:57.715
Don't make the mistake of going here.

06:58.095 --> 07:06.146
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

07:06.266 --> 07:12.133
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

07:12.213 --> 07:16.118
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

07:16.098 --> 07:17.802
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

07:18.063 --> 07:20.007
One, two, three.

07:20.148 --> 07:23.115
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

07:23.295 --> 07:25.500
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

07:26.162 --> 07:27.866
One, two, three.

07:27.926 --> 07:29.430
Rock forward.

07:29.510 --> 07:30.532
So I'm on my toes now.

07:31.034 --> 07:33.259
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

07:33.299 --> 07:33.640
Hold.

07:34.261 --> 07:34.562
One, two.

07:34.542 --> 07:53.783
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're gonna go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

07:54.134 --> 07:56.397
One, two, three, lean.

07:57.178 --> 07:59.441
One, two, three, heels back down.

07:59.501 --> 08:03.526
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

08:03.806 --> 08:12.136
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

08:12.156 --> 08:16.522
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

08:16.882 --> 08:23.230
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

08:23.210 --> 08:26.033
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

08:26.073 --> 08:28.375
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

08:28.775 --> 08:31.558
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

08:31.898 --> 08:33.600
I need to be flat through the hips.

08:33.900 --> 08:36.302
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

08:36.823 --> 08:41.267
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

08:41.628 --> 08:44.430
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

08:44.691 --> 08:52.558
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

08:52.538 --> 09:02.479
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

09:02.920 --> 09:11.458
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

09:13.177 --> 09:41.513
So with my shoulder elevated bridge march, my shoulders are gonna be on a bench, hips up.

09:42.758 --> 09:45.424
From here, I'm really focusing on glutes and hamstrings.

09:46.065 --> 10:00.919
I'm gonna drive, get that 90 degree angle at the knee, same while keeping those hips up, and bring it down controlled, making sure those hips stay nice and stable, driving up opposite leg, and down nice and controlled.

10:18.128 --> 10:26.256
This exercise is one of my favorite for getting people into lateral plyometrics, which takes your ankle, your feet, spring and health to a whole nother level.

10:26.296 --> 10:30.420
Now I need you to do this pain free and that's why we're starting at such a low point.

10:30.681 --> 10:32.262
Now a lot of you guys can skip this.

10:32.643 --> 10:37.447
If you do level one pain free, keep moving on until you find the level that actually challenges you.

10:37.768 --> 10:39.289
There is seven levels here.

10:39.590 --> 10:43.073
Okay, but I want you all to start at level one, feel it out and then gradually work up.

10:43.053 --> 11:10.984
so for your first level we need to deload our body weight so we use assistance so i can get two stable chairs and i can just push down into those chairs and that deloads my body weight i could also hook some bands around a power rack and hold on to those bands right below my chin because that deloads my body weight you could hold on to some poles or hold on to a power rack hold on to a friend's shoulders so find some way to have it assisted and deload your body weight personally i like to use chairs whenever possible

11:10.964 --> 11:35.537
i'm going to set up in a split stance okay most of my pressure is on this front foot but i do want to keep this back foot down because that provides a second form of assistance all right as we progress later on we will pick it up and go to a true single leg hop but for your first couple levels i do want that back foot down all right so i'm in this split stance and this thigh is shooting straight down and i'm on the ball of the foot here

11:36.142 --> 11:44.572
I'm gonna come to the balls of the feet on the front foot so that heel is lifted just off the ground, and I'm gonna use a marker, and I'm gonna mark out six inches, okay?

11:44.912 --> 11:49.277
So just mark out a couple lines, it could be pieces of tape, whatever you want, at six inches.

11:49.338 --> 11:58.268
So I know that I need the outside of my foot, outside of this one, and the inside of my foot, my big toe, outside of this one when I'm going side to side, all right?

11:58.248 --> 11:59.650
So I'm set up in my split stance.

12:00.090 --> 12:02.313
For the first variation, it is a double hop.

12:02.333 --> 12:08.542
We're not ready to redirect all that energy yet, so I want you to get there and do a little double hop and then push to the next side.

12:08.582 --> 12:11.826
So it's one, two, one, two, one, two, one, two.

12:11.966 --> 12:21.859
If you're starting at baseline and you haven't done plyometrics in a really long time, you might wanna push pretty hard into the chairs or have a lot of band tension to really deload your body weight.

12:21.839 --> 12:23.843
and I'm just doing this little side to side.

12:24.544 --> 12:26.528
I'm not even fully extending my knee.

12:26.548 --> 12:31.497
I'm keeping that knee with that slight bend and I'm just doing a double hop and hopping over to that other side.

12:31.717 --> 12:34.041
If you can do that pain free, let's move on to level two.

12:34.222 --> 12:39.772
Level two is the same exact setup, but now I take away the double hop and now I'm just springing side to side.

12:40.233 --> 12:42.717
So I want a good short ground contact

12:42.697 --> 13:07.506
and I'm going side to side one key point for all these variations is I want the toes forward so when you come here we're not turning the feet the toe stays forward the whole time so I'm really strengthening up medial ankle here I'm really strengthening up lateral ankle here alright so toes straight forward side to side if you can do that pain free we can start to kind of decrease that assistance a little bit to where I'm maybe just holding for a little bit of balance

13:07.621 --> 13:08.422
And let's work there.

13:08.462 --> 13:12.649
If I can do that pain-free, now I'm gonna go on to level three.

13:12.869 --> 13:14.752
For level three, we have the same setup.

13:14.792 --> 13:17.636
I'm still at six inches, but I removed the hand support.

13:17.877 --> 13:20.921
So now the only additional support I have is this back leg.

13:20.961 --> 13:22.484
And again, I'm light on this back leg.

13:22.504 --> 13:26.630
So this is almost a true unilateral single leg hop, but not quite.

13:26.670 --> 13:28.834
I still have a little bit of pressure in that back leg.

13:29.234 --> 13:31.738
And now I'm just side to side.

13:31.718 --> 13:37.711
quick springy ground contacts, and I'm just getting six inches over and back, okay?

13:37.731 --> 13:41.098
If I can do that pain-free, let's move on to level four.

13:41.358 --> 13:44.384
In level four, we're removing the training wheels, all right?

13:44.465 --> 13:46.268
Now it's time to really get on one leg.

13:46.349 --> 13:48.192
It's a true unilateral hop.

13:48.212 --> 13:49.415
I'm springy side to side.

13:49.455 --> 13:52.782
I'm still only getting six inches, so I'm right here.

13:52.762 --> 14:14.618
i want to maintain that quick ground contact and i'm just back and forth all right true single leg hop so we just went from jv to varsity now let's go division one so level five and of course we've got to be pain free we've got to be feeling good if you are pain free we're going back to that single leg hop but now i'm taking it to 12 inches okay so i move out my markers

14:14.598 --> 14:22.711
You technically don't have to have the markers, but I love to just be able to measure it and know that I'm progressing the right way, and I have a goal with that distance.

14:22.771 --> 14:28.841
So now I'm just single leg back and forth, covering 12 inches, all right?

14:28.861 --> 14:30.864
Nice and springy if I'm pain-free.

14:31.245 --> 14:32.627
Now we're going pro, okay?

14:32.988 --> 14:34.070
This is level six.

14:34.490 --> 14:36.854
At level six, I'm going to 18 inches.

14:37.555 --> 14:39.238
Move my markers out.

14:39.607 --> 14:42.213
Now, as we get further, you can't get off balance.

14:42.293 --> 14:43.275
You can't get sloppy.

14:43.295 --> 14:49.248
If you feel sloppy or you're getting stuck in quicksand, long ground contacts revert back to the previous level.

14:49.689 --> 14:50.331
Master that.

14:50.692 --> 14:53.919
Build up the endurance as well at that level and then move up.

14:54.139 --> 14:56.805
So at level six, now I'm at 18 inches.

14:56.937 --> 15:00.805
And I still got to be nice and springy, short ground contact time.

15:01.487 --> 15:05.556
Do notice the farther I get, I don't want your body to come all the way with you.

15:05.876 --> 15:10.527
So it's not like this because then the foot doesn't really hit the angles that we're trying to work.

15:10.767 --> 15:13.072
We're trying to work these angles, right?

15:13.473 --> 15:16.279
Angles that we really see in sports.

15:16.259 --> 15:39.786
so I got to keep my body relatively in the center and let my foot move so when I'm here I got to think about my shoulders are still in the middle and when I'm here shoulders stay in the middle so it's like my chest doesn't move and it's just the foot going back and forth because if I just jump like this you see how I'm not getting those same angles okay now level 7 if we're a hundred percent pain-free

15:41.521 --> 16:10.566
this is where we go to stud ninja level 24 inches springy back and forth boom same thing keeping that chest in the center and just letting that lower body go back and forth all right so now these angles are getting bigger the forces on the foot and ankle are high and it's rapid loading it's fast loading so this is what really starts to prepare us for sports and keep in mind guys when you go to make a cut if the foot and the ankle give out that's when you see

16:10.546 --> 16:28.251
knee valgus that's when you see achilles problems and acl and other things a lot of it starts from ankle instability in these positions so this is one of the best drills now the only way that i would progress this is i would go a weighted vest most athletes don't get here because we're going higher repetition

16:28.231 --> 16:35.457
But if you're feeling good and bouncy and pain free, there's nothing wrong with starting with a light weighted vest and gradually going up on that weighted vest.

16:35.477 --> 16:47.768
Now in the case of the 1% freak athlete who gets here, they're feeling springy and bouncy and they run out of weighted vest, like they go beyond 20 and their gym doesn't have 20, then we can start progressing by adding some height.

16:47.788 --> 16:49.009
So I could add like a little hurdle.

16:49.329 --> 16:52.532
Say we add like a 6 inch or a 12 inch hurdle right there in the center.

16:52.953 --> 16:58.137
Now I can progress by actually adding a little bit of height along with that distance.

16:58.117 --> 16:59.940
So we'll call those the optional levels.

17:00.100 --> 17:02.143
Let's say level eight is the way to vest.

17:02.163 --> 17:04.566
Let's say level nine would be the height.

17:04.906 --> 17:09.212
And let's say level 10 would be the way to vest and the height over that hurdle.

17:09.433 --> 17:09.653
All right.

17:09.853 --> 17:11.476
But remember, this is extensive plyos.

17:11.536 --> 17:12.938
We're going for high repetition.

17:13.318 --> 17:15.822
So don't try to be Superman and go straight there.

17:16.122 --> 17:18.806
We do want these lower level ground contacts.

17:18.826 --> 17:24.373
And that's what really builds the actual structure of the foot and of the ankle and lower leg.

17:24.694 --> 17:25.575
Let's get to work.

17:46.160 --> 17:52.309
So for our standing vertical jumps, what we're doing here is just working on our standing vertical mechanics.

17:52.629 --> 18:03.085
Nothing fancy, but we have to lay down that foundation right now so that we can set you up for the other jumping phases, the other biometric phases that are going to come in the future.

18:03.445 --> 18:05.608
So I just want you to jump as high as you can.

18:05.829 --> 18:08.052
We're not going to go rapid off the ground.

18:08.072 --> 18:15.643
We're going to land and we're going to stick for one second so that you can kind of self-check and just become aware of your body positioning.

18:15.623 --> 18:17.728
Now, if you can do this in a mirror, great.

18:18.069 --> 18:22.982
If you can film yourself, especially if you can film yourself in slow motion, that's even better.

18:23.343 --> 18:25.669
Okay, so I'm just going to jump as high as I can.

18:25.709 --> 18:30.983
I'm going to stick for one second, and then I'm going to stand up and go again.

18:30.963 --> 18:34.427
Work on jumping as high as you can with perfect mechanics.

18:35.168 --> 18:46.823
If you don't know what perfect mechanics are, of course refer back to the mechanics section and really go in and kind of audit your jumping style and figure out what exactly it is that you're doing wrong.

18:46.963 --> 18:54.473
We're going to lay that foundation right now so that in the future we can really start to get results when we go into our plyometric phase.

19:16.154 --> 19:23.187
For the rear foot elevated progressions, first I want you to prove to yourself that you can master a regular body weight split squat.

19:23.648 --> 19:25.412
And so come into that split squat position.

19:26.033 --> 19:28.518
On this one, make sure that your shoulders stay above the hips.

19:28.878 --> 19:33.387
You can just hold onto bands or hold onto a chair if you need it for some additional support.

19:33.367 --> 19:51.831
and then just drop straight down right so we're here the knee should be kind of right above the toe not beyond the toe on this variation and then I'm coming back up all right see if you could touch that back knee to the ground or just hover it two inches off the floor or put a little pillow or a pad beneath that knee tap

19:52.182 --> 19:53.384
and come back up, all right?

19:53.744 --> 19:59.653
As long as you can do that pain free, and if you can't, you can always use more support with the hands and really deload your body weight.

20:00.114 --> 20:08.086
If you pass that pain free and you're feeling good, then go body weight, and I'm just dropping straight down, regular split squat, all right?

20:08.506 --> 20:12.913
If you can do that pain free, now let's move on to the actual rear foot elevated.

20:12.933 --> 20:15.557
So level three is where the rear foot elevated actually starts.

20:15.577 --> 20:17.239
Let's bring back our assistance.

20:17.520 --> 20:21.666
Some of you guys don't even have to mess with this stage if you're experienced and you've done this before.

20:21.646 --> 20:24.049
But if this is your first time, you might want to use some assistance.

20:24.590 --> 20:25.651
Take a good size step.

20:26.112 --> 20:28.274
Put your hands on the chairs or hold your band.

20:29.075 --> 20:32.440
Put your back foot up and I want the shoelaces down on the bench.

20:32.480 --> 20:33.981
I don't want you curled like this.

20:34.482 --> 20:37.186
Ideally, this bench is going to be below knee height.

20:37.566 --> 20:39.609
If it's above knee height, it's going to be too high.

20:39.629 --> 20:42.552
It's going to be more of a hip flexor stretch than a strengthening exercise.

20:43.073 --> 20:44.174
So take a good size step.

20:44.535 --> 20:46.337
Put your back foot up here.

20:46.317 --> 21:12.493
come down to the floor and ideally you have a little pillow or a pad that you can rest your knee on set up from the bottom up always so this is our start position and i want to get my knee right above my toe okay so i draw a straight line down from the knee it's right above the toe and then from there i lift so i'm here and then i come all the way back down tap that knee or get it about two inches off the floor

21:12.473 --> 21:34.212
and come back down if I master that pain-free I can get rid of my assistance same thing now body weight again reset from the bottom always knees above the toe I don't want all of your pressure on the toes I want equal pressure between the heel and the toes and you're really thinking about kind of pushing through that midfoot and so I'm here

21:34.377 --> 21:35.159
Come back down.

21:35.319 --> 21:35.600
Tap.

21:36.241 --> 21:39.708
Now on this variation, your shoulders aren't going to stay all the way up.

21:39.889 --> 21:41.913
They will tilt forward just a little bit.

21:42.214 --> 21:45.541
So at that bottom position, notice that I have a slight angle.

21:45.941 --> 21:47.585
I'm not all the way over like this.

21:47.825 --> 21:49.589
That'll make it even more hip dominant.

21:49.930 --> 21:51.914
But I'm not all the way up like this.

21:51.974 --> 21:53.297
I'm somewhere in the middle.

21:53.277 --> 21:59.307
Alright, if you master that and you're pain-free, let's move on to the dumbbell stage.

21:59.568 --> 22:02.973
Once you start adding some load, we can always go with a weighted vest as well.

22:03.394 --> 22:07.020
But I like to go with the dumbbells and I just set them up here.

22:07.060 --> 22:09.885
Set up knee above the toe.

22:11.587 --> 22:12.669
And then I go from there.

22:12.849 --> 22:14.271
It doesn't count as your first rep.

22:14.992 --> 22:17.156
That would be your first repetition.

22:17.276 --> 22:20.862
Now, most athletes can stay with the dumbbells for a really long time.

22:21.383 --> 22:30.197
The only way that we're ever going to ditch the dumbbells is if week after week you get so strong that grip strength becomes a limiting factor.

22:30.778 --> 22:37.188
Some of us that will happen, meaning you're no longer really getting a lower body workout because the dumbbells are so heavy.

22:37.208 --> 22:39.231
Let's say you're holding 80 pounds in each hand.

22:39.211 --> 22:45.723
the grip strength is actually the hardest part, so you can't go up any further because the grip strength is the limiting factor.

22:46.425 --> 22:59.329
If you happen to get to that point, which less than 5% of athletes are gonna get there, but if you do, our other option is to, one, add more weight of vest, so I would stay at the 80-pounders, add a weight of vest,

22:59.309 --> 23:01.391
Or I can move on to the Smith machine.

23:01.591 --> 23:05.475
So if you have access to a Smith machine, it's an unbelievable option here.

23:05.555 --> 23:12.641
I don't like a traditional barbell here because getting set up and getting the back leg up with a heavy barbell gets a little bit sketchy.

23:12.721 --> 23:15.924
Also, if you lose balance, it's a little bit too dangerous for me.

23:15.944 --> 23:22.850
The Smith machine provides the additional support that you need to really just produce force and get strong.

23:23.130 --> 23:26.614
We get our balance and stability elsewhere anyways, so we don't need it now.

23:27.314 --> 23:29.316
So I'm coming in my Smith machine.

23:30.308 --> 23:35.814
And I typically want to think about setting up with the very back of my heel underneath the bar.

23:36.154 --> 23:38.937
And for most of you guys, that's going to get you a good position.

23:39.357 --> 23:41.880
Start with just the barbell so you can feel it out a little bit.

23:41.960 --> 23:46.384
You might need to play around with it a little bit to find what that optimal length is with the bench.

23:46.404 --> 23:47.725
You got to feel it out a little bit.

23:48.226 --> 23:53.411
So then if I come down, you'll notice that in this position, my knee ends up right above the toes.

23:53.431 --> 23:55.253
So that's the position that we want to be in.

23:56.043 --> 24:23.653
slight torso lean i'm not completely upright right about here i'm tapping that back knee down to that mat or down to the floor and then exploding back up obviously the weight is going to be pretty heavy so you're trying to move it up fast but you're not going to move it that fast because it's heavy another key point with a rear foot elevated the back leg is a non-working leg you should feel a stretch in the hip flexors

24:23.633 --> 24:28.043
but people start to go heavy and they just push down harder on that back leg.

24:28.203 --> 24:34.898
You really gotta think of it as a non-working leg, like it's just there for a little bit of additional support, but I don't wanna be pushing down.

24:34.959 --> 24:38.948
And if you feel like you are pushing down, then maybe the weight is a little bit too heavy.

24:39.028 --> 24:41.794
I want all of the load on that front leg.

24:41.774 --> 24:49.022
As always, with any variation, start light, feel it out, master the form, and then session after session, we can start to go up.

24:49.342 --> 24:52.586
And this is an exercise that we can get really, really strong on.

24:52.726 --> 25:01.756
And it's a beautiful thing because I'm overloading the legs, but I'm not overloading the spine because it's gonna be about half the weight that we would normally be working with a two-leg squat.

25:02.196 --> 25:04.098
So the spine gets a little bit less load.

25:04.439 --> 25:06.941
The legs actually end up getting more load.

25:07.222 --> 25:11.106
So this is an unbelievable exercise for just building that pure strength.

25:21.464 --> 25:23.086
So here we have a couple options.

25:23.146 --> 25:28.973
If you have the hyperextension machine like this, then we're going to do hyperextension holds with the plate drop.

25:29.293 --> 25:34.940
If you do not have this, then we're just going to get two benches or two boxes and we're going to do a reverse plank.

25:34.960 --> 25:36.482
We're working the same muscles.

25:36.782 --> 25:38.424
So if you don't have this, don't worry.

25:38.684 --> 25:42.509
But if you do have the hyperextension machine, I would start light.

25:42.689 --> 25:47.775
I would start with maybe a 10 or 25 pound plate at the most.

25:47.755 --> 26:14.893
and we're just going to come in and hold in this full extension okay so for hyperextension machine doesn't really mean that we want to go into hyperextension we just want full extension where i'm flat all the way from my head down through the heel okay and contrary to popular belief this isn't really a back exercise this is actually glutes and hamstrings okay so if you're very inexperienced and you have no strength in the post chain and the glute

26:14.873 --> 26:17.137
then you can just hold right here.

26:18.178 --> 26:20.001
You can just hold for about 30 seconds.

26:20.262 --> 26:23.427
That's going to get you a good workout.

26:23.447 --> 26:27.914
If you've been training for a while, maybe for a couple of years, then you can do the plate drop.

26:27.954 --> 26:32.421
So I'm going to hold right here and I'm just going to drop the plate and catch it at full extension.

26:32.822 --> 26:35.707
So it's drop, catch, bring it back up.

26:35.727 --> 26:38.451
Drop, catch, bring it back up.

26:41.873 --> 26:48.643
So every time that I'm dropping the plate and going to catch it, it gives what's called a perturbation.

26:48.663 --> 26:55.133
So it's forcing me down into flexion, but my glutes and my hamstrings are resisting that flexion.

26:55.613 --> 27:01.863
So they're resisting that perturbation, and that's what gets the hamstrings and the glutes and the post chain nice and strong.

27:01.883 --> 27:08.893
So for this, how you count the reps, every time you drop the plate, catch it, and bring it back up, that counts as one rep.

27:08.873 --> 27:25.292
Now, if that's too easy, then yes, you can go up to a 35-pound plate or a 45-pound plate, but for the most part, I would rather see you have just perfect form, stay completely in that full extension, and really just squeeze the boots as hard as you can.

27:25.592 --> 27:33.261
We've never had anybody go above a 45-pound plate because if you're doing this the right way, you're going to really feel that burn in the post chain.

27:43.838 --> 27:48.485
For this exercise, we have a couple different options and a couple progressions that I want you guys to get through.

27:48.525 --> 27:52.531
So first and foremost, we got to make sure that we can do a double leg calf raise.

27:52.571 --> 28:00.863
So standard calf raise, you can put your feet up on a bumper plate, feet close together, or you could do this on a step.

28:01.463 --> 28:04.648
If you have stairs at home, you can put it on a step and hold on to the sides.

28:05.089 --> 28:07.192
Do make sure that you have something to hold on to.

28:07.212 --> 28:09.475
I don't want balance to be the limiting factor here.

28:09.455 --> 28:12.420
So half your foot is on, half your foot is off.

28:12.801 --> 28:15.706
Hold on to something, come down, and come up.

28:15.887 --> 28:22.418
Don't force the range of motion, but if you have the range of motion to drop your heels just below the plate, then that's fine.

28:22.759 --> 28:23.741
A lot of us don't.

28:24.101 --> 28:28.329
A lot of us, when we do drop down here, we might get some Achilles or plantar fascia pain.

28:28.790 --> 28:30.693
Work with what you have, all right?

28:30.713 --> 28:32.256
So it could look like this.

28:32.236 --> 28:37.866
or you could get a little bit of depth a little bit lower and bring the heels down a little bit, okay?

28:38.247 --> 28:39.288
Make sure that we got that.

28:39.549 --> 28:40.991
Can we do 15 calf raises?

28:41.052 --> 28:42.674
For a lot of us, the answer is yes.

28:43.115 --> 28:46.441
If the answer is yes and you don't have any pain, then we go to one leg.

28:46.481 --> 28:49.266
Now with one leg, we really need additional support.

28:49.246 --> 28:57.924
So I like to bring in a bench and use this leg as additional support because if I'm holding on to something now, I have no potential for loading.

28:58.245 --> 29:02.514
I want to keep my hands free so that I can grab dumbbells with both sides, all right?

29:02.534 --> 29:06.242
So I'm using a bench and I'm using a bumper plate.

29:06.222 --> 29:10.108
Same thing, half my foot is on, my heels are off.

29:10.128 --> 29:14.915
This leg, I have some pressure, just enough to balance, and then I'm just doing a calf raise here.

29:16.017 --> 29:17.519
Don't push up with this leg.

29:17.579 --> 29:19.122
This leg is just there for stability.

29:19.462 --> 29:21.766
Most of my weight is on this back leg.

29:22.547 --> 29:27.775
And again, if I have the range of motion, I can drop my heels down a little bit and get some good range of motion.

29:27.755 --> 29:51.028
in sports we definitely do need that range of motion through the calf but again work with what we have don't force anything that you don't currently have now if we can complete the recommended reps so for example let's say the recommended reps for the week is 15 then we move on to the next level that's holding dumbbells so I'm in the same position and now I'm just holding dumbbells to the side and this is the variation that we're gonna stick with for most of this phase

29:51.008 --> 29:54.012
Now, do make sure that you're not making this into a soleus calf raise.

29:54.032 --> 29:54.913
That would be a bent knee.

29:54.933 --> 29:59.899
We do want an extended knee so that we're really working that gastroc muscle, okay?

30:00.280 --> 30:10.833
So now most of us, just each session, try to go up five pounds in each hand, gradually overload, and we can get up until the point where we're holding 60s, 70s, 80s.

30:10.893 --> 30:14.097
Now, at some point, your grip might become the limiting factor.

30:14.077 --> 30:20.324
If we get so heavy that grip becomes a limiting factor, the other option is going to be a Smith machine.

30:20.704 --> 30:28.053
Now if you do pass the dumbbell stage and the grip strength does become a limiting factor, which is probably only 10-15% of you that's actually going to make it here.

30:28.513 --> 30:34.840
If you do make it here and you don't have a Smith machine, I'd rather you not do this with a barbell because you're just going to be too unstable.

30:35.101 --> 30:37.363
The other option would be a leg press.

30:37.383 --> 30:43.510
You can do a regular straight leg calf raise, single leg calf raise on a leg press because you have loading potential there.

30:43.490 --> 30:50.162
But if you do have access to a Smith machine where it locks in, that's going to be our best option because it provides the stability for us.

30:50.183 --> 30:54.210
So now we can really just focus on tissue tolerance and strength of that calf.

30:54.230 --> 30:56.334
You guys know that we get our stability elsewhere.

30:56.374 --> 30:57.997
We get our balance training elsewhere.

30:58.057 --> 31:01.363
We do a much better job than just a unsupported calf raise.

31:01.404 --> 31:06.493
So when we're doing calf raises, we want some extra support so we can just focus on that strength.

31:07.925 --> 31:09.608
So same thing, heel hanging off.

31:09.728 --> 31:11.632
I got the bumper plate right underneath.

31:11.832 --> 31:15.719
So the back part of the bumper plate should be directly underneath the Smith machine.

31:16.019 --> 31:20.888
And then I can just lift this leg up just a little bit, not up 90 degrees, just a little bit here.

31:21.409 --> 31:25.356
And then I can work with my range of motion and just come up, all right?

31:25.376 --> 31:27.139
I'm not exploding too fast.

31:27.540 --> 31:31.587
It's gonna be about half a second to one second up and about a second down.

31:31.567 --> 31:34.652
Now, you might have a natural bend in the knee just a little bit.

31:34.832 --> 31:35.974
That's okay.

31:36.034 --> 31:42.585
You definitely don't want a hyperextended knee, but you also don't want a deep knee bend because that's going to make it into more of a soleus raise.

31:42.885 --> 31:48.394
We're really trying to work that gastroc, so do make sure you keep a relatively straight knee with just a slight bend.

31:48.374 --> 31:51.078
Now, with this exercise, you don't run out of loading potential.

31:51.459 --> 32:10.230
Obviously, just start with the barbell, feel it out, and then you can start to go up 10 pounds on each side, 20 pounds on each side, and you can just keep going as long as your mechanics look really good and as long as you're feeling good and you're not feeling any joint pain, Achilles pain, plantar fascia pain, then we can continue to load throughout the program on this exercise.

32:32.789 --> 32:36.814
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

32:36.934 --> 32:38.736
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

32:38.776 --> 32:41.479
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

32:41.520 --> 32:48.528
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

32:48.548 --> 32:54.515
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

32:54.775 --> 32:58.560
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

32:58.540 --> 33:02.407
So, I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

33:02.848 --> 33:06.694
So, it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

33:07.255 --> 33:14.608
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

33:15.601 --> 33:42.013
your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate that means we are not actually getting all of that air all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs so i want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in okay so we're here in deep through the nose let the stomach rise seven seconds once you finish that seven seconds it's out through the nose

33:44.947 --> 33:46.869
and that stomach will fall, okay?

33:47.590 --> 33:50.233
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

33:50.974 --> 34:00.005
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

34:00.786 --> 34:08.556
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

34:08.796 --> 34:12.861
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

34:12.841 --> 34:41.717
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

34:41.967 --> 34:44.471
happens in the parasympathetic state.

34:45.012 --> 34:49.160
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

34:49.500 --> 35:03.064
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

35:04.968 --> 35:08.273
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

35:08.293 --> 35:12.579
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

35:12.859 --> 35:19.669
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

35:19.969 --> 35:23.454
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's OK.

35:23.734 --> 35:31.245
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

35:31.225 --> 35:35.577
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

35:35.998 --> 35:37.983
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

35:38.003 --> 35:40.510
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

35:40.790 --> 35:42.856
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

35:43.137 --> 35:45.363
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

35:45.643 --> 35:47.428
I'm just gonna lean forward.

35:47.408 --> 35:50.133
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

35:50.454 --> 35:57.607
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

35:57.627 --> 36:02.717
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

36:03.625 --> 36:07.494
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

36:07.515 --> 36:10.382
15 seconds on the other side as well.

36:10.522 --> 36:12.647
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

36:12.848 --> 36:14.271
So that is the back of the thigh.

36:14.512 --> 36:18.782
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

36:18.762 --> 36:22.489
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

36:22.649 --> 36:25.054
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

36:25.414 --> 36:27.719
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

36:28.099 --> 36:29.662
So now look at this back foot.

36:29.682 --> 36:31.085
I'm not curling the toe under.

36:31.485 --> 36:35.573
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

36:35.553 --> 36:38.996
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

36:39.416 --> 36:44.781
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

36:45.082 --> 36:58.193
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

36:58.554 --> 37:03.298
All right, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

37:03.278 --> 37:06.843
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

37:07.424 --> 37:08.125
Same thing.

37:08.446 --> 37:11.330
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

37:11.631 --> 37:14.775
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

37:15.056 --> 37:18.942
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

37:19.242 --> 37:23.869
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

37:23.849 --> 37:51.477
this knee as far towards the chest as i can now a lot of you as you pull the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors if that's the case try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here but this is a glute stretch for this left leg but at the same time for a lot of you this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side

37:51.457 --> 37:54.522
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

37:54.963 --> 38:01.074
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

38:01.094 --> 38:04.119
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

38:04.139 --> 38:06.303
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

38:06.543 --> 38:10.410
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

38:10.390 --> 38:12.192
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

38:12.253 --> 38:15.136
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

38:15.217 --> 38:16.118
So I'm 90 there.

38:16.478 --> 38:19.262
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

38:19.623 --> 38:21.926
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

38:22.286 --> 38:26.953
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

38:27.333 --> 38:30.317
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

38:30.578 --> 38:33.361
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

38:33.762 --> 38:38.929
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

38:39.533 --> 38:45.839
All right, so I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

38:46.600 --> 38:50.344
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

38:50.464 --> 38:55.769
For my calf stretch, I'm basically gonna do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

38:56.130 --> 39:02.636
So I'm here, I'm gonna push back like this, I'm gonna straighten out my arms, and now I'm just gonna cross over.

39:03.037 --> 39:09.203
So I'm gonna put my left foot over that right foot, and I'm gonna try to get that right heel

39:09.183 --> 39:12.629
down towards the floor or as close as I can.

39:13.110 --> 39:19.542
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

39:19.802 --> 39:25.993
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

39:26.374 --> 39:30.702
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

39:31.508 --> 39:34.619
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

39:34.680 --> 39:35.402
That's okay.

39:35.764 --> 39:38.955
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

39:39.437 --> 39:42.970
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

39:45.700 --> 39:47.283
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

39:47.443 --> 39:49.127
I have a long roller from Skills.

39:49.387 --> 39:56.260
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

39:56.621 --> 40:01.971
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

40:01.951 --> 40:05.115
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

40:05.475 --> 40:14.607
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

40:14.627 --> 40:21.075
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

40:21.776 --> 40:28.144
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

40:28.124 --> 40:30.448
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

40:30.468 --> 40:31.890
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

40:32.230 --> 40:40.603
So I'm just going to sit on the roller, and I'm going to cross over this foot, and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

40:41.004 --> 40:52.782
So I'm just going to start in the center, and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings, and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

40:53.015 --> 40:54.777
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

40:55.077 --> 40:58.502
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

40:58.582 --> 41:06.792
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

41:06.852 --> 41:10.596
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

41:11.037 --> 41:12.398
And our next one we're flipping over.

41:12.458 --> 41:15.602
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

41:15.662 --> 41:22.971
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

41:22.951 --> 41:29.261
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

41:29.982 --> 41:32.967
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

41:33.408 --> 41:38.657
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

41:39.117 --> 41:45.668
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

41:45.648 --> 41:48.553
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

41:48.734 --> 41:50.457
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

41:50.777 --> 42:04.602
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

42:06.320 --> 42:13.154
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

42:13.354 --> 42:16.921
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

42:17.202 --> 42:20.469
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

42:21.030 --> 42:23.715
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

42:23.735 --> 42:25.419
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

42:25.719 --> 42:27.062
I'm gonna go from the heel

42:27.042 --> 42:32.653
all the way down through the ball of the foot, and I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

42:33.215 --> 42:40.991
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

42:41.311 --> 42:44.157
We got to really start to loosen that up.

42:45.503 --> 42:50.155
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

42:50.416 --> 42:53.945
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

42:53.965 --> 42:59.138
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

42:59.519 --> 43:01.183
Everything else is extra credit.

43:14.984 --> 43:18.050
So for our hamstring floss, we're just gonna go lying on our back.

43:18.411 --> 43:27.909
I wanna make sure that my left leg stays down, so I'm actively pushing this left knee through the ground, pushing my left heel down through the ground, and I'm just gonna pull back.

43:28.009 --> 43:35.524
So here in this position, I'm gonna feel a little bit of a stretch in my glute, a little bit of a stretch in that opposite hip flexor, and then I'm just

43:35.504 --> 43:39.234
Extending that knee and coming back into flexion.

43:39.254 --> 43:41.119
So it's extension, flexion.

43:41.240 --> 43:43.406
So we call this hamstring floss.

43:43.727 --> 43:47.898
We're gradually trying to get that range of motion bigger and bigger.

43:48.279 --> 43:51.488
In each rep, we're trying to extend that knee more and more.

44:05.423 --> 44:09.007
So for the supine kickover, supine means facing up.

44:09.187 --> 44:15.354
So we're laying on our back, and I'm just gonna kick over this way, so I'm basically swinging my leg across.

44:15.795 --> 44:22.302
I am trying to keep that knee as straight as I can, and I'm just gonna tap that toe as high as I can, okay?

44:22.322 --> 44:27.988
And then I'm gonna bring it back the other way, and I'm gonna tap my toe on this side as high as I can.

44:27.968 --> 44:31.715
Okay, so it's over the body and then open it up.

44:31.815 --> 44:33.759
And we're just getting a gradual swing.

44:34.240 --> 44:39.570
Each rep, try to loosen up more and more and really swing that foot as high as we can.

44:39.710 --> 44:48.407
You're going to feel a big stretch on the outside of the hips and I'm going to feel a big stretch on the inside of the legs when I'm opening up to that side.

45:00.959 --> 45:07.394
For our PNF v-sit, basically what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna go in a v-sit position.

45:07.634 --> 45:11.322
I can put my hands down on the floor and I'm gonna sit up as high as I can.

45:11.423 --> 45:16.875
So the first part of this drill is we're gonna actively push our legs as hard as I can.

45:16.855 --> 45:20.280
down into the ground, and you're gonna hold that for seven seconds.

45:20.340 --> 45:29.094
So it's push as hard as you can, you gotta really contract your hamstrings, contract your glutes, and push down into the ground like you're trying to lift your body off the ground.

45:29.775 --> 45:37.227
After seven seconds, get up nice and tall, take a deep breath, and we're just relaxing and stretching down the center.

45:37.207 --> 45:39.871
So I want to get as far forward as I can.

45:40.151 --> 45:42.275
I'm not going to have a big rounded back.

45:42.335 --> 45:46.441
I do want to keep my shoulder blades pinched together and my back nice and flat.

45:46.802 --> 45:49.085
And then I'm just reaching forward as far as I can.

45:49.105 --> 45:51.469
I'm going to hold for 10 seconds.

45:51.489 --> 45:53.311
Now that's one rep.

45:53.351 --> 45:55.535
To finish this set, we need three reps.

45:55.595 --> 45:56.616
So then I'm right back in.

45:57.117 --> 45:57.918
Nice and tall.

45:58.159 --> 45:59.681
Push down as hard as I can.

45:59.901 --> 46:00.402
Seven.

46:00.803 --> 46:01.364
Relax.

46:01.564 --> 46:03.226
Breathe nice and deep.

46:03.246 --> 46:04.328
And then stretch.

46:04.308 --> 46:05.089
down the middle.

46:05.369 --> 46:08.453
If you do want to go for your toes, you could do that as well.

46:08.493 --> 46:10.395
You're still going to be stretching the same muscles.

46:10.736 --> 46:18.425
So you could grab your ankles, pull yourself forward, or you could grab your toes and pull yourself forward as long as you're not rounding that back.

46:18.886 --> 46:24.132
So the contraction allows our muscles to then relax more afterwards.

46:24.352 --> 46:27.075
So that's any type of PNF stretching.

46:27.115 --> 46:34.264
You're going to get more range of motion because our muscles are contracting and always after a hard muscle contraction,

46:34.244 --> 46:37.236
The muscles are primed for that relaxation.

46:37.256 --> 46:42.718
So then when you take a big breath and we relax and stretch, we're going to get a little further than we normally would.

47:17.495 --> 47:21.243
For the 90-90 hip opener, I'm just gonna start in a 90-90 position.

47:21.343 --> 47:32.588
So that means I'm sitting here, I'm gonna roll over towards my left glute, and I'm gonna get in a 90 degree position with that knee, and then this back leg is also gonna be 90 degrees.

47:33.008 --> 47:37.478
So we have 90 here, we have 90 here, and we have 90 here.

47:37.745 --> 47:41.631
Okay, from there, I'm going to square my chest with this knee.

47:42.072 --> 47:43.494
So I'm nice and tall right here.

47:43.934 --> 47:46.378
Now from there, my left hand is back.

47:46.759 --> 47:49.202
I'm going to thread my right arm underneath.

47:49.803 --> 47:52.347
And the ultimate goal is to get the right shoulder down to the floor.

47:52.668 --> 47:56.413
A lot of you might not have that mobility yet, but just get as low as we can comfortably.

47:56.794 --> 47:58.016
So I'm going to thread through.

47:58.036 --> 48:01.902
One, two.

48:02.523 --> 48:04.085
And then I'm going to roll to the next side.

48:04.205 --> 48:05.507
So hand down.

48:06.061 --> 48:15.282
I get into this position, same thing, 90, 90, 90, and then I thread my left hand through, shoulder to the ground.

48:15.683 --> 48:22.078
I should feel that stretch sort of in the outside of the hip, and we're just going to flow straight through.

48:26.446 --> 48:32.273
Okay, so after the desired reps there, then I'm going back to my original position, 90-90.

48:32.633 --> 48:34.475
I'm gonna have both hands on the floor.

48:34.515 --> 48:36.598
I'm gonna keep this leg grounded.

48:36.638 --> 48:43.465
I cannot lift this knee or this ankle off the ground, and I'm just gonna raise as high as I can and back down.

48:43.826 --> 48:44.967
So now it's just an opener.

48:46.108 --> 48:49.212
A lot of you will go here, and the tendency is to open up here.

48:49.272 --> 48:50.514
We gotta stay grounded.

48:50.954 --> 48:55.339
My goal is to get the sole of that foot flat on the floor.

48:55.960 --> 49:00.206
So I want to get all the way flat if possible and back down.

49:01.168 --> 49:03.851
After the desired reps, I'm flowing straight through.

49:03.912 --> 49:07.697
Same thing, I'm down and open.

49:10.601 --> 49:13.425
We should feel that stretch on the opener in here.

49:13.646 --> 49:17.992
So inside of the leg that's grounded, inside of the leg that's moving as well.

49:19.114 --> 49:21.377
Really opening up those hips.

49:37.591 --> 49:41.017
For the hip flexor tilt, I'm just gonna use a box of bench or a chair.

49:41.718 --> 49:46.547
I'm gonna get my back foot up so the top of my foot is on the bench.

49:46.607 --> 49:48.190
I'm not gonna go with my toe on the bench.

49:48.590 --> 49:51.135
Top of the foot on the bench, I'm just gonna come down.

49:51.455 --> 49:55.903
Now I wanna get my heel touching my hips or as close as I can get to that.

49:55.883 --> 49:57.865
If you have a pad, that'd be better.

49:57.905 --> 49:59.207
I wouldn't do this on hardwood.

49:59.387 --> 50:01.069
Make sure you got a nice, soft surface.

50:01.569 --> 50:02.330
So I'm touching.

50:02.731 --> 50:06.595
I'm in a straight line from my knee all the way up through my shoulders.

50:07.136 --> 50:08.297
And I'm just going to sit here.

50:08.437 --> 50:09.999
First, I'm going to get in a good position.

50:10.499 --> 50:12.442
This ankle should be under that knee.

50:13.102 --> 50:16.907
I should already feel a little bit of a stretch in that hip flexor, a little bit in the quad as well.

50:17.327 --> 50:19.810
Now from here, I'm getting nice and wide.

50:19.850 --> 50:21.011
I'm not going to lean forward.

50:21.352 --> 50:23.494
