50:23.474 --> 50:52.266
and together now at the top with straight elbows not bent elbows at the top I'm going to lean towards the lead leg so that's to my right as far as I can hold for five and then come right back okay and then we're hands down all the way up hands together nice and tall elongate through the spine and lean towards that lead leg three two one

50:52.313 --> 50:54.898
back down, and we're back through.

51:14.386 --> 51:18.816
For the kneeling adductor pulse, I'm gonna go half kneel position.

51:19.437 --> 51:22.083
I'm going to point the toe out so I'm not curled.

51:22.284 --> 51:23.286
I'm pointed like this.

51:23.326 --> 51:26.353
Top of the foot is down towards the floor.

51:27.683 --> 51:30.046
I'm gonna go straight out with this leg.

51:30.067 --> 51:32.009
This knee's gonna be nice and straight.

51:32.029 --> 51:34.413
Okay, from here, I'm gonna do some rocks.

51:34.433 --> 51:35.915
So I'm gonna rock straight back.

51:36.496 --> 51:40.682
I need my hips to touch my heels or as close as you can.

51:41.183 --> 51:46.010
And you're gonna really feel that stretch in the adductor and you'll feel a certain angle in that adductor.

51:46.070 --> 51:49.395
And now we're gonna switch that angle by rocking forward.

51:49.716 --> 51:54.062
So I'm gonna rock back one second and then I'm gonna creep the hands forward

51:54.768 --> 52:18.683
nice and far until I get close to full hip extension and then creep it back so when I go forward I get a different angle of those adductors and then when I go back I get the complete opposite side of the muscle so I'm back and I'm forward as far as I can all right make sure in this position

52:19.574 --> 52:24.204
where I'm rocking forward, I'm getting to that full hip extension.

52:24.244 --> 52:29.235
So some people stay tucked right there or some people go too far where they're in hip hyperextension.

52:29.817 --> 52:36.351
I just need to get all the way out until I'm in a straight line from that knee all the way up through that shoulder.

52:54.472 --> 52:58.979
So for the loaded ankle mobility, I'm going to get here nice and tall half kneeling position.

52:59.400 --> 53:00.982
Ankle is underneath that knee.

53:01.343 --> 53:06.731
I'm going to keep that heel down on the ground and I'm going to drive my knee as far as I can over the toes.

53:07.032 --> 53:08.614
I'm aiming for that second toe.

53:08.694 --> 53:09.756
So not over the big toe.

53:09.776 --> 53:11.198
We're aiming kind of down the middle.

53:12.376 --> 53:17.325
Already I feel a little stretch back in my Achilles in the back of the ankle joint.

53:17.906 --> 53:20.129
Now from here, I'm gonna load that position.

53:20.149 --> 53:22.233
So I'm going straight into a step up.

53:22.253 --> 53:26.841
So it's just here, step up, and then we're right back down, reset.

53:27.462 --> 53:32.907
push, drive, get that good ankle mobility, and then from there we step up.

53:32.947 --> 53:38.332
So I don't want you to get there and then come back and step up, I want you to load that max position.

53:38.892 --> 53:43.917
So we're in this max position, we got that good shin angle, and from there we step up.

53:44.297 --> 53:54.887
All right, so we're adding load to our ankle mobility, so we're not just working on ankle mobility, we're also working on stabilizing every degree of range of motion that we attain through that stretch.

54:15.809 --> 54:20.876
So for our standing hip CARS, CARS stands for Controlled Articular Rotation.

54:21.196 --> 54:25.021
So this is a FRC concept, Functional Range Conditioning.

54:25.061 --> 54:35.134
So this is getting us nice and mobile, but building stability at these short and long muscle lengths, which prevents injuries, helps us get more explosive.

54:35.154 --> 54:39.420
So it's a little bit different in your normal type of stretching.

54:39.400 --> 54:48.693
Rule number one, we've gotta create tension through the body, tension through the upper body, so I need you to squeeze the fist and keep a tight core in this whole drill, okay?

54:48.713 --> 55:01.931
From here, I'm just gonna lift up as high as I can with that right knee, and then from there, instead of a normal hip circle where we're down like that, I'm gonna turn the foot outwards, all right?

55:02.111 --> 55:06.657
So I'm gonna turn the foot out, and then I'm gonna kick it around

55:06.637 --> 55:09.382
and bring it down and then I'm gonna reverse that.

55:09.402 --> 55:15.031
So now I'm gonna kick back as far as I can and then I'm bringing it all the way back around.

55:15.392 --> 55:22.384
So I like to pretend like there's a really high fence right here and I gotta step over that imaginary fence.

55:22.364 --> 55:51.747
and then bring it down some of you balance is going to be an issue here it's okay if you want to hold on to something hold on to two poles or hold on to a power rack whatever you got to do all that matters here is that we're getting the full range of motion through that hip joint so i don't want to leave any range of motion left in the tank i want you to really get every single range of motion so that we can build that stability at those ranges and really free up your hips for more movement

56:10.593 --> 56:13.359
For the butterfly liftoffs, I'm just gonna use my shoes.

56:13.640 --> 56:17.508
You could use two cones or you could even play some basketball on each side.

56:18.169 --> 56:20.795
So we're gonna get whatever we're using, I'm going with the shoes.

56:21.035 --> 56:22.679
I'm gonna get it in line with my knees.

56:22.959 --> 56:24.683
