56:26.788 --> 56:28.471
I can't pull my heels any closer.

56:28.511 --> 56:30.255
And I'm just gonna put,

56:30.590 --> 56:35.239
the shoes in lines with the knees and then the heels of the shoe is going to be in line with the midfoot.

56:35.720 --> 56:45.618
So from here, stage one, if you don't have good mobility or good stability, we're going to place our hands on the floor, get up nice and tall, and then it's just hip liftovers.

56:45.899 --> 56:48.143
So I'm going to lift up over the shoe.

56:48.123 --> 56:49.988
and I'm gonna put my foot down.

56:50.028 --> 56:55.902
Notice how the knee is completely straight, and then I bring it back together, and I'm going with the other side.

56:56.283 --> 56:59.211
Notice as I lift up, I'm not starting to collapse the torso.

56:59.531 --> 57:01.757
The torso never changes.

57:02.198 --> 57:03.381
Put it down.

57:03.361 --> 57:21.150
bring it back now the second level and if you can do this then we're just gonna go straight up we're gonna develop some tension in our arms so squeeze the palms get that core nice and tight and then from there we're doing the same thing lift over put it down come back

57:21.130 --> 57:23.354
and the other side, all right?

57:23.374 --> 57:27.082
So the feeling that you're gonna get on this is not necessarily a stretch.

57:27.262 --> 57:35.559
So this is working on our mobility, but we're feeling more of a little bit of a burn in our hip flexors, almost like a cramp type feel.

57:35.799 --> 57:39.747
You shouldn't be cramping, but you should get that feeling where you're almost gonna cramp

57:39.727 --> 57:43.733
because what we're doing is we're working on the stability in a short-range position.

57:44.414 --> 57:55.189
So a lot of athletes have good stability when they're in their mid-range, but when the hip flexors are in this shortened range, it's really hard to move and stay stable.

57:55.569 --> 58:03.000
So what we're doing is we're working on that short-range stability, which goes a really long way in injury prevention and becoming more athletic.

58:20.328 --> 58:25.936
So for the posterior liftoffs, I'm just going to use my shoes and I'm going to place them at the bottom of my hands.

58:25.996 --> 58:33.546
So wherever my hands reach to, I'm going to place the toe at that level and I'm going to make sure that they're almost touching my thighs.

58:33.586 --> 58:34.948
So they're nice and close to my body.

58:35.369 --> 58:36.931
Now I'm just going to put my arms up here.

58:37.451 --> 58:44.801
I'm going to go 90 degrees here and then I'm going to lift up, bring it out and put it down.

58:46.083 --> 58:48.166
Bring it up and back around.

58:48.186 --> 58:48.867
