01:07:59.378 --> 01:08:01.202
And then from there, pull up.

01:08:01.182 --> 01:08:08.574
and I'm not letting that band pull me in, so I'm staying nice and strong with that right oblique, and I'm flat with the shoulders.

01:08:08.914 --> 01:08:13.641
I'm consciously squeezing the core in this position for the full duration of the set.

01:08:13.782 --> 01:08:19.511
Now, if you don't have the cable or the band, then you're just gonna go in a side plank position, and you're gonna get nice and flat.

01:08:19.531 --> 01:08:23.697
So option one is bent knees, stack them together, stack the legs.

01:08:23.677 --> 01:08:30.666
Get nice and flat here and then we're just going to lift this leg, stay nice and flat through that knee all the way through the shoulder.

01:08:31.087 --> 01:08:35.072
I'm feeling that on the inside, so I'm feeling that on the right side of the core.

01:08:35.393 --> 01:08:36.775
I'm going to feel that in the hips here.

01:08:36.835 --> 01:08:38.837
I'm going to feel that in my right hip as well.

01:08:39.158 --> 01:08:44.105
I can just get this other arm and hang out in this position nice and strong and stable.

01:08:44.465 --> 01:08:47.389
If that is too easy, then go straight leg.

01:08:47.369 --> 01:08:50.880
And we're up nice and flat from the feet all the way through the shoulders.

01:08:50.980 --> 01:08:51.963
Squeeze that core.

01:08:52.324 --> 01:08:56.376
And then we're just going to lift here and other hand up.

01:08:56.396 --> 01:08:56.637
All right.

01:09:22.402 --> 01:09:28.011
one of the best ways to strengthen up the muscles of our feet and the muscles of our toes is Michael Jackson leans.

01:09:28.331 --> 01:09:40.731
So all we're doing is our best impression of Michael Jackson, and we're just gonna plant our feet, keep my heels down on the ground, and I'm just gonna lean forward as far as I can, keeping the hips flat,

01:09:42.382 --> 01:10:12.085
and press back okay so over time you're going to get better and better at this and you're going to be able to lean a little further and further each time now the further we lean the more the toes got to dig into the ground to stabilize you so that you don't fall flat on your face so we should feel a burn through the toes and through the midfoot now make sure that the heels stay down on the ground a very common uh

01:10:12.065 --> 01:10:19.951
fault on this exercise is people lean forward and their toes or their heels start to lift off the ground.

01:10:19.991 --> 01:10:24.927
We got to keep the heels down, lean and push back with your toes.

01:10:45.325 --> 01:10:53.855
So for our rip through Palof press, I can either use a cable or if you don't have a cable, then you can just use a band that's hooked up to something nice and stable.

01:10:54.176 --> 01:10:55.257
So I'm going to use a cable here.

01:10:55.637 --> 01:10:57.159
I'm going to use a normal cable handle.

01:10:57.179 --> 01:10:58.521
I'm going to interlock my fingers.

01:10:58.901 --> 01:11:02.646
I want this cable to be about belly button or a little higher.

01:11:02.666 --> 01:11:05.870
It needs to be somewhere between belly button and chest height.

01:11:06.731 --> 01:11:08.633
So I'm going to bring it out a little bit.

01:11:08.866 --> 01:11:12.332
I'm going to start in a normal stance, almost like a triple threat position.

01:11:12.733 --> 01:11:16.880
Kind of pretend like you have a basketball here, and it's going to be step and rip through.

01:11:17.201 --> 01:11:21.108
So it's kind of a jab and a rip through to that opposite knee.

01:11:22.009 --> 01:11:27.279
I don't need to be 100% of my explosiveness here, maybe 80, 85%.

01:11:27.639 --> 01:11:33.169
But if I go too fast, this cable is going to kind of jerk up and down, and we don't want that right now.

01:11:33.149 --> 01:11:36.997
We're just gonna go about 80-85% of our max explosiveness.

01:11:37.578 --> 01:11:46.256
Boom, and then I'm gonna keep my elbows straight, and I'm gonna bring it to the center, and then back down to that knee, and then I'm back to that start position.

01:11:46.837 --> 01:11:53.892
Okay, so I got the ball on that outside shoulder, hard rip to the left knee, stabilize for a second.

01:11:53.872 --> 01:11:59.620
elbows straight, right in front of that chest, back down to that knee, and then I'm coming back.

01:12:00.081 --> 01:12:09.033
Now the most common mistake here is people going here, keeping the elbows bent and bringing it here, and then we don't get that anti-rotation of the core.

01:12:09.373 --> 01:12:22.191
So if I go here and then I really straighten out, then that cable is pulling me this way and I gotta resist that and keep my chest forward and my hands right in front of my chest, so I'm really firing up those core muscles.

01:12:22.171 --> 01:12:37.193
as always we want to start light you may you might start 10 or 20 pounds and as you get better we're gonna go up and up and up and we want to go basically as heavy as we can with great form I don't want to see that form breaking down we're gonna do the

01:12:37.173 --> 01:13:02.303
desired reps to one side and then I'm gonna flip the other way and then you're going to that right knee same exact thing so this exercise is great for not only core strength but it's functional core strength this really translates to sports you've got to be able to get to that position and then build that anti rotation because a lot of times you go to rip through and you get jammed up you get a bump from somebody and you start to fly into rotation

01:13:02.283 --> 01:13:08.514
But if you can maintain your posture and you can push through that contact, then you're going to be dominant on the court.

01:13:08.875 --> 01:13:16.508
So many people are held back because there's players that on the court, they're giving you a bump to one side of the body and we lose our posture.

01:13:16.528 --> 01:13:20.375
We've got to be able to keep that chest forward and get to where we want to go.

01:13:20.696 --> 01:13:23.160
And it all starts with strength drills like this.

01:13:35.712 --> 01:13:38.575
So for the independent toe raises, we're gonna wanna go barefoot.

01:13:39.136 --> 01:13:41.198
If you can, go without socks as well.

01:13:41.299 --> 01:13:50.449
So first, I'm just gonna make sure that I got that good arch in my foot, so I'm on my three points of contact, ball of the foot, ball of the pinky foot, and heel.

01:13:51.511 --> 01:13:57.277
From here, I'm gonna raise my big toe as high as I can, and I'm gonna get my other four toes down.

01:13:57.658 --> 01:14:03.745
So I'm gonna hold for three seconds, and I'm pushing these other four toes as hard as I can into the ground.

01:14:03.725 --> 01:14:07.171
So one, two, three, and then I'm gonna switch.

01:14:07.291 --> 01:14:15.905
Now I'm gonna push my big toe down, and I'm gonna raise my other four toes as high as I can, and I'm pushing my big toe down as hard as I can right now.

01:14:16.125 --> 01:14:18.629
One, two, three, switch.

01:14:18.649 --> 01:14:20.873
One, two, three, switch.

01:14:21.511 --> 01:14:23.997
Okay, so this is working on those intrinsic muscles of the feet.

01:14:24.358 --> 01:14:33.439
Now, a lot of you don't have the ability to do this right now because you have no control over your feet because you've been wearing stiff-soled basketball shoes for so long.

01:14:33.980 --> 01:14:36.627
If that's the case, you need to manually hold them down.

01:14:36.927 --> 01:14:38.912
So I would grab something to sit on,

01:14:42.352 --> 01:14:43.654
And I'm showing you one at a time.

01:14:43.714 --> 01:14:45.476
Normally you would do this two at a time though.

01:14:46.156 --> 01:14:46.317
Okay.

01:14:47.138 --> 01:14:50.401
So normally we want to go unassisted if possible.

01:14:50.802 --> 01:14:57.069
If you can't do that, I'm going to hold these toes down and I'm going to raise my big toe.

01:14:57.970 --> 01:14:58.270
Okay.

01:14:58.290 --> 01:15:04.798
And then after three seconds, I'm going to hold or push my big toes down and I'm going to lift my other four toes.

01:15:05.572 --> 01:15:11.559
All right, so this is gonna kind of neurologically train you to move your toes independently without each other.

01:15:11.920 --> 01:15:17.706
And then after a couple weeks, you should be able to take away the manual pressure and just do it normal.

01:15:18.227 --> 01:15:24.254
Okay, this is gonna have a huge impact on your overall athleticism and your injury prevention long-term.

01:15:24.574 --> 01:15:27.858
So this drill seems like it's kind of weird, kind of easy.

01:15:27.878 --> 01:15:29.240
You might feel like you're gonna cramp.

01:15:29.300 --> 01:15:30.321
It feels very different.

01:15:30.662 --> 01:15:35.207
Keep in mind, these are the things that go a long way for your long-term athleticism.

01:15:47.287 --> 01:15:49.831
For the reach-through turns, I'm gonna go push-up position.

01:15:50.232 --> 01:15:53.798
I'm gonna reach my right leg under as far as I can.

