Two hand up.

01:22:30.686 --> 01:22:31.348
Not out here.

01:22:31.428 --> 01:22:32.891
We need it back behind the head.

01:22:33.492 --> 01:22:34.133
And stand.

01:22:34.674 --> 01:22:34.914
All right.

01:22:35.395 --> 01:22:36.758
So that's one cycle.

01:22:37.018 --> 01:22:38.141
We're going to go for three of these.

01:22:39.102 --> 01:22:40.525
So it's one, two, three.

01:22:40.859 --> 01:22:50.255
one, two, three, lean, one, two, three, heels back down, one, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

01:22:50.556 --> 01:22:58.870
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

01:22:58.890 --> 01:23:00.032
And then we're gonna come back,

01:23:00.012 --> 01:23:03.195
hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up stand.

01:23:03.576 --> 01:23:09.883
We just gotta get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

01:23:09.943 --> 01:23:12.806
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

01:23:12.826 --> 01:23:15.148
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

01:23:15.529 --> 01:23:18.332
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

01:23:18.652 --> 01:23:20.334
I need to be flat through the hips.

01:23:20.634 --> 01:23:23.337
I'm driving through my mid foot right now.

01:23:23.317 --> 01:23:27.966
and now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

01:23:28.367 --> 01:23:42.034
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult, but if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers.

01:23:42.588 --> 01:23:49.205
and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

01:23:49.646 --> 01:23:57.947
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

01:24:15.308 --> 01:24:27.427
For our penultimate acceleration, what we want to do is just hook up to a band, make sure that band is hooked up to something stable like a power rack, and all I'm going to do is accelerate through that penultimate step, okay?

01:24:27.447 --> 01:24:33.917
So if I'm simulating jumping right-left, I got to drive off this left foot and get as far as I can with it.

01:24:34.278 --> 01:24:40.287
Remember, it's that reverse sling, so as that right foot goes up, the arms go back, okay?

01:24:40.267 --> 01:24:40.848
Boom!

01:24:41.269 --> 01:24:47.401
And really push through that band and work on that acceleration and that explosiveness into the penultimate.

01:24:47.601 --> 01:25:00.587
Remember on the penultimate, we're trying to cover distance, we're trying to do it explosively, and we're trying to drop the hips low so that when we step into that jump position, we're at the bottom position of the jump and we can go straight up.

01:25:00.567 --> 01:25:07.553
What we can't do is take that weak penultimate and then we have to lower from there and get up.

01:25:07.573 --> 01:25:08.754
Time is valuable.

01:25:09.195 --> 01:25:16.962
Every millisecond has to be spent on accelerating through the jump instead of having to eccentrically drop and then get up.

01:25:17.222 --> 01:25:23.307
So I need you to drive through this band, create distance and drop the hips nice and low.

01:25:23.327 --> 01:25:26.410
We do the desired reps on one side and then I go to the other side.

01:25:26.490 --> 01:25:30.574
So now it's a left right and I'm driving off of this right foot.

01:25:30.554 --> 01:25:36.530
Notice I'm not even jumping because if that band tension is high and you jump, you're getting pulled back.

01:25:36.690 --> 01:25:37.733
We don't even want to do that.

01:25:38.094 --> 01:25:45.694
I just want to simulate the jump so I can come up to the toes and I want to work on that acceleration through the penultimate.

01:26:12.910 --> 01:26:19.140
So for my mechanics primer, I'm working on three aspects of jumping mechanics all in one drill.

01:26:19.220 --> 01:26:21.964
So I'm doing four reps on all of these.

01:26:22.245 --> 01:26:24.028
So the first one, we're working on the arms.

01:26:24.308 --> 01:26:29.977
The arm swings 10 to 20% of your overall vertical jump height, so we have to get the arms right.

01:26:30.398 --> 01:26:33.703
The biggest mistake that athletes make is they go short

01:26:33.683 --> 01:26:35.505
with that counter movement arm swing.

01:26:35.705 --> 01:26:39.468
When it comes to max jump height, we want a big arm swing.

01:26:39.529 --> 01:26:45.975
So arms stay straight, pinky is at least even with the earlobe at the top of that backswing.

01:26:46.055 --> 01:26:50.659
So for this first one, I'm going to stay completely stiff through the torso.

01:26:50.679 --> 01:26:54.743
So I'm going to brace that core and then we're going to do what I call Gumby arms.

01:26:54.783 --> 01:26:57.806
We're going to really relax our arms and then we're just going to swing.

01:26:58.166 --> 01:27:00.248
Alright, so one, two,

01:27:00.734 --> 01:27:04.218
three, four, we're getting them as high as we can.

01:27:04.598 --> 01:27:07.201
We're working on that dynamic shoulder mobility.

01:27:07.741 --> 01:27:11.385
Now from there, after those four, we're gonna use a speed drop.

01:27:11.405 --> 01:27:19.334
So I still want the pinky at least at earlobe level and we're dropping down as fast as I can into the jump.

01:27:19.354 --> 01:27:24.079
I'm not gonna actually take off, I'm just working on that drop and then I'm coming up to the toes.

01:27:24.499 --> 01:27:26.421
Okay, so it's here, drop.

01:27:26.401 --> 01:27:28.143
Get up to the toes, all right?

01:27:28.223 --> 01:27:30.946
Drop as fast as you can, get up to the toes.

01:27:30.986 --> 01:27:38.615
So we're working on that eccentric rate of force development, so that's how fast we can stop ourselves and reverse back up.

01:27:38.935 --> 01:27:44.201
So I want you to think about your normal jump mechanics, but right now I want you to focus on two things.

01:27:44.582 --> 01:27:47.145
Speed drop, don't just let gravity take you down.

01:27:47.165 --> 01:27:49.127
I want you to attack the floor.

01:27:49.648 --> 01:27:54.433
So go down fast, but then also keep that arm swing high, all right?

01:27:54.413 --> 01:27:55.395
Four reps there.

01:27:55.736 --> 01:27:58.642
Okay, and then the last one is the penultimate stride.

01:27:58.682 --> 01:28:01.268
So one of the most important aspects of jump mechanics.

01:28:01.649 --> 01:28:06.018
So I'm going to step, the penultimate step is the second to last step.

01:28:06.299 --> 01:28:11.450
So if I'm planting right left, the penultimate step is that right foot stride.

01:28:11.711 --> 01:28:13.755
So I want you to think about distance.

01:28:13.735 --> 01:28:15.859
If I take a short step, my hips are here.

01:28:16.280 --> 01:28:19.407
If I take a long step, my hips are now down here.

01:28:19.788 --> 01:28:26.742
So I lowered my hips, I lowered my center of gravity, so now when I step in, I'm already at the bottom position and it's straight up.

01:28:27.303 --> 01:28:29.668
So for right now, I want you to focus on distance.

01:28:29.688 --> 01:28:34.258
So I gotta push off this left leg hard to cover distance.

01:28:34.238 --> 01:28:40.307
So I want you to think about the distance and the big arm swing and not collapsing our torso too much.

01:28:40.447 --> 01:28:45.514
You can have a little collapse, but we want that torso pretty vertical on that penultimate step.

01:28:45.915 --> 01:28:48.319
So I'm just going a right-left plant.

01:28:50.061 --> 01:28:50.842
Get up to the toes.

01:28:50.862 --> 01:28:51.904
You don't need to jump yet.

01:28:52.405 --> 01:28:54.167
Turn around and we go back.

01:28:54.307 --> 01:28:56.150
Now it's left-right.

01:28:56.771 --> 01:28:57.472
Okay?

01:28:57.612 --> 01:28:58.493
So that's one rep.

01:28:58.874 --> 01:28:59.835
I'm doing four of these.

01:28:59.896 --> 01:29:00.817
So now I come back.

01:29:01.959 --> 01:29:02.980
Right-left.

01:29:04.546 --> 01:29:05.529
left, right.

01:29:05.729 --> 01:29:08.556
Okay, so this drill is a mechanics primer.

01:29:08.877 --> 01:29:16.176
We gotta really, really work on those mechanics so that we can put ourselves in good positions to produce optimal power.

01:29:16.517 --> 01:29:17.239
Let's get it.

01:29:20.628 --> 01:29:25.655
So for our penultimate max touch, all we're doing is taking a two-step.

01:29:25.675 --> 01:29:28.920
So as always, refer back to the mechanic section.

01:29:29.220 --> 01:29:33.326
With the penultimate, we don't want to gradually walk into the jump.

01:29:33.666 --> 01:29:38.533
I want to stride, and I really want to push and accelerate through that penultimate step.

01:29:39.094 --> 01:29:41.337
So if I'm going right and left,

01:29:41.317 --> 01:29:47.165
pushing off this right foot as far as I can, which is going to lower my center of gravity.

01:29:47.485 --> 01:29:52.612
It's going to get me into that jump position nice and low so that everything is up from there.

01:29:53.553 --> 01:29:53.654
Okay?

01:29:53.674 --> 01:30:00.022
So what I want you to work on is really covering distance with that two-step and then just get up as high as you can.

01:30:00.082 --> 01:30:05.890
If you're on the court, try to rip off the rim, try to dunk the basketball or touch as high as you can on the backboard.

01:30:05.870 --> 01:30:10.844
If you don't have anything to reach for, then just do the penultimate and jump as high as you can.

01:30:11.686 --> 01:30:19.086
So we do the desired reps on right-left, and then we come back and we do the desired reps on left-right.

01:30:34.085 --> 01:30:40.172
So for the penultimate hurdle hop, I'm gonna set up four hurdles, and for me, I'm gonna set them up about 12 feet apart.

01:30:40.492 --> 01:30:43.836
Now, the better we get at this drill, the further we can set them up.

01:30:43.956 --> 01:30:49.362
So the whole goal is to create as much separation as I can with that penultimate step.

01:30:49.382 --> 01:30:58.231
So remember, if I'm right-left jumping, the penultimate is that right foot, which is gonna lower my hips and lower my center of gravity, okay?

01:30:58.251 --> 01:31:02.556
So I'm gonna jump as high as I can over the first hurdle.

01:31:02.536 --> 01:31:09.204
Now as soon as I land, I'm going to use that energy from the floor to push myself into that next step.

01:31:09.224 --> 01:31:11.186
Okay, so it's going to be a jump here.

01:31:11.387 --> 01:31:15.291
As soon as I land, I'm just popping right into that right left.

01:31:15.311 --> 01:31:19.076
So now I'm going to take off jump, land, push.

01:31:19.096 --> 01:31:22.420
Okay, the whole time I'm using that arm swing.

01:31:22.440 --> 01:31:28.968
This drill is all about timing, all about coordination, and all about getting big distance with that step.

01:31:29.268 --> 01:31:30.670
Let's look at it full speed.

01:31:42.850 --> 01:31:47.899
Now once we complete one side of right, left, we're gonna bring it back the other way, left, right.

01:31:48.160 --> 01:31:50.324
So now the penultimate stride is the left foot.

01:31:50.544 --> 01:31:57.838
We gotta stride as far as we can, cover that distance with the left foot, and then recover right foot, get up as high as we can.

01:31:59.033 --> 01:32:05.904
So for our 15 foot approach jump, all we're doing is measuring out 15 feet and then jumping and touching as high as we can.

01:32:06.324 --> 01:32:11.532
So I can go on the basketball court and just start at the free throw line and then aim to touch the rim.

01:32:11.913 --> 01:32:14.677
Or if you can already touch the rim, try to dunk.

01:32:14.697 --> 01:32:18.363
If you can already dunk, try to do a dunk that you've never done before, okay?

01:32:18.383 --> 01:32:22.970
All we're concerned about is getting as high as you possibly can on every rep.

01:32:23.230 --> 01:32:26.475
This is an opportunity for you to work on your jumping mechanics.

01:32:26.455 --> 01:32:29.683
So I just want you to start with a lean.

01:32:29.963 --> 01:32:35.136
So you're gonna kind of start with a lean and then fall into it and then take that recovery step.

01:32:35.376 --> 01:32:37.902
So that's gonna help us build up our acceleration.

01:32:38.384 --> 01:32:40.769
And most of us are probably gonna get three steps.

01:32:41.230 --> 01:32:43.215
So if I was jumping right, left,

01:32:43.195 --> 01:32:46.160
Then I would lean and I would take a step with my left.

01:32:46.540 --> 01:32:49.925
So then it's left, right, left, take off.

01:32:50.847 --> 01:32:51.989
Now everybody's different.

01:32:52.109 --> 01:32:58.118
Maybe you can get four steps or five steps based on your leg length and your speed.

01:32:58.138 --> 01:33:00.782
But most of us will probably get three or four.

01:33:00.762 --> 01:33:11.361
So after the certain amount of reps with right, left, we're gonna go to the other side, and then it's lean, fall, right, left, right, get up as high as we can.

01:33:11.942 --> 01:33:20.998
Use this as an opportunity to work on your form, and if you can, film yourself or have a friend film you so that you can look at your mechanics afterwards.

01:33:23.560 --> 01:33:28.908
So for the one foot approach touch, all we're gonna do is start as far back as you want.

01:33:29.148 --> 01:33:32.613
So you could start three, you could start NBA three, or even beyond.

01:33:32.894 --> 01:33:43.729
I want you to build up max speed and go off one foot, touch as high as you can, or work on dunking, or if you can already dunk, work on doing something that you've never done before, okay?

01:33:43.990 --> 01:33:47.975
We wanna use this as an opportunity to work on your single leg jump mechanics.

01:33:48.336 --> 01:33:50.479
When it comes to taking off one leg,

01:33:50.459 --> 01:33:54.791
Strength matters, but what matters more is mechanics and practice.

01:33:55.092 --> 01:34:05.481
So really use this as an opportunity to get good quality reps, and that's what's gonna take your single leg jump to the next level more than any other exercise we do.

01:34:13.156 --> 01:34:17.222
So for the high knees, I'm going about 60 to 70 percent, nothing too fast.

01:34:17.503 --> 01:34:19.806
And I'm just going to get my thighs up parallel to the floor.

01:34:20.127 --> 01:34:23.171
I'm going to pump the arms in the opposite way.

01:34:23.191 --> 01:34:28.219
So when my right leg is up, my left hand is up and I'm just going to get that blood flowing.

01:34:28.479 --> 01:34:33.887
I'm going to stay on the balls of my feet so my heels are never hitting and I'm just quickly popping off the ground.

01:34:34.188 --> 01:34:38.995
We're going to get the muscles going, going to get our blood flowing and get prepped for this workout.

01:34:48.779 --> 01:34:52.386
Okay, so for the wall side series, I'm just gonna hold on to the wall.

01:34:52.667 --> 01:34:57.116
I wanna be in water, that's about belly button height, and I'm just gonna go side swing.

01:34:57.156 --> 01:35:02.526
So I'm swinging my leg, I'm gonna keep my knee pretty straight, I'm gonna keep my foot dorsiflexed.

01:35:02.546 --> 01:35:08.458
So that means I'm not pointing the toes down, I'm pulling the toes up, so I should not be hitting my feet on the floor.

01:35:08.438 --> 01:35:19.437
And I'm going to explosively go in as high as I can and then explosively go out with that leg as high as I can like I'm trying to get it out of water on both sides.

01:35:19.678 --> 01:35:28.573
This is going to really fire up those hip muscles, all those little hip stabilizer muscles and the abductors and adductors are going to start to really get activated.

01:35:38.965 --> 01:35:45.714
So for our explosive flexion extension, what I'm going to do is I'm actually going to start with my knee high, thigh around parallel to the floor.

01:35:46.094 --> 01:35:50.901
I'm going to get a little bit of a lean into this wall and I'm going to really explode into extension.

01:35:50.961 --> 01:35:54.245
So full hip extension, almost like a donkey kick.

01:35:54.265 --> 01:35:57.970
As hard as I can, I need to come to full extension like I would in a sprint.

01:35:58.430 --> 01:36:03.036
And now from there, I'm going to quickly reverse it and come right back into flexion.

01:36:03.357 --> 01:36:07.302
So I'm coming right back to that thigh parallel knee high position.

01:36:07.282 --> 01:36:16.506
Okay, I'm going to take a one to two second pause there and then I'm going to go again, really gear up, really visualize between each rep and then explode as fast as you can through the water.

01:36:16.526 --> 01:36:19.956
So it's donkey kick, right back and pause.

01:36:20.597 --> 01:36:23.164
Extension, flexion, pause.

01:36:23.144 --> 01:36:32.774
I want you to really transition quickly from extension into flexion just like you would in a jump or just like you would especially when you're sprinting.

01:36:33.114 --> 01:36:38.940
You've got to extend and then quickly flex to drive that knee back up for that next stride.

01:36:39.460 --> 01:36:41.582
We're going to be really working the glutes here.

01:36:41.622 --> 01:36:49.270
We're really working the hip flexors and we're working them very, very quickly just like we work them in sprinting and jumping.

01:37:01.433 --> 01:37:06.801
So for the hip openers, I'm going to drive my knee up so that my thigh is parallel to the floor.

01:37:07.141 --> 01:37:12.669
And then from there, I'm going to aggressively open it up as far as I can without rotating my pelvis.

01:37:12.930 --> 01:37:16.435
So I don't want you to open up and then all of a sudden start rotating that way.

01:37:16.755 --> 01:37:20.681
I want to keep my chest and my hips squared off to this wall.

01:37:20.981 --> 01:37:24.867
And I'm just going to use that leg to really open up and work on that range of motion.

01:37:25.267 --> 01:37:29.173
I'm going to explode back in and then I'm going to explode down.

01:37:29.573 --> 01:37:30.715
Okay, so that's one rep.

01:37:30.695 --> 01:37:35.181
So it's explode up, explode out, explode in and explode down.

01:37:35.501 --> 01:37:39.306
And this is going to really fire up those little hip muscles that we don't normally work.

01:37:39.747 --> 01:37:43.031
Abductors, adductors, internal and external rotators.

01:37:43.372 --> 01:37:46.996
All of these muscles are super important for on-court explosiveness.

01:37:47.277 --> 01:37:50.661
And these are the muscles that we don't really get to work as much in the weight room.

01:38:00.968 --> 01:38:05.657
So for my internal, external rotations, I'm just going to sit with my feet in the water.

01:38:06.779 --> 01:38:09.424
Water's coming about halfway up on my shins.

01:38:09.484 --> 01:38:11.067
I'm going to hold my knee in place.

01:38:11.348 --> 01:38:13.732
So I don't want this knee to move at all.

01:38:14.073 --> 01:38:15.516
I don't want to start going here.

01:38:15.916 --> 01:38:17.620
I want this knee to stay stationary.

01:38:17.680 --> 01:38:19.483
I want the hip to stay stationary.

01:38:19.463 --> 01:38:20.726
and I'm just rotating.

01:38:21.047 --> 01:38:27.122
So think about bringing the foot as far as I can this way, and then as far as I can that way.

01:38:27.142 --> 01:38:32.295
So we're going internal, external rotation, and I'm going explosive as I can.

01:38:32.616 --> 01:38:36.325
So I'm just getting it going, big speed.

01:38:37.132 --> 01:38:38.955
keeping that knee nice and stable.

01:38:39.356 --> 01:38:40.879
I'm not moving in and out.

01:38:40.919 --> 01:38:47.111
I'm strictly rotating through the hip to get my foot through water as fast as I can.

01:38:47.472 --> 01:38:54.465
Now, this is really training that explosiveness and the mobility of our internal and external rotators of the hip.

01:38:54.445 --> 01:39:03.801
And we don't get the opportunity to train these internal-external rotators that often in the weight room because there's not that many ways that we can load it.

01:39:04.161 --> 01:39:18.445
So this is a very good way to get some strength, get some speed and explosiveness in those internal and external rotators, which go a long way with first-step explosiveness, with lateral movements, and ton of movements that we see in sports.

01:39:30.524 --> 01:39:48.060
So for our sprinter kicks, what I want to do is basically simulate the stride of a sprint, and now it's not going to be exact because we're not on land, but what we want to do here is really just work the hamstrings, work the hip flexors in a similar position that we would be seeing in an all-out sprint.

01:39:48.040 --> 01:40:14.132
okay so what i want to do is i'm thinking about high toe high knee okay so i'm going big range of motion even exaggerating it a little more than you might get in a real sprint and think about bringing the toe up even with the opposite knee or over that opposite knee and then from there i'm going to straighten out the knee and i'm just going to kick as hard as i can as explosive as i can back into that cycle so we're going full cycles

01:40:14.112 --> 01:40:19.741
Similar to a sprint, I'm pumping the arms so when the left knee is high, my right hand is high.

01:40:20.262 --> 01:40:23.167
We're going as fast as we can.

01:40:23.527 --> 01:40:38.331
And again, we are exaggerating that range of motion more than we would even get in a sprint because I want to teach you to get strong at your end range of motion because that's where most of the injuries happen is when we're at that end range of our muscle length.

01:40:38.311 --> 01:40:57.771
okay so i want you to exaggerate it get the knee high extend the knee and then pull back forcefully now make sure that you do keep your foot dorsiflex so if you point your toe down you're going to hit it on the bottom of the floor i need you to pull your toes up as high as we can pull your feet up this way and then work from there

01:41:13.006 --> 01:41:20.034
So for my foot cross jump, you can either do this without a line or if you have a line in your pool, I would straddle that line.

01:41:20.454 --> 01:41:26.901
And then from there, I'm gonna go crossover, back to neutral, crossover the other way, back to neutral.

01:41:27.021 --> 01:41:31.766
And then on that one, I'm popping off the ground as high as I can, as quick as I can.

01:41:32.187 --> 01:41:36.111
Okay, so it's over cross, back to neutral.

01:41:36.091 --> 01:42:05.085
over cross back to neutral and hop off the ground okay so full speed that's cross cross jump cross cross jump all right we got to get as quick as we can over that line and don't start going really small because still keep the feet spread and get a full range of motion and then as soon as the feet get back after that second cross you got to really pop off the ground with that minimal knee bend and get as high as we possibly can

01:42:05.065 --> 01:42:14.937
This is a great drill for working on your quickness, working on things like crossover steps, which we use so often in all sports, especially basketball.

01:42:15.397 --> 01:42:21.265
And we're working on coming from these different footwork positions to getting up in the air, getting vertically.

01:42:21.565 --> 01:42:34.060
So many people can jump high when it's the perfect scenario, but when their feet are coming out of a cross or they're coming out of a lateral slide or they're coming off a step back, they can't get their feet set and quickly pop off the ground.

01:42:34.040 --> 01:42:35.882
That's what this drill is going to work on.

01:42:47.759 --> 01:43:07.119
So for my one leg linear quick hops, basically what I'm doing is I'm gonna start with my left knee high, my right hand up, and now I'm just gonna jump off this right leg and I'm gonna drive the right knee just so that my thigh is parallel to the floor and then I'm back down and I'm moving forward with each step.

01:43:07.420 --> 01:43:09.882
In this first one, it's all about speed.

01:43:09.942 --> 01:43:14.627
So I don't care about getting out of water, I just need to get this right thigh

01:43:14.607 --> 01:43:18.132
parallel to the floor and back down as quick as I can.

01:43:18.533 --> 01:43:19.454
Okay, so it's here.

01:43:20.235 --> 01:43:23.159
Drive, drive, drive, drive.

01:43:23.179 --> 01:43:25.643
All right, and I'm just moving forward with each step.

01:43:25.903 --> 01:43:33.814
It's all about that speed that we can get the knee up and get that foot back down and then pop forward a little bit with each step.

01:43:33.794 --> 01:43:39.901
So what this drill is doing is it's really teaching us to pop off the ground nice and quick, nice and reactive.

01:43:40.241 --> 01:43:42.344
And I'm really getting that good knee drive.

01:43:42.364 --> 01:43:45.247
So I'm getting a ton of good explosive hip flexor work.

01:43:45.527 --> 01:43:50.833
And then I'm getting a ton of good glute and hip extensor work as I'm pushing back down.

01:43:51.154 --> 01:43:56.880
Of course, the water is resisting my leg in multiple directions that we wouldn't normally get in the weight room.

01:43:57.421 --> 01:44:02.907
So it's a really great way to train and build explosiveness here in that flexion extension pattern.

01:44:19.171 --> 01:44:23.255
Now for the one leg linear hops, we're not going for speed.

01:44:23.395 --> 01:44:24.556
We're now going for height.

01:44:24.836 --> 01:44:27.899
So it's the same exact thing as with the quick hops.

01:44:28.339 --> 01:44:34.165
But now I'm jumping off my right leg and I'm going to drive it as high as I can out of water.

01:44:34.205 --> 01:44:37.588
So I want to get my knee all the way out of water and then back down.

01:44:38.048 --> 01:44:38.209
Okay.

01:44:38.229 --> 01:44:43.934
So as I jump and I'm driving that right knee, I'm going back with the right hand.

01:44:44.054 --> 01:44:45.836
I'm going up with the left hand.

01:44:46.116 --> 01:44:48.418
And then as I land, we're back.

01:44:51.217 --> 01:44:54.861
Now, I don't want you to get stuck in quicksand on the floor.

01:44:54.961 --> 01:45:00.826
I still want you to pop off the ground quick, but the number one priority of this drill is jump height.

01:45:00.846 --> 01:45:09.255
So even if it takes you a little bit longer to get off the ground, I just want you to think about getting your knee as high as you can out of water.

01:45:09.555 --> 01:45:10.896
If you can get it a foot out, great.

01:45:11.137 --> 01:45:13.639
If you can get it two feet out of water, even better.

01:45:13.939 --> 01:45:17.663
Just get as high as you can, and we're moving forward with each step.

01:45:17.643 --> 01:45:21.430
Now this drill is great for developing our single leg vertical jump.

01:45:21.791 --> 01:45:30.748
Of course, the water is resisting us, but we are getting a very similar joint angle that we would be getting on a single leg takeoff.

01:45:30.768 --> 01:45:33.694
On a single leg takeoff, we can't load up super low.

01:45:33.674 --> 01:45:59.985
on this drill since we are trying to get off the ground quick we are getting a very similar angle where we can't load too low we're kind of getting this minimal knee bend and then we're creating maximum power even though we have that small knee bend so it's very specific to how we're working on the court with a single leg jump and then it's also really developing hip flexor on both sides it's developing hip extensor on both sides

01:45:59.965 --> 01:46:05.638
and we're just getting a ton of muscle activation and power development out of this drill.

01:46:25.717 --> 01:46:29.283
So for our single leg pause jumps, I'm going to stay on the ball of my foot.

01:46:29.323 --> 01:46:31.646
So I never want to hit my heel on this drill.

01:46:31.987 --> 01:46:39.619
And I'm just going to start low, actually lower than I would in a normal single leg takeoff because here we're working on concentric power.

01:46:40.000 --> 01:46:41.883
So I want to start ball of the foot.

01:46:42.003 --> 01:46:43.766
I want my chin just out of water.

01:46:44.066 --> 01:46:45.889
And then I'm going to go opposite hand up.

01:46:45.949 --> 01:46:49.615
Pretend like you have the ball in this hand and you're going to go up for a dunk.

01:46:49.595 --> 01:46:53.541
So pretend like there's a string between the elbow and the right knee.

01:46:53.902 --> 01:46:59.170
I'm going to explode, jump off my left leg, and I'm going up for a dunk with the right hand.

01:46:59.491 --> 01:47:03.537
I'm coming right back down, and I'm pausing in that same position.

01:47:03.958 --> 01:47:04.859
Okay, so I'm here.

01:47:05.480 --> 01:47:07.083
One, two, drive.

01:47:07.523 --> 01:47:09.887
Get that big baseline dunk, and then we're right back.

01:47:10.128 --> 01:47:11.029
One, two, go.

01:47:11.009 --> 01:47:11.710
Drive.

01:47:12.251 --> 01:47:13.172
Right back down.

01:47:13.192 --> 01:47:13.993
One, two.

01:47:14.754 --> 01:47:14.955
Drive.

01:47:15.295 --> 01:47:17.118
I got to get as high as I can.

01:47:17.158 --> 01:47:18.279
And here's the thing.

01:47:18.620 --> 01:47:23.727
You're going to really start to burn in the quads and a little bit in the glutes and the calves on this exercise.

01:47:24.067 --> 01:47:27.472
I need you to maintain explosiveness throughout the set.

01:47:27.853 --> 01:47:29.575
It's a little bit higher rep.

01:47:29.555 --> 01:47:32.543
than what we do on a lot of our other plyometrics.

01:47:32.563 --> 01:47:44.374
So I want you to take this as an opportunity to teach your body how to maintain explosiveness when we're tired instead of only being fresh for two or three reps and then giving out.

01:47:58.073 --> 01:48:07.825
All right, so for our flutter fatigue jumps, basically what I want to do is I want to get your legs nice and fatigued, and then I want to work on explosiveness while we're nice and fatigued.

01:48:08.085 --> 01:48:16.295
Now, we don't do a lot of this in the program, but it is super important because we have to be able to also jump high in the fourth quarter and have that explosion capacity.

01:48:16.575 --> 01:48:22.903
So what we're going to do, we're going to tire out your legs here, 15 seconds all out, as quick as we can, short flutter kick.

01:48:22.923 --> 01:48:27.068
So I'm leaning back on the side, and I'm going to bring my legs up.

01:48:27.048 --> 01:48:32.862
And I'm going to go small flutters, as small as I can, as fast as I can to keep my body floating.

01:48:33.504 --> 01:48:34.005
So I'm here.

01:48:34.928 --> 01:48:37.273
I'm going all out for 15 seconds.

01:48:37.434 --> 01:48:41.985
As soon as I'm done, I step away from the wall and I'm jumping.

01:48:44.007 --> 01:48:52.301
Okay, as soon as I'm done with my five jumps, I come right back to the wall without resting and I'm right back in 15 second flutter.

01:48:52.682 --> 01:48:57.069
Okay, so if I do a flutter and then the five jumps, that's only one rep.

01:48:57.089 --> 01:49:05.543
So then look at the reps that I wrote out and so say it's five reps, that means you have five cycles back and forth and back and forth until you're done.

01:49:05.523 --> 01:49:08.709
So it's not going to be do flutters, take a break, do jumps.

01:49:08.750 --> 01:49:09.992
This is going to be very rapid.

01:49:10.373 --> 01:49:11.675
No breaks straight through.

01:49:11.695 --> 01:49:13.319
Should build up some fatigue.

01:49:13.720 --> 01:49:19.431
Should learn how to become explosive while our legs are nice and fatigued and have that lactic acid build up.

01:49:21.487 --> 01:49:23.830
Alright, so welcome to our deload day.

01:49:24.010 --> 01:49:35.083
Now, you might be saying, I feel good, my muscles are fine, my nervous system's good, I'm not overtrained, but there's other systems within the body that could be overtrained and you don't even know it.

01:49:35.203 --> 01:49:47.798
For example, the fascia, a lot of times we keep pounding and pounding and we're losing fascial elasticity and we're drying out the fascia and that leads to decreased explosiveness and efficiency and vertical jump.

01:49:47.778 --> 01:50:13.870
and so it's good after four hard weeks of training to take a couple days of deload so i'm assuming you got the four weeks consecutively without taking breaks and then you took saturday sunday off now we're going to take monday and tuesday as our deload and then we're going to get back in wednesday and hit it hard now if you had vacations or you didn't do the last four weeks consecutively then maybe you can skip the deload and go straight into the

01:50:13.850 --> 01:50:14.852
the intense workout.

01:50:14.933 --> 01:50:19.765
If you want to do that, go to Friday's day and you're going to replicate that today on Monday.

01:50:20.206 --> 01:50:23.995
Go to Thursday's workout and you're going to replicate that on Tuesday.

01:50:24.416 --> 01:50:30.812
If you are on the deload days, the rules are you need one alternative sport.

01:50:30.792 --> 01:50:40.364
Okay, so if you're a basketball player, I want you to go do something different that involves cutting, that might be an aerobic workout, something where you have fun.

01:50:40.384 --> 01:50:44.929
The key is you've got to introduce your body to different movements and you need to have fun.

01:50:45.250 --> 01:50:50.376
So on my D-Load, I like to go play tennis or handball, racquetball, something like that.

01:50:50.716 --> 01:50:58.065
Whatever you want to do, just make sure it's not some sort of crazy dangerous sport that you're going to get injured with because I need you healthy for the rest of this program.

01:50:58.045 --> 01:51:00.929
One sport, okay, we need one massage day.

01:51:01.009 --> 01:51:15.088
So either go get a massage or self massage, foam roll, kind of take the foam roll workout we've been doing and just extend that and just do twice the amount of reps or twice the amount of length on each muscle group.

01:51:15.108 --> 01:51:17.791
We need one mobility or flexibility workout.

01:51:18.092 --> 01:51:21.917
Now you could do the mobility day or you could go do a yoga class.

01:51:21.937 --> 01:51:24.420
You could go do hot yoga, whatever you want to do.

01:51:24.460 --> 01:51:26.483
I do advise that you do something different.

01:51:26.463 --> 01:51:35.637
Just to kind of keep it fresh in your mind because the key is on the deload, I don't want you to be doing the same stuff that we've been doing the last four weeks.

01:51:35.877 --> 01:51:39.182
And then the fourth thing that we need is an aerobic workout.

01:51:39.203 --> 01:51:46.113
So if the sport that you went and played like tennis was enough aerobically and you got a good sweat, then you can skip this part.

01:51:46.514 --> 01:51:54.546
But if you feel like you didn't get a great aerobic workout, then go for a jog, very slow pace, anywhere from 10 to 30 minutes.

01:51:54.526 --> 01:51:58.456
A huge part of recovery is your aerobic base.

01:51:58.596 --> 01:52:04.611
The better our aerobic base, the faster and the better we're going to be able to recover and adapt.

01:52:05.393 --> 01:52:06.637
Alright, so those four things.

01:52:07.018 --> 01:52:11.168
Alternative sport, self massage or go get a massage.

01:52:11.148 --> 01:52:36.244
mobility or yoga or some type of stretching routine and an aerobic workout all right we need to do that on Monday and then we're going to do that again on Tuesday by Wednesday every system within the body is going to be fully recovered and we're ready to get after it I understand some of you might be in season of course you got to go do your practice you got to go do your skills training I just want you I just want to give you a little break

01:52:36.224 --> 01:52:46.875
from the weight room and I want your fascia to just fully recover so that we can get it hydrated and we can also get that elasticity where we need it to be for max explosiveness.

01:52:49.927 --> 01:52:50.648
What's up, guys?

01:52:50.768 --> 01:52:54.395
Quick minimum dose advice for phase two, day three.

01:52:55.176 --> 01:52:56.799
Already very short and efficient.

01:52:56.879 --> 01:52:58.743
We really shouldn't have to skip anything.

01:52:59.123 --> 01:53:03.190
If we do skip something, it would be the partial leg press.

01:53:03.210 --> 01:53:06.256
We're getting plenty of strength work on Monday and Friday.

01:53:06.717 --> 01:53:11.445
I put that in there just to squeeze in a little bit of strength work.

01:53:11.425 --> 01:53:19.678
But this day is mostly about that sprint and about the reactive plyos that we're doing and the 10-minute freestyle dunk session.

01:53:19.958 --> 01:53:21.440
That's what's most important here.

01:53:21.981 --> 01:53:25.086
So if you don't have the time, skip that partial leg press.

01:53:25.266 --> 01:53:28.030
Make sure that we do the 7-Eleven breathing as always.

01:53:28.291 --> 01:53:31.335
And if we're already flexible, we can skip that static stretch.

01:53:31.616 --> 01:53:32.337
Let's do it.

01:53:32.502 --> 01:53:34.066
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

01:53:34.186 --> 01:53:35.891
I have a long roller from Skills.

01:53:36.152 --> 01:53:43.371
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

01:53:43.391 --> 01:53:48.705
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

01:53:48.685 --> 01:53:51.889
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

01:53:52.229 --> 01:54:01.360
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

01:54:01.380 --> 01:54:07.827
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

01:54:08.528 --> 01:54:14.895
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

01:54:14.875 --> 01:54:17.219
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

01:54:17.239 --> 01:54:18.641
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

01:54:18.981 --> 01:54:27.374
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

01:54:27.755 --> 01:54:39.533
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

01:54:39.766 --> 01:54:41.548
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

01:54:41.848 --> 01:54:45.252
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

01:54:45.332 --> 01:54:53.561
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

01:54:53.621 --> 01:54:59.147
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

01:54:59.207 --> 01:55:02.391
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

01:55:02.411 --> 01:55:09.739
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

01:55:09.719 --> 01:55:16.010
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

01:55:16.731 --> 01:55:19.717
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

01:55:20.157 --> 01:55:25.407
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

01:55:25.867 --> 01:55:32.419
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

01:55:32.399 --> 01:55:35.305
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

01:55:35.486 --> 01:55:37.229
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

01:55:37.550 --> 01:55:51.319
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

01:55:53.088 --> 01:55:59.898
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

01:56:00.098 --> 01:56:03.644
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

01:56:03.964 --> 01:56:07.229
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

01:56:07.770 --> 01:56:10.474
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

01:56:10.514 --> 01:56:12.156
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

01:56:12.477 --> 01:56:16.303
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

01:56:16.743 --> 01:56:19.888
And I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

01:56:19.868 --> 01:56:27.716
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

01:56:28.056 --> 01:56:30.758
We got to really start to loosen that up.

01:56:32.340 --> 01:56:36.924
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

01:56:37.185 --> 01:56:40.668
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

01:56:41.429 --> 01:56:45.893
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

01:56:46.253 --> 01:56:47.935
Everything else is extra credit.

01:56:51.037 --> 01:56:54.780
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

01:56:55.060 --> 01:56:57.643
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

01:56:57.703 --> 01:57:01.666
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

01:57:02.167 --> 01:57:08.772
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

01:57:09.113 --> 01:57:12.776
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

01:57:12.816 --> 01:57:14.217
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

01:57:14.617 --> 01:57:17.740
And I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

01:57:18.080 --> 01:57:19.542
I'm not going too fast yet.

01:57:19.862 --> 01:57:21.043
We're gonna go for 37.

01:57:21.023 --> 01:57:22.205
on the skips.

01:57:22.225 --> 01:57:26.774
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

01:57:26.794 --> 01:57:34.910
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

01:57:35.250 --> 01:57:37.474
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

01:57:37.495 --> 01:57:39.859
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

01:57:39.839 --> 01:57:45.127
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

01:57:45.587 --> 01:57:47.189
We're just getting that blood flowing.

01:57:47.470 --> 01:57:48.812
We're gonna go for 30 seconds.

01:57:48.992 --> 01:57:53.358
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

01:57:53.679 --> 01:57:55.481
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

01:57:55.561 --> 01:58:05.155
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

01:58:05.135 --> 01:58:12.748
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

01:58:16.492 --> 01:58:20.859
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

01:58:21.059 --> 01:58:26.408
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

01:58:26.688 --> 01:58:33.239
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

01:58:33.659 --> 01:58:41.672
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

01:58:41.652 --> 01:58:46.601
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

01:58:46.942 --> 01:58:52.552
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

01:58:53.233 --> 01:58:55.578
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

01:58:55.918 --> 01:59:00.527
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

01:59:01.168 --> 01:59:03.492
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

01:59:04.417 --> 01:59:08.045
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

01:59:08.466 --> 01:59:10.851
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

01:59:10.911 --> 01:59:12.936
One, two, three.

01:59:12.996 --> 01:59:15.261
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

01:59:15.602 --> 01:59:17.606
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

01:59:17.646 --> 01:59:18.248
That's okay.

01:59:18.588 --> 01:59:20.172
Let's just get as low as we can there.

01:59:20.653 --> 01:59:21.615
One, two, three.

01:59:21.595 --> 01:59:23.319
three and back up.

01:59:23.379 --> 01:59:25.383
So three there, three there.

01:59:25.704 --> 01:59:32.017
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

01:59:32.097 --> 01:59:35.545
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

01:59:35.605 --> 01:59:39.553
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor

01:59:39.533 --> 01:59:44.703
Keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

01:59:45.023 --> 01:59:51.936
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

01:59:52.417 --> 01:59:55.483
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom.

01:59:55.463 --> 02:00:17.083
and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

02:00:22.058 --> 02:00:28.585
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

02:00:28.605 --> 02:00:30.067
Don't make the mistake of going here.

02:00:30.427 --> 02:00:38.496
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

02:00:38.596 --> 02:00:44.503
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

02:00:44.563 --> 02:00:48.387
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

02:00:48.367 --> 02:00:50.151
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

02:00:50.411 --> 02:00:52.355
One, two, three.

02:00:52.495 --> 02:00:55.462
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

02:00:55.642 --> 02:00:57.846
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

02:00:57.887 --> 02:01:00.211
One, two, three.

02:01:00.271 --> 02:01:01.775
Rock forward.

02:01:01.855 --> 02:01:02.897
So I'm on my toes now.

02:01:03.378 --> 02:01:05.602
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

02:01:05.643 --> 02:01:05.963
Hold.

02:01:06.605 --> 02:01:06.865
One, two.

02:01:06.845 --> 02:01:26.136
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

02:01:26.470 --> 02:01:35.881
one, two, three, lean, one, two, three, heels back down, one, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

02:01:36.161 --> 02:01:44.470
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

02:01:44.490 --> 02:01:48.855
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

02:01:49.216 --> 02:01:55.583
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

02:01:55.563 --> 02:01:58.367
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

02:01:58.427 --> 02:02:00.750
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

02:02:01.130 --> 02:02:03.934
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

02:02:04.254 --> 02:02:05.956
I need to be flat through the hips.

02:02:06.236 --> 02:02:08.660
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

02:02:09.180 --> 02:02:13.606
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

02:02:13.986 --> 02:02:16.930
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

02:02:16.910 --> 02:02:27.221
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm going to get a really good isometric contraction and I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

02:02:28.282 --> 02:02:34.829
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

02:02:35.249 --> 02:02:39.394
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

02:02:39.414 --> 02:02:41.155
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

02:02:41.416 --> 02:02:43.558
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

02:02:43.538 --> 02:02:47.903
So for our top end speed workout, we're going to keep it very efficient and simple.

02:02:48.244 --> 02:02:55.633
The reason why we're doing this is top end sprint speed exposes the body to a lot of things that we don't get anywhere else, right?

02:02:55.673 --> 02:03:03.002
Like building the proper stiffness through the Achilles and through the quad tendon and the proper compliance through the hip flexor tendons.

02:03:03.363 --> 02:03:08.169
And it kind of builds the tendons exactly how the human body was meant to be.

02:03:08.590 --> 02:03:12.955
And we can't get that through doing basic mobility, basic static stretching.

02:03:12.935 --> 02:03:31.905
and also the ground contact times are so fast that we have to learn how to apply force quicker and quicker and quicker and so we're not just getting faster when we work on top end sprint speed we're also going to improve our vertical jump especially off one leg because the ground contact time is so short

02:03:31.885 --> 02:03:37.033
Now, if you're on a track, basically mark out 54 meters.

02:03:37.354 --> 02:03:43.824
I do 54 meters because it's about a quart and a half, and so that's what I have our guys do, and they've got some pretty good results off that.

02:03:43.864 --> 02:03:48.612
So mark out 54 feet, and then halfway put another cone.

02:03:48.592 --> 02:03:54.779
All right, so you have a cone here, you have a cone halfway, and then you have a cone at that 54 meters.

02:03:54.799 --> 02:03:57.121
So for this phase, we have six sprints.

02:03:57.341 --> 02:04:00.745
All right, so it's six sets of one with a break in between.

02:04:01.206 --> 02:04:05.210
For the first one, I want you to accelerate at 50%.

02:04:05.510 --> 02:04:09.274
So start split stance, and you're just going to accelerate at 50%.

02:04:09.795 --> 02:04:13.038
Once you hit the cone, you're going to then accelerate to 75%.

02:04:14.160 --> 02:04:15.561
So we're not all out yet.

02:04:15.541 --> 02:04:21.494
all the way through that 54 meters, walk it back nice and slow, take a minute, take as long as you need.

02:04:21.895 --> 02:04:24.962
Once you feel good to go, we're back in for that second set.

02:04:25.082 --> 02:04:33.921
Now, we're accelerating at 75%, and then I want you, once you hit that first cone, go up to 90%, alright?

02:04:34.402 --> 02:04:35.625
Walk it back.

02:04:35.605 --> 02:04:39.009
Now the next one, I want you to accelerate at 90%.

02:04:39.990 --> 02:04:42.533
Once you hit the first cone, boost it up to 100%.

02:04:43.614 --> 02:04:46.677
So that's the first one where we're all out walking back.

02:04:46.997 --> 02:04:49.821
The last three are 100% all the way through.

02:04:49.881 --> 02:04:56.748
If you have a friend, keep time, try to beat your time, all three reps, because we need max intent.

02:04:56.808 --> 02:04:58.950
I need you going as fast as you possibly can.

02:04:59.391 --> 02:05:03.115
Week after week, we should be decreasing our sprint time.

02:05:03.095 --> 02:05:17.202
Now, if you cannot get it on a track, I really advise trying to find somewhere where you have that long distance because if you have to do this on a basketball court, that's okay, but you can't reach true top end speed on a basketball court, right?

02:05:17.262 --> 02:05:24.816
By the time we get out of acceleration and we start to raise up into that top end speed, we're already almost getting to that baseline.

02:05:24.796 --> 02:05:27.943
So we already have to decelerate and change directions.

02:05:28.384 --> 02:05:31.570
And so that's a huge thing in basketball and volleyball.

02:05:31.590 --> 02:05:36.060
We never are exposed to top end speed because everything is acceleration.

02:05:36.080 --> 02:05:44.678
But like I said, it's so beneficial to train top end speed because it translates to other qualities such as single leg jumping.

02:05:45.232 --> 02:05:47.096
I really advise trying to find the space.

02:05:47.638 --> 02:05:59.485
If you have to do it on a basketball court, then go ahead and just accelerate early and try to get as fast as we can early on and sprint through the baseline, walk it back.

02:05:59.786 --> 02:06:04.236
You can go up to 10 sets instead of just doing six sets.

02:06:26.204 --> 02:06:31.891
So for our bound two-foot takeoff combo, first of all, I'm going for distance and height.

02:06:31.971 --> 02:06:38.298
So if you're doing it on a basketball court, I would start just inside the high school three-point line, heels on the line.

02:06:38.739 --> 02:06:40.861
I'm gonna start with a broad jump, all right?

02:06:40.941 --> 02:06:46.388
So I'm rocking the arms back, I'm getting a good torso angle, I'm jumping as far forward.

02:06:46.768 --> 02:06:53.856
Now, instead of landing on two, I'm gonna land on one, and I'm gonna stay stiff with that foot and spring into that next bound.

02:06:54.217 --> 02:06:55.198
So I'm gonna jump,

02:06:55.178 --> 02:07:05.834
I'm going to land right, and then I'm going to jump to my left, and from my left, I get straight into my penultimate step, so then it's right, left, take off, all right?

02:07:06.174 --> 02:07:10.701
So we're building a ton of explosiveness, stiffness, reactive strength.

02:07:10.721 --> 02:07:15.448
Most importantly, we're building that rhythm into the two-foot takeoff.

02:07:15.428 --> 02:07:22.781
So it would be as far as I can, right, left, right, left, take off.

02:07:22.801 --> 02:07:27.408
Off this left foot, I don't want you to think about a bound where you're getting as far as you can.

02:07:27.829 --> 02:07:34.821
I just want you to get a little bit of distance and kind of build that rhythm into that long penultimate stride.

02:07:35.341 --> 02:07:38.687
If you're doing it on the court, the further out we can get, the better.

02:07:38.727 --> 02:07:48.927
So if I'm good there and I can get up and, you know, say you can only touch the backboard, if you can get up and touch the backboard, then okay, cool, let's move it back.

02:07:49.047 --> 02:07:52.233
Let's go NBA three and then let's move it back a couple feet.

02:07:52.574 --> 02:07:58.665
If you can get back close to the circle at the half court line, that's pretty impressive, all right?

02:07:58.645 --> 02:08:04.451
I do want you to get max jump height, though, so I need you to touch as high as you can.

02:08:04.812 --> 02:08:06.514
Let's say you can already touch the rim.

02:08:06.794 --> 02:08:08.696
You need to do this with a basketball, all right?

02:08:08.976 --> 02:08:18.267
And I need you to get up there and throw down some dunks, try some different dunks that you've never worked on before, and let's try to really push ourselves to get that max jump height.

02:08:18.487 --> 02:08:20.469
So, of course, we're going to switch sides on this.

02:08:20.509 --> 02:08:27.437
So if I said three sets of two, that means two broad right, left, right,

02:08:28.210 --> 02:08:31.941
two of those, and then two on the other side to complete one set.

02:08:32.161 --> 02:08:34.408
So then the other side would be just the opposite.

02:08:34.448 --> 02:08:42.591
So it would be off two, land left, right, left, right, turn stance that way, get up probably left hand.

02:09:14.870 --> 02:09:17.532
for our power skip single leg takeoff.

02:09:17.552 --> 02:09:25.900
This is a fun combination exercise that builds single leg jumping ability, single leg explosiveness, and builds rhythm into our single leg takeoff.

02:09:25.920 --> 02:09:29.022
So what I wanna do is I wanna get four total skips.

02:09:29.383 --> 02:09:37.029
So if I'm starting at half court, if I'm skipping far forward, I'm not gonna be able to get in four skips and an approach.

02:09:37.049 --> 02:09:41.714
So I need to think about getting up and just getting forward a couple feet with each step.

02:09:42.154 --> 02:09:44.556
Okay, so I'm high as I can,

02:09:44.536 --> 02:09:48.620
jump right, land right, straight into that next stride, jump as high as I can.

02:09:48.680 --> 02:09:59.973
And then after my four, so two each side, I land and I take off as fast as I can straight into that single leg takeoff.

02:10:00.473 --> 02:10:03.997
Again, if you can touch the rim, get up and try to grab the rim.

02:10:04.057 --> 02:10:09.002
If you can already grab the rim, do it with the basketball and try to end with the dunk, all right?

02:10:09.423 --> 02:10:11.645
Challenge yourself on that portion.

02:10:11.785 --> 02:10:14.188
So if we had three sets of two,

02:10:14.168 --> 02:10:25.191
it would be left lane line going off the left foot and then it would be two on the right lane line coming middle going off the right foot.

02:10:27.280 --> 02:10:44.984
Now for our freestyle dunks, I put this in there because it's so important that you get out there and just allow your body to explore different approaches, different type of dunks, different type of jumping styles without me always trying to put you in a box and trying to get you to do one thing.

02:10:45.304 --> 02:10:50.892
So I want you to set the clock for 10 minutes and I just want you to go get as many dunks as you can.

02:10:50.872 --> 02:10:52.816
Take breaks in between if you need to.

02:10:52.876 --> 02:10:55.400
I do want you to be recovered in between reps.

02:10:55.420 --> 02:10:57.464
This isn't an endurance workout.

02:10:57.825 --> 02:10:59.167
So get up off one foot.

02:10:59.207 --> 02:11:00.029
Throw it down.

02:11:00.490 --> 02:11:01.091
Walk back.

02:11:01.111 --> 02:11:03.255
Recover for 30 seconds to a minute.

02:11:03.575 --> 02:11:04.237
Come back in.

02:11:04.357 --> 02:11:05.499
Take off off two.

02:11:05.940 --> 02:11:07.142
Try to dunk left.

02:11:07.122 --> 02:11:10.206
Whatever you want to do, get creative and do it.

02:11:10.466 --> 02:11:14.512
Of course, if you can't dunk and all you can do is touch the bottom of the net, I don't care.

02:11:14.532 --> 02:11:15.794
I still want you to get creative.

02:11:16.154 --> 02:11:17.676
Come in off two and touch the net.

02:11:17.996 --> 02:11:20.600
Come from baseline, get up off one and touch the net.

02:11:20.920 --> 02:11:23.904
Try to jump off left, touch with the left.

02:11:24.325 --> 02:11:33.417
Variability, variety is the key to longevity and it's super important for building kind of well-rounded explosiveness and athleticism.

02:11:33.397 --> 02:11:41.168
And so I want you to get creative, set the clock for 10 minutes and just freestyle dunk.

02:11:50.081 --> 02:11:51.984
For this exercise, I want to double up the band.

02:11:52.024 --> 02:11:53.546
So I'm going around the power rack.

02:11:53.566 --> 02:11:55.089
I just don't want one side of the band.

02:11:55.109 --> 02:11:57.392
I want both the bands so that I have extra tension.

02:11:57.652 --> 02:12:01.598
Or if you have a friend who's holding you back, don't go through like this.

02:12:02.557 --> 02:12:21.291
both sides are going to go around you and then your friend would hold you back from there because we want a little extra tension on this one the other key point for the setup is we do need something to hold on to maybe you have a box that i can lean into maybe i have a wall that i can lean into or you could do this in a power rack so i'm going to walk forward

02:12:21.490 --> 02:12:22.512
And I'm gonna grab on.

02:12:23.134 --> 02:12:25.379
I'm gonna bring my heel just off the floor.

02:12:25.399 --> 02:12:27.624
I should be able to slide a piece of paper underneath that heel.

02:12:28.226 --> 02:12:29.849
And then I just come to full hip extension.

02:12:30.110 --> 02:12:33.037
I'm bringing this leg close to parallel, but not quite to parallel.

02:12:33.257 --> 02:12:35.803
Top of the calf touching the hamstring.

02:12:36.087 --> 02:13:00.677
and i'm just holding in this position it's a maximal squeeze in this right glute now the whole concept here is we can't work a muscle and we can't get power out of that muscle unless i can feel that muscle and so this teaches you how to really feel that glute it also strengthens the glute in the most important position because in things like upright sprinting that glute needs to contract its hardest near and at hip extension so i'm here

02:13:00.944 --> 02:13:24.387
full hip extension get up squeeze that glute as hard as i possibly can make sure that we do have the toe pointed straight forward and i'm just paused in this position squeezing that glute as hard as i can if you do it the right way you'll feel a huge burn in the glute you might almost feel like it's going to cramp up because you're not used to getting a hundred percent maximal contraction that's completely okay

02:13:31.893 --> 02:13:36.379
So for my single leg box step down, I'm gonna go on a box around knee height.

02:13:36.899 --> 02:13:39.523
Basically, I'm going to hold a light dumbbell.

02:13:39.563 --> 02:13:42.567
So I can go 15s in each hand at the most.

02:13:42.587 --> 02:13:47.913
So what I want you to do with the dumbbells is as we go down, I want you to kick it forward.

02:13:48.294 --> 02:13:53.601
So if you keep them down by your hip, you're not gonna have the right counterbalance and you're gonna fall backwards.

02:13:53.661 --> 02:13:57.426
So as you're going down, the dumbbells go higher and higher and higher.

02:13:57.766 --> 02:14:00.870
And then once that foot taps and we're on the way up, it's the opposite.

02:14:00.850 --> 02:14:04.736
we come up with the lower body and we go down with the weight.

02:14:04.756 --> 02:14:06.799
So you're using it as a counterbalance.

02:14:07.400 --> 02:14:13.369
So I want you to go on the edge of that box, make sure that box is heavy enough so you're not tipping over.

02:14:13.689 --> 02:14:18.657
If you need to, put some dumbbells on the side of that box to counterbalance.

02:14:18.637 --> 02:14:21.322
So I'm just going to drop down normal squat mechanics.

02:14:21.362 --> 02:14:23.325
I got a break from the hip first.

02:14:23.365 --> 02:14:25.970
So it's a hip hinge and then I go down with the knee.

02:14:26.350 --> 02:14:30.036
I do not want your knee going too far over your toe on this drill.

02:14:30.417 --> 02:14:31.279
So it's going to be here.

02:14:31.299 --> 02:14:35.346
Tap that heel and explode up.

02:14:35.406 --> 02:14:37.509
I do not want you to push off the ground.

02:14:37.830 --> 02:14:39.733
I just want you to tap that heel.

02:14:41.013 --> 02:14:41.794
and explode.

02:14:41.834 --> 02:14:46.602
It's gonna be two to three seconds down and then as fast as we can on the way up.

02:14:46.622 --> 02:14:49.707
So you could start maybe five, tens, or 15 pounders.

02:14:50.087 --> 02:14:53.513
If you do wanna load heavier, let's go with that vest.

02:14:53.533 --> 02:15:01.806
If it's taking you over a second to get up, then you're loaded too much because I wanna see you get up fast so that it translates to our high speed strength.

02:15:25.698 --> 02:15:29.724
For our 7-Eleven breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

02:15:29.844 --> 02:15:31.627
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

02:15:31.687 --> 02:15:34.391
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

02:15:34.431 --> 02:15:40.920
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

02:15:41.461 --> 02:15:47.409
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

02:15:47.690 --> 02:15:51.435
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

02:15:51.415 --> 02:16:07.517
okay so i'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill all right so it's in through the nose and i want to make sure that my stomach is rising okay your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

02:16:08.510 --> 02:16:11.574
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

02:16:11.594 --> 02:16:18.304
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

02:16:18.865 --> 02:16:24.653
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

02:16:24.673 --> 02:16:28.339
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

02:16:30.642 --> 02:16:34.888
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

02:16:37.838 --> 02:16:39.761
and that stomach will fall, okay?

02:16:40.482 --> 02:16:43.145
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

02:16:43.886 --> 02:16:52.919
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

02:16:53.700 --> 02:17:01.691
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

02:17:01.711 --> 02:17:05.736
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

02:17:05.716 --> 02:17:34.626
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

02:17:34.859 --> 02:17:37.383
happens in the parasympathetic state.

02:17:37.924 --> 02:17:42.052
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

02:17:42.412 --> 02:17:55.956
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

02:17:57.843 --> 02:18:01.148
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

02:18:01.168 --> 02:18:05.454
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

02:18:05.734 --> 02:18:12.544
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

02:18:12.844 --> 02:18:16.329
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's OK.

02:18:16.609 --> 02:18:24.120
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

02:18:24.100 --> 02:18:28.451
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

02:18:28.872 --> 02:18:30.857
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

02:18:30.877 --> 02:18:33.383
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

02:18:33.664 --> 02:18:35.729
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

02:18:36.009 --> 02:18:38.235
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

02:18:38.536 --> 02:18:40.320
I'm just gonna lean forward.

02:18:40.300 --> 02:18:43.004
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

02:18:43.325 --> 02:18:50.476
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

02:18:50.496 --> 02:18:55.324
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

02:18:56.646 --> 02:19:01.093
And I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

02:19:01.394 --> 02:19:03.257
15 seconds on the other side as well.

02:19:03.377 --> 02:19:05.520
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

02:19:05.700 --> 02:19:07.122
So that is the back of the thigh.

02:19:07.362 --> 02:19:11.668
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

02:19:11.688 --> 02:19:15.374
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

02:19:15.534 --> 02:19:17.957
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

02:19:18.298 --> 02:19:20.621
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

02:19:20.981 --> 02:19:22.544
So now look at this back foot.

02:19:22.564 --> 02:19:24.246
I'm not curling the toe under.

02:19:24.226 --> 02:19:31.878
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor, and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

02:19:32.299 --> 02:19:37.667
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

02:19:37.968 --> 02:19:46.521
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down

02:19:46.501 --> 02:19:51.049
to the elbows, and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

02:19:51.469 --> 02:19:55.877
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

02:19:56.358 --> 02:20:01.026
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side, same thing.

02:20:01.346 --> 02:20:04.231
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

02:20:04.531 --> 02:20:07.817
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

02:20:07.797 --> 02:20:11.804
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

02:20:12.085 --> 02:20:19.739
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

02:20:20.120 --> 02:20:25.610
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

02:20:26.011 --> 02:20:31.341
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

02:20:31.321 --> 02:20:36.047
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

02:20:36.447 --> 02:20:44.097
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

02:20:44.598 --> 02:20:47.401
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

02:20:47.862 --> 02:20:53.869
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

02:20:53.849 --> 02:20:56.914
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

02:20:56.994 --> 02:20:59.137
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

02:20:59.397 --> 02:21:03.063
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

02:21:03.523 --> 02:21:05.106
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

02:21:05.126 --> 02:21:08.050
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

02:21:08.110 --> 02:21:09.012
So I'm 90 there.

02:21:09.372 --> 02:21:12.156
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

02:21:12.517 --> 02:21:14.820
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

02:21:15.181 --> 02:21:19.888
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

02:21:20.228 --> 02:21:23.333
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

02:21:23.313 --> 02:21:26.239
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

02:21:26.600 --> 02:21:31.370
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

02:21:31.410 --> 02:21:32.854
All right.

02:21:32.874 --> 02:21:38.766
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

02:21:39.508 --> 02:21:43.296
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

02:21:43.276 --> 02:21:48.623
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

02:21:49.003 --> 02:21:52.228
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

02:21:52.328 --> 02:21:55.492
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

02:21:55.892 --> 02:22:05.605
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

02:22:05.922 --> 02:22:07.764
So I do have a straight knee.

02:22:08.165 --> 02:22:10.668
So we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius.

02:22:10.708 --> 02:22:12.390
That's the outside calf muscle.

02:22:12.670 --> 02:22:18.858
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

02:22:19.218 --> 02:22:23.283
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

02:22:24.484 --> 02:22:27.508
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

02:22:27.568 --> 02:22:28.289
That's okay.

02:22:28.649 --> 02:22:31.833
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

02:22:32.314 --> 02:22:35.878
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

02:22:38.812 --> 02:22:44.503
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase two, day two and day four.

02:22:45.184 --> 02:22:52.138
So if we're already mobile and flexible, we can take out all of these mobility drills and skip straight to the core.

02:22:52.899 --> 02:22:58.069
Do not skip anything on the core or anything on the feet or the ankles.

02:22:58.049 --> 02:23:03.843
It's absolutely essential that we build up our feet, our ankles, and our core in this phase.

02:23:04.084 --> 02:23:05.708
It's going to help you out long term.

02:23:05.988 --> 02:23:11.101
And if you have the time and the energy, we can always do this day again on Saturday.

02:23:11.161 --> 02:23:15.231
So if you have the time and energy, do Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday.

02:23:15.531 --> 02:23:16.193
Let's get it.

02:23:16.308 --> 02:23:17.872
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

02:23:18.012 --> 02:23:19.717
I have a long roller from Skills.

02:23:19.977 --> 02:23:27.195
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

02:23:27.215 --> 02:23:32.629
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

02:23:32.609 --> 02:23:35.692
So you can either go this way or you could go vertical.

02:23:36.053 --> 02:23:45.183
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

02:23:45.203 --> 02:23:51.630
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

02:23:52.351 --> 02:23:58.718
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

02:23:58.698 --> 02:24:01.041
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

02:24:01.061 --> 02:24:02.463
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

02:24:02.804 --> 02:24:11.195
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

02:24:11.576 --> 02:24:23.372
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

02:24:23.588 --> 02:24:25.370
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

02:24:25.670 --> 02:24:29.074
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

02:24:29.154 --> 02:24:37.384
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

02:24:37.444 --> 02:24:42.970
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

02:24:43.050 --> 02:24:46.214
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

02:24:46.234 --> 02:24:53.562
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

02:24:53.542 --> 02:24:59.832
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

02:25:00.553 --> 02:25:03.537
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

02:25:03.978 --> 02:25:09.226
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

02:25:09.687 --> 02:25:16.157
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

02:25:16.137 --> 02:25:19.121
Our next one is going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

02:25:19.321 --> 02:25:21.063
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

02:25:21.364 --> 02:25:35.142
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

02:25:36.910 --> 02:25:43.720
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

02:25:43.921 --> 02:25:47.466
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

02:25:47.786 --> 02:25:51.031
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

02:25:51.592 --> 02:25:54.296
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

02:25:54.316 --> 02:25:55.999
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

02:25:56.299 --> 02:26:00.285
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

02:26:00.265 --> 02:26:03.230
and I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

02:26:03.791 --> 02:26:14.730
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping, we gotta really start to loosen that up.

02:26:16.077 --> 02:26:20.750
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

02:26:20.991 --> 02:26:24.520
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

02:26:24.560 --> 02:26:29.735
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

02:26:30.096 --> 02:26:31.740
Everything else is extra credit.

02:26:34.910 --> 02:26:58.690
okay so for our lateral line stretch we want to get in a power rack or have something to hold on to you can even use like a trx or you can do this without anything to hold on to i'll show you that regression so basically what we're doing is i'm going to step in here if i'm holding on with my left hand at about belly button height then i'm going to take my left foot

02:26:58.872 --> 02:27:00.434
over the right foot.

02:27:01.455 --> 02:27:06.640
Now I'm going to take my right hand over the head and I'm going to grab onto that power rack.

02:27:07.721 --> 02:27:10.604
Already I feel a little bit of a stretch through that lateral line.

02:27:10.704 --> 02:27:17.732
So it's through the outside of this leg and then all the way up through that lat.

02:27:18.072 --> 02:27:23.958
So I'm going to push away with my left hand and I'm going to pull with my right hand.

02:27:24.279 --> 02:27:26.601
So it's push, pull

02:27:27.020 --> 02:27:35.103
into that stretch, I'm gonna feel it big time through that lateral aspect of the hip, through that oblique area, and then into the lats.

02:27:35.123 --> 02:27:42.042
This stretch is so important for athleticism because our fascial system is connected all the way from the feet,

02:27:42.022 --> 02:28:04.114
all the way up through the shoulder everything is connected and so when we do stretches in isolation it doesn't truly work so we have to stretch the whole body as a system to really get good mobility gains and really build that good fascial health so then remember on the other side it would be right over left push away with the right hand

02:28:04.094 --> 02:28:28.785
pull with the left hand and I'm only going to hold for a couple seconds and then I'm going to come back to neutral and then grab with the left hand one two and come back to neutral so we're making it dynamic this isn't just a static stretch now if you don't have anything to hold on to you can also do this without the rack it's not going to be as good but we can still cross over right over left and then just pretend like we're here

02:28:28.765 --> 02:28:30.972
and shift as far as I can.

02:28:31.012 --> 02:28:39.079
I'm thinking about pushing my hip out this way and getting my left hand as far over my head as I can and then coming back.

02:28:58.482 --> 02:29:06.248
If you don't have the ankle mobility to keep your heels down and sit in that bottom position, then I want you to grab the weight

02:29:06.903 --> 02:29:09.808
and I want you to come into a deep squat without the heels on the ground.

02:29:10.249 --> 02:29:20.888
So I would just come forward, my knees are over the toes, and now from here, elbows are resting on the knees, and I'm just trying to sit the heels down as low as I can.

02:29:21.689 --> 02:29:23.552
If you can get the heels down, great.

02:29:23.873 --> 02:29:31.847
Then we're going to just rock side to side, trying to drive that knee right over the middle of my foot.

02:29:31.827 --> 02:30:00.501
and we're just gonna rock there now if you can't get your heels down just get as far as you can all right so i'm pushing my heels as close as i can but they are off the ground now from here i'm just gonna rock side to side all right and we're still gonna get ankle mobility even though the heels aren't on the ground and week after week we can start to get the heels closer and closer until we're able to really sit with a flat foot and rock side to side and get that good ankle mobility

02:30:13.947 --> 02:30:17.332
So for our hamstring floss, we're just going to go lying on our back.

02:30:17.352 --> 02:30:26.846
I want to make sure that my left leg stays down, so I'm actively pushing this left knee through the ground, pushing my left heel down through the ground, and I'm just going to pull back.

02:30:26.966 --> 02:30:34.437
So here in this position, I'm going to feel a little bit of a stretch in my glute, a little bit of a stretch in that opposite hip flexor, and then I'm just

02:30:34.417 --> 02:30:38.144
Extending that knee and coming back into flexion.

02:30:38.164 --> 02:30:40.048
So it's extension, flexion.

02:30:40.128 --> 02:30:42.372
So we call this hamstring flop.

02:30:42.653 --> 02:30:46.841
We're gradually trying to get that range of motion bigger and bigger.

02:30:47.222 --> 02:30:50.468
In each rep, we're trying to extend that knee more and more.

02:31:04.370 --> 02:31:07.974
So for the supine kickover, supine means facing up.

02:31:08.154 --> 02:31:11.838
So we're laying on our back and I'm just gonna kick over this way.

02:31:11.898 --> 02:31:14.321
So I'm basically swinging my leg across.

02:31:14.742 --> 02:31:21.269
I am trying to keep that knee as straight as I can and I'm just gonna tap that toe as high as I can, okay?

02:31:21.289 --> 02:31:26.935
And then I'm gonna bring it back the other way and I'm gonna tap my toe on this side as high as I can.

02:31:26.915 --> 02:31:30.702
Okay, so it's over the body and then open it up.

02:31:30.782 --> 02:31:32.706
And we're just getting a gradual swing.

02:31:33.147 --> 02:31:38.497
Each rep, try to loosen up more and more and really swing that foot as high as we can.

02:31:38.717 --> 02:31:47.353
I'm going to feel a big stretch on the outside of the hips and I'm going to feel a big stretch on the inside of the legs when I'm opening up to that side.

02:32:20.577 --> 02:32:24.342
For the 90-90 hip opener, I'm just going to start in a 90-90 position.

02:32:24.422 --> 02:32:35.657
So that means I'm sitting here, I'm going to roll over towards my left glute, and I'm going to get in a 90-degree position with that knee, and then this back leg is also going to be 90 degrees.

02:32:36.077 --> 02:32:40.543
So we have 90 here, we have 90 here, and we have 90 here.

02:32:40.844 --> 02:32:44.727
Okay, from there, I'm gonna square my chest with this knee.

02:32:45.148 --> 02:32:46.589
So I'm nice and tall right here.

02:32:47.030 --> 02:32:49.452
Now from there, my left hand is back.

02:32:49.852 --> 02:32:55.417
I'm gonna thread my right arm underneath, and the ultimate goal is to get the right shoulder down to the floor.

02:32:55.757 --> 02:32:59.501
A lot of you might not have that mobility yet, but just get as low as we can comfortably.

02:32:59.881 --> 02:33:01.082
So I'm gonna thread through.

02:33:01.122 --> 02:33:07.188
One, two, and then I'm gonna roll to the next side.

02:33:07.288 --> 02:33:08.809
So hand down.

02:33:09.143 --> 02:33:18.383
I get into this position, same thing, 90, 90, 90, and then I thread my left hand through, shoulder to the ground.

02:33:18.784 --> 02:33:25.178
I should feel that stretch sort of in the outside of the hip, and we're just going to flow straight through.

02:33:29.528 --> 02:33:34.916
Okay, so after the desired reps there, then I'm going back to my original position, 90-90.

02:33:35.096 --> 02:33:37.560
I'm gonna have both hands on the floor.

02:33:38.081 --> 02:33:39.683
I'm gonna keep this leg grounded.

02:33:39.723 --> 02:33:46.533
I cannot lift this knee or this ankle off the ground, and I'm just gonna raise as high as I can and back down.

02:33:46.913 --> 02:33:48.075
So now it's just an opener.

02:33:48.115 --> 02:33:52.301
A lot of you will go here, and the tendency is to open up here.

02:33:52.361 --> 02:33:53.623
We gotta stay grounded.

02:33:54.044 --> 02:33:58.270
My goal is to get the sole of that foot flat on the floor.

02:33:59.060 --> 02:34:03.306
So I wanna get all the way flat if possible and back down.

02:34:04.248 --> 02:34:06.951
After the desired reps, I'm flowing straight through.

02:34:06.992 --> 02:34:10.797
Same thing, I'm down and open.

02:34:13.701 --> 02:34:16.485
We should feel that stretch on the opener in here.

02:34:16.746 --> 02:34:21.112
So inside of the leg that's grounded, inside of the leg that's moving as well.

02:34:22.214 --> 02:34:24.477
Really opening up those hips.

02:34:40.673 --> 02:34:44.100
For the hip flexor tilt, I'm just gonna use a box of bench or a chair.

02:34:44.821 --> 02:34:49.651
I'm gonna get my back foot up so the top of my foot is on the bench.

02:34:49.691 --> 02:34:51.274
I'm not gonna go with my toe on the bench.

02:34:51.675 --> 02:34:54.220
Top of the foot on the bench, I'm just gonna come down.

02:34:54.560 --> 02:34:58.969
Now I wanna get my heel touching my hips or as close as I can get to that.

02:34:58.949 --> 02:35:00.952
If you have a pad, that'd be better.

02:35:00.992 --> 02:35:02.294
I wouldn't do this on hardwood.

02:35:02.474 --> 02:35:04.156
Make sure you got a nice soft surface.

02:35:04.657 --> 02:35:05.418
So I'm touching.

02:35:05.818 --> 02:35:09.684
I'm in a straight line from my knee all the way up through my shoulders.

02:35:10.224 --> 02:35:11.386
And I'm just going to sit here.

02:35:11.526 --> 02:35:13.088
First, I'm going to get in a good position.

02:35:13.589 --> 02:35:15.532
This ankle should be under that knee.

02:35:16.193 --> 02:35:19.998
I should already feel a little bit of a stretch in that hip flexor, a little bit in the quad as well.

02:35:20.418 --> 02:35:22.922
Now from here, I'm getting nice and wide.

02:35:22.942 --> 02:35:24.104
I'm not going to lean forward.

02:35:24.444 --> 02:35:26.627
And I'm going arms out.

02:35:26.607 --> 02:35:40.431
and together now at the top with straight elbows not bent elbows at the top i'm going to lean towards the lead leg so that's to my right as far as i can hold for five

02:35:40.799 --> 02:35:58.015
then come right back okay and then we're hands down all the way up hands together nice and tall elongate through the spine and lean towards that lead leg three two one back down and we're back through

02:36:17.485 --> 02:36:21.875
For the kneeling adductor pulse, I'm gonna go half kneel position.

02:36:22.537 --> 02:36:25.163
I'm going to point the toe out so I'm not curled.

02:36:25.363 --> 02:36:26.366
I'm pointed like this.

02:36:26.406 --> 02:36:29.453
Top of the foot is down towards the floor.

02:36:30.766 --> 02:36:33.150
I'm gonna go straight out with this leg.

02:36:33.170 --> 02:36:35.093
This knee's gonna be nice and straight.

02:36:35.113 --> 02:36:37.477
Okay, from here, I'm gonna do some rocks.

02:36:37.537 --> 02:36:38.979
So I'm gonna rock straight back.

02:36:39.580 --> 02:36:43.807
I need my hips to touch my heels or as close as you can.

02:36:44.268 --> 02:36:49.095
And you're gonna really feel that stretch in the adductor and you'll feel a certain angle in that adductor.

02:36:49.155 --> 02:36:52.481
And now we're gonna switch that angle by rocking forward.

02:36:52.801 --> 02:36:57.128
So I'm gonna rock back one second and then I'm gonna creep the hands forward.

02:36:57.850 --> 02:37:21.765
nice and far until I get close to full hip extension and then creep it back so when I go forward I get a different angle of those adductors and then when I go back I get the complete opposite side of the muscle so I'm back and I'm forward as far as I can all right make sure in this position

02:37:22.673 --> 02:37:27.264
where I'm rocking forward, I'm getting to that full hip extension.

02:37:27.284 --> 02:37:32.316
So some people stay tucked right there or some people go too far where they're in hip hyperextension.

02:37:32.917 --> 02:37:39.433
I just need to get all the way out until I'm in a straight line from that knee all the way up through that shoulder.

02:38:03.038 --> 02:38:08.107
So for our standing hip CARS, CARS stands for Controlled Articular Rotation.

02:38:08.427 --> 02:38:12.254
So this is a FRC concept, Functional Range Conditioning.

02:38:12.294 --> 02:38:22.412
So this is getting us nice and mobile, but building stability at these short and long muscle lengths, which prevents injuries, helps us get more explosive.

02:38:22.432 --> 02:38:26.599
So it's a little bit different in your normal type of stretching.

02:38:26.579 --> 02:38:31.004
Rule number one, we've got to create tension through the body, tension through the upper body.

02:38:31.024 --> 02:38:35.068
So I need you to squeeze the fist and keep a tight core in this whole drill.

02:38:35.929 --> 02:38:42.396
From here, I'm just going to lift up as high as I can with that right knee.

02:38:42.837 --> 02:38:48.543
And then from there, instead of a normal hip circle where we're down like that, I'm going to turn the foot outwards.

02:38:49.324 --> 02:38:53.869
So I'm going to turn the foot out, and then I'm going to kick it around

02:38:53.849 --> 02:38:56.414
and bring it down, and then I'm gonna reverse that.

02:38:56.474 --> 02:39:02.264
So now I'm gonna kick back as far as I can, and then I'm bringing it all the way back around.

02:39:02.605 --> 02:39:09.597
So I like to pretend like there's a really high fence right here, and I gotta step over that imaginary fence.

02:39:09.577 --> 02:39:38.942
and then bring it down some of you balance is going to be an issue here it's okay if you want to hold on to something hold on to two poles or hold on to a power rack whatever you got to do all that matters here is that we're getting the full range of motion through that hip joint so i don't want to leave any range of motion left in the tank i want you to really get every single range of motion so that we can build that stability at those ranges and really free up your hips for more movement

02:40:06.108 --> 02:40:35.507
my overhead deep squat progression so I'm just gonna start nice and high my feet are a little wider than shoulder width apart and I'm just gonna come into a hamstring stretch okay back nice and flat I get as low as I can hold one two three from here I get my hands to the ground and I'm trying to come into a deep squat I'm gonna hold in this position for three seconds I'm gonna lean forward okay push my knees out with my elbows hold one three

02:40:35.487 --> 02:40:47.404
two, three, rock back, get my heels down to the ground, three seconds here, and then raise one overhead, raise two overhead, and stand.

02:40:47.705 --> 02:40:53.293
Okay, so that's one rep, we have three reps, so now I'm cycling straight back into it.

02:40:53.273 --> 02:40:54.716
Okay, so it's three seconds there.

02:40:55.157 --> 02:40:56.419
Deep squat, three seconds.

02:40:56.800 --> 02:40:58.283
Lean forward, three seconds.

02:40:58.683 --> 02:40:59.986
Rock it back, three seconds.

02:41:00.266 --> 02:41:01.789
One up, two up, stand.

02:41:02.130 --> 02:41:04.555
If you can't get in that deep squat position, it's okay.

02:41:04.815 --> 02:41:10.506
Let's just try to get as low as we possibly can and gradually get better every time we do this warmup.

02:41:31.715 --> 02:41:37.043
So for our stability ball, stir the pot, I'm going to go in a normal plank position on the stability ball.

02:41:37.284 --> 02:41:43.133
If you're a beginner and that is too hard already, then just hold in that position and forget the exercise.

02:41:43.153 --> 02:41:44.395
Just hold for 30 seconds.

02:41:44.996 --> 02:41:49.143
But assuming that you're intermediate or advanced, we'll go on to the next progression.

02:41:49.223 --> 02:41:53.149
So I need the elbows directly underneath the shoulder.

02:41:53.129 --> 02:41:55.094
I'm coming into that flat position.

02:41:55.374 --> 02:41:57.279
To make it easier, we go feet wider.

02:41:57.580 --> 02:42:00.326
To make it harder, we go feet more narrow.

02:42:00.707 --> 02:42:07.864
Now from here, I'm staying flat all the way through, and I'm just going to make circles, big controlled circles.

02:42:09.430 --> 02:42:14.036
That way, once I finish the desired reps, I get to the other side.

02:42:14.456 --> 02:42:17.881
Once I finish there, I go straight front back.

02:42:18.301 --> 02:42:26.331
All right, so it's stir the pot this way, stir the pot the other way, and then a rocker forwards, backwards.

02:42:26.611 --> 02:42:28.474
We're going to light up that front core.

02:42:28.754 --> 02:42:35.202
We're going to get a ton of the intrinsic core muscles fired up to stabilize that spine as we're moving on the ball.

02:42:57.693 --> 02:43:02.960
So for my ball of the foot, one leg turns, I'm going to come to the ball of my foot.

02:43:03.021 --> 02:43:04.943
I cannot have my heel down on the ground.

02:43:05.324 --> 02:43:09.470
So we're decreasing that surface area, increasing the stability needs.

02:43:09.850 --> 02:43:13.175
So I'm here pointing that big foot down into the ground.

02:43:13.355 --> 02:43:15.178
First, just find your balance.

02:43:15.238 --> 02:43:19.003
Now, if you find it there, we are coming into an RDL.

02:43:19.424 --> 02:43:21.547
Try to get the torso flat to the ground.

02:43:21.887 --> 02:43:23.970
And now from here, I'm just doing turns.

02:43:23.950 --> 02:43:28.617
All right, as big as I can back to neutral and then to the other side.

02:43:28.977 --> 02:43:40.534
Now those turns might be very small like this to start, but as you get better, I want you to do full turns, full rotations up as high as we can.

02:43:40.574 --> 02:43:50.768
All right, you're going to feel the feet completely burning from the toes all the way back up through the heels, back up through the calves, lateral aspects of the calves.

02:43:50.748 --> 02:43:57.130
The lower leg and the feet are going to be firing the outside muscles and those small intrinsic muscles.

02:44:18.457 --> 02:44:24.005
For my stability ball roll up, I can either use a stability ball or I could just use a basketball.

02:44:24.506 --> 02:44:31.677
I need this foot, my right foot off the ground about six inches, this leg straight up in the air and point the toe.

02:44:32.038 --> 02:44:33.780
If I can have it at 90 degrees, great.

02:44:34.101 --> 02:44:37.005
Most of you won't, it'll be a little bit lower.

02:44:36.985 --> 02:44:38.728
And you're just going to pin opposite.

02:44:38.808 --> 02:44:40.972
So left leg high, right hand.

02:44:41.373 --> 02:44:48.425
And now I'm just going to gradually move it up about six inches with each hand until it reaches the toe.

02:44:48.846 --> 02:44:53.413
And then I bring it back down until I'm all the way back down to the ground with my head.

02:44:53.774 --> 02:44:58.943
All right, so it's here all the way up to the toe and right back down.

02:45:03.479 --> 02:45:10.672
Alright, once I finish the desired reps with that left leg, right hand, I switch and I go right leg, left hand.

02:45:10.712 --> 02:45:16.463
Alright, we're going to feel a big burn in that front core, anterior abdominal muscles get fired up.

02:45:16.523 --> 02:45:24.778
This is a good one to actually get your front core nice and ripped if your body fat is low enough, your abs are going to start to pop out a little bit with this exercise.

02:45:41.482 --> 02:45:47.629
So for the arch squats, we're gonna be working on the intrinsic muscles of our feet, extremely important for our athleticism.

02:45:48.029 --> 02:45:52.193
So if I were to just put my foot normal, I have a little arch, but it's nothing much.

02:45:52.514 --> 02:45:54.776
Right now, I wanna exaggerate that arch in my foot.

02:45:55.016 --> 02:45:57.659
So what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna pull my toes up.

02:45:58.060 --> 02:46:02.524
Notice, just by pulling my toes up in the air, I already established a pretty good arch.

02:46:03.065 --> 02:46:05.327
Now, I have three points of contact.

02:46:05.628 --> 02:46:10.813
That's below the ball of my foot, below the pinky toe, and my heel.

02:46:10.793 --> 02:46:12.174
So we call that the tripod.

02:46:12.495 --> 02:46:14.316
So those are the only things touching the ground.

02:46:14.396 --> 02:46:15.858
I already have that good arch.

02:46:15.878 --> 02:46:22.023
Now, between the ball of my big toe and my heel, I'm thinking about contracting or shortening it.

02:46:22.103 --> 02:46:27.628
So I'm pulling, almost like I'm gripping the ground and I'm building that arch even more.

02:46:28.009 --> 02:46:34.815
You'll feel a burn on the top of your foot and the tendons on the top of your foot will start to kind of pop out a little bit, it seems like.

02:46:35.395 --> 02:46:40.480
Now, from there, I'm gonna hold that position, which is hard if you don't have that strength.

02:46:40.460 --> 02:46:49.197
So I'm doing that with both feet, toes up, squeeze the floor, really shorten, almost like I'm pulling my toes back in towards my foot.

02:46:49.838 --> 02:46:51.080
And then I'm just going to squat.

02:46:51.601 --> 02:46:53.545
I'm going to get down pretty close to parallel.

02:46:53.946 --> 02:46:57.793
And then on the way up, I'm going to push my toes in and reverse it.

02:46:57.773 --> 02:47:03.659
Okay, so I got my arch, I'm squatting, I'm gonna push my toe in and reverse it.

02:47:04.140 --> 02:47:19.236
So when I'm going to come up, my first movement is toes down, like I'm gripping the floor or curling my toes into the ground as hard as I can, and that's gonna build a connection between the toes and the post chain, especially the toes and the glutes.

02:47:19.817 --> 02:47:27.105
Okay, so this is gonna feel kind of awkward at first, but once you get that toe press, you're gonna really start to activate more glutes in the squat.

02:47:27.085 --> 02:47:35.083
Okay, so again, toes up, build that arch, scrunch between the ball of the big toe and the heel.

02:47:35.464 --> 02:47:36.506
I got that good arch.

02:47:37.007 --> 02:47:43.221
I'm gonna squat slow and press the toe, get up, squeeze those glutes at the top, and then I just go again.

02:47:45.074 --> 02:48:06.458
push come up toes up squeeze build that arch and go okay so you should feel that burn a little bit in the arch mostly on the top of the foot on the front of the ankle maybe even up into the front of the shins a little bit there's a lot of muscles in that region of the lower leg that pull the arch up

02:48:06.438 --> 02:48:18.017
So we wanna build that arch and we wanna strengthen those intrinsic muscles of the feet to really strengthen up and become stable and build that base for explosiveness later on.

02:48:33.337 --> 02:48:39.264
So for my prone pull through, I'm just gonna use a dumbbell or if you have like a sandbag or even a medicine ball, you can use that.

02:48:39.585 --> 02:48:41.127
I'm gonna use 10 pounds right now.

02:48:41.467 --> 02:48:42.809
Don't worry about going too heavy.

02:48:42.849 --> 02:48:44.391
This is all about stabilization.

02:48:44.731 --> 02:48:51.940
So I'm trying to keep my back as flat as I can as I reach through, whereas most people drop into rotation, all right?

02:48:51.960 --> 02:48:55.665
So I'm gonna bring my hands just underneath my shoulders

02:48:55.645 --> 02:49:01.233
My knees are underneath my hips, and then I'm just going to push up my knees, and I'm going to stabilize.

02:49:01.534 --> 02:49:06.762
My feet are going to be a little bit wider, and the harder we want to make it, we can go more and more narrow.

02:49:07.683 --> 02:49:15.595
So I'm going to start out a little wider than shoulder width apart with the feet, and then I'm just going to reach across, pull through, and back down.

02:49:15.936 --> 02:49:19.621
And then I reach across the other way, pull through, and back down.

02:49:19.741 --> 02:49:20.843
So that's one rep.

02:49:21.599 --> 02:49:23.882
So, this may feel easy.

02:49:24.243 --> 02:49:27.327
This may feel like it's something where you're not getting a huge burn.

02:49:27.367 --> 02:49:28.569
That's completely fine.

02:49:29.190 --> 02:49:31.053
It's all about balance and stabilization.

02:49:31.574 --> 02:49:36.301
I want you to be able to balance a water bottle on your lower back during this exercise.

02:49:36.761 --> 02:49:49.480
So, if your friend sets a water bottle on your back, I want you to be able to get through all the reps without rotating and knocking it over.

02:50:04.850 --> 02:50:08.334
So for my mini band ankle turns, I'm going to use a mini band.

02:50:08.394 --> 02:50:13.080
Make sure that it's light enough tension to where you can really move it and it's not too much tension.

02:50:13.380 --> 02:50:16.263
If it's too much tension, then you're going to try to do it all from the hips.

02:50:16.544 --> 02:50:18.506
I want you really just working through the ankles here.

02:50:18.867 --> 02:50:19.347
All right.

02:50:19.367 --> 02:50:31.161
So I actually like to hold on to a foam roller or a small ball in between my thighs because now if I do it by externally rotating at the hip, this is going to drop.

02:50:31.141 --> 02:50:36.852
And so I gotta squeeze onto this and hold this and now it ensures that I'm really working just with the ankles.

02:50:37.273 --> 02:50:41.902
So I'm gonna go out around and down to the ground and bring it back.

02:50:41.942 --> 02:50:44.948
So it's almost like a half circle or a quarter of a circle.

02:50:45.429 --> 02:50:49.898
So I'm pointing toes in and I'm going out down to the ground.

02:50:49.938 --> 02:50:52.643
The toes are pointing out and then I come back around.

02:50:53.045 --> 02:51:05.383
Alright, so making that little corridor circle, I'm gonna really feel this all the way down the outside of the lower leg and through the lateral, the outside aspect of my ankles.

02:51:29.579 --> 02:51:35.917
OK, for the cable drop step rotation, I'm just going to use a cable start light and build up gradually.

02:51:36.418 --> 02:51:38.223
Or if you don't have a cable, you could use a band.

02:51:38.644 --> 02:51:43.759
I'm going to interlock my fingers over the handle and just step it out just a little bit.

02:51:44.080 --> 02:51:44.601
All right.

02:51:44.581 --> 02:51:47.045
We're gonna come into a quarter squat position.

02:51:47.466 --> 02:51:51.552
Now, to open up that way, I gotta drive off the right foot.

02:51:51.933 --> 02:51:57.682
I'm gonna open up and I'm gonna get that left foot down so that that toe is pointing that way, all right?

02:51:57.702 --> 02:52:01.628
I don't want you to go into rotation until that foot is down.

02:52:01.608 --> 02:52:07.725
So I'm here, I push into position, and then from here I can open up and explode there.

02:52:07.765 --> 02:52:16.630
Now on the way back I'm coming halfway, hands right in front of the chest, fire up that core, two to three seconds, and then release back to the start.

02:52:16.610 --> 02:52:22.416
So it's foot down, explode, come back, hold, and release.

02:52:23.277 --> 02:52:35.971
So the reason that I want you to not just go into rotation but get the foot down first is we're then gonna work that kind of recoil core, we're gonna work as the full fascia system instead of just muscular power.

02:52:36.852 --> 02:52:45.942
So we're getting here, and then we get a good recoil through the core, and we get here, and then coming back, we get that pause,

02:52:45.922 --> 02:52:46.764
and then release.

02:52:47.306 --> 02:52:52.479
Do the desired reps to one side and then go back and do the desired reps to the other side.

02:52:52.860 --> 02:52:57.332
We should feel a burn through the core especially the side in the obliques.

02:53:00.839 --> 02:53:07.030
All right, so for my big toe extension, I'm just going to use a box, but you could use a wall or anything flat and stable.

02:53:07.531 --> 02:53:15.845
Basically, I'm just going to try to keep the sole of my foot on the ground, and I'm going to try to get my big toe straight up onto that box.

02:53:16.726 --> 02:53:18.109
So I'm not going to quite get it.

02:53:18.189 --> 02:53:21.655
If you have a true 90 degree range of motion, you're going to be able to get it.

02:53:21.675 --> 02:53:26.323
Most people don't quite have that, but I want to get as close as I can.

02:53:26.843 --> 02:53:28.646
And I'm just gonna hold in that position.

02:53:28.666 --> 02:53:33.093
If you wanna rock a little bit, it should be slight discomfort.

02:53:33.113 --> 02:53:36.879
It shouldn't be so uncomfortable that you're like screaming or really in pain.

02:53:37.340 --> 02:53:39.103
Otherwise, we're putting on too much of a stretch.

02:53:39.824 --> 02:53:43.570
But I can rock forward just a little bit.

02:53:44.967 --> 02:53:49.513
And I feel that stretch through the bottom of the foot all the way through that big toe.

02:53:49.934 --> 02:53:56.182
Big toe extension is so important, especially for us as basketball players because we're wearing these stiff-soled shoes.

02:53:56.562 --> 02:53:59.006
And so we lose a lot of that range of motion.

02:53:59.406 --> 02:54:08.258
And so if I'm going to sprint and my feet lock up here or my toe doesn't have that extension, locks up there, I can't get to full hip extension.

02:54:08.238 --> 02:54:17.533
I have to be able to get close to that 70, 80 degree range of motion to actually get full hip extension and the glutes are the most powerful muscle in the body.

02:54:17.934 --> 02:54:24.745
And so just by limiting my big toe extension, I may not be getting 100% of that contraction

02:54:24.725 --> 02:54:46.682
through the glutes so just to walk without problems you need 30 degrees of range of motion but to really sprint we want to get closer to that 70 80 and on this drill since we have a little assistance and it's passive we want to get maybe closer to 90. like i said i can't quite get there most of you probably won't but we want to get close to that 90 degrees

02:54:48.890 --> 02:54:49.612
What's up, guys?

02:54:49.792 --> 02:54:54.462
Quick minimum dose advice for day one and day five of phase two.

02:54:55.043 --> 02:54:59.593
So if we have to, we can skip the foam roller circuit if we're really pressed for time.

02:55:00.034 --> 02:55:01.297
Do the dynamic warmup.

02:55:01.658 --> 02:55:04.063
Knee over toe split squat, we could skip this.

02:55:04.043 --> 02:55:11.071
if we're pressed for time or if you have any type of knee pain or you're prone to patellar tendonitis, we can skip this exercise.

02:55:11.552 --> 02:55:14.155
Always do the reverse plank alternating hold.

02:55:14.475 --> 02:55:19.421
It's very important for getting some strength through the post chain, through the hamstrings, through the glutes.

02:55:19.681 --> 02:55:22.925
Ring or cone change of direction, always do this.

02:55:23.065 --> 02:55:24.687
At least get through two sets here.

02:55:25.147 --> 02:55:29.072
Very essential for setting up optimal change of direction long term.

02:55:29.052 --> 02:55:34.082
We can skip this line series, so the line agility series, the next three exercises.

02:55:34.503 --> 02:55:36.166
If we're pressed for time, skip those.

02:55:36.486 --> 02:55:44.462
I really recommend doing them, but if you're pressed for time or you're already beating yourself up on the court with a ton of skills training, skip that.

02:55:44.628 --> 02:55:47.953
We have to do our overcoming isometric rack pull.

02:55:48.434 --> 02:55:51.478
So essential for getting maximal muscle activation.

02:55:52.099 --> 02:55:59.069
One of the highest force outputs that we can possibly get from any exercise, we get from this overcoming isometric rack pull.

02:55:59.169 --> 02:56:00.631
So do not skip that.

02:56:01.112 --> 02:56:02.794
Do not skip the assisted jumps.

02:56:03.355 --> 02:56:11.567
Absolutely necessary for revving up that nervous system, activating fast switch muscle fibers faster than we're used to with regular jumping.

02:56:11.968 --> 02:56:14.331
Deadlift RDL eccentric combo.

02:56:14.311 --> 02:56:23.700
Definitely want to get this, very essential for building up our ability to absorb forces, and then we're also building up our ability to explode concentrically.

02:56:24.681 --> 02:56:28.665
RFESS oscillating split squat, definitely do this exercise.

02:56:28.966 --> 02:56:35.192
If you're really pressed for time, take it down to two sets, but this exercise is absolutely essential for this phase.

02:56:35.633 --> 02:56:39.076
Achilles spring combo, we definitely want to do this exercise.

02:56:39.096 --> 02:56:41.078
7-11 breathing, we

02:56:41.058 --> 02:56:46.068
Always want to do the 7-Eleven breathing to get us back into that parasympathetic nervous system.

02:56:46.469 --> 02:56:49.154
Kickstart our recovery and static stretching.

02:56:49.475 --> 02:56:51.960
If you're already flexible and mobile, skip it.

02:56:52.281 --> 02:56:56.870
If you're extremely tight, then you do want to keep that static stretch in there.

02:56:57.171 --> 02:56:57.852
Let's get it.

02:56:58.051 --> 02:56:59.575
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

02:56:59.736 --> 02:57:01.440
I have a long roller from Skills.

02:57:01.681 --> 02:57:08.559
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

02:57:08.920 --> 02:57:14.355
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

02:57:14.335 --> 02:57:40.427
so you could either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well

02:57:40.407 --> 02:57:42.730
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

02:57:42.770 --> 02:57:44.172
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

02:57:44.512 --> 02:57:52.923
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

02:57:53.283 --> 02:58:05.098
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

02:58:05.315 --> 02:58:07.077
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

02:58:07.377 --> 02:58:10.782
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

02:58:10.882 --> 02:58:19.092
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

02:58:19.152 --> 02:58:24.678
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

02:58:24.718 --> 02:58:27.902
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

02:58:27.942 --> 02:58:35.271
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

02:58:35.251 --> 02:58:41.558
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

02:58:42.259 --> 02:58:45.243
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

02:58:45.283 --> 02:58:50.930
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

02:58:51.390 --> 02:58:58.018
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

02:58:57.998 --> 02:59:00.842
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

02:59:01.022 --> 02:59:02.765
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

02:59:03.085 --> 02:59:16.885
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

02:59:18.603 --> 02:59:25.432
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

02:59:25.632 --> 02:59:29.216
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

02:59:29.497 --> 02:59:32.761
You could use golf ball, baseball, anything hard.

02:59:33.302 --> 02:59:36.005
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

02:59:36.025 --> 02:59:37.707
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

02:59:38.008 --> 02:59:41.832
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

02:59:42.273 --> 02:59:45.437
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

02:59:45.417 --> 02:59:53.245
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

02:59:53.586 --> 02:59:56.309
We got to really start to loosen that up.

02:59:57.890 --> 03:00:02.455
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

03:00:02.716 --> 03:00:06.239
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

03:00:06.279 --> 03:00:11.445
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

03:00:11.805 --> 03:00:13.467
Everything else is extra credit.

03:00:16.501 --> 03:00:20.246
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

03:00:20.526 --> 03:00:23.129
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

03:00:23.189 --> 03:00:27.134
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

03:00:27.655 --> 03:00:34.223
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

03:00:34.583 --> 03:00:38.288
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

03:00:38.308 --> 03:00:39.689
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

03:00:40.090 --> 03:00:43.234
And I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

03:00:43.574 --> 03:00:45.276
I'm not going too fast yet.

03:00:45.256 --> 03:00:47.460
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

03:00:47.701 --> 03:00:52.249
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

03:00:52.269 --> 03:01:00.425
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

03:01:00.725 --> 03:01:02.509
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

03:01:02.990 --> 03:01:05.374
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

03:01:05.354 --> 03:01:30.670
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're going to go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch i'm just going to go over with the left leg i'm going to come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

03:01:30.650 --> 03:01:38.331
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

03:01:41.973 --> 03:01:46.339
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

03:01:46.540 --> 03:01:51.888
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

03:01:52.168 --> 03:01:58.718
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

