04:39:16.163 --> 04:39:18.967
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

04:39:19.027 --> 04:39:21.350
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

04:39:21.730 --> 04:39:24.534
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

04:39:24.855 --> 04:39:26.557
I need to be flat through the hips.

04:39:26.837 --> 04:39:29.240
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

04:39:29.761 --> 04:39:34.207
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

04:39:34.588 --> 04:39:37.371
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

04:39:37.652 --> 04:39:45.462
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

04:39:45.442 --> 04:39:55.417
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

04:39:55.858 --> 04:40:04.210
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

04:40:17.217 --> 04:40:22.007
For the plate overhead deep squat, we're really working on mobility and trunk stability.

04:40:22.067 --> 04:40:23.771
So I want you to start light.

04:40:23.791 --> 04:40:26.697
You can start with a five or a 10 pound plate overhead.

04:40:27.299 --> 04:40:29.163
I'm doing it with a 25 pound plate.

04:40:29.403 --> 04:40:34.113
If you're really strong and your mobility is really, really good, you can get all the way up to a 45 pound plate.

04:40:34.234 --> 04:40:35.817
So what I'm gonna do

04:40:35.797 --> 04:40:44.559
is I'm just gonna start on the ground, regular squat position, feet a little wider than shoulder width apart, and I'm going straight up with the plate.

04:40:44.840 --> 04:40:51.055
So I gotta keep this plate right above my head without tipping forward, and then from here I'm just going into a deep squat.

04:40:51.035 --> 04:41:07.339
as low as i can and right back up i got to actively push back and up with the shoulders so that i don't drop out in front of my forehead like that all right try to keep the heels down all the way through this exercise

04:41:07.319 --> 04:41:19.972
You're probably going to feel a burn in the shoulders because we're getting a ton of stability and isometric strength there, but I really want you to strengthen up the erectors, these are the back muscles, the core muscles, and work on that hip and ankle mobility as well.

04:41:20.473 --> 04:41:35.368
Now, if that is too difficult for you, and as you go down you're pitching it out in front of your forehead and you can't keep it back, or you can keep it back but your heels are coming up at the bottom, then go ahead and use some small plates

04:41:36.428 --> 04:41:50.827
to elevate your heels and then go through the same exact thing and you're going to find that you can drop a lot lower because we're sort of taking the ankles out of the equation with that heel lift.

04:41:50.967 --> 04:42:04.444
So start there if that's more comfortable and then gradually, day after day, week after week, try to progress to getting away from the elevated heels and getting back to regular deep squat with your heels on the ground.

04:42:21.299 --> 04:42:48.960
For our AI walks we're getting nice and shifty so all it is an AI cross is a loose cross where basically he gets thin so feet are narrow and then he takes a wide step out notice this knee drops in to sell that movement and he shifts his body weight so it's not here like a lot of you guys do or in football where your torso stays here you got to shift that torso over this knee so I'm really shifting my weight this direction

04:42:48.940 --> 04:42:50.662
That's what really sells this move.

04:42:51.123 --> 04:42:53.146
And then I just pop the other way.

04:42:53.386 --> 04:42:59.035
So the other mistake that people make is after they get that shift, they try to explode into that next step.

04:42:59.696 --> 04:43:04.603
From this position, you have no leverage to really put force in the ground and cover distance.

04:43:04.623 --> 04:43:09.189
And so after this sell, you want a repositioning step where we're here.

04:43:09.890 --> 04:43:15.859
So that left foot is just a recovery step so that we have a good chin angle so that I can push off the other way.

04:43:16.430 --> 04:43:19.572
Right, so your typical AI cross would be here, boom.

04:43:19.973 --> 04:43:48.580
boom so your cell leg becomes your stride leg not how most of you guys do it where this is your cell leg and then this is your stride leg that's not how we get out of a cut so all we're doing is accumulating reps with that ai cross we're gonna get nice and rhythmic with it so we're just going side to side i don't need you sprinting out we're just working on that shiftiness aspect so all it is long step shift the torso here recover one two

04:43:48.560 --> 04:44:02.181
push here recover one two push all right so that's all it is and then we're just walking it for 10 yards so i'm just here here here here

04:44:04.422 --> 04:44:19.364
All right, reminder, regardless of the sport, if we're always down in this low position, we're not as shifty as we would be if we can kind of get up, relax, get thin, and then push ourselves into that low position, shift our body weight.

04:44:19.384 --> 04:44:26.835
So notice when I'm recovering from here, I'm raising back up and then I'm striking this foot

04:44:26.815 --> 04:44:54.679
to push and sell in this direction and then i cross now this is going to be my strike foot so i'm here strike push and then recover strike push recover strike push recover all right that's all it is that's an ai hanging cross funny thing is it's the same footwork for a lot of sports anybody who has to shift somebody else is going to end up using this same footwork at one time or another

04:45:14.318 --> 04:45:21.968
So for my non-counter movement lateral to sprint, I'm sitting on a box where my thighs will be parallel to the floor.

04:45:22.950 --> 04:45:24.932
So you can sit on a box, a bench, a chair.

04:45:25.373 --> 04:45:35.026
Now the reason that we're starting sitting down is so that we take out the stretch shortening cycle, which then means my nervous system has to activate muscle fibers faster.

04:45:35.126 --> 04:45:37.930
So this is training that rate of force development.

04:45:37.910 --> 04:45:40.594
I'm going to start with a little bit of a torso lean.

04:45:40.914 --> 04:45:45.320
So I'm in a good position where my feet are a little bit wider than shoulder width.

04:45:45.641 --> 04:45:46.763
I'm in this little lean.

04:45:47.063 --> 04:45:54.273
So as soon as I lift up this foot and push off as hard as I can with that right foot, I'm going to get very far laterally.

04:45:54.654 --> 04:45:54.874
All right.

04:45:55.214 --> 04:45:56.997
So it's going to be a lateral jump.

04:45:57.077 --> 04:45:58.119
So it's boom.

04:45:58.579 --> 04:46:04.828
Now, once I land on this left foot, a lot of you guys are going to fade and try to take off in your sprint.

04:46:04.808 --> 04:46:07.855
We need to stop that habit right now.

04:46:08.235 --> 04:46:13.988
I need you to change directions sharp so it's lateral, plant, straight to linear.

04:46:14.289 --> 04:46:20.522
The athletes that can change from lateral to linear become the most successful athletes.

04:46:20.502 --> 04:46:32.136
It's here, push out, drive, push straight forward, work on that stride length, get somewhere with that first step and really sprint it through as fast as you can, half court.

04:46:32.717 --> 04:46:41.548
If you have a full court to yourself or you're doing this on the track, why not take it to a full 94 feet and really start to work on that sprint speed.

04:46:50.320 --> 04:46:58.761
For this exercise, if you have a med ball in the range of 10 to 25 pounds, let's use that and we're just gonna do our high tosses.

04:46:58.962 --> 04:47:01.448
I wanna stand right over it, all right?

04:47:01.488 --> 04:47:06.962
So I want my hips right over that med ball so that when I go down, I'm not reaching forward.

04:47:06.942 --> 04:47:35.599
so my hips are right over this med ball from there it's a hip hinge so think about how you would set up for a deadlift position right here that's the same position that we're in for this med ball toss so hinge from the hips knees above the shoelace and then i'm just grabbing that ball right here i should feel like i'm wound up in the post chain so my glutes are already on a good little pre-stretch and i'm ready for big explosion and then from there i'm just tossing so it's just

04:47:36.322 --> 04:47:53.402
toss as high as I can you see that triple extension I'm coming up to the toes just like I'm gonna jump but I don't actually have to take off I'm trying to put all that energy into getting the ball as high as I possibly can now a lot of people end up throwing it forward

04:47:53.500 --> 04:47:57.628
If you're throwing it forward, you're probably also staying tucked at the hip here.

04:47:57.949 --> 04:48:02.058
I need you to get tall at the top so we get that full hip extension.

04:48:02.679 --> 04:48:06.346
Now, if you do that, the ball is going to go straight up and straight down.

04:48:06.948 --> 04:48:09.152
Sometimes it might even go back a little bit.

04:48:09.352 --> 04:48:11.577
So the ball might end up landing behind you.

04:48:11.557 --> 04:48:13.322
I'm completely okay with that.

04:48:13.984 --> 04:48:18.175
Do make sure to get out of the way because you got a heavy medicine ball falling towards your head.

04:48:18.235 --> 04:48:26.157
So I need you to toss and then watch the ball the entire time and get out of the way until the ball hits the floor.

04:48:26.272 --> 04:48:29.236
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a medicine ball.

04:48:29.256 --> 04:48:32.199
If you don't have a medicine ball, you can just do a box jump.

04:48:32.499 --> 04:48:39.808
We're still getting some good concentric power and we're not getting the same eccentric forces that we would get when we land on the ground.

04:48:40.068 --> 04:48:42.431
Remember, it's not about the height of that box.

04:48:42.491 --> 04:48:47.717
We wanna be able to jump up and float to that box and land in a quarter squat position.

04:48:47.778 --> 04:48:53.224
So if it's so high that you can barely get on there, then that's causing more harm than good.

04:48:53.204 --> 04:48:58.531
Now for the beginner athlete, if you don't have a medicine ball to toss, I also don't want you jumping yet.

04:48:58.551 --> 04:49:00.733
So what you're going to do is just speed squats.

04:49:01.034 --> 04:49:05.439
So you can come down about one second and explode up nice and fast to the toes.

04:49:05.960 --> 04:49:12.768
If you don't have the balance and you need to do this on a TRX strap or hold on to a power rack or a pole for some balance, that's fine.

04:49:13.168 --> 04:49:16.933
What we're after is exposing your body to some fast movements.

04:49:16.913 --> 04:49:21.400
For beginners, we can actually stimulate and train some fast twitch muscle fibers.

04:49:21.721 --> 04:49:25.487
For your intermediate and advanced athletes, that's not going to be enough.

04:49:25.507 --> 04:49:30.395
So that's why I advise you doing either the box jump or the medicine ball toss.

04:49:30.495 --> 04:49:37.707
But no matter which exercise you choose, I want you to make sure that we're getting max intent, max explosiveness on the way up.

04:49:37.687 --> 04:49:48.034
Now, if you are a beginner, but the speed squats are too easy, you can always add a weighted vest, add a backpack, something like that, some light handheld weights, some way that you can gradually start to overload it.

04:49:48.335 --> 04:49:53.147
Get yourself a little bit of additional load while we're still exploding up fast to the toes.

04:50:09.462 --> 04:50:13.847
So for our velocity squats, we are gonna do a box squat.

04:50:13.867 --> 04:50:15.389
If you don't have the box, that's okay.

04:50:15.409 --> 04:50:16.811
You can still do a normal squat.

04:50:16.851 --> 04:50:19.634
Without the box, we're just going down to parallel to the floor.

04:50:19.875 --> 04:50:25.141
So I need you to find a box that is about parallel to the floor when you're sitting on it.

04:50:25.181 --> 04:50:28.305
So my thighs are parallel in that position, all right?

04:50:28.685 --> 04:50:31.909
I'm gonna go about 60% of my max.

04:50:31.929 --> 04:50:39.418
You guys know I'm not huge on maxing out, but kind of estimate what your one rep max would be, then go about 60% of that.

04:50:39.398 --> 04:50:49.079
Now with that weight, we're going to control down for one to two seconds, we're going to sit on the box for maybe half a second, and then we're going to explode up as fast as we can.

04:50:49.119 --> 04:50:54.792
Now, if your feet are leaving the ground, it's too light, we got to go up.

04:50:54.772 --> 04:51:00.201
Generally, what we're going for is max power, which is around 0.8 meters per second.

04:51:00.241 --> 04:51:03.225
Now, I know you guys probably don't have the velocity trackers.

04:51:03.646 --> 04:51:07.833
If you don't have the velocity trackers, that's completely okay because those are expensive.

04:51:07.853 --> 04:51:09.175
I want you to time yourself.

04:51:09.235 --> 04:51:11.699
So set up a phone and time yourself.

04:51:12.039 --> 04:51:18.349
You want to get up from the bottom to the very top on your toes at about 0.5 seconds.

04:51:18.329 --> 04:51:25.521
So if it's taking you longer than a half a second to get from here to here, then it's too slow, okay?

04:51:25.882 --> 04:51:28.226
So is that gonna be super accurate?

04:51:28.266 --> 04:51:32.252
No, it's not, but when you time yourself, you can start to see the improvements.

04:51:32.733 --> 04:51:40.126
And most importantly, because you have that stopwatch on you, you're gonna get max intent and you're gonna really start to compete with yourself.

04:51:40.146 --> 04:51:43.932
Of course, this is an advanced exercise.

04:51:43.912 --> 04:51:55.107
If you do not have perfect squat form and you're not experienced in the back squat, you are going to do a normal either goblet squat or back squat just with perfect form, slow and controlled.

04:51:55.468 --> 04:52:02.798
We only do max intent if you are pretty well trained and your form is absolutely perfect on the back squat.

04:52:02.778 --> 04:52:10.807
So, if my one rep max was around 300, I'm gonna go around 185 on the bar, and I'm gonna try to accelerate as fast as I can.

04:52:10.867 --> 04:52:12.930
Now, it is cheating coming to the toes.

04:52:13.170 --> 04:52:20.178
You're not supposed to come to your toes, but most of our competitive athletes, when they get max intent, they do end up bringing the heels up a little bit.

04:52:20.538 --> 04:52:23.702
If you're advanced and you have the balance to do that, okay, great.

04:52:23.962 --> 04:52:29.088
But just keep in mind, it is more of an injury risk when we're coming up on the toes because we're off balance.

04:52:29.168 --> 04:52:32.532
And if you are using a velocity tracker, it throws it off a little bit.

04:52:32.512 --> 04:52:36.516
If you do come up on the toes, I'm not mad at you as long as you can keep your balance.

04:52:37.537 --> 04:52:41.581
So I'm coming in and I'm going to go ahead and demo with the real weight because why not?

04:52:42.802 --> 04:52:44.224
Alright, I'm stepping back.

04:52:44.464 --> 04:52:46.947
You got to make sure that you're close enough to that box, alright?

04:52:46.967 --> 04:52:48.648
I don't want to miss the box on the way down.

04:52:49.189 --> 04:52:52.512
Normal squat mechanics, my feet are a little wider than shoulder width.

04:52:52.953 --> 04:52:57.878
I personally like to point the toes out a little bit so I angle them out so they're not straightforward.

04:52:58.278 --> 04:53:02.282
I'm going to sit down slow, pause for half a second and then explode up.

04:53:04.709 --> 04:53:07.734
All right, as fast as I can, nice and explosive.

04:53:08.495 --> 04:53:13.643
That was probably right around 0.8 meters per second, ooh, 0.79.

04:53:14.404 --> 04:53:18.170
Again, if you don't have the velocity tracker, do it with a phone and you can put it in an app.

04:53:18.190 --> 04:53:23.378
There's apps like Coach's Eye that have a stopwatch feature for very cheap, probably a couple dollars.

04:53:23.699 --> 04:53:25.722
Now, on the actual box squat,

04:53:25.702 --> 04:53:28.166
I don't want you to really relax on the box.

04:53:28.186 --> 04:53:34.397
So a lot of people come down, they sit, they relax, and they lose that tension, and that can be really problematic.

04:53:34.717 --> 04:53:37.823
I need you to sit and maintain that angle, alright?

04:53:37.863 --> 04:53:41.529
So I'm here, my knees stay in that position, I'm not rocking back.

04:53:42.110 --> 04:53:49.583
So I'm keeping that same angle, I'm keeping tension through the core nice and tight, and then explode up as fast as I can from there.

04:53:49.563 --> 04:53:57.328
I'm okay with you sitting down for half a second or a second, but I do not want you to relax and lose that angle.

04:54:05.543 --> 04:54:12.510
So for our 15 foot approach jump, all we're doing is measuring out 15 feet and then jumping and touching as high as we can.

04:54:12.890 --> 04:54:18.116
