04:58:02.431 --> 04:58:04.253
First, just find your balance.

04:58:04.313 --> 04:58:08.057
Now, if you find it there, we're coming into an RDL.

04:58:08.478 --> 04:58:10.600
Try to get the torso flat to the ground.

04:58:10.960 --> 04:58:12.782
And now from here, I'm just doing turns.

04:58:12.762 --> 04:58:17.689
All right, as big as I can back to neutral and then to the other side.

04:58:18.070 --> 04:58:29.628
Now those turns might be very small like this to start, but as you get better, I want you to do full turns, full rotations up as high as we can.

04:58:29.668 --> 04:58:39.883
All right, you're going to feel the feet completely burning from the toes all the way back up through the heels, back up through the calves, lateral aspects of the calves.

04:58:39.863 --> 04:58:46.245
The lower leg and the feet are going to be firing the outside muscles and those small intrinsic muscles.

04:59:10.762 --> 04:59:14.787
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

04:59:14.907 --> 04:59:16.710
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

04:59:16.750 --> 04:59:19.453
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

04:59:19.494 --> 04:59:25.982
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

04:59:26.523 --> 04:59:32.471
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

04:59:32.751 --> 04:59:36.516
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

04:59:36.496 --> 04:59:40.403
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

04:59:40.824 --> 04:59:45.171
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

04:59:45.231 --> 04:59:52.564
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

04:59:53.573 --> 04:59:56.297
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

04:59:56.657 --> 05:00:03.386
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

05:00:03.927 --> 05:00:09.715
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

05:00:09.735 --> 05:00:13.380
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

05:00:15.703 --> 05:00:19.969
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

05:00:22.919 --> 05:00:24.842
and that stomach will fall, okay?

05:00:25.543 --> 05:00:28.187
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

05:00:28.948 --> 05:00:37.981
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

05:00:38.762 --> 05:00:46.774
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

05:00:46.794 --> 05:00:50.800
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

05:00:50.780 --> 05:01:19.707
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I gotta get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

05:01:19.940 --> 05:01:22.443
happens in the parasympathetic state.

05:01:22.984 --> 05:01:27.129
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

05:01:27.470 --> 05:01:41.087
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

05:01:43.042 --> 05:01:47.848
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for day three of phase four.

05:01:48.309 --> 05:01:51.293
Same thing here, very short and efficient workout.

05:01:51.473 --> 05:01:59.544
Don't skip anything unless you're in season and your encore volume is too high to handle all of these additional plyometrics.

05:01:59.844 --> 05:02:05.892
If that's the case, go back to phase one or phase two, take some of those strength exercises and put them in here.

05:02:06.412 --> 05:02:12.981
Do not beat yourself down in season or do not push through knee pain or back pain, any type of joint pain.

05:02:12.961 --> 05:02:16.945
If you have any type of pain, you're going to skip the workout.

05:02:17.225 --> 05:02:22.270
But assuming you're good to go and you're healthy, then let's get nice and elastic, nice and bouncy.

05:02:22.611 --> 05:02:23.071
Let's get it.

05:02:23.492 --> 05:02:25.033
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

05:02:25.193 --> 05:02:26.895
I have a long roller from Skills.

05:02:27.135 --> 05:02:34.002
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

05:02:34.363 --> 05:02:39.728
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

05:02:39.708 --> 05:02:42.873
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

05:02:43.213 --> 05:02:52.046
So if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

05:02:52.387 --> 05:02:58.817
Now always if we want more pressure we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

05:02:59.518 --> 05:03:05.867
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

05:03:05.847 --> 05:03:08.190
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

05:03:08.210 --> 05:03:09.632
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

05:03:09.973 --> 05:03:18.365
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

05:03:18.745 --> 05:03:25.595
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

05:03:25.956 --> 05:03:30.522
And then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

05:03:30.755 --> 05:03:32.537
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

05:03:32.817 --> 05:03:36.242
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

05:03:36.322 --> 05:03:44.532
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

05:03:44.612 --> 05:03:48.356
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

05:03:48.777 --> 05:03:50.138
And our next one we're flipping over.

05:03:50.198 --> 05:03:53.342
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

05:03:53.402 --> 05:04:00.711
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

05:04:00.691 --> 05:04:06.981
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

05:04:07.722 --> 05:04:10.707
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

05:04:11.148 --> 05:04:16.397
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

05:04:16.857 --> 05:04:23.408
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

05:04:23.388 --> 05:04:26.294
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

05:04:26.474 --> 05:04:28.197
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

05:04:28.538 --> 05:04:42.325
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

05:04:44.060 --> 05:04:50.888
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

05:04:51.088 --> 05:04:54.653
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

05:04:54.933 --> 05:04:58.217
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

05:04:58.758 --> 05:05:01.441
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

05:05:01.461 --> 05:05:03.143
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

05:05:03.443 --> 05:05:07.468
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

05:05:07.448 --> 05:05:10.395
and I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

05:05:10.957 --> 05:05:18.715
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

05:05:19.056 --> 05:05:21.863
We got to really start to loosen that up.

05:05:23.260 --> 05:05:27.913
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

05:05:28.153 --> 05:05:31.683
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

05:05:31.703 --> 05:05:36.897
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

05:05:37.258 --> 05:05:38.923
Everything else is extra credit.

05:05:42.025 --> 05:05:45.770
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

05:05:46.031 --> 05:05:48.635
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

05:05:48.695 --> 05:05:52.661
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

05:05:53.161 --> 05:05:59.751
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

05:06:00.091 --> 05:06:03.777
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

05:06:03.817 --> 05:06:05.199
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

05:06:05.620 --> 05:06:08.744
And I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

05:06:09.085 --> 05:06:10.767
I'm not going too fast yet.

05:06:10.747 --> 05:06:12.971
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

05:06:13.211 --> 05:06:17.759
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

05:06:17.779 --> 05:06:21.946
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

05:06:22.367 --> 05:06:25.913
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

05:06:26.234 --> 05:06:27.997
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

05:06:28.378 --> 05:06:30.882
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here.

05:06:30.862 --> 05:06:56.160
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're going to go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch i'm just going to go over with the left leg i'm going to come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

05:06:56.140 --> 05:07:03.771
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

05:07:07.480 --> 05:07:11.847
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

05:07:12.047 --> 05:07:17.396
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

05:07:17.657 --> 05:07:22.905
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

05:07:22.985 --> 05:07:24.227
I'm keeping that knee in.

05:07:24.648 --> 05:07:32.661
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can.

05:07:32.641 --> 05:07:54.497
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

05:07:55.405 --> 05:07:59.010
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

05:07:59.451 --> 05:08:03.917
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

05:08:03.978 --> 05:08:06.241
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

05:08:06.581 --> 05:08:08.604
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

05:08:08.644 --> 05:08:09.225
That's okay.

05:08:09.566 --> 05:08:11.148
Let's just get as low as we can there.

05:08:11.629 --> 05:08:12.570
One, two, three.

05:08:12.550 --> 05:08:39.047
three and back up so three there three there i'm gonna go five cycles and then i'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo i'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and i'm going down i'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because i want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat

05:08:39.027 --> 05:08:42.910
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

05:08:43.371 --> 05:08:47.374
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

05:08:47.714 --> 05:08:53.259
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

05:08:53.860 --> 05:09:08.072
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

05:09:13.047 --> 05:09:19.575
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

05:09:19.595 --> 05:09:21.056
Don't make the mistake of going here.

05:09:21.417 --> 05:09:29.486
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

05:09:29.587 --> 05:09:35.473
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

05:09:35.534 --> 05:09:39.358
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

05:09:39.338 --> 05:09:41.161
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

05:09:41.381 --> 05:09:43.344
One, two, three.

05:09:43.484 --> 05:09:46.468
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

05:09:46.628 --> 05:09:48.851
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

05:09:48.871 --> 05:09:51.195
One, two, three.

05:09:51.255 --> 05:09:52.737
Rock forward.

05:09:52.837 --> 05:09:53.878
So I'm on my toes now.

05:09:54.359 --> 05:09:56.542
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

05:09:56.622 --> 05:09:56.943
Hold.

05:09:57.564 --> 05:09:58.545
One, two, three.

05:09:58.930 --> 05:09:59.311
Three.

05:09:59.651 --> 05:10:00.373
Rock it back.

05:10:00.733 --> 05:10:02.537
Heels back down to the ground.

05:10:02.797 --> 05:10:04.441
One, two, three.

05:10:04.521 --> 05:10:05.723
I need one hand up.

05:10:06.264 --> 05:10:07.046
Two hand up.

05:10:07.286 --> 05:10:07.987
Not out here.

05:10:08.028 --> 05:10:09.490
We need it back behind the head.

05:10:10.091 --> 05:10:10.733
And stand.

05:10:11.274 --> 05:10:11.514
All right.

05:10:11.995 --> 05:10:13.358
So that's one cycle.

05:10:13.618 --> 05:10:14.740
We're going to go for three of these.

05:10:15.702 --> 05:10:17.125
So it's one, two, three.

05:10:17.459 --> 05:10:19.722
One, two, three, lean.

05:10:20.503 --> 05:10:22.786
One, two, three, heels back down.

05:10:22.826 --> 05:10:26.852
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

05:10:27.152 --> 05:10:35.483
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

05:10:35.503 --> 05:10:39.849
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

05:10:40.210 --> 05:10:46.538
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

05:10:46.518 --> 05:10:49.384
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

05:10:49.424 --> 05:10:51.709
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

05:10:52.130 --> 05:10:54.935
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

05:10:55.256 --> 05:10:56.939
I need to be flat through the hips.

05:10:57.240 --> 05:10:59.645
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

05:11:00.166 --> 05:11:04.595
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

05:11:04.976 --> 05:11:07.902
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

05:11:07.882 --> 05:11:18.218
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm going to get a really good isometric contraction and I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

05:11:19.280 --> 05:11:25.810
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

05:11:26.251 --> 05:11:30.397
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

05:11:30.417 --> 05:11:32.381
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

05:11:32.401 --> 05:11:34.564
So we've got to get them revved up for our session.

05:11:56.752 --> 05:12:01.678
So for my band resisted first step explosion, I'm gonna use any super band.

05:12:02.038 --> 05:12:05.602
If you have two bands, link them together so you have a little more range to work with.

05:12:05.983 --> 05:12:07.104
If not, that's okay.

05:12:07.444 --> 05:12:10.268
Just hook your one super band up to a power rack.

05:12:10.488 --> 05:12:13.872
The key here is we're starting with a level change.

05:12:13.892 --> 05:12:19.278
So that means we're gonna start high and then we're gonna drop down into that athletic stance.

05:12:19.298 --> 05:12:23.603
Too often we start in an athletic stance and then we move from there.

05:12:23.583 --> 05:12:46.047
but in basketball in any sport there's a lot of times where we're caught in this high position and then we have to go so we have to get down back into that athletic stance and then work from there so like a basketball player a lot of times is selling a shot so they're here and then they decide to get to the rim then they got to drop into this stance so it's a level change

05:12:46.027 --> 05:12:52.555
So what we're gonna do is we're gonna start high on the toes, and I'm just gonna drop down into a square stance, all right?

05:12:52.875 --> 05:13:00.263
So it's here, drop, and then once I land there, it's a crossover step, push out as far as we can, and walk it back.

05:13:00.323 --> 05:13:01.965
So it's crossover step.

05:13:02.025 --> 05:13:05.590
If I'm going right, I'm going cross with the left.

05:13:05.910 --> 05:13:12.057
I'm getting as far as I can in that one step, nice and explosive, nice and quick, and then walk it back, all right?

05:13:12.097 --> 05:13:14.700
So then I go to the other side, start high,

05:13:14.680 --> 05:13:17.863
drop, push, and come back.

05:13:18.624 --> 05:13:22.048
I need you to think about really pushing into the floor.

05:13:22.548 --> 05:13:28.675
So it's high, drop, and then really push as hard as you can down and back.

05:13:29.015 --> 05:13:30.436
We're getting a good shin angle.

05:13:30.496 --> 05:13:39.726
So if you film yourself on this exercise, you'll notice when you take off at toe off, we should have that really good shin angle.

05:13:39.942 --> 05:13:46.834
A lot of you are going to have more of a vertical shin angle, which means you put force in the ground this way instead of this way.

05:13:47.515 --> 05:13:54.648
So the more horizontal we can get that shin, the more we're going to get forward with that step.

05:13:55.029 --> 05:13:56.331
All right, let's get it.

05:14:13.135 --> 05:14:18.765
So for the dumbbell power skips, we're gonna go anywhere from a 10 pound dumbbell to a 50 pound dumbbell.

05:14:19.025 --> 05:14:25.857
We've never had an athlete who needed more than 50 pounds because again, this is all about reactiveness, all about quickness off the floor.

05:14:26.238 --> 05:14:29.203
So I generally go around 25 pounds for these.

05:14:29.183 --> 05:14:31.747
So I'm gonna start high with the right foot.

05:14:32.107 --> 05:14:36.314
I'm gonna go into the floor and it's gonna be a low skip right, high skip left.

05:14:36.594 --> 05:14:40.500
So every time my left comes down and hits the floor, I get up as high as I can.

05:14:40.961 --> 05:14:43.244
So it's right, low, left, high.

05:14:43.505 --> 05:14:45.447
Right, low, left, high.

05:14:47.931 --> 05:14:49.133
So your right foot

05:14:49.872 --> 05:14:51.374
is just your recovery foot.

05:14:51.675 --> 05:14:53.938
Your left leg is really the working leg here.

05:14:54.579 --> 05:14:56.722
And then on the way back, I'm gonna switch it.

05:14:57.142 --> 05:14:58.644
So now I start left high.

05:14:59.205 --> 05:15:01.549
I'm gonna go left, low, right, get up.

05:15:02.009 --> 05:15:03.892
Left, low, right, get up.

05:15:04.573 --> 05:15:07.597
Key component here is the stiffness aspect.

05:15:08.278 --> 05:15:11.442
As I'm going into that jump, I don't want the heel to collapse.

05:15:11.803 --> 05:15:16.349
I gotta stay strong on the ball of the foot without the heel hitting the ground.

05:15:17.131 --> 05:15:18.192
On the high jump,

05:15:18.610 --> 05:15:23.079
I'm going to land at about a 45 degree angle and I'm going to push up from there.

05:15:23.560 --> 05:15:26.927
So I don't want you striding out and getting really low.

05:15:27.408 --> 05:15:34.442
I want you to produce high power with a short range of motion, just like we often have to do in a game off one foot.

05:15:52.445 --> 05:15:56.309
So for my one leg hurdle jumps, I'm just going to set up two hurdles.

05:15:56.650 --> 05:15:59.413
I'm going to set them up about 15 feet apart to start.

05:15:59.673 --> 05:16:03.937
That's kind of the easier version and then we're going to mix up that distance later on.

05:16:04.098 --> 05:16:06.761
So I'm going with the high hurdles from Champion Sports.

05:16:06.841 --> 05:16:07.742
You don't need these.

05:16:08.062 --> 05:16:11.045
All you need is a small hurdle, even a pair of shoes.

05:16:11.105 --> 05:16:12.086
It doesn't really matter.

05:16:12.447 --> 05:16:20.055
All that matters is that you're jumping as high as you can and that we have some sort of marker so that we can work on our timing and our steps.

05:16:20.035 --> 05:16:23.720
I'm going to jump over, so I'm going to get a running start, I don't care how far it is.

05:16:24.641 --> 05:16:29.768
Running start, jump off the left, get over that hurdle, now you're going to land on your right.

05:16:30.048 --> 05:16:33.853
So then it would be right, left, right, left, take off again.

05:16:33.893 --> 05:16:40.322
So we're always taking off with our left and then we get over and we land with our right, okay?

05:16:40.302 --> 05:17:03.559
so so much of the jump is timing rhythm and being able to take short steps and then long steps and so the easier version is where we have this set distance and it's like at least 15 feet apart so we can get those multiple steps in between now as you get good at this I want you to mess around with that distance so start to shorten it up maybe you put it

05:17:03.539 --> 05:17:09.865
you know, eight to ten feet away and now all you can do is get a right left jump.

05:17:10.145 --> 05:17:11.727
So I want you to split it up by days.

05:17:11.807 --> 05:17:17.952
I think the first day that you do this drill, let's just keep it long distance to where it's the most comfortable for you.

05:17:18.032 --> 05:17:20.074
We can get multiple steps.

05:17:20.575 --> 05:17:31.605
The next day maybe shorten it up a little bit and then the next day shorten it up even more to the point where we're getting over this first hurdle and all we can do is get right left jump.

05:17:31.585 --> 05:17:39.604
all right so i want you to mix it up the more you mix up the distance the harder it's going to be for you and the more versatile we're going to be with our jumps

05:17:58.366 --> 05:18:07.498
So for my explosive box step up, this is a traditional step up jump so I need that box bench or chair to be nice and stable and right below knee height.

05:18:07.618 --> 05:18:09.200
I do not want it above knee height.

05:18:09.741 --> 05:18:12.745
I'm going to get my whole foot on there so my heel is on.

05:18:12.785 --> 05:18:16.650
A mistake that people make is they bring that heel off and just the ball of the foot on.

05:18:17.071 --> 05:18:21.276
So I want that full heel on, right knee up, left hand up.

05:18:21.637 --> 05:18:27.985
I'm going to have to collapse the torso just a little bit so I don't want a completely upright torso on this exercise so I'm bringing

05:18:27.965 --> 05:18:32.195
that torso forward just a little bit, and then from here, it's just pier explosion.

05:18:32.556 --> 05:18:33.719
Get as high as you can.

05:18:34.160 --> 05:18:42.339
Now, if you wanna drive this opposite leg, you can, but a lot of people end up clipping their toe on the box or the bench or the chair.

05:18:42.760 --> 05:18:45.687
So if that's the case, you can just leave that foot behind.

05:18:45.667 --> 05:18:52.934
So you can either drive and come back down or you can leave this leg back and come back down.

05:18:53.274 --> 05:18:59.280
I would prefer if you do drive that but again it is kind of tough for some people because they do clip that foot.

05:18:59.781 --> 05:19:08.149
So max explosiveness, I'm getting off this box as high as I can and then I'm landing and I get back up into that jump as quick as I can.

05:19:08.589 --> 05:19:10.792
Now do not jump if you're off balance.

05:19:10.812 --> 05:19:13.294
So a lot of people, they get a good first jump

05:19:13.274 --> 05:19:16.539
they come down and then they're off balance and they try to jump again.

05:19:16.940 --> 05:19:19.925
You have to regather your balance before you go.

05:19:20.305 --> 05:19:35.029
When you get good at this, it can start to become very rapid where you don't have to rebalance, but your first couple times doing this exercise, you might have to come down, reset for a couple seconds, and then go from there, all right?

05:19:35.009 --> 05:19:38.772
Now make sure that this foot lands first.

05:19:38.993 --> 05:19:42.916
So a lot of people they jump and then they try to bring the back foot down first.

05:19:43.216 --> 05:19:44.998
That's too much stress on that back leg.

05:19:45.538 --> 05:19:52.204
So I need you to land and absorb contact with that front leg first and then that back leg hits.

05:19:53.005 --> 05:19:55.267
Don't push off that back leg too hard.

05:19:55.727 --> 05:19:57.909
Think about that back leg as a non-working leg.

05:19:57.969 --> 05:19:59.170
It's just a long for the ride.

05:19:59.511 --> 05:20:02.273
So I don't want you to really push off the ground.

05:20:02.653 --> 05:20:04.375
Just tap and go.

05:20:07.257 --> 05:20:13.909
So this exercise is great for building that concentric power, that power on the way up, especially off one leg.

05:20:14.290 --> 05:20:19.219
This is going to translate to double leg jumping, and it's going to translate to single leg jumping.

05:20:19.540 --> 05:20:24.148
This is a very conventional, old school exercise that stands the test of time.

05:20:24.409 --> 05:20:27.154
This consistently gets athletes more explosive.

05:20:27.695 --> 05:20:28.236
Let's get it.

05:20:30.848 --> 05:20:48.532
Now for our freestyle dunks, I put this in there because it's so important that you get out there and just allow your body to explore different approaches, different type of dunks, different type of jumping styles without me always trying to put you in a box and trying to get you to do one thing.

05:20:48.872 --> 05:20:54.460
So I want you to set the clock for 10 minutes and I just want you to go get as many dunks as you can.

05:20:54.440 --> 05:20:56.384
Take breaks in between if you need to.

05:20:56.424 --> 05:20:58.968
I do want you to be recovered in between reps.

05:20:58.988 --> 05:21:01.032
This isn't an endurance workout.

05:21:01.393 --> 05:21:02.715
So get up off one foot.

05:21:02.775 --> 05:21:03.617
Throw it down.

05:21:04.058 --> 05:21:04.639
Walk back.

05:21:04.679 --> 05:21:06.803
Recover for 30 seconds to a minute.

05:21:07.143 --> 05:21:07.785
Come back in.

05:21:07.925 --> 05:21:09.047
Take off off two.

05:21:09.087 --> 05:21:10.710
Try to dunk left.

05:21:10.690 --> 05:21:13.774
Whatever you want to do, get creative and do it.

05:21:14.014 --> 05:21:18.040
Of course, if you can't dunk and all you can do is touch the bottom of the net, I don't care.

05:21:18.100 --> 05:21:19.342
I still want you to get creative.

05:21:19.702 --> 05:21:21.204
Come in off two and touch the net.

05:21:21.564 --> 05:21:24.148
Come from baseline, get up off one and touch the net.

05:21:24.468 --> 05:21:27.432
Try to jump off left, touch with the left.

05:21:27.893 --> 05:21:36.985
Variability, variety is the key to longevity and it's super important for building kind of well-rounded explosiveness and athleticism.

05:21:36.965 --> 05:21:41.710
And so I want you to get creative, set the clock for 10 minutes, and just freestyle dunk.

05:21:44.508 --> 05:21:47.752
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

05:21:47.772 --> 05:21:52.037
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

05:21:52.337 --> 05:21:59.145
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

05:21:59.445 --> 05:22:02.929
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

05:22:03.189 --> 05:22:10.718
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

05:22:10.698 --> 05:22:15.049
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

05:22:15.470 --> 05:22:17.455
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

05:22:17.475 --> 05:22:19.981
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

05:22:20.262 --> 05:22:22.327
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

05:22:22.607 --> 05:22:24.833
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

05:22:25.134 --> 05:22:26.918
I'm just gonna lean forward.

05:22:26.898 --> 05:22:29.603
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

05:22:29.944 --> 05:22:36.937
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

05:22:37.098 --> 05:22:41.927
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

05:22:43.249 --> 05:22:47.758
And I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

05:22:47.992 --> 05:22:49.954
15 seconds on the other side as well.

05:22:49.974 --> 05:22:52.117
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

05:22:52.297 --> 05:22:53.719
So that is the back of the thigh.

05:22:53.959 --> 05:22:58.164
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

05:22:58.285 --> 05:23:01.969
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

05:23:02.129 --> 05:23:04.552
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

05:23:04.893 --> 05:23:07.215
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

05:23:07.576 --> 05:23:09.138
So now look at this back foot.

05:23:09.158 --> 05:23:10.560
I'm not curling the toe under.

05:23:10.960 --> 05:23:15.045
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

05:23:15.025 --> 05:23:18.471
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

05:23:18.892 --> 05:23:24.261
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

05:23:24.562 --> 05:23:37.945
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

05:23:37.925 --> 05:23:41.070
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

05:23:41.130 --> 05:23:42.452
That's the inside of the leg.

05:23:42.953 --> 05:23:46.319
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

05:23:46.920 --> 05:23:47.601
Same thing.

05:23:47.941 --> 05:23:50.826
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

05:23:51.127 --> 05:23:54.432
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

05:23:54.412 --> 05:23:58.376
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

05:23:58.697 --> 05:24:06.345
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

05:24:06.705 --> 05:24:12.191
Now a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

05:24:12.591 --> 05:24:18.057
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

05:24:18.037 --> 05:24:40.483
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here but this is a glute stretch for this left leg but at the same time for a lot of you this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side so i'm just going to pull back hold for 15 seconds after 15 seconds i'm switching over to that other side trying to get full range of motion with that other leg

05:24:40.463 --> 05:24:43.546
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

05:24:43.586 --> 05:24:45.748
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

05:24:45.988 --> 05:24:49.651
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

05:24:50.112 --> 05:24:51.693
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

05:24:51.733 --> 05:24:54.616
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

05:24:54.696 --> 05:24:55.597
So I'm 90 there.

05:24:55.957 --> 05:24:58.740
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

05:24:59.120 --> 05:25:01.402
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

05:25:01.763 --> 05:25:06.467
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

05:25:06.807 --> 05:25:09.930
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

05:25:09.910 --> 05:25:12.815
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

05:25:13.196 --> 05:25:17.984
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

05:25:18.004 --> 05:25:19.426
All right.

05:25:19.466 --> 05:25:25.356
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

05:25:26.097 --> 05:25:29.944
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

05:25:29.924 --> 05:25:35.212
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

05:25:35.592 --> 05:25:38.817
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

05:25:38.917 --> 05:25:42.122
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

05:25:42.502 --> 05:25:52.236
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

05:25:52.503 --> 05:25:54.365
So I do have a straight knee.

05:25:54.765 --> 05:25:57.267
So we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius.

05:25:57.307 --> 05:25:59.249
That's the outside calf muscle.

05:25:59.269 --> 05:26:05.435
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

05:26:05.815 --> 05:26:09.899
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

05:26:11.080 --> 05:26:14.102
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

05:26:14.142 --> 05:26:14.883
That's okay.

05:26:15.243 --> 05:26:18.426
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

05:26:18.907 --> 05:26:22.510
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

05:26:25.223 --> 05:26:25.904
What's up, guys?

05:26:26.144 --> 05:26:31.152
Quick minimum dose advice for day two and day four of phase four.

05:26:31.933 --> 05:26:34.537
Really, there's nothing that we can take out here.

05:26:35.038 --> 05:26:46.675
If you're super mobile and super flexible, we can take out all of the mobility work and just do the core exercises, but I do recommend that we get in everything as is on this program.

05:26:46.655 --> 05:26:50.982
It's already a pretty short workout, so it's not going to take too long to get through.

05:26:51.463 --> 05:26:56.511
As always, on our core workouts, you can do this Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday.

05:26:56.891 --> 05:26:58.194
Saturday is optional.

05:26:58.274 --> 05:27:02.520
You don't have to do it, but if you have the time and you have the energy, might as well do it.

05:27:02.821 --> 05:27:03.482
Let's get it.

05:27:29.433 --> 05:27:36.042
So for my rocking dead bug, I'm going to use a basketball, volleyball, or any type of ball around this size.

05:27:36.142 --> 05:27:38.105
If you don't have that, that's okay.

05:27:38.125 --> 05:27:41.429
So I'm going to pin it between my knee and my elbow.

05:27:42.210 --> 05:27:45.715
Now if I don't have the ball, then I'm just going to try to touch elbow to knee.

05:27:46.015 --> 05:27:49.720
It's actually going to be a little bit more difficult, a little more hip range of motion.

05:27:49.700 --> 05:27:53.964
So if I have that ball, I'm going to pin it, lay your head back.

05:27:54.325 --> 05:27:57.368
I want to make sure that I'm not hyperextended in my back.

05:27:57.408 --> 05:28:03.234
So a lot of people come into this extension where if you slipped your hand under there, there's a big space.

05:28:03.675 --> 05:28:06.658
I want you to think about crushing that person's hand.

05:28:06.998 --> 05:28:08.019
So push down.

05:28:08.480 --> 05:28:11.983
You got to think about posteriorly tilting your pelvis.

05:28:12.003 --> 05:28:15.207
So that means like if you had a tail, you would tuck your tail.

05:28:15.587 --> 05:28:18.390
And when you tuck your tail, you make your back flat.

05:28:18.370 --> 05:28:24.760
So from that flat back position, I am just going to extend the opposite side.

05:28:24.800 --> 05:28:30.549
So I'm going to extend my left hand with the left leg at the same time.

05:28:30.970 --> 05:28:32.752
Notice I'm not touching the floor.

05:28:33.113 --> 05:28:39.483
So I just come a couple inches off the ground with that left side and I come back to that base position.

05:28:39.523 --> 05:28:41.206
Now from there I rock up.

05:28:41.546 --> 05:28:42.808
So it's a rock position.

05:28:42.788 --> 05:29:04.088
get the heels down to the ground and rock back slowly that counts as one rep and then you go back in now notice that i'm not using a bunch of momentum to rock up i want to start from this dead stopped position and then i just create that momentum with my core so it's dead stop position i'm nice and still

05:29:05.519 --> 05:29:06.422
and rock up.

05:29:06.763 --> 05:29:11.620
So if you do that the right way, that rock up is gonna really fire up the front abs.

05:29:12.021 --> 05:29:17.419
All right, so we do the desired reps to one side and then you go to the other side and finish out.

05:29:31.017 --> 05:29:34.102
So for our hamstring floss, we're just going to go lying on our back.

05:29:34.443 --> 05:29:43.939
I want to make sure that my left leg stays down, so I'm actively pushing this left knee through the ground, pushing my left heel down through the ground, and I'm just going to pull back.

05:29:44.040 --> 05:29:51.793
So here in this position, I'm going to feel a little bit of a stretch in my glute, a little bit of a stretch in that opposite hip flexor, and then I'm just

05:29:51.773 --> 05:29:55.420
extending that knee and coming back into flexion.

05:29:55.440 --> 05:29:57.104
So it's extension, flexion.

05:29:57.344 --> 05:29:59.428
So we call this hamstring floss.

05:29:59.729 --> 05:30:03.937
We're gradually trying to get that range of motion bigger and bigger.

05:30:04.298 --> 05:30:07.504
In each rep, we're trying to extend that knee more and more.

05:30:21.490 --> 05:30:25.275
So for my rocking plank, I'm going to come into a normal plank position.

05:30:25.395 --> 05:30:28.079
So that means elbows directly underneath the shoulder.

05:30:29.621 --> 05:30:31.904
From here, I'm going to make sure that I'm nice and flat.

05:30:32.004 --> 05:30:33.406
So I don't want my hips sagging.

05:30:33.727 --> 05:30:35.289
I don't want my hips hiking up.

05:30:36.050 --> 05:30:39.835
I'm nice and flat, straight line from the back of my head down through my heels.

05:30:40.276 --> 05:30:45.743
Now, as long as I own that position and I feel good there, I'm going to start to rock.

05:30:45.723 --> 05:31:15.730
forwards and backwards nice and slow this whole time I'm really bracing my core I'm squeezing that core now in jumping we need to have the core to fire reflexively so that means we don't consciously think about squeezing the core otherwise it kills the fluidity of our movement so in training that's when we want to get that core nice and stable and teach ourselves how to really brace it and then on the court it'll start to happen more and more naturally

05:31:15.710 --> 05:31:24.226
So make sure that you are squeezing the core like you're taking a big punch from Mike Tyson, but you still have to breathe throughout this exercise.

05:31:24.526 --> 05:31:29.235
A lot of people start to hold their breath and that limits how long they can go on the exercise.

05:31:29.476 --> 05:31:32.241
So make sure that you are breathing, but we are squeezing.

05:31:32.461 --> 05:31:35.507
It's gonna get our core nice and strong, nice and stable.

05:31:38.373 --> 05:31:43.681
Okay so for our lateral line stretch we want to get in a power rack or have something to hold on to.

05:31:43.722 --> 05:31:48.830
You can even use like a TRX or you can do this without anything to hold on to.

05:31:48.850 --> 05:31:50.052
I'll show you that regression.

05:31:50.472 --> 05:31:53.918
So basically what we're doing is I'm going to step in here.

05:31:53.958 --> 05:32:02.271
If I'm holding on with my left hand at about belly button height then I'm going to take my left foot

05:32:02.251 --> 05:32:28.630
over the right foot all right now I'm gonna take my right hand over the head and I'm gonna grab onto that power rack all right already I feel a little bit of a stretch through that lateral line so it's through the outside of this leg and then all the way up through that lat so I'm gonna push away with my left hand and I'm gonna pull with my right hand so it's push

05:32:29.403 --> 05:32:31.687
pull into that stretch.

05:32:31.707 --> 05:32:38.579
I'm going to feel it big time through that lateral aspect of the hip, through that oblique area, and then into the lats.

05:32:38.599 --> 05:32:45.370
This stretch is so important for athleticism because our fascial system is connected all the way from the feet.

05:32:45.350 --> 05:33:07.560
all the way up through the shoulder everything is connected and so when we do stretches in isolation it doesn't truly work so we have to stretch the whole body as a system to really get good mobility gains and really build that good fascial health so then remember on the other side it would be right over left push away with the right hand

05:33:07.540 --> 05:33:35.404
pull with the left hand and I'm only gonna hold for a couple seconds and then I'm gonna come back to neutral and then grab with the left hand one two and come back to neutral so we're making it dynamic this isn't just a static stretch now if you don't have anything to hold on to you can also do this without the rack it's not gonna be as good but we can still cross over right over left and then just pretend like we're here and shift as far as I can I'm thinking about

05:33:35.384 --> 05:33:42.542
pushing my hip out this way and getting my left hand as far over my head as I can and then coming back.

05:33:57.794 --> 05:34:03.182
So for my cable cross swing, what I'm gonna do is mimic my single leg dunking motion.

05:34:03.262 --> 05:34:07.609
So a lot of you would go up for a single leg dunk and you would get that cross swing.

05:34:07.649 --> 05:34:13.738
So if you were jumping off your right, you would load up the ball at the right hip, you would dip that left shoulder.

05:34:14.139 --> 05:34:19.306
So we're really loading up the core and then recoiling from the core.

05:34:19.326 --> 05:34:22.772
So it's not just the ball going, it's a full core movement.

05:34:23.192 --> 05:34:26.417
So we're gonna mimic that now with a little bit of resistance.

05:34:27.410 --> 05:34:28.935
So I'm interlocking the fingers.

05:34:29.778 --> 05:34:32.006
I'm going to come into a split stance.

05:34:32.027 --> 05:34:35.800
So notice the inside foot is always forward.

05:34:36.050 --> 05:34:39.873
So the foot that is closest to that cable machine is always forward.

05:34:40.514 --> 05:34:47.140
I'm going to load up here and then I'm just going to drive diagonally and I'm going to hold at the top.

05:34:47.440 --> 05:34:50.283
So I want to get this thigh parallel to the floor if I can.

05:34:50.743 --> 05:34:53.966
Now, half the battle is just the stability at the top.

05:34:54.106 --> 05:34:56.348
A lot of you, that's going to be the hard part.

05:34:56.668 --> 05:35:04.015
Make sure that you're going light enough to where you can stabilize at the top and will gradually go heavier as we get better.

05:35:03.995 --> 05:35:10.022
Alright, so we're here, we load, explode and hold and then come back down.

05:35:12.888 --> 05:35:19.576
I want you to think about driving with the leg, but also dipping that shoulder and ripping with the core.

05:35:20.016 --> 05:35:21.318
Should be nice and explosive.

05:35:21.478 --> 05:35:26.964
Of course, on a cable machine, we're not going 100% explosiveness, otherwise the weight's gonna be jerked up and down.

05:35:27.145 --> 05:35:29.868
So maybe 80% of your max explosion.

05:35:30.288 --> 05:35:34.854
Also, the faster you rip, the harder it is to stabilize at the top.

05:35:35.214 --> 05:35:42.102
So make sure that you're going at a speed where you're still controlled enough so that you can be balanced and stabilized at the top.

05:35:42.082 --> 05:35:46.229
