05:36:37.936 --> 05:36:39.599
Make sure you got a nice, soft surface.

05:36:40.099 --> 05:36:40.881
So I'm touching.

05:36:41.261 --> 05:36:45.167
I'm in a straight line from my knee all the way up through my shoulders.

05:36:45.688 --> 05:36:46.849
And I'm just going to sit here.

05:36:46.990 --> 05:36:48.552
First, I'm going to get in a good position.

05:36:49.033 --> 05:36:50.996
This ankle should be under that knee.

05:36:51.657 --> 05:36:55.442
I should already feel a little bit of a stretch in that hip flexor, a little bit in the quad as well.

05:36:55.883 --> 05:36:58.367
Now from here, I'm getting nice and wide.

05:36:58.407 --> 05:36:59.528
I'm not going to lean forward.

05:36:59.909 --> 05:37:02.072
And I'm going arms out.

05:37:02.052 --> 05:37:13.783
and together now at the top with straight elbows not bent elbows at the top I'm going to lean towards the lead leg so that's to my right as far as I can

05:37:14.523 --> 05:37:18.170
hold for five, and then come right back, okay?

05:37:18.190 --> 05:37:27.248
And then we're hands down, all the way up, hands together, nice and tall, elongate through the spine, and lean towards that lead leg.

05:37:27.288 --> 05:37:33.460
Three, two, one, back down, and we're back through.

05:37:46.670 --> 05:38:08.847
for my side plank row i'm going to use a cable if you don't have a cable we can always use a band here i'm going to make sure that that cable is on the lower setting so that we're nice and flat with this cable and it's not aiming up and it's not aiming down so i'm going to get into my side plank position on that forearm i'm going to make sure that i'm grounded from the wrist all the way through the elbow

05:38:08.827 --> 05:38:11.709
And I'm just gonna stack the feet nice and flat.

05:38:12.270 --> 05:38:16.213
I'm gonna be nice and flat from toes all the way up to my head.

05:38:16.574 --> 05:38:19.176
Okay, I'm not sinking, I'm not hiking the hips up.

05:38:19.556 --> 05:38:24.120
And then from here, it's just a regular row, all right?

05:38:24.140 --> 05:38:29.405
So I'm gonna feel that a lot in the obliques, especially the one on the underside.

05:38:29.525 --> 05:38:34.569
So if I'm on my right side, that right oblique side of the core is gonna be fired up, all right?

05:38:34.589 --> 05:38:37.092
If that is too difficult, we can always stack the feet.

05:38:37.532 --> 05:38:38.833
So you could always

05:38:38.813 --> 05:38:44.791
come to more of a 90 degree position and stack the legs that way.

05:38:45.112 --> 05:38:49.144
Alright so that's just a little bit of a easier version.

05:38:49.782 --> 05:38:55.489
But I think most of you by this time can go ahead and get into that full stack position.

05:38:55.589 --> 05:38:59.975
So we're flat all the way from the feet through the back of the head.

05:39:00.476 --> 05:39:01.617
Get up nice and high.

05:39:02.198 --> 05:39:07.244
But I do think that most of you guys are probably ready to go to that flat position with the straight legs.

05:39:07.264 --> 05:39:08.045
So try that out.

05:39:08.426 --> 05:39:12.011
Start with a light weight as always and gradually go up.

05:39:12.051 --> 05:39:16.296
We do want to go as heavy as we can as long as we're maintaining that posture.

05:39:16.276 --> 05:39:20.968
If we're going so heavy that we're starting to go into rotation to pull, that's too heavy.

05:39:21.248 --> 05:39:28.767
This is more of an anti-rotation, anti-lateral flexion exercise, so I want you to stay stable while your arm moves.

05:39:41.521 --> 05:39:50.354
For the elastic leg kicks, I want you to think about a normal, kind of like a Frankenstein kick, except we're gonna make it a little bit more rapid and a little bit more elastic.

05:39:50.655 --> 05:40:02.112
So I'm just gonna stay on the balls of the feet and I'm just gonna kick up my leg as high as I can, keeping that knee straight, and I'm just gonna switch every time so it ends up being a little bit of a skip.

05:40:02.092 --> 05:40:30.748
and then a straight leg kick okay now i'm popping off the balls of the feet i do not want your heels to touch the floor and the reason why we're doing this you're not going to get a huge burn this isn't a huge workout we're trying to get some elasticity back in the post chain some elasticity in the hamstrings so a lot of people sit there and stretch or even do a normal dynamic warm-up for elasticity the thing is for a muscle to actually increase elasticity it has to be under one second

05:40:30.728 --> 05:40:56.584
so if i'm going slow with that kick or if i'm holding a stretch it might increase what we call plasticity how flexible we get but not elasticity and elasticity is what actually translates to sprinting and jumping so everything has to be under one second and that's why we're going rapid with those kicks i want you to think about getting that leg up as quick as you can as high as you can

05:40:58.049 --> 05:41:03.001
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for day one and day five of phase four.

05:41:03.061 --> 05:41:05.568
So this is getting into our elasticity phase.

05:41:06.570 --> 05:41:09.137
You'll notice that workouts are already pretty efficient.

05:41:09.558 --> 05:41:11.683
Really try not to take out anything here.

05:41:12.064 --> 05:41:14.590
The only time I would take anything out of these days

05:41:14.570 --> 05:41:16.072
is if you're in season.

05:41:16.393 --> 05:41:19.837
If you're in season, the plyometric volume might be too high.

05:41:20.238 --> 05:41:26.246
Go back to phase one or phase two, add in some of those strength exercises and take out some of the reactive plyos.

05:41:26.487 --> 05:41:27.828
It'll be easier on your knees.

05:41:28.449 --> 05:41:32.074
But assuming you're in off season, get after this workout plan.

05:41:32.134 --> 05:41:33.356
Don't skip anything.

05:41:34.077 --> 05:41:34.738
Of course,

05:41:34.718 --> 05:41:37.684
Always make sure that your knees feel good to go.

05:41:37.724 --> 05:41:45.560
You don't want to push through any type of joint pain, because plyometrics can lead to significant injury if you're pushing through pain.

05:41:45.941 --> 05:41:54.679
Could be one of the best things we do for the vertical jump, or if we push through pain and plow through it without listening to our body, it's going to cause more pain.

05:41:54.899 --> 05:41:56.322
So you've got to be cautious here.

05:41:56.656 --> 05:41:58.200
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

05:41:58.320 --> 05:42:00.024
I have a long roller from Skills.

05:42:00.284 --> 05:42:07.160
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

05:42:07.521 --> 05:42:12.893
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

05:42:12.873 --> 05:42:16.018
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

05:42:16.359 --> 05:42:25.193
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

05:42:25.534 --> 05:42:32.265
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

05:42:32.245 --> 05:42:58.978
or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well so the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain we're going to focus on the hamstrings so i'm just going to sit on the roller and i'm going to cross over this foot and i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee so i'm just going to start in the center and then i'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so i can get the outside of my hamstrings

05:42:58.958 --> 05:43:03.670
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

05:43:03.903 --> 05:43:05.685
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

05:43:05.985 --> 05:43:09.389
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

05:43:09.469 --> 05:43:17.698
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

05:43:17.758 --> 05:43:21.522
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

05:43:21.923 --> 05:43:23.284
And our next one we're flipping over.

05:43:23.324 --> 05:43:26.528
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

05:43:26.548 --> 05:43:33.876
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

05:43:33.856 --> 05:43:40.147
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

05:43:40.868 --> 05:43:43.854
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

05:43:44.294 --> 05:43:49.544
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

05:43:50.004 --> 05:43:56.556
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

05:43:56.536 --> 05:43:59.442
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

05:43:59.622 --> 05:44:01.365
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

05:44:01.686 --> 05:44:15.473
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

05:44:17.208 --> 05:44:24.055
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

05:44:24.235 --> 05:44:27.799
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

05:44:28.079 --> 05:44:31.362
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

05:44:31.903 --> 05:44:34.606
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

05:44:34.626 --> 05:44:36.287
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

05:44:36.588 --> 05:44:40.411
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

05:44:40.872 --> 05:44:43.975
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

05:44:43.955 --> 05:44:51.865
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

05:44:52.186 --> 05:44:54.909
We got to really start to loosen that up.

05:44:56.492 --> 05:45:01.058
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

05:45:01.318 --> 05:45:04.843
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

05:45:04.883 --> 05:45:10.049
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

05:45:10.410 --> 05:45:12.072
Everything else is extra credit.

05:45:15.089 --> 05:45:18.855
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

05:45:19.135 --> 05:45:21.699
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

05:45:21.779 --> 05:45:25.745
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

05:45:26.246 --> 05:45:32.836
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

05:45:33.197 --> 05:45:36.882
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

05:45:36.902 --> 05:45:38.565
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

05:45:38.545 --> 05:45:41.790
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

05:45:42.131 --> 05:45:43.613
I'm not going too fast yet.

