05:52:05.898 --> 05:52:07.460
So I want you to do without a ball.

05:52:07.820 --> 05:52:09.943
I like to just pretend like I have a ball, all right?

05:52:10.163 --> 05:52:14.388
If you're not a basketball player, if you're a volleyball player, just move in different ways.

05:52:14.568 --> 05:52:18.553
However you want to move, whatever's natural to you, freestyle.

05:52:18.533 --> 05:52:30.131
Even if you're doing footwork patterns that you don't need for your sport, it's going to get you better, just your general human capabilities and your overall movement, which then kind of takes up that potential.

05:52:30.171 --> 05:52:37.082
So then when you go back to the specifics of your sport, all of a sudden that becomes a lot easier.

05:52:37.062 --> 05:52:38.023
Let's see how this looks.

05:52:38.223 --> 05:52:40.366
So I would start at like the baseline.

05:52:40.446 --> 05:52:51.679
I'm going to go to about half court or if you're in like a small area like a weight room, you can just do this stationary and kind of just stay in the same spot but just move around a little bit within that area.

05:52:51.779 --> 05:52:55.003
So I'm going to do this going towards the half court.

05:52:55.423 --> 05:53:02.952
So I'm just whatever I want to do and I'm just freestyling intuitively.

05:53:03.151 --> 05:53:16.532
kind of get in your bag, maybe hit some different footwork combinations that you normally wouldn't hit and just get loose, freestyle, work in some different ways that's a little bit out of your comfort zone.

05:53:16.552 --> 05:53:18.676
Maybe you go ahead and pretend like you're Kyrie Irving.

05:53:19.036 --> 05:53:24.585
I want you to get out of your bag and enter a whole new level with this freestyle footwork.

05:53:25.146 --> 05:53:25.767
Let's get it.

05:54:37.162 --> 05:55:04.620
our progressive bounds i'm going to go baseline to the opposite free throw line so three quarters court it's going to be somewhere around 70 feet if you're out on a track or you're doing this on a field mark out 70 feet i think that's about 21 meters or something around there so the reason we're doing these progressive bounds this is probably the best thing that we can do for reactive plyometrics and that really translates to our quick jumps so our single leg jumps there's nothing better

05:55:04.600 --> 05:55:06.982
for our single leg takeoff than this drill.

05:55:07.343 --> 05:55:09.565
Better than any strength exercise you could ever do.

05:55:09.925 --> 05:55:12.047
Now, it's very high intensity.

05:55:12.128 --> 05:55:17.933
So if you don't have a good base of strength, you probably will end up with some overuse injuries.

05:55:18.194 --> 05:55:19.715
This might hurt your knees a little bit.

05:55:20.035 --> 05:55:22.058
Do not push past the point of pain.

05:55:22.158 --> 05:55:32.548
So if you feel the knees start to hurt or the ankles or the hips or the back, any joint start to really hurt outside of just our normal muscles, stop this exercise because it is very intense.

05:55:32.528 --> 05:55:49.828
chances are good you either have a previous injury or you're at risk for some type of overuse injury or we just don't have a good base of strength and we got to go back to phase one maybe or phase two and redo our strength phase so that we can get stronger and our muscles and tendons can be more prepared

05:55:49.808 --> 05:55:51.950
for this high intensity plyo activity.

05:55:52.131 --> 05:55:57.417
So on my alternating right, left, right, left, what I'm going to do is I'm trying to get off the ground as quick as I can.

05:55:57.717 --> 05:55:58.818
I got a dorsiflex.

05:55:58.838 --> 05:56:06.187
So I got to pull my toe up towards my head and really dorsiflex so that I can think about kind of slapping the floor with the ball of the foot.

05:56:06.487 --> 05:56:07.688
And I'm just popping off.

05:56:07.728 --> 05:56:09.510
I'm driving that opposite knee.

05:56:09.831 --> 05:56:12.694
I'm going for height and I'm going for distance.

05:56:12.674 --> 05:56:14.797
So I don't want you just getting horizontal.

05:56:15.118 --> 05:56:16.660
I don't want you just getting vertical.

05:56:16.980 --> 05:56:22.288
It's got to be a combination of the two where we are going for that height and we're getting distance.

05:56:22.328 --> 05:56:25.873
But most importantly on this drill, we need a quick ground contact.

05:56:26.113 --> 05:56:34.686
We don't want to really lower and slowly, gradually dip into our joints because then it becomes more and more muscles and less tendons.

05:56:34.746 --> 05:56:37.770
We got to be springy and reactive off the ground.

05:56:37.750 --> 05:56:41.994
Now, when I get to more of the single leg bounds, it becomes a little more difficult on coordination.

05:56:42.355 --> 05:56:47.259
I do want to make sure that you're still getting off the ground quick and you're always driving that opposite knee.

05:56:47.279 --> 05:56:53.926
So if I'm right, right, right, I got to be right, drive the left, land right, drive the left.

05:56:53.946 --> 05:56:56.548
So I'm still driving the left every single time.

05:56:57.149 --> 05:57:05.277
We always have to have that opposite knee drive with the opposite arm drive, whether I'm doing alternating or whether I'm doing same side bounds.

05:57:26.317 --> 05:57:30.685
So for my double leg hurdle jumps, what I'm going to do is set up two hurdles.

05:57:30.725 --> 05:57:34.031
They can either be high or low or just a pair of shoes.

05:57:34.091 --> 05:57:36.395
All we need is something to mark off the jump.

05:57:36.435 --> 05:57:39.341
So I'm setting them up at least 15 feet apart.

05:57:39.861 --> 05:57:45.692
This first one, typically I like to go a little bit lower and the second one I like to go a little bit higher.

05:57:45.672 --> 05:57:47.995
So get nice and relaxed, don't be tense.

05:57:48.536 --> 05:57:51.920
If you're tense in this drill, your timing is all gonna be lost.

05:57:52.140 --> 05:58:01.252
So you gotta be nice and relaxed and loose, get over this first one, you're gonna land, and don't get stuck in quicksand, I need you to pop off the ground.

05:58:01.272 --> 05:58:06.479
So once we land, pop into that next step, get your feet right, get over again.

05:58:06.579 --> 05:58:08.041
So it's always the same approach.

05:58:08.401 --> 05:58:13.468
So if I'm starting right left, then this second one is also gonna be right left.

05:58:13.448 --> 05:58:16.714
And then on the next rep, it's left right.

05:58:17.175 --> 05:58:22.424
So I'm left right over the first hurdle, get my steps right, left right over the second hurdle.

05:58:22.525 --> 05:58:28.776
So what this is doing is it's working on timing, it's working on the distance of these steps, working on rhythm.

05:58:29.217 --> 05:58:33.124
The better we get, the shorter we can get with the hurdles.

05:58:33.465 --> 05:58:35.188
So I want you to mess around with the length.

05:58:35.288 --> 05:58:36.891
Start with the full 15 feet.

05:58:36.871 --> 05:58:55.335
for the first session the next time that you do these try to shorten them up a little bit all the way to the point where we're getting over and then we're getting only one or two steps and we're getting over that next hurdle all right mix it up give yourself some variety challenge yourself with the distance let's get it

05:59:16.831 --> 05:59:24.303
So for the barbell RDL, you could use a trap bar or if you just have dumbbells, you could hold the dumbbell right here by the top of the dumbbell.

05:59:24.584 --> 05:59:26.226
But if you have a barbell, we're going to use that.

05:59:26.567 --> 05:59:29.792
We're going to go about shoulder width apart or a little bit wider.

05:59:29.832 --> 05:59:34.961
And then from there, just outside your hips, I want you to turn your hands.

05:59:35.281 --> 05:59:37.565
So you have one palm facing that way.

05:59:37.846 --> 05:59:39.488
You have one palm facing the other way.

05:59:39.849 --> 05:59:42.433
From there, we're going to get down and we're going to pick the barbell up.

05:59:42.413 --> 05:59:51.833
like it's a normal deadlift so I'm just gonna pick it up here my back stays flat alright you can kind of reset from there now

05:59:51.982 --> 05:59:57.009
Just like on a pull through or any hip hinge, I'm gonna start with that slight bend in my knees.

05:59:57.049 --> 05:59:58.611
So I'm not completely stiff-legged.

05:59:58.831 --> 06:00:00.513
I'm slightly bending the knees.

06:00:00.854 --> 06:00:06.501
Now from there, it's not a squat or it's not a normal deadlift where I continue to bend my knees.

06:00:06.682 --> 06:00:08.184
That would be a normal deadlift.

06:00:08.604 --> 06:00:13.631
Right now, I wanna push my hips back as far as I can without bending my knees more.

06:00:13.991 --> 06:00:20.520
So I'm basically keeping this bar as close as I can to my body and I'm shifting the hips back

06:00:20.500 --> 06:00:23.526
I'm gonna get to about mid shin, and then I'm coming back up.

06:00:24.007 --> 06:00:24.788
Okay, so it's here.

06:00:25.630 --> 06:00:30.699
Hip hinge, push the hips back, back, back, back, back, and come back up, okay?

06:00:31.120 --> 06:00:38.053
At the bottom, you should feel a big stretch in the hamstrings, and then at the top, you should really be squeezing your glutes

06:00:38.033 --> 06:00:42.225
if you just pitch forward, you're gonna feel a lot of it in your back.

06:00:42.546 --> 06:00:50.870
You shouldn't feel this exercise in your back, maybe a slight burn in the back, but most of it should be the hamstrings and the glutes, okay?

06:00:51.150 --> 06:00:52.414
Another common mistake,

06:00:52.968 --> 06:01:15.786
is getting the barbell out too far in front you've got to really slide it right down your thighs and then right down your shins where it's almost completely touching okay now the other thing if you're good with this hip hinge in your advance let's start to think about the feet so look at my toes as i'm going down i'm lifting my toes up in the air okay so it's toes up

06:01:17.048 --> 06:01:22.956
And then as soon as I change directions to go up, I'm pushing my toes as hard as I can into the ground.

06:01:23.256 --> 06:01:25.259
So it's toes in and lift.

06:01:25.759 --> 06:01:30.486
That's gonna really build that connection between my toes, my feet, and my glutes.

06:01:30.506 --> 06:01:35.732
Okay, so on the way down, toes up, up, up, and push into the ground and stand.

06:01:35.873 --> 06:01:38.616
So we are focusing on this as an eccentric exercise.

06:01:38.837 --> 06:01:41.200
So I wanna go three seconds down, one second up.

06:01:41.480 --> 06:01:43.022
So it's gonna be one,

06:01:43.002 --> 06:01:47.687
two, three, and one second up, okay?

06:01:47.707 --> 06:01:51.431
One, two, three, one second up.

06:01:51.451 --> 06:02:04.945
Now, when you're digging your toes into the ground, really think about pushing that big toe as hard as you can and kind of like you're trying to crack the floor with that big toe, and then that's gonna build that connection with the glutes, okay?

06:02:05.345 --> 06:02:12.132
Now, if this is too difficult with a barbell, step back, work on your form with a light dumbbell or even body weight.

06:02:12.112 --> 06:02:19.303
If you're having a tough time with your hip hinge, you can always just go here with your hands and just work on getting your hips back, back, back.

06:02:19.784 --> 06:02:20.065
All right.

06:02:20.626 --> 06:02:23.310
The other thing is we don't need to obsess over depth.

06:02:23.630 --> 06:02:29.139
So we don't always need to go all the way down to the floor, especially if you have bigger weights on the outside.

06:02:29.480 --> 06:02:33.767
You're only going to be able to get the barbell to about mid shin or just below your knees.

06:02:34.167 --> 06:02:35.950
Don't obsess with going lower than that.

06:02:36.331 --> 06:02:39.115
The studies have shown that lower isn't always better here.

06:03:03.580 --> 06:03:07.625
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

06:03:07.725 --> 06:03:09.547
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

06:03:09.567 --> 06:03:12.291
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

06:03:12.311 --> 06:03:18.839
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

06:03:19.360 --> 06:03:25.307
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

06:03:25.587 --> 06:03:29.352
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

06:03:29.332 --> 06:03:33.239
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

06:03:33.660 --> 06:03:37.486
So it's in through the nose, and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

06:03:38.047 --> 06:03:45.400
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you, when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

06:03:46.409 --> 06:03:49.473
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

06:03:49.493 --> 06:03:56.222
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

06:03:56.763 --> 06:04:02.531
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

06:04:02.551 --> 06:04:06.216
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

06:04:08.519 --> 06:04:12.805
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

06:04:15.738 --> 06:04:17.660
and that stomach will fall, okay?

06:04:18.381 --> 06:04:21.003
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

06:04:21.784 --> 06:04:30.773
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

06:04:31.594 --> 06:04:39.342
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

06:04:39.602 --> 06:04:43.686
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

06:04:43.666 --> 06:05:12.559
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

06:05:12.758 --> 06:05:15.262
happens in the parasympathetic state.

06:05:15.802 --> 06:05:19.949
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

06:05:20.289 --> 06:05:33.889
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

06:05:35.945 --> 06:05:41.855
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase five, day one and day five.

06:05:42.196 --> 06:05:51.832
So if we have to and we're pressed for time, skip that foam roller circuit, do the dynamic warm up, do the band rapid laterals and the lateral bound one foot jump.

06:05:52.173 --> 06:05:55.118
If you're really pressed for time, cut those down to two sets.

06:05:55.098 --> 06:05:59.283
Skip the over speed block step and the acceleration zone jumps.

06:05:59.604 --> 06:06:04.170
I love these for mechanics, but that is one that we could skip if we're pressed for time.

06:06:04.830 --> 06:06:09.977
Also, if your mechanics are already really good, we may not need that as much.

06:06:10.257 --> 06:06:12.761
It's still beneficial just to groove the pattern.

06:06:13.081 --> 06:06:21.612
Even people with good mechanics should be continually doing drills like this, but it's way less important, especially if your mechanics are already mastered.

06:06:21.592 --> 06:06:26.439
Moving down, we always have to do our hang high pull and our pogo box taps.

06:06:26.899 --> 06:06:30.104
That's possibly the most important aspect of this day.

06:06:30.985 --> 06:06:34.630
If you were really pressed for time, you could cut it down to three sets instead of four sets.

06:06:35.171 --> 06:06:40.478
So split stance pin squat and the rhythm quarter squat, you can choose one of those.

06:06:40.458 --> 06:06:44.022
I would like to do both with the split stance pin squat.

06:06:44.042 --> 06:06:50.390
We're working with more heavier weights, a little bit more explosive, so we're training those fast twitch muscle fibers.

06:06:50.410 --> 06:06:56.197
The rhythm squat is more about rhythm and coordination and stretch reflex, stretch shortening cycle.

06:06:56.638 --> 06:07:01.003
So they're training different things, but if you're pressed for time, just choose one of them.

06:07:01.304 --> 06:07:09.874
So you could choose the split stance pin squat and take out the rhythm quarter squat, or just take out the split stance pin squat and just do the rhythm quarter squat.

06:07:09.854 --> 06:07:16.406
So you're supersetting it either way, whichever one you choose, you're supersetting that with the cable pull through.

06:07:16.807 --> 06:07:20.974
We have to do that because this is our hip dominant exercise for the day.

06:07:21.054 --> 06:07:26.985
We're really getting some good work through the glutes and through the hamstrings, so we need this exercise for the post chain.

06:07:26.965 --> 06:07:30.608
and then we always wanna do our sub max long duration jumps.

06:07:31.429 --> 06:07:36.974
If you're pressed for time, you could cut these down to two sets, but do make sure that you are doing that exercise.

06:07:37.495 --> 06:07:39.897
As always, we're gonna do our 7-11 breathing.

06:07:40.197 --> 06:07:45.482
It's only three minutes, and it gets us in that parasympathetic zone, which boosts our recovery.

06:07:46.182 --> 06:07:51.007
The static stretch we could skip, unless you are overly tight, then you wanna do that.

06:07:51.427 --> 06:07:56.652
Most of you guys, by now, have built a level of flexibility where we don't really need much more.

06:07:56.632 --> 06:07:58.555
so you can skip that static stretch.

06:07:58.875 --> 06:08:00.457
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

06:08:00.597 --> 06:08:02.280
I have a long roller from Skills.

06:08:02.560 --> 06:08:09.429
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

06:08:09.790 --> 06:08:15.197
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

06:08:15.177 --> 06:08:18.284
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

06:08:18.624 --> 06:08:27.504
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

06:08:27.804 --> 06:08:34.519
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

06:08:34.499 --> 06:08:41.009
or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

06:08:41.390 --> 06:08:43.633
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

06:08:43.653 --> 06:08:45.055
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

06:08:45.396 --> 06:08:53.789
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

06:08:54.169 --> 06:09:01.300
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

06:09:01.280 --> 06:09:05.940
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

06:09:06.173 --> 06:09:07.955
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

06:09:08.235 --> 06:09:11.659
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

06:09:11.739 --> 06:09:19.949
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

06:09:20.029 --> 06:09:25.555
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

06:09:25.615 --> 06:09:28.799
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

06:09:28.819 --> 06:09:36.147
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

06:09:36.127 --> 06:09:42.418
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

06:09:43.139 --> 06:09:46.125
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

06:09:46.565 --> 06:09:51.815
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

06:09:52.275 --> 06:09:58.827
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

06:09:58.807 --> 06:10:01.713
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

06:10:01.894 --> 06:10:03.617
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

06:10:03.958 --> 06:10:17.727
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

06:10:19.495 --> 06:10:26.324
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

06:10:26.505 --> 06:10:30.049
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

06:10:30.370 --> 06:10:33.634
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

06:10:34.175 --> 06:10:36.859
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

06:10:36.879 --> 06:10:38.561
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

06:10:38.862 --> 06:10:42.887
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

06:10:42.867 --> 06:10:45.813
and I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

06:10:46.374 --> 06:10:57.315
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping, we gotta really start to loosen that up.

06:10:58.662 --> 06:11:03.314
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

06:11:03.575 --> 06:11:07.104
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

06:11:07.124 --> 06:11:12.297
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

06:11:12.678 --> 06:11:14.342
Everything else is extra credit.

06:11:17.410 --> 06:11:21.156
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

06:11:21.436 --> 06:11:24.000
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

06:11:24.081 --> 06:11:28.047
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

06:11:28.548 --> 06:11:35.158
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

06:11:35.479 --> 06:11:39.164
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

06:11:39.204 --> 06:11:40.887
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

06:11:40.867 --> 06:11:44.072
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

06:11:44.433 --> 06:11:45.915
I'm not going too fast yet.

06:11:46.215 --> 06:11:48.359
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

06:11:48.599 --> 06:11:53.146
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

06:11:53.166 --> 06:11:57.373
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

06:11:57.754 --> 06:12:01.319
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

06:12:01.620 --> 06:12:03.863
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

06:12:03.883 --> 06:12:06.267
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

06:12:06.247 --> 06:12:11.536
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed, so we're not all out.

06:12:11.996 --> 06:12:13.559
We're just getting that blood flowing.

06:12:13.859 --> 06:12:15.202
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

06:12:15.402 --> 06:12:19.729
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

06:12:20.049 --> 06:12:21.852
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

06:12:21.932 --> 06:12:31.528
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

06:12:31.508 --> 06:12:39.173
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

06:12:42.882 --> 06:12:47.227
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

06:12:47.447 --> 06:12:52.753
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

06:12:53.053 --> 06:12:59.600
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

06:13:00.020 --> 06:13:08.029
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

06:13:08.009 --> 06:13:12.977
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

06:13:13.317 --> 06:13:18.925
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

06:13:19.607 --> 06:13:21.950
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

06:13:22.291 --> 06:13:26.898
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

06:13:27.538 --> 06:13:29.882
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

06:13:30.790 --> 06:13:34.439
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

06:13:34.860 --> 06:13:37.226
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

06:13:37.286 --> 06:13:39.311
One, two, three.

06:13:39.371 --> 06:13:41.657
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

06:13:41.977 --> 06:13:43.982
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

06:13:44.022 --> 06:13:44.624
That's okay.

06:13:44.965 --> 06:13:46.549
Let's just get as low as we can there.

06:13:47.030 --> 06:13:47.972
One, two, three.

06:13:47.952 --> 06:14:14.449
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

