If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

06:16:10.864 --> 06:16:15.229
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

06:16:15.590 --> 06:16:21.957
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

06:16:21.937 --> 06:16:24.741
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

06:16:24.801 --> 06:16:27.124
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

06:16:27.524 --> 06:16:30.308
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

06:16:30.628 --> 06:16:32.330
I need to be flat through the hips.

06:16:32.611 --> 06:16:35.034
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

06:16:35.555 --> 06:16:40.000
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

06:16:40.361 --> 06:16:43.145
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

06:16:43.425 --> 06:16:51.235
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

06:16:51.215 --> 06:17:01.210
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

06:17:01.631 --> 06:17:09.983
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

06:17:16.730 --> 06:17:19.956
So for my band rapid laterals, I'm gonna link together two bands.

06:17:20.057 --> 06:17:23.443
If you only have one band, as long as it stretches pretty far, you should be fine.

06:17:23.904 --> 06:17:31.820
So what I'm gonna do is step out, get some pretty good tension on this band, and now this is gonna be a very different movement than what you're used to.

06:17:32.080 --> 06:17:35.447
It's a lateral, but it's not the same mechanics as a slide.

06:17:35.427 --> 06:17:38.812
What I want to work on is a relatively stiff change of direction.

06:17:38.892 --> 06:17:44.619
So if you look at the best change of direction athletes, they're not always lowering low and then exploding out.

06:17:44.880 --> 06:17:50.808
They're landing with that already bent knee in its lowest position and then they're pushing out from there.

06:17:51.248 --> 06:17:58.618
So that stiff leg landing, not completely locked out, but a slight bend in the knee is one of the best ways to change directions.

06:17:59.039 --> 06:18:05.147
So what we're going to do is get a nice base here and then I'm just going to go here.

06:18:05.127 --> 06:18:07.030
Stiff leg, push out.

06:18:07.451 --> 06:18:08.954
Stiff leg, push out.

06:18:09.234 --> 06:18:10.837
So I'm replacing this foot.

06:18:11.378 --> 06:18:20.655
One foot is always going there in the middle, and notice my chest stays over that leg, so I'm not moving my body back and forth.

06:18:23.268 --> 06:18:28.875
and then we just gradually build up that speed and pop off the ground as quick as you can.

06:18:29.295 --> 06:18:32.599
It's hot lava on the outsides, we gotta get off quick.

06:18:33.220 --> 06:18:44.453
We are gonna tilt our shoulder a little bit, so if I'm going to change direction this way, my left shoulder's gonna tilt down a little bit, and then when I'm coming this way, right shoulder's gonna tilt down a little bit, all right?

06:18:44.873 --> 06:18:48.297
Chest is never moving, and we always have

06:18:48.277 --> 06:18:49.781
one foot in the center.

06:18:50.243 --> 06:18:55.939
Again, I do not want you to lock out the leg, but I also don't want you to bend low.

06:18:56.421 --> 06:19:02.438
I want you to be somewhere in the middle where we have that slight bend in the knee and we're popping off the ground.

06:19:20.339 --> 06:19:27.331
So for my lateral bound one foot jump, I'm going to use a normal lateral bound so this left knee is behind the right knee.

06:19:27.631 --> 06:19:31.017
I'm loaded up in this skater position and I push out.

06:19:31.238 --> 06:19:38.250
Now if my skills are good at absorbing that force, I can transition vertically off that one foot.

06:19:38.530 --> 06:19:43.258
So I'm going to land and I'm going to jump and then I'm going to land and push.

06:19:43.930 --> 06:20:00.825
jump i still need to work on getting as far as i can laterally now the hard part is transitioning from lateral to vertical all right everybody gets here and then they want to fade into that jump i need you to absorb force and transition sharp

06:20:00.805 --> 06:20:06.393
So you're almost making like a 90 degree angle and then we go back that other way, alright?

06:20:06.894 --> 06:20:16.287
If this is too difficult and your form is sloppy and all over the place, go back to a basic single leg change of direction bound, alright?

06:20:16.748 --> 06:20:24.619
This is more advanced and it does take some really good absorption skills to be able to transition vertical, so don't rush it.

06:20:24.599 --> 06:20:32.179
but if you're good and you can hit maybe three or four in a row without messing up, then you're probably at a good level and let's keep pushing.

06:20:50.992 --> 06:20:57.178
So for my overspeed block step, we're working on the angle of that block step or that second foot contact.

06:20:57.339 --> 06:21:03.545
So what I'm gonna do is strap together two bands, two super bands, get it around something nice and stable.

06:21:03.885 --> 06:21:14.116
Now if you only have one band, that might be okay as long as it really stretches pretty far, but if it's very thick and strong and you can't get very far, you're not gonna have the room here.

06:21:14.497 --> 06:21:17.860
So if that's the case, then skip this drill altogether.

06:21:17.840 --> 06:21:25.751
Because I don't want you to run into the power rack or whatever you have it tied down to so I'm linking these two bands together.

06:21:25.771 --> 06:21:27.854
I want you to start off with some light tension.

06:21:27.914 --> 06:21:31.680
We don't need too much to start So start off with some light tension.

06:21:31.720 --> 06:21:42.295
I'm just going to go into my normal jump mechanics my penultimate step arms are going back I'm going to use that turn and I'm going to work on the angle of this left foot so I'm here and

06:21:42.275 --> 06:21:45.299
Boom, and I'm just coming up to the toes, okay?

06:21:45.780 --> 06:21:52.750
So you'll notice as I go into that penultimate step, this band is pulling me in faster than what I'm used to.

06:21:53.251 --> 06:21:59.980
The only way that we can stop that and decelerate that is to get that good negative shin angle.

06:21:59.960 --> 06:22:11.986
So a lot of good jumpers have a 40 degree shin angle to the floor, whereas lower level jumpers who don't transition well have closer to a 60 or sometimes even closer to a 90 degree.

06:22:12.407 --> 06:22:14.431
And if we have that shin angle, we go forward.

06:22:14.491 --> 06:22:18.660
So if I were to step in like this with vertical shins,

06:22:18.640 --> 06:22:19.942
I'm getting pulled forward.

06:22:20.222 --> 06:22:24.950
So this band is giving me the feedback that I need to fix my mechanics naturally.

06:22:25.250 --> 06:22:28.095
You probably don't even have to think about the angle of this.

06:22:28.375 --> 06:22:31.560
You're naturally going to get it as long as you're using that turn.

06:22:32.682 --> 06:22:37.810
So as you get good, we can step back a little bit and now I'm going to really add that long pin ultimate.

06:22:39.232 --> 06:22:45.542
You see how I naturally stopped with that really good angle and that's going to help me transition vertically.

06:22:58.718 --> 06:23:05.206
So for my acceleration zone jumps, I'm going to start in a push-up position with my hand behind the cone.

06:23:05.867 --> 06:23:15.219
I'm going to push up as hard as I can, and I'm just going to get my foot under me, and then I'm going to push off this right foot into a bound.

06:23:15.279 --> 06:23:22.348
So the reason we're starting in that push-up position is because when we get that foot down on the ground, we have a really steep shin angle.

06:23:22.408 --> 06:23:27.915
So it's good for teaching acceleration, and it's good for getting us to really build up that speed.

06:23:27.895 --> 06:23:37.513
I'm going to get that foot down, I'm going to bound out, I'm going to land on my left, I'm going to bound as far as I can, and then I'm landing right left in my jump stance.

06:23:38.555 --> 06:23:45.027
So it's right, left, right, left, get up as high as you can.

06:23:45.428 --> 06:23:46.809
There's two parts to this drill.

06:23:47.130 --> 06:23:51.675
We're working on the acceleration and I want you to work on getting as far as you can.

06:23:52.376 --> 06:23:58.963
So if you can, have a friend watch you and have them tell you where your block foot lands.

06:23:58.983 --> 06:24:01.205
So that front foot, where is it landing?

06:24:01.546 --> 06:24:03.408
And we want to get further and further every time.

06:24:03.448 --> 06:24:06.191
Now, if you could film yourself, do it.

06:24:06.591 --> 06:24:09.394
But wherever that block foot is, we want to mark it off.

06:24:09.374 --> 06:24:12.418
So, I'm going to put a cone where my block foot is.

06:24:12.498 --> 06:24:14.660
For me, it's around 12 to 13 feet.

06:24:15.642 --> 06:24:17.844
So, I'm marking off at 12 feet.

06:24:18.165 --> 06:24:20.408
If you have the measuring tape, use it.

06:24:20.748 --> 06:24:22.230
If you don't, then just eyeball it.

06:24:23.411 --> 06:24:26.855
Now, from there, I'm working on that block step.

06:24:26.896 --> 06:24:29.118
So, I have all this momentum coming forward.

06:24:29.559 --> 06:24:35.466
I got to be able to get a steep angle and get that timing of the block foot so that I can get vertical.

06:24:35.446 --> 06:24:40.234
If you stepped in here with vertical shin angles, we fall forward.

06:24:40.474 --> 06:24:43.239
We're probably going to cover 5 feet on that jump.

06:24:43.800 --> 06:24:47.325
I want you to cover no more than 3 feet on that jump.

06:24:47.706 --> 06:24:50.491
So 12 feet is where I'm taking off from.

06:24:50.511 --> 06:24:53.155
15 feet is my goal for landing.

06:24:53.195 --> 06:24:56.460
So I have another cone marked off at 15 feet.

06:24:56.821 --> 06:25:00.627
So now if I hit that block the right way, I'm going to get vertical.

06:25:00.607 --> 06:25:03.555
and I'm gonna land before that cone, all right?

06:25:04.016 --> 06:25:15.527
So again, if you don't have the measuring tape, just eyeball it and just get as far as you can, get as high as you can, and try to cover the least amount of distance on that actual jump.

06:25:29.783 --> 06:25:33.890
So for my hang high pull, we can either use a trap bar or a barbell.

06:25:33.910 --> 06:25:35.372
I would prefer a trap bar.

06:25:35.933 --> 06:25:38.137
But if you don't have a trap bar, we could use a barbell.

06:25:38.177 --> 06:25:40.060
So I'm going to go with a hook grip.

06:25:40.320 --> 06:25:48.514
So that means I'm bringing my thumb around the bar and then I'm bringing my finger over that thumb or some people like to go two fingers over that thumb.

06:25:48.794 --> 06:25:50.537
That way it doesn't slip out of our hands.

06:25:50.958 --> 06:25:55.926
So with my arm length, I like to go right around the seams.

06:25:55.906 --> 06:26:20.547
on the barbell now a lot of you have longer arms you might want to go a little bit wider that's okay so I'm just going to deadlift it up all right now this is my base position notice I have a slight bend in the knee from there think about our RDL that we've been doing where we shift the hip backwards so I don't go down by bending my knees and if you do that by bending your knees you're actually going to

06:26:20.527 --> 06:26:22.230
feel a little pain in your back.

06:26:22.391 --> 06:26:26.839
So I want you to keep that initial knee bend and then from there it's just hips back.

06:26:27.039 --> 06:26:28.923
So I'm hinging from the hips.

06:26:29.304 --> 06:26:36.898
Once that bar gets right above knee height, I'm exploding as hard as I can and I'm just throwing this bar upwards.

06:26:37.452 --> 06:26:38.634
like that, okay?

06:26:38.934 --> 06:26:41.818
So I wanna end in this position.

06:26:42.258 --> 06:26:48.426
Now, it's not a lift where we do the hinge, we come here, and then we lift with the upper body.

06:26:48.927 --> 06:26:56.777
All of that momentum is coming from the lower body, transferring through the core into the upper body, so I need it to be fast and explosive.

06:26:56.757 --> 06:26:59.642
So we're here, and then boom, finish high.

06:26:59.683 --> 06:27:04.812
You finish on the toes like you're gonna take off for a jump, but your feet aren't gonna actually leave the ground.

06:27:05.013 --> 06:27:07.056
Okay, this is a advanced exercise.

06:27:07.417 --> 06:27:15.913
Now, if you've been Olympic lifting, you're probably familiar with brushing that bar so you can kinda hit it on the top of the thigh and pop up.

06:27:15.893 --> 06:27:23.782
The majority of us, we don't need to worry about that right now because that's going to take a long time to learn and it's going to hurt your thighs for a while in the process.

06:27:23.802 --> 06:27:31.291
So I'd rather you just think about getting to that good RDL position, that good hinge position, and then just exploding as hard as we can.

06:27:31.872 --> 06:27:38.560
Only thing, if you're not going to brush and you're not going to hit the top of the thigh, I don't want you to bring the barbell out here.

06:27:39.021 --> 06:27:42.745
I still need it to be on one line all the way up.

06:27:42.725 --> 06:27:43.928
close to the body.

06:27:44.048 --> 06:27:48.319
If we get it out away from the body, we're probably going to feel it more in the lower back.

06:27:48.599 --> 06:27:50.183
We're going to decrease our power output.

06:27:50.644 --> 06:27:52.108
Now there's a couple different options here.

06:27:52.710 --> 06:28:00.649
After we finish, we can either just drop the barbell if you're in a gym that allows that and you have bumper plates, or you can actually catch it.

06:28:00.629 --> 06:28:28.898
so catching it is higher stress to the body but it's also good because you get a little eccentric work on the catch so it's not a bad thing personally since i have the bumper plates i prefer to rip as hard as i can drop it and then pick it back up and go from there okay now if you have a trap bar it's going to be the same thing except slight bend in the knees hinge from the hips go all the way until your knuckles are even with your knee and then it's going to be explode from there all the way up to the toes

06:28:28.878 --> 06:28:56.691
toss that barbell up now on the barbell we would normally finish here with the trap bar we finish here so the elbows go backwards instead of out to the side alright now if these are too advanced for you because this is you know several years of strength training before you should really be doing explosive hang high pulls if that's the case I want you to go hinge

06:28:57.515 --> 06:28:58.657
and explode to the toes.

06:28:59.038 --> 06:29:06.250
So it's just hinge here, explode to the toes, and then back to that slight bend in the knee, hinge, boom, explode to the toes.

06:29:06.591 --> 06:29:10.638
You're really thinking about pulling the hips through, squeezing the glutes.

06:29:11.039 --> 06:29:16.147
This is gonna set a good foundation and really build towards that hang high pull.

06:29:16.408 --> 06:29:17.650
All right, let's get it.

06:29:34.707 --> 06:29:44.899
So for my pogo box taps, what I'm gonna do is basically a normal pogo hop where I'm staying off the heels, I'm staying on the balls of the feet, I'm getting nice and springy.

06:29:45.179 --> 06:29:49.244
It's all about short ground contact time, so I'm just springing off the ground.

06:29:49.644 --> 06:29:52.788
Now, what I'm gonna do is it's gonna be very rapid.

06:29:52.908 --> 06:30:02.419
I get up, I tap the box and I push back off, come down off two feet, make sure you land at the same time, pop back on, other foot goes on.

06:30:02.399 --> 06:30:04.664
We're never fully landing on the box.

06:30:04.684 --> 06:30:05.867
This is going to be rapid.

06:30:06.268 --> 06:30:08.954
So we're not jumping up and landing.

06:30:09.315 --> 06:30:14.727
I literally just want you to push back off that box, hit and spring up again.

06:30:14.767 --> 06:30:17.674
This is going to really work on our elasticity.

06:30:17.654 --> 06:30:23.584
Very good for making the Achilles into an energy storing and releasing monster.

06:30:23.884 --> 06:30:25.487
That's where a lot of our athleticism is.

06:30:25.828 --> 06:30:30.555
Again, we do not want to load up low on this specific exercise.

06:30:30.856 --> 06:30:34.462
We want a minimal knee bend and we want to pop off nice and quick.

06:30:34.822 --> 06:30:37.206
Make sure that we do not collapse the torso.

06:30:37.507 --> 06:30:39.931
We got to keep the torso nice and upright.

06:30:40.271 --> 06:30:44.218
Keep that stability through the core on this whole exercise.

06:30:44.198 --> 06:30:50.987
I like to start at a box that's a round knee height or a little bit below or a chair or a bench or something nice and stable.

06:30:51.508 --> 06:30:54.232
As you get better, you can raise it higher and higher and higher.

06:30:54.292 --> 06:31:00.200
Now, we do not want to be landing or we do not want to get up on the box and have too much of a bent knee.

06:31:00.501 --> 06:31:06.549
So if I have to get up and I have to land like this and push off, then that box is probably too high.

06:31:06.949 --> 06:31:12.417
I want to land with almost straight knees with a little bit of a bend in my knees at the top.

06:31:12.397 --> 06:31:14.964
like that, and then push off from there.

06:31:15.064 --> 06:31:19.938
So don't become obsessed with the box height, just be obsessed with the quickness off the ground.

06:31:20.258 --> 06:31:27.418
And even if you're on a low box, just think about jumping as high as you can and floating to that box instead of barely getting on the box.

06:31:27.398 --> 06:31:43.515
Now for some of you guys your balance isn't quite to this level yet so when you start getting rapid and you start pushing off it's hard for you to get balance and go back up or at the top your balance isn't good enough to just land and push off the right amount so you're kind of stumbling on the top of the box.

06:31:43.535 --> 06:31:51.263
If that's the case I want you just to regress and just get up and just stick on one foot and then you can just step it back down.

06:31:51.243 --> 06:31:57.126
Or if you can, stick it on one foot and then hop down and get up, stick it on the other foot.

06:31:57.527 --> 06:32:01.422
But again, once you pass that level, start to go more rapid with it.

06:32:12.809 --> 06:32:16.339
So for our rhythm quarter squats, we're gonna use a barbell.

06:32:16.980 --> 06:32:21.934
If you don't have a barbell, you could just use dumbbells, but we really want to use a barbell if possible.

06:32:22.475 --> 06:32:25.664
We're gonna go about 50% of your one rep max.

06:32:25.684 --> 06:32:28.371
So we're going pretty light to start.

06:32:28.351 --> 06:32:30.716
If you feel good, we can gradually go up.

06:32:31.116 --> 06:32:35.345
So what we're going to do is a normal barbell back squat position.

06:32:36.226 --> 06:32:39.934
What you're going to do is you're going to get down into a quarter squat position.

06:32:40.134 --> 06:32:43.260
So we don't want anything past 90 degrees.

06:32:43.601 --> 06:32:49.212
We actually want to mimic the knee angle that you're going to use in a double leg takeoff.

06:32:49.192 --> 06:33:16.482
I'm gonna get down to let's say maybe 60 to 75 degrees I'm not going all the way to 90 so I'm gonna get to that quarter squat position the easier version is just quarter squat and up the heels stay down and we're just gonna kind of bounce in that position and develop some rhythm there so I don't want you to get to the top and then rest and go again we're just getting that rhythmic

06:33:16.462 --> 06:33:17.244
quarter squat.

06:33:17.284 --> 06:33:20.029
So just working on that top half of the range of motion.

06:33:20.410 --> 06:33:25.540
Now the better version, if you have the balance, we're going to come all the way up to the toes.

06:33:25.901 --> 06:33:29.669
So it's going to be here all the way up to the toes at the top.

06:33:30.450 --> 06:33:36.242
So you sort of go into that little calf raise and then we drop back down into that quarter squat.

06:33:36.222 --> 06:33:41.413
All right guys, now keep in mind, it's about the load on the muscles, not the load on the bar.

06:33:41.674 --> 06:33:43.818
So don't worry about going too heavy.

06:33:43.858 --> 06:33:48.368
Again, start around 50% of your one rep max.

06:33:48.708 --> 06:33:50.592
If that's too light, then you can go up.

06:33:51.114 --> 06:33:54.180
But keep in mind, if you feel like you could jump

06:33:54.160 --> 06:34:16.050
off the ground with the barbell then it's too light because then by not jumping we're holding back and we're not actually getting max intent so it should be a weight where you couldn't easily leave the floor all right because i do want your toes to stay down on the ground on this exercise

06:34:29.142 --> 06:34:32.886
So for my cable pull through, we can use any normal cable machine.

06:34:32.947 --> 06:34:34.949
I'm showing it on a Kaiser machine right now.

06:34:34.969 --> 06:34:36.351
You don't need a Kaiser.

06:34:36.591 --> 06:34:37.933
We just need a normal cable.

06:34:38.353 --> 06:34:42.298
So I'm going to use the rope and I'm going to grab underhand.

06:34:42.358 --> 06:34:45.762
So like a tricep extension, you might be used to an overhand grip.

06:34:45.982 --> 06:34:47.364
We're actually going underhand.

06:34:47.604 --> 06:34:50.087
So my thumbs are facing up.

06:34:50.067 --> 06:34:54.853
and I'm grabbing that rope, stand up and step out a little bit.

06:34:55.454 --> 06:34:57.016
We're going to go pretty wide with the feet.

06:34:57.577 --> 06:35:02.002
Now I'm going to go slight bend in the knees and all it is is that normal RDL position.

06:35:02.483 --> 06:35:06.128
Work on that hip hinge so it's hips back as far as we can.

06:35:06.188 --> 06:35:09.392
Our knees don't bend anymore from there.

06:35:09.372 --> 06:35:10.994
Hips back, back, back.

06:35:11.074 --> 06:35:13.556
I'm loading up the hamstrings and the glutes.

06:35:13.576 --> 06:35:17.681
And then at that bottom position, we're gonna explode up at the top.

06:35:17.741 --> 06:35:23.126
I need you to squeeze your glutes as hard as you can and really pull the hips through.

06:35:23.166 --> 06:35:26.550
At the top, we get a two second isometric hold.

06:35:26.870 --> 06:35:29.513
So that means we squeeze the glutes as hard as we can.

06:35:29.553 --> 06:35:32.796
One, two, and then we release, we go back into it.

06:35:33.157 --> 06:35:36.600
So hip hinge, boom, explode on the way up.

06:35:36.620 --> 06:35:39.143
Squeeze the glutes at the top, okay?

06:35:39.443 --> 06:35:49.718
Now, if your form is good here and you're feeling it in the right places, we should really feel like we're getting a huge stretch in the hamstrings and in the glutes at the bottom position.

06:35:50.139 --> 06:35:54.566
And then when we explode, you should feel a big burn in the glutes and a little bit in the hamstrings as well.

06:35:55.206 --> 06:35:59.693
If we're feeling good on that, then what I want you to do is actually pull your toes up.

06:36:00.014 --> 06:36:04.320
So as you go down, your toes are off the floor, all right?

06:36:04.300 --> 06:36:12.015
As soon as we reverse to get up, I push my big toe down into the ground as hard as I can and I explode.

06:36:12.416 --> 06:36:18.208
So we're trying to build that neurological connection between the big toe and the glute.

06:36:19.009 --> 06:36:24.300
So if you're not advanced and you're just getting used to this exercise, don't worry about that aspect yet.

06:36:24.280 --> 06:36:30.670
But, if you're good to go, then start to slam that toe into the ground on the way up, squeeze at the top.

06:36:31.030 --> 06:36:42.728
This is a very good exercise for the post chain, glutes, hamstring, training that hip hinge and improving our velocity on the hip extension, which is essential for the vertical jump.

06:36:58.115 --> 06:37:03.904
So for my sub max long duration jumps, what I'm gonna do is almost like a jump rope.

06:37:03.944 --> 06:37:08.410
So you know in a jump rope, we're just nice and low and we're rhythmic and we're bouncy.

06:37:08.691 --> 06:37:14.319
We're not going maximal on this and we're also not lowering low and trying to use a lot of muscular power.

06:37:14.639 --> 06:37:19.286
We're really just using for the most part that Achilles to spring us off the ground.

06:37:19.386 --> 06:37:22.791
So there's gonna be three different aspects here.

06:37:22.952 --> 06:37:26.637
So your first one is gonna be a side to side hop.

06:37:26.617 --> 06:37:30.121
Okay, I'm springing off the balls of the feet.

06:37:30.482 --> 06:37:34.366
I am not letting my heel collapse down to the ground, all right?

06:37:34.847 --> 06:37:40.414
We're not going maximal, we're just going light and bouncy off the ground, okay?

06:37:40.434 --> 06:37:42.396
Now I'm going for a certain amount of time.

06:37:42.516 --> 06:37:46.761
Pay attention to the reps because we're going to go up every week.

06:37:46.781 --> 06:37:49.865
So let's say for week one, you're 30 seconds.

06:37:50.165 --> 06:37:52.728
Okay, now the second one is gonna be front back.

06:37:52.869 --> 06:37:55.712
So I'm just switching the feet

06:37:55.692 --> 06:38:13.887
forwards and backwards same concept springing off the balls of the feet not letting that heel collapse let's say it's 30 seconds there so then the third one is just a normal low level jump same thing spring off the ball of the foot heel cannot collapse okay now

06:38:13.867 --> 06:38:16.771
What we're gonna do is really go long duration.

06:38:16.791 --> 06:38:27.524
You're gonna get a big burn in the calves, but what we're doing is we're reforming the fascia of our feet and we're training that Achilles to be an energy storing and releasing monster.

06:38:27.664 --> 06:38:29.366
So pay attention to the reps.

06:38:29.667 --> 06:38:33.472
This is one exercise where I do want you close to your max.

06:38:33.852 --> 06:38:36.696
So let's say week one, I gave you 30, 30, 30.

06:38:36.756 --> 06:38:38.518
That's a minute and a half total.

06:38:38.838 --> 06:38:43.384
If you got to the end of that minute and a half and you feel like you could have kept going,

06:38:43.364 --> 06:38:44.646
you need to keep going.

06:38:44.686 --> 06:38:53.141
You need to push close to your max because if we want the fascia to actually reform around the feet and we want the Achilles to adapt, we gotta push close to that max.

06:38:53.161 --> 06:38:56.687
So for some of you, that might be two minutes, that might be three minutes.

06:38:56.968 --> 06:39:00.153
I just need you to progressively increase each week.

06:39:00.494 --> 06:39:07.566
So if I were to do this drill right now, I would probably get about three minutes and at that three minute mark, personally,

06:39:07.546 --> 06:39:11.952
I would start to feel like almost like a cramp in the calf.

06:39:12.233 --> 06:39:14.616
I would feel a burn of like a nine out of 10.

06:39:14.936 --> 06:39:16.699
I don't need you to get to a 10 out of 10.

06:39:16.999 --> 06:39:22.026
I don't need your calves to fully cramp up, but get close to that point and then stop.

06:39:22.327 --> 06:39:23.609
Take as long as you want.

06:39:23.689 --> 06:39:28.896
Sometimes I take up to two or three minutes in between sets and then I go back in.

06:39:28.876 --> 06:39:38.512
For most of you, I want you to follow the rep range, but again, if that is too easy, extend it, make sure you jot down your time, and each week I want you to go up on that.

06:39:38.892 --> 06:39:43.941
By the end of the four weeks, our fascia and our achilles is gonna be a lot springier.

06:39:43.981 --> 06:39:47.486
We're gonna be storing and releasing elastic energy like never before.

06:39:47.807 --> 06:39:48.488
Let's get it.

06:40:14.152 --> 06:40:18.177
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

06:40:18.297 --> 06:40:20.100
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

06:40:20.140 --> 06:40:22.843
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

06:40:22.884 --> 06:40:29.392
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

06:40:29.913 --> 06:40:35.861
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

06:40:36.141 --> 06:40:39.906
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

06:40:39.886 --> 06:40:55.988
okay so i'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill all right so it's in through the nose and i want to make sure that my stomach is rising okay your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

06:40:56.963 --> 06:41:00.027
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

06:41:00.047 --> 06:41:06.756
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

06:41:07.317 --> 06:41:13.105
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

06:41:13.125 --> 06:41:16.790
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

06:41:19.093 --> 06:41:23.359
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

06:41:26.309 --> 06:41:28.232
and that stomach will fall, okay?

06:41:28.933 --> 06:41:31.577
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

06:41:32.338 --> 06:41:41.371
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

06:41:42.152 --> 06:41:49.924
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

06:41:50.184 --> 06:41:54.190
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

06:41:54.170 --> 06:42:23.097
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

06:42:23.330 --> 06:42:25.835
happens in the parasympathetic state.

06:42:26.376 --> 06:42:30.525
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

06:42:30.866 --> 06:42:44.494
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

06:42:46.313 --> 06:42:49.618
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

06:42:49.638 --> 06:42:53.905
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

06:42:54.185 --> 06:43:00.996
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

06:43:01.296 --> 06:43:04.782
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

06:43:05.062 --> 06:43:12.574
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

06:43:12.554 --> 06:43:16.905
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

06:43:17.326 --> 06:43:19.311
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

06:43:19.331 --> 06:43:21.837
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

06:43:22.118 --> 06:43:24.183
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

06:43:24.463 --> 06:43:26.689
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

06:43:26.990 --> 06:43:28.774
I'm just gonna lean forward.

06:43:28.754 --> 06:43:49.530
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

06:43:49.848 --> 06:43:51.811
15 seconds on the other side as well.

06:43:51.831 --> 06:43:53.974
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

06:43:54.155 --> 06:43:55.577
So that is the back of the thigh.

06:43:55.817 --> 06:44:00.124
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

06:44:00.144 --> 06:44:03.829
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

06:44:03.990 --> 06:44:06.413
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

06:44:06.754 --> 06:44:09.077
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

06:44:09.438 --> 06:44:11.000
So now look at this back foot.

06:44:11.020 --> 06:44:12.683
I'm not curling the toe under.

06:44:12.663 --> 06:44:16.509
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

06:44:17.090 --> 06:44:20.335
And I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

06:44:20.756 --> 06:44:26.124
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

06:44:26.425 --> 06:44:39.666
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

06:44:39.646 --> 06:44:42.949
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

06:44:42.989 --> 06:44:44.311
That's the inside of the leg.

06:44:44.791 --> 06:44:48.175
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

06:44:48.755 --> 06:44:49.436
Same thing.

06:44:49.776 --> 06:44:52.659
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

06:44:52.980 --> 06:44:56.103
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

06:44:56.383 --> 06:45:00.287
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

06:45:00.567 --> 06:45:05.232
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

06:45:05.212 --> 06:45:08.155
this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

06:45:08.555 --> 06:45:14.020
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

06:45:14.440 --> 06:45:24.529
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

06:45:24.910 --> 06:45:32.957
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

06:45:32.937 --> 06:45:35.843
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

06:45:36.305 --> 06:45:42.218
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

06:45:42.438 --> 06:45:45.425
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

06:45:45.485 --> 06:45:47.650
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

06:45:47.870 --> 06:45:51.739
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

06:45:51.719 --> 06:45:53.522
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

06:45:53.582 --> 06:45:56.486
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

06:45:56.546 --> 06:45:57.448
So I'm 90 there.

06:45:57.808 --> 06:46:00.613
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

06:46:00.973 --> 06:46:03.257
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

06:46:03.617 --> 06:46:08.304
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

06:46:08.665 --> 06:46:11.650
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

06:46:11.930 --> 06:46:14.714
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

06:46:15.095 --> 06:46:20.443
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

06:46:20.423 --> 06:46:27.192
All right, so I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

06:46:27.933 --> 06:46:31.698
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

06:46:31.798 --> 06:46:37.105
For my calf stretch, I'm basically gonna do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

06:46:37.466 --> 06:46:40.710
So I'm here, I'm gonna push back like this.

06:46:40.790 --> 06:46:42.212
I'm gonna straighten out my arms.

06:46:42.192 --> 06:46:43.954
And now I'm just going to cross over.

06:46:44.335 --> 06:46:53.967
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

06:46:54.448 --> 06:46:56.210
So I do have a straight knee.

06:46:56.630 --> 06:46:59.114
So we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius.

06:46:59.174 --> 06:47:00.876
That's the outside calf muscle.

06:47:01.136 --> 06:47:07.324
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

06:47:07.705 --> 06:47:12.030
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

06:47:12.837 --> 06:47:15.947
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

06:47:16.008 --> 06:47:16.730
That's okay.

06:47:17.091 --> 06:47:20.282
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

06:47:20.342 --> 06:47:24.315
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

06:47:27.113 --> 06:47:27.754
What's up guys?

06:47:28.034 --> 06:47:32.379
Minimum dose advice for day two and day four of phase five.

06:47:32.580 --> 06:47:36.464
Really there is no minimum dose advice for this workout as you see.

06:47:36.525 --> 06:47:39.048
It's extremely short, extremely fast.

06:47:39.728 --> 06:47:48.379
There are some days in our workouts where we're going to go short and light and efficient because we can't just beat the body up

06:47:48.359 --> 06:47:49.380
all year round.

06:47:49.520 --> 06:47:54.266
Sometimes we have to have those days that are actually a little bit lighter than previous phases.

06:47:54.326 --> 06:47:56.629
So you'll notice that there's no mobility.

06:47:56.669 --> 06:48:03.497
You should have built all the mobility by now if that's not the case and add in some mobility drills from the earlier phases.

06:48:03.777 --> 06:48:07.542
But most of you guys have built enough mobility, so we're not super setting anything there.

06:48:07.842 --> 06:48:11.406
So you can pretty much do this workout as is the leg climb.

06:48:11.827 --> 06:48:13.028
You're going to get a good burn.

06:48:13.589 --> 06:48:17.353
You could increase the reps if you don't feel a big burn, but you really should.

06:48:17.333 --> 06:48:19.516
Feel it burn in the front core.

06:48:19.857 --> 06:48:24.703
Hand walkout is not really a big burning exercise, but it is fatiguing overall.

06:48:24.784 --> 06:48:28.269
It's full body stability, so that is pretty tiring.

06:48:28.749 --> 06:48:34.978
Da Vinci plank, the way we make that harder is we always just load up more and more and more, so we never want to increase reps.

06:48:35.038 --> 06:48:36.380
We don't want to hold for longer.

06:48:36.661 --> 06:48:42.649
We just want to see how much total weight we can hold in that 30 second time frame with perfect form.

06:48:42.629 --> 06:48:51.745
and then that cable drop step rotation, same thing, the reps are pretty low, so we can progressively overload by increasing the amount of weight we use.

06:49:02.761 --> 06:49:05.847
So for my leg climbs, I'm going to go down on my back.

06:49:05.907 --> 06:49:08.031
I'm going to make sure that my back is nice and flat.

06:49:08.411 --> 06:49:11.016
I'm going to go about six inches off the ground with this left leg.

06:49:11.336 --> 06:49:15.384
And then I'm going to go straight up 90 degree angle with that right leg.

06:49:15.464 --> 06:49:16.806
Some of you will be a little less.

06:49:16.846 --> 06:49:17.427
That's okay.

06:49:17.788 --> 06:49:18.189
All right.

06:49:18.209 --> 06:49:21.134
So what I'm going to do is three grabs to the leg.

06:49:21.174 --> 06:49:24.600
So it's going to be left, right, left.

06:49:25.101 --> 06:49:25.342
All right.

06:49:25.923 --> 06:49:27.165
Left, right, left.

06:49:27.584 --> 06:49:48.175
left and i'm not really grabbing and pulling on my leg much i'm just doing this so that i can really control that contraction from the abs all right so left right left and come back down left right left and come back down so this is really isolating the abs we're also getting a very good

06:49:48.155 --> 06:49:56.797
hamstring stretch, and for some of you it'll be an opposite hip flexor stretch because when we're in that 90 degree angle, a lot of you guys like to lift that leg.

06:49:57.138 --> 06:50:03.514
So we're getting hip flexor, we're getting hamstring, and then you're gonna feel we're gonna get a huge abdominal contraction.

06:50:03.915 --> 06:50:04.476
Let's do it.

06:50:25.128 --> 06:50:36.187
So for my hand walkouts, I'm gonna go wrist directly underneath the shoulder, knees directly underneath the hips, and then I'm just pushing up a couple inches so my knees are off the ground.

06:50:36.628 --> 06:50:43.280
I'm gonna keep my torso nice and flat, and I'm just gonna step forward a couple inches with the hands every time.

06:50:43.660 --> 06:50:45.263
Now for some of you,

06:50:45.563 --> 06:50:50.629
You might only be able to get about right there, and then you walk it back.

06:50:50.669 --> 06:50:52.151
That counts as one rep.

06:50:52.171 --> 06:50:58.219
The better you get, the further out you can actually get the hands.

06:50:58.900 --> 06:51:03.806
I don't want you to get so far that you start to hyperextend from the lower back.

06:51:04.106 --> 06:51:07.611
I want you to go to that point where you can still stay flat from the back.

06:51:07.631 --> 06:51:13.258
You can feel a good burn in the core, but you're not quite losing that position, all right?

06:51:13.238 --> 06:51:18.265
Should feel this a lot in the front abs, a little bit in the obliques, a little bit in the low back.

06:51:18.686 --> 06:51:22.871
Of course, we're going to feel a lot in the shoulders because we're getting a lot of shoulder stability.

06:51:23.633 --> 06:51:26.036
This is going to be a full body stability exercise.

06:51:26.376 --> 06:51:28.479
You should feel a little bit of a burn in the core.

06:51:28.880 --> 06:51:35.449
If you feel it more in your lower back, then you're probably doing something wrong and you'll want to regress this to a normal plank.

06:51:48.440 --> 06:51:51.003
So for the DaVinci plank, I'm gonna use a cable machine.

06:51:51.283 --> 06:51:53.446
If you don't have a cable machine, we could use a band.

06:51:53.866 --> 06:51:59.412
And if you don't have a band, then we're gonna go with this side plank with a leg raise, which I'll show in a second.

06:51:59.452 --> 06:52:05.218
But if we have that cable machine, right now I'm going on 100 pounds because I've been doing this for a long time.

06:52:05.679 --> 06:52:07.321
I would really say start very light.

06:52:07.461 --> 06:52:09.283
Start all the way at 30 pounds.

06:52:09.303 --> 06:52:10.444
Try it out, see how it feels.

06:52:10.504 --> 06:52:15.730
If it's easy, then go up and up and up until we can find that perfect weight.

06:52:15.710 --> 06:52:21.152
But so I'm going to set this up where the cable is in line with my mid chest.

06:52:21.172 --> 06:52:22.417
Now I'm just going to step out.

06:52:22.437 --> 06:52:23.762
I'm going to pick it up.

06:52:23.945 --> 06:52:47.185
take a step out I'm in this wide stance my chest is right over my pelvis so I'm not leaning shoulders nice and flat now from there I'm gonna lift up this outside foot so I'm gonna lift up my left leg about an inch off the ground and I'm just stabilizing so I got to stabilize with this right leg but more importantly this left side of the core right now is fired up and I'm going left arm out

06:52:47.165 --> 06:52:49.230
My shoulders should be completely flat.

06:52:49.551 --> 06:52:51.656
Most of you naturally will be here.

06:52:52.077 --> 06:52:55.586
I need you to fight with that core to get your shoulders flat.

06:52:55.967 --> 06:52:58.032
And we're holding, we're feeling that in the left oblique.

06:52:58.052 --> 06:53:00.538
We'll feel it a little bit in the right oblique and the core as well.

06:53:01.140 --> 06:53:03.385
But mostly on that left side of the core.

06:53:03.365 --> 06:53:06.533
And then after we're done, make sure that you walk it back in.

06:53:06.594 --> 06:53:08.799
Don't just let it pull you back in, of course.

06:53:09.120 --> 06:53:12.249
So if we don't have the cable machine, we could use a band.

06:53:12.549 --> 06:53:17.864
I'm using a one inch perform better band, hook it up to a tree or to a power rack or something nice and stable.

06:53:18.204 --> 06:53:19.568
I'm going to pull out

06:53:19.548 --> 06:53:22.491
as far as I can really to get some really good tension on that.

06:53:22.872 --> 06:53:24.494
And then I'm going to pull that right foot up.

06:53:24.554 --> 06:53:26.856
Of course, that outside foot is always the one that comes up.

06:53:27.337 --> 06:53:30.680
Other arm straight out and I'm just holding in that position.

06:53:30.700 --> 06:53:32.042
So that's actually not enough tension.

06:53:32.442 --> 06:53:39.050
I'd have to go a little further and then from there pull up and I'm not letting that band pull me in.

06:53:39.130 --> 06:53:43.214
So I'm staying nice and strong with that right oblique and I'm flat with the shoulders.

06:53:43.555 --> 06:53:48.340
I'm consciously squeezing the core in this position for the full duration of the set.

06:53:48.320 --> 06:53:54.108
Now, if you don't have the cable or the band, then you're just gonna go in a side plank position, and you're gonna get nice and flat.

06:53:54.148 --> 06:54:05.322
So option one is bent knees, stack them together, stack the legs, get nice and flat here, and then we're just gonna lift this leg, stay nice and flat through that knee all the way through the shoulder.

06:54:05.723 --> 06:54:09.728
I'm feeling that on the inside, so I'm feeling that on the right side of the core.

06:54:10.029 --> 06:54:11.451
I'm gonna feel that in the hips here.

06:54:11.771 --> 06:54:13.453
I'm gonna feel that in my right hip as well.

06:54:13.814 --> 06:54:15.576
I can just get this other arm.

06:54:15.556 --> 06:54:18.684
and hang out in this position, nice and strong and stable.

06:54:19.085 --> 06:54:31.236
If that is too easy, then go straight leg, and we're up nice and flat from the feet all the way through the shoulders, squeeze that core, and then we're just gonna lift here and other hand up, all right?

06:54:55.837 --> 06:55:04.501
Okay, for the cable drop step rotation, I'm just going to use a cable, start light and build up gradually, or if you don't have a cable, you could use a band.

06:55:04.922 --> 06:55:10.858
I'm going to interlock my fingers over the handle and just step it out just a little bit, all right?

06:55:10.838 --> 06:55:13.304
We're going to come into a quarter squat position.

06:55:13.365 --> 06:55:17.816
Now to open up that way, I got to drive off the right foot.

06:55:18.197 --> 06:55:23.070
I'm going to open up and I'm going to get that left foot down so that that toe is pointing that way.

06:55:23.330 --> 06:55:23.551
All right.

06:55:23.992 --> 06:55:27.902
I don't want you to go into rotation until that foot is down.

06:55:27.882 --> 06:55:34.014
So I'm here, I push into position, and then from here I can open up and explode there.

06:55:34.034 --> 06:55:43.111
Now on the way back, I'm coming halfway, hands right in front of the chest, fire up that core, two to three seconds, and then release back to the start.

06:55:43.492 --> 06:55:48.000
So it's foot down, explode, come back, hold.

06:55:47.980 --> 06:55:48.641
and release.

06:55:49.542 --> 06:56:02.217
So the reason that I want you to not just go into rotation but get the foot down first is we're then going to work that kind of recoil core, we're going to work as the full fascia system instead of just muscular power.

06:56:03.118 --> 06:56:13.330
So we're getting here and then we get a good recoil through the core and we get here and then coming back we get that pause and then release.

06:56:13.310 --> 06:56:18.767
do the desired reps to one side, and then go back and do the desired reps to the other side.

06:56:19.129 --> 06:56:23.623
We should feel a burn through the core, especially the side in the obliques.

06:56:27.130 --> 06:56:31.614
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase five, day three.

06:56:32.055 --> 06:56:42.125
So if we're pressed for time, take out that foam roller circuit, do the dynamic warm up, do the band deceleration, take out the back pedal deceleration.

06:56:43.906 --> 06:56:47.530
Do the angled cone bounds, if you want to take that down to two sets you can.

06:56:48.090 --> 06:56:53.035
Do the rotational hurdle jumps, if you have the time, you can take that down to two sets as well.

06:56:53.876 --> 06:56:55.838
Take out the one leg jump,

06:56:55.818 --> 06:56:59.244
Still do the one foot approach jump.

06:56:59.264 --> 06:57:01.788
We still got to rep out those one foot mechanics.

06:57:01.988 --> 06:57:04.131
Do the one leg RDL swings.

06:57:04.171 --> 06:57:05.514
It's already very low rep.

06:57:05.534 --> 06:57:07.897
We're only doing a couple reps on each side.

06:57:08.559 --> 06:57:11.704
Do the single leg broad hop to vertical hop.

06:57:11.724 --> 06:57:15.590
If you want to take down the reps from four to two, you can do that.

06:57:15.910 --> 06:57:17.573
So still do your four sets.

06:57:17.553 --> 06:57:20.257
but keep it down at around two reps each side.

06:57:20.557 --> 06:57:24.223
If you have time, do the hyperextension hold plate drop.

06:57:24.243 --> 06:57:33.256
It's good to go back to an exercise that we've done before and be able to progressively overload so we can now get a harder squeeze in that post chain.

06:57:33.757 --> 06:57:38.404
We can use a heavier weight than we're doing back when we did these in phase one.

06:57:38.804 --> 06:57:43.932
So if you can do that, if you had to take it down to two sets, that's okay.

06:57:43.912 --> 06:57:50.083
If you have to skip the sub max long duration jumps, you can do that because we're already doing it in Monday, Friday.

06:57:50.464 --> 06:57:52.227
I really recommend that you get that in.

06:57:53.049 --> 06:58:02.266
But of course, if we are pressed for time or our volume on the court is so high that we have to decrease our volume here, that is one exercise that you could take out.

06:58:02.286 --> 06:58:04.570
As always, do our 7-Eleven breathing.

06:58:04.550 --> 06:58:07.634
and then we don't even have a static stretch in here.

06:58:07.754 --> 06:58:13.160
So if you need the static stretch because you're overly tight, you can always add it in at the end of your workout.

06:58:13.521 --> 06:58:15.083
Most of you guys probably don't need it.

06:58:15.403 --> 06:58:15.783
Let's get it.

06:58:15.884 --> 06:58:17.445
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

06:58:17.586 --> 06:58:19.308
I have a long roller from Skills.

06:58:19.548 --> 06:58:26.416
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

06:58:26.777 --> 06:58:32.143
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

06:58:32.123 --> 06:58:35.268
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

06:58:35.609 --> 06:58:44.765
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

06:58:44.785 --> 06:58:51.516
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

06:58:51.496 --> 06:58:58.007
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

06:58:58.387 --> 06:59:00.591
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

06:59:00.631 --> 06:59:02.033
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

06:59:02.374 --> 06:59:10.808
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

06:59:11.148 --> 06:59:18.280
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

06:59:18.260 --> 06:59:22.887
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

06:59:23.153 --> 06:59:24.935
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

06:59:25.235 --> 06:59:28.638
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

06:59:28.739 --> 06:59:36.947
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

06:59:37.007 --> 06:59:42.532
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

06:59:42.612 --> 06:59:45.776
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

06:59:45.796 --> 06:59:53.143
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

06:59:53.123 --> 06:59:59.415
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

07:00:00.117 --> 07:00:15.807
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

07:00:15.787 --> 07:00:17.551
Our next one is going to be for the adductors.

07:00:17.571 --> 07:00:18.693
That's the inside of the leg.

07:00:18.874 --> 07:00:20.617
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

07:00:20.938 --> 07:00:34.707
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

07:00:36.475 --> 07:00:43.283
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

07:00:43.484 --> 07:00:47.048
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

07:00:47.348 --> 07:00:50.593
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

07:00:51.153 --> 07:00:53.857
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

07:00:53.897 --> 07:00:55.559
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

07:00:55.859 --> 07:00:59.884
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

07:00:59.864 --> 07:01:02.790
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

07:01:03.352 --> 07:01:09.264
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day.

07:01:09.565 --> 07:01:11.108
We're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

07:01:11.449 --> 07:01:14.295
We got to really start to loosen that up.

07:01:15.642 --> 07:01:20.534
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

07:01:20.555 --> 07:01:24.084
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

07:01:24.104 --> 07:01:29.297
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

07:01:29.658 --> 07:01:31.322
Everything else is extra credit.

07:01:34.424 --> 07:01:38.169
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

07:01:38.449 --> 07:01:41.012
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

07:01:41.093 --> 07:01:45.058
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary, just in case you don't have much room.

07:01:45.558 --> 07:01:52.147
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

07:01:52.507 --> 07:01:56.172
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

07:01:56.212 --> 07:01:57.934
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

07:01:57.914 --> 07:02:01.118
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

07:02:01.459 --> 07:02:02.901
I'm not going too fast yet.

07:02:03.221 --> 07:02:05.384
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

07:02:05.624 --> 07:02:10.170
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

07:02:10.190 --> 07:02:18.321
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

07:02:18.641 --> 07:02:20.864
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

07:02:20.884 --> 07:02:23.247
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

07:02:23.227 --> 07:02:48.525
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch i'm just gonna go over with the left leg i'm gonna come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid-shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

07:02:48.505 --> 07:02:56.220
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

07:02:59.879 --> 07:03:04.246
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

07:03:04.446 --> 07:03:09.794
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

07:03:10.074 --> 07:03:16.624
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

07:03:17.045 --> 07:03:25.077
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

07:03:25.057 --> 07:03:29.986
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

07:03:30.326 --> 07:03:35.936
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

07:03:36.618 --> 07:03:38.982
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

07:03:39.302 --> 07:03:43.931
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

07:03:44.572 --> 07:03:46.896
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

07:03:47.804 --> 07:03:51.452
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

07:03:51.873 --> 07:03:54.239
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

07:03:54.279 --> 07:03:56.323
One, two, three.

07:03:56.383 --> 07:03:58.649
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

07:03:58.989 --> 07:04:01.014
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

07:04:01.034 --> 07:04:01.635
That's okay.

07:04:01.976 --> 07:04:03.560
Let's just get as low as we can there.

07:04:04.041 --> 07:04:05.003
One, two, three.

07:04:04.983 --> 07:04:31.429
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

07:04:31.409 --> 07:04:35.312
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

07:04:35.773 --> 07:04:39.776
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

07:04:40.137 --> 07:04:45.661
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

07:04:46.282 --> 07:05:00.454
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

07:05:05.463 --> 07:05:11.971
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

07:05:11.991 --> 07:05:13.453
Don't make the mistake of going here.

07:05:13.834 --> 07:05:21.884
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

07:05:22.004 --> 07:05:27.871
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

07:05:27.951 --> 07:05:31.656
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

07:05:31.906 --> 07:05:51.028
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one two

07:05:51.008 --> 07:05:51.709
Three.

07:05:52.050 --> 07:05:52.771
Rock it back.

07:05:53.132 --> 07:05:54.935
Heels back down to the ground.

07:05:55.196 --> 07:05:56.819
One, two, three.

07:05:56.919 --> 07:05:58.121
I need one hand up.

07:05:58.662 --> 07:05:59.464
Two hand up.

07:05:59.684 --> 07:06:00.366
Not out here.

07:06:00.426 --> 07:06:01.889
We need it back behind the head.

07:06:02.490 --> 07:06:03.131
And stand.

07:06:03.672 --> 07:06:04.373
All right.

07:06:04.393 --> 07:06:05.756
So that's one cycle.

07:06:06.016 --> 07:06:07.139
We're going to go for three of these.

07:06:08.100 --> 07:06:09.523
So it's one, two, three.

07:06:09.858 --> 07:06:12.141
One, two, three, lean.

07:06:12.902 --> 07:06:15.164
One, two, three, heels back down.

07:06:15.224 --> 07:06:19.249
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

07:06:19.550 --> 07:06:27.880
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

07:06:27.900 --> 07:06:32.245
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

07:06:32.605 --> 07:06:38.953
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

07:06:38.933 --> 07:06:41.756
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

07:06:41.816 --> 07:06:44.119
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

07:06:44.519 --> 07:06:47.322
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

07:06:47.643 --> 07:06:49.344
I need to be flat through the hips.

07:06:49.625 --> 07:06:52.047
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

07:06:52.568 --> 07:06:56.993
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

07:06:57.373 --> 07:07:00.156
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

07:07:00.436 --> 07:07:08.265
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

07:07:08.245 --> 07:07:18.222
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

07:07:18.643 --> 07:07:26.997
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

07:07:36.494 --> 07:07:48.375
So for my band decelerations, if you have one band that should be okay as long as it stretches pretty far, you're just gonna have to watch out and make sure you don't run into the power rack or whatever you have it tied down to.

07:07:48.796 --> 07:07:53.744
I personally like to link together two bands so I have a little bit more distance to work with.

07:07:54.165 --> 07:07:55.768
So I'm gonna pull it out.

07:07:55.748 --> 07:07:57.892
I'm gonna get some pretty good tension on it, all right?

07:07:57.952 --> 07:08:00.537
So now I'm just gonna go into a deceleration.

07:08:00.597 --> 07:08:07.951
It's like I'm driving one way, I'm planning that right leg, stopping on a dime, absorbing, and then I'm pushing back, okay?

07:08:07.971 --> 07:08:08.973
And then I go the other way.

07:08:09.374 --> 07:08:12.981
The footwork is right, left, right,

07:08:12.961 --> 07:08:15.123
left, right, left, okay?

07:08:15.143 --> 07:08:17.785
So it's almost just like a foot replacement.

07:08:17.805 --> 07:08:23.470
So it's gonna be here, push back here, push back here, push back here, all right?

07:08:23.490 --> 07:08:28.814
I'm just changing directions as sharp as I can, nice and rapid off the ground.

07:08:29.055 --> 07:08:38.643
This is really going to train our ability to decelerate linearly, create space, pull back, get open shots, create for your teammates.

07:08:39.203 --> 07:08:42.546
Deceleration is just as important, if not more,

07:08:42.526 --> 07:08:44.911
important than explosive acceleration.

07:08:45.194 --> 07:08:45.921
Let's get it.

07:09:03.805 --> 07:09:12.946
So for my angled cone bounds, what I'm gonna do is set up six cones, or if you only have three cones, you could set up three and then switch them for the other side.

07:09:13.468 --> 07:09:21.747
Or if you don't have cones at all, you could just use shoes or anything just to mark off a spot, give yourself a goal to aim for.

07:09:21.727 --> 07:09:24.653
I'm gonna go in the middle and I'm gonna aim here.

07:09:24.713 --> 07:09:26.557
So I'm starting on my right foot.

07:09:26.977 --> 07:09:30.885
I'm gonna go with my left foot so I bound forward and I push back.

07:09:31.206 --> 07:09:32.348
I need that to be quick.

07:09:32.769 --> 07:09:36.817
Use the floor as a springboard, bounce off, do not sink into quicksand.

07:09:36.897 --> 07:09:38.520
So it's here.

07:09:38.500 --> 07:09:45.915
push back, once I get back to that middle, I gotta get balance on one foot and get vertical, so jump as high as you can, all right?

07:09:45.975 --> 07:09:58.720
So it's here, here, jump, land, boom, get out to that side, land, and then get to the back, open up, so you can see how I'm pointing the toe out, I'm getting that little rotation.

07:09:58.868 --> 07:10:04.415
boom, push back, and jump, stick on one foot, and then I go to the other side.

07:10:04.916 --> 07:10:13.427
So it's here, left foot jump, here, left foot jump, here, left foot jump, okay?

07:10:13.447 --> 07:10:21.417
We're really working on those angled cuts, getting explosive multi-directionally, and then always getting back to the middle

07:10:21.397 --> 07:10:24.159
getting balanced and getting straight up and down.

07:10:24.580 --> 07:10:33.668
Now the most common mistake that I see on this drill is people get back to the middle and then they fade off to the side on their jump, all right?

07:10:33.968 --> 07:10:41.335
I need you to decelerate and get straight up, straight down, come right back down and then be explosive, push out.

07:10:41.655 --> 07:10:47.561
Half the battle is force absorption and becoming balanced before we re-accelerate.

07:10:47.861 --> 07:10:50.323
Let's work on that, let's get to the next level.

07:11:12.308 --> 07:11:16.072
So for my rotational hurdle jump, I'm going to have a hurdle in the middle.

07:11:16.332 --> 07:11:18.475
I'm going to have two small hurdles on the outside.

07:11:18.715 --> 07:11:20.657
Of course, if you're going to have the big hurdle, it doesn't matter.

07:11:20.797 --> 07:11:24.562
All we need is maybe a little hurdle, a shoe, a cone, doesn't really matter.

07:11:24.642 --> 07:11:34.292
So what I want to do is I want to set this up to where if I'm standing in the middle, I want to push out laterally and I have to really put some force in the ground.

07:11:34.312 --> 07:11:36.034
So I don't want you to be able to just step over.

07:11:36.335 --> 07:11:38.417
I want you to have to push out.

07:11:38.397 --> 07:12:01.823
push out laterally and then as soon as this foot lands you got to transition quick as you can like it's hot lava get back over that cone add a little rotation to it and then I'm stepping in left right so then it's a left right regular takeoff over that hurdle now I'm gonna land on this other side now I got to go rotationally so I got to go here boom

07:12:01.803 --> 07:12:22.191
boom okay so I'm always open to this side so I land here I get here push now it's right left get over okay now of course I land here boom open this way so now it's right left right all right this is kind of confusing to start

07:12:22.171 --> 07:12:25.457
Once you get in a groove, you're going to start to feel that rhythm.

07:12:25.477 --> 07:12:27.080
It's going to feel nice and smooth.

07:12:27.361 --> 07:12:35.335
We're going to build that versatile jumping style where we can step in left-right, right-left, off a lateral, off a spin.

07:12:35.636 --> 07:12:36.478
It doesn't matter.

07:12:36.638 --> 07:12:38.461
We get to that max vert either way.

07:12:38.762 --> 07:12:39.403
Let's get it.

07:12:58.317 --> 07:13:05.829
So for my one leg RDL swing, what I'm going to do is hold two dumbbells by my side, come to an RDL position.

07:13:05.890 --> 07:13:10.197
So that's one leg, slight bend in the knee, of course, from there it's a hip hinge.

07:13:10.257 --> 07:13:11.679
We're not going down with the knee.

07:13:12.360 --> 07:13:20.714
Once I get down to that bottom position, which right now is going to be hands past the knee, so the dumbbells are going to be about mid shin for most of us.

07:13:20.915 --> 07:13:24.240
If you're a little more tight, it's okay to just go down to your knees.

07:13:24.220 --> 07:13:29.711
So I'm gonna get down to that position and then I'm gonna aggressively swing as fast as I can.

07:13:30.152 --> 07:13:34.921
I'm gonna keep my elbow straight and I'm gonna swing the dumbbells up over my head.

07:13:35.262 --> 07:13:43.699
I'm gonna bring it down while I'm still on one leg to challenge our stability and then bring that other foot down, bring the dumbbells back down, okay?

07:13:43.719 --> 07:13:45.823
So it's slight bend.

07:13:45.803 --> 07:13:50.649
RDL as fast as we can, explode up to that top position.

07:13:51.129 --> 07:13:56.315
Do not think about doing an RDL and then a lift with the arms.

07:13:56.595 --> 07:14:04.224
The arms are just swinging from the momentum of that aggressive explosive hip extension, okay?

07:14:04.244 --> 07:14:10.191
So it's here, swing them, stabilize at the top, bring it down, come all the way down.

07:14:10.171 --> 07:14:17.626
Now, this is a very advanced exercise, so if you don't have the stability to do it on one leg, I'd rather you come to a split stance.

07:14:17.766 --> 07:14:23.297
So, if you did it in a split stance, we would be here in a little baby split.

07:14:23.918 --> 07:14:25.742
My feet have to be pretty narrow.

07:14:25.782 --> 07:14:29.209
I can't be very wide, otherwise I'm going to hit the dumbbell on my leg.

07:14:29.847 --> 07:14:31.050
Here, baby split.

07:14:31.110 --> 07:14:32.755
From there, it's a hip hinge.

07:14:33.116 --> 07:14:34.700
Notice I'm on the ball of the foot.

07:14:34.740 --> 07:14:39.172
I'm not on my heel on that back leg, but I am on my heel on the front leg.

07:14:39.232 --> 07:14:42.641
So it's gonna be here and then boom, explode.

07:14:43.102 --> 07:14:45.128
Come back down and reset.

07:14:45.629 --> 07:14:46.371
Boom.

07:14:46.351 --> 07:14:49.415
explode, come back down, reset.

07:14:50.056 --> 07:14:56.224
Now, if you're going to do that single leg RDL, start very light, start 5 or 10 pound dumbbells in each hand.

07:14:56.965 --> 07:14:59.569
Once you're good to go, then you can start to go up.

07:14:59.589 --> 07:15:02.192
Most of our athletes don't get past 20 pounds.

07:15:02.212 --> 07:15:11.565
20 pounds is a pretty good weight as far as really just utilizing that hip extension velocity, hamstring and glute, instead of using the arms.

07:15:11.545 --> 07:15:16.681
If you start to get too heavy, you're gonna feel like you're really working your arms to lift, alright?

07:15:17.062 --> 07:15:20.593
So, start light, gradually go up, let's get it.

07:15:38.663 --> 07:15:47.173
So for my single leg broad hop to vertical hop, what I'm going to do is set up a cone as far as I can away from my hurdle.

07:15:47.253 --> 07:15:49.857
Now, if you don't have the high hurdle, that's okay.

07:15:50.117 --> 07:15:54.983
You can always use a low hurdle or just a shoe or some sort of marker to know what you're getting over.

07:15:55.323 --> 07:16:02.612
Doesn't necessarily have to be a high hurdle, but I'm going to set up here and I'm going to balance on one leg, same

07:16:02.592 --> 07:16:23.740
position as our single leg rdl so we hinge from the hips we throw our arms back our torso goes down towards the floor so i end up almost parallel to the torso with that chest and then i swing this leg and i explode as far as i can and i land on that same leg so it's a broad hop

07:16:23.720 --> 07:16:29.975
Alright, so boom, get as far as I can and then it's a reactive vertical jump.

07:16:29.995 --> 07:16:33.263
So as soon as you land, I don't want you to sink low and get up.

07:16:33.283 --> 07:16:36.271
I want you to boom, get off the floor nice and reactive.

07:16:36.632 --> 07:16:41.323
Get over the hurdle and then either land on two feet or land one, two.

07:16:41.303 --> 07:16:54.655
Okay, so I need you to get as far as you possibly can with that first one, and the further we can set that cone back, the better, and then the higher we get on the single leg vertical hop, the better.

07:17:08.152 --> 07:17:09.794
So here we have a couple options.

07:17:09.854 --> 07:17:15.280
If you have the hyperextension machine like this, then we're going to do hyperextension holds with the plate drop.

07:17:16.020 --> 07:17:21.666
If you do not have this, then we're just going to get two benches or two boxes and we're going to do a reverse plank.

07:17:21.686 --> 07:17:23.208
We're working the same muscles.

07:17:23.488 --> 07:17:25.390
So if you don't have this, don't worry.

07:17:25.410 --> 07:17:29.254
But if you do have the hyperextension machine, I would start light.

07:17:29.415 --> 07:17:34.480
I would start with maybe a 10 or 25 pound plate at the most.

07:17:34.460 --> 07:17:57.514
and we're just going to come in and hold in this full extension okay so for hyperextension machine doesn't really mean that we want to go into hyperextension we just want full extension where i'm flat all the way from my head down through the heel okay and contrary to popular belief this isn't really a back exercise this is actually glutes and hamstrings okay so

07:17:57.494 --> 07:18:03.844
If you're very inexperienced and you have no strength in the post chain and the glutes, then you can just hold right here.

07:18:04.906 --> 07:18:06.789
You can just hold for about 30 seconds.

07:18:06.809 --> 07:18:09.814
That's going to get you a good workout.

07:18:10.174 --> 07:18:14.661
If you've been training for a while, maybe for a couple of years, then you can do the plate drop.

07:18:14.701 --> 07:18:19.549
So I'm going to hold right here and I'm just going to drop the plate and catch it at full extension.

07:18:19.569 --> 07:18:21.873
So it's drop, catch, bring it back up.

07:18:22.454 --> 07:18:24.497
Drop, catch, bring it back up.

07:18:28.477 --> 07:18:35.366
So every time that I'm dropping the plate and going to catch it, it gives what's called a perturbation.

07:18:35.386 --> 07:18:41.874
So it's forcing me down into flexion, but my glutes and my hamstrings are resisting that flexion.

07:18:42.335 --> 07:18:48.583
So they're resisting that perturbation, and that's what gets the hamstrings and the glutes and the post chain nice and strong.

07:18:48.603 --> 07:18:55.632
So for this, how you count the reps, every time you drop the plate, catch it, and bring it back up, that counts as one rep.

07:18:55.612 --> 07:19:11.992
Now, if that's too easy, then yes, you can go up to a 35-pound plate or a 45-pound plate, but for the most part, I would rather see you have just perfect form, stay completely in that full extension, and really just squeeze the boots as hard as you can.

07:19:12.312 --> 07:19:20.522
We've never had anybody go above a 45-pound plate because if you're doing this the right way, you're going to really feel that burn in the post chain.

07:19:33.698 --> 07:19:39.505
So for my sub max long duration jumps, what I'm gonna do is almost like a jump rope.

07:19:39.545 --> 07:19:44.011
So you know in a jump rope, we're just nice and low and we're rhythmic and we're bouncy.

07:19:44.271 --> 07:19:49.898
We're not going maximal on this and we're also not lowering low and trying to use a lot of muscular power.

07:19:50.239 --> 07:19:54.865
We're really just using for the most part that Achilles to spring us off the ground.

07:19:54.965 --> 07:19:58.409
So there's gonna be three different aspects here.

07:19:58.549 --> 07:20:02.254
So your first one is gonna be a side to side hop.

07:20:02.234 --> 07:20:05.699
Okay, I'm springing off the balls of the feet.

07:20:06.080 --> 07:20:09.965
I am not letting my heel collapse down to the ground, all right?

07:20:10.426 --> 07:20:16.014
We're not going maximal, we're just going light and bouncy off the ground, okay?

07:20:16.034 --> 07:20:17.997
Now I'm going for a certain amount of time.

07:20:18.117 --> 07:20:22.303
Pay attention to the reps because we're going to go up every week.

07:20:22.383 --> 07:20:25.468
So let's say for week one, you're 30 seconds.

07:20:25.768 --> 07:20:28.332
Okay, now the second one is gonna be front back.

07:20:28.472 --> 07:20:31.296
So I'm just switching the feet

07:20:31.276 --> 07:20:49.420
forwards and backwards same concept springing off the balls of the feet not letting that heel collapse let's say it's 30 seconds there so then the third one is just a normal low level jump same thing spring off the ball of the foot heel cannot collapse okay now

07:20:49.400 --> 07:20:52.363
What we're gonna do is really go long duration.

07:20:52.383 --> 07:21:03.114
You're gonna get a big burn in the calves, but what we're doing is we're reforming the fascia of our feet and we're training that Achilles to be an energy storing and releasing monster.

07:21:03.255 --> 07:21:04.936
So pay attention to the reps.

07:21:05.257 --> 07:21:09.061
This is one exercise where I do want you close to your max.

07:21:09.441 --> 07:21:12.284
So let's say week one, I gave you 30, 30, 30.

07:21:12.344 --> 07:21:14.106
That's a minute and a half total.

07:21:14.426 --> 07:21:18.951
If you got to the end of that minute and a half and you feel like you could have kept going,

07:21:18.931 --> 07:21:43.065
you need to keep going you need to push close to your max because if we want the fascia to actually reform around the feet and we want the Achilles to adapt we got to push close to that max so for some of you that might be two minutes that might be three minutes I just need you to progressively increase each week so if I were to do this drill right now I would probably get about three minutes and at that three minute mark personally

07:21:43.045 --> 07:21:47.534
I would start to feel like almost a cramp in the calf.

07:21:47.834 --> 07:21:50.219
I would feel a burn of like a 9 out of 10.

07:21:50.539 --> 07:21:52.303
I don't need you to get to a 10 out of 10.

07:21:52.603 --> 07:21:57.633
I don't need your calves to fully cramp up, but get close to that point and then stop.

07:21:57.934 --> 07:21:59.216
Take as long as you want.

07:21:59.296 --> 07:22:04.446
Sometimes I take up to two or three minutes in between sets and then I go back in.

07:22:04.426 --> 07:22:14.105
For most of you, I want you to follow the rep range, but again, if that is too easy, extend it, make sure you jot down your time, and each week, I want you to go up on that.

07:22:14.486 --> 07:22:19.536
By the end of the four weeks, our fascia and our Achilles is gonna be a lot springier.

07:22:19.596 --> 07:22:23.083
We're gonna be storing and releasing elastic energy like never before.

07:22:23.404 --> 07:22:24.105
Let's get it.

07:22:49.752 --> 07:22:53.777
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

07:22:53.898 --> 07:22:55.700
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

07:22:55.740 --> 07:22:58.444
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

07:22:58.484 --> 07:23:04.993
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

07:23:05.513 --> 07:23:11.722
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

07:23:11.742 --> 07:23:15.507
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

07:23:15.487 --> 07:23:19.375
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

07:23:19.816 --> 07:23:24.166
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

07:23:24.226 --> 07:23:31.622
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

07:23:32.564 --> 07:23:35.627
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

07:23:35.647 --> 07:23:42.355
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

07:23:42.915 --> 07:23:48.682
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

07:23:48.702 --> 07:23:52.366
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

07:23:54.668 --> 07:23:58.993
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

07:24:01.893 --> 07:24:03.815
and that stomach will fall, okay?

07:24:04.536 --> 07:24:07.159
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

07:24:07.940 --> 07:24:16.951
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

07:24:17.752 --> 07:24:25.502
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

07:24:25.762 --> 07:24:29.807
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

07:24:29.787 --> 07:24:58.663
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

07:24:58.913 --> 07:25:01.417
happens in the parasympathetic state.

07:25:01.978 --> 07:25:06.424
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

07:25:06.444 --> 07:25:20.044
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

07:25:22.015 --> 07:25:22.696
What's up guys?

07:25:22.796 --> 07:25:26.401
Quick minimum dose advice for day three of phase six.

07:25:26.601 --> 07:25:30.747
This is already a very efficient workout so we shouldn't have to cut out much.

07:25:31.187 --> 07:25:40.199
If we're going to cut out anything, it would be the foam roller circuit and it would be the last exercise, hamstring chair alternating holds.

07:25:40.980 --> 07:25:42.202
This is a great exercise.

07:25:42.242 --> 07:25:49.592
You can get sort of a maximal muscle contraction in the hamstrings, get a good mind muscle connection to those hamstrings.

07:25:49.572 --> 07:25:53.300
but if there's one that you have to skip, it would be that one.

07:25:53.941 --> 07:25:56.346
All the other exercises are extremely important.

07:25:56.747 --> 07:26:06.568
The oscillating ankle stiffness, you could take that down to two or three sets, but this is a very important exercise, so I wouldn't completely skip that exercise.

07:26:06.751 --> 07:26:08.315
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

07:26:08.456 --> 07:26:10.140
I have a long roller from Skills.

07:26:10.401 --> 07:26:17.279
You could do long or you could do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you could just get it from Walmart or Target.

07:26:17.640 --> 07:26:23.055
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

07:26:23.035 --> 07:26:26.142
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

07:26:26.482 --> 07:26:35.642
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

07:26:35.662 --> 07:26:42.377
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

07:26:42.357 --> 07:26:48.868
or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

07:26:49.248 --> 07:26:51.472
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

07:26:51.492 --> 07:26:52.914
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

07:26:53.255 --> 07:27:01.649
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

07:27:02.029 --> 07:27:09.141
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

07:27:09.121 --> 07:27:13.815
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

07:27:14.032 --> 07:27:43.971
the next one we're going to do is the calves so we're just going to cross over and i'm going to push myself up so my hips are off the ground and i'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle so we're going over that calf and also over that achilles and our next one we're flipping over we're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad so i'm going to go on the side of the roller now and i'm just going to come down into a plank position and i'm going to roll

07:27:43.951 --> 07:27:50.261
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

07:27:50.982 --> 07:27:53.967
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

07:27:54.408 --> 07:27:59.657
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

07:28:00.117 --> 07:28:06.668
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

07:28:06.648 --> 07:28:09.554
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

07:28:09.754 --> 07:28:11.497
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

07:28:11.798 --> 07:28:25.585
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

07:28:27.337 --> 07:28:34.147
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

07:28:34.347 --> 07:28:37.933
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

07:28:38.213 --> 07:28:41.478
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

07:28:42.019 --> 07:28:44.723
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

07:28:44.743 --> 07:28:46.426
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

07:28:46.726 --> 07:28:50.552
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

07:28:50.992 --> 07:28:54.137
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

07:28:54.117 --> 07:29:01.965
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

07:29:02.306 --> 07:29:04.989
We got to really start to loosen that up.

07:29:06.610 --> 07:29:11.175
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

07:29:11.436 --> 07:29:14.959
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

07:29:15.680 --> 07:29:20.125
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

07:29:20.505 --> 07:29:22.167
Everything else is extra credit.

07:29:25.252 --> 07:29:29.001
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

07:29:29.281 --> 07:29:31.867
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

07:29:31.927 --> 07:29:35.916
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

07:29:36.397 --> 07:29:43.193
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

07:29:43.173 --> 07:29:46.997
I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

07:29:47.017 --> 07:29:48.419
I want to stay on the ball of the foot.

07:29:48.819 --> 07:29:51.922
And I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

07:29:52.303 --> 07:29:53.764
I'm not going too fast yet.

07:29:54.065 --> 07:29:56.207
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

07:29:56.447 --> 07:30:00.992
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

07:30:01.012 --> 07:30:05.196
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

07:30:05.597 --> 07:30:09.161
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

07:30:09.461 --> 07:30:11.543
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

07:30:11.523 --> 07:30:18.290
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here, maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed.

07:30:18.370 --> 07:30:19.351
So we're not all out.

07:30:19.831 --> 07:30:21.413
We're just getting that blood flowing.

07:30:21.693 --> 07:30:23.034
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

07:30:23.194 --> 07:30:27.579
For the over under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

07:30:27.899 --> 07:30:29.701
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

07:30:29.781 --> 07:30:35.847
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can.

07:30:35.907 --> 07:30:39.370
But we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

07:30:39.350 --> 07:30:47.048
Okay, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

07:30:50.723 --> 07:30:53.287
All right, we're gonna go five over and back.

07:30:53.548 --> 07:30:55.090
So it's 10 total touches.

07:30:55.290 --> 07:30:58.996
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch.

07:30:59.017 --> 07:31:00.619
So I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

07:31:00.900 --> 07:31:06.128
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position and I'm not out like this.

07:31:06.208 --> 07:31:07.470
I'm keeping that knee in.

07:31:07.891 --> 07:31:15.904
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can.

07:31:15.884 --> 07:31:37.740
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

07:31:38.648 --> 07:31:42.257
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

07:31:42.698 --> 07:31:45.104
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

07:31:45.144 --> 07:31:47.149
One, two, three.

07:31:47.229 --> 07:31:49.495
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

07:31:49.835 --> 07:31:51.840
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

07:31:51.900 --> 07:31:52.482
That's okay.

07:31:52.823 --> 07:31:54.427
Let's just get as low as we can there.

07:31:54.888 --> 07:31:55.830
One, two, three.

07:31:55.810 --> 07:32:22.273
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

07:32:22.253 --> 07:32:26.158
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

07:32:26.619 --> 07:32:30.624
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

07:32:30.965 --> 07:32:36.492
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

07:32:37.113 --> 07:32:51.332
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

07:32:56.273 --> 07:33:02.783
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

07:33:02.823 --> 07:33:04.286
Don't make the mistake of going here.

07:33:04.666 --> 07:33:12.739
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

07:33:12.839 --> 07:33:18.708
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

07:33:18.788 --> 07:33:22.534
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

07:33:22.514 --> 07:33:24.377
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

07:33:24.638 --> 07:33:26.581
One, two, three.

07:33:26.721 --> 07:33:29.687
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

07:33:29.867 --> 07:33:32.071
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

07:33:32.732 --> 07:33:34.435
One, two, three.

07:33:34.495 --> 07:33:35.978
Rock forward.

07:33:36.078 --> 07:33:37.120
So I'm on my toes now.

07:33:37.601 --> 07:33:39.825
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

07:33:39.865 --> 07:33:40.185
Hold.

07:33:40.826 --> 07:33:41.147
One, two.

07:33:41.127 --> 07:34:00.368
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

07:34:00.702 --> 07:34:02.965
One, two, three, lean.

07:34:03.746 --> 07:34:06.009
One, two, three, heels back down.

07:34:06.069 --> 07:34:10.094
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

07:34:10.394 --> 07:34:18.704
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

07:34:18.724 --> 07:34:23.090
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

07:34:23.450 --> 07:34:29.798
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

07:34:29.778 --> 07:34:32.641
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

07:34:32.661 --> 07:34:34.944
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

07:34:35.364 --> 07:34:38.147
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

07:34:38.488 --> 07:34:40.169
I need to be flat through the hips.

07:34:40.470 --> 07:34:42.872
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

07:34:43.393 --> 07:34:47.818
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

07:34:48.198 --> 07:34:51.241
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

07:34:51.261 --> 07:34:59.110
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

07:34:59.090 --> 07:35:01.435
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers.

07:35:02.518 --> 07:35:09.053
And we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

07:35:09.494 --> 07:35:17.773
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

07:35:17.753 --> 07:35:22.138
So for our top end speed workout, we're going to keep it very efficient and simple.

07:35:22.478 --> 07:35:29.887
The reason why we're doing this is top end sprint speed exposes the body to a lot of things that we don't get anywhere else, right?

07:35:29.907 --> 07:35:37.236
Like building the proper stiffness through the Achilles and through the quad tendon and the proper compliance through the hip flexor tendons.

07:35:37.596 --> 07:35:42.381
And it kind of builds the tendons exactly how the human body was meant to be.

07:35:42.802 --> 07:35:47.187
And we can't get that through doing basic mobility, basic static stretching.

07:35:47.167 --> 07:35:54.457
And also the ground contact times are so fast that we have to learn how to apply force quicker and quicker and quicker.

07:35:54.898 --> 07:35:59.384
And so we're not just getting faster when we work on top end sprint speed.

07:35:59.625 --> 07:36:06.154
We're also going to improve our vertical jump, especially off one leg because the ground contact time is so short.

07:36:06.134 --> 07:36:11.260
Now, if you're on a track, basically mark out 54 meters.

07:36:11.581 --> 07:36:18.029
I do 54 meters because it's about a quart and a half, and so that's what I have our guys do, and they've got some pretty good results off that.

07:36:18.089 --> 07:36:22.654
So mark out 54 feet, and then halfway put another cone.

07:36:23.015 --> 07:36:28.762
All right, so you have a cone here, you have a cone halfway, and then you have a cone at that 54 meters.

07:36:29.062 --> 07:36:31.385
So for this phase, we have six sprints.

07:36:31.586 --> 07:36:35.230
All right, so it's six sets of one with a break in between.

07:36:35.210 --> 07:36:39.418
For the first one, I want you to accelerate at 50%.

07:36:39.738 --> 07:36:43.024
So start split stance and you're just going to accelerate at 50%.

07:36:44.006 --> 07:36:47.612
Once you hit the cone, you're going to then accelerate to 75%.

07:36:47.793 --> 07:36:49.516
So we're not all out yet.

07:36:50.057 --> 07:36:53.964
All the way through that 54 meters, walk it back nice and slow.

07:36:53.984 --> 07:36:55.747
Take a minute, take as long as you need.

07:36:56.148 --> 07:36:57.370
Once you feel good to go,

07:36:57.350 --> 07:37:15.696
we're back in for that second set now we're accelerating at 75 percent and then i want you to once you hit the that first cone go up to 90 percent all right walk it back now the next one i want you to accelerate at 90 percent once you hit the first cone

07:37:15.676 --> 07:37:37.330
boost it up to a hundred percent so that's the first one where we're all out walking back the last three are a hundred percent all the way through if you have a friend keep time try to beat your time all three reps because we need max intent i need you going as fast as you possibly can week after week we should be decreasing our sprint time

07:37:37.310 --> 07:37:51.410
Now, if you cannot get it on a track, I really advise trying to find somewhere where you have that long distance because if you have to do this on a basketball court, that's okay, but you can't reach true top-end speed on a basketball court, right?

07:37:51.470 --> 07:37:58.941
By the time we get out of acceleration and we start to raise up into that top-end speed, we're already almost getting to that baseline.

07:37:59.181 --> 07:38:02.406
So we already have to decelerate and change directions.

07:38:02.386 --> 07:38:18.893
and so that's a huge thing in basketball and volleyball we never are exposed to top end speed because everything is acceleration but like i said it's so beneficial to train top end speed because it translates to other qualities such as single leg jumping so

07:38:19.463 --> 07:38:21.305
I really advise trying to find the space.

07:38:21.865 --> 07:38:33.697
If you have to do it on a basketball court, then go ahead and just accelerate early and try to get as fast as we can early on and sprint through the baseline, walk it back.

07:38:33.997 --> 07:38:44.948
You can go up to 10 sets instead of just doing six sets.

07:39:00.436 --> 07:39:06.123
So for our bound two foot takeoff combo, first of all, I'm going for distance and height.

07:39:06.203 --> 07:39:12.510
So if you're doing it on a basketball court, I would start just inside the high school three point line, heels on the line.

07:39:12.971 --> 07:39:14.492
I'm going to start with a broad jump.

07:39:14.773 --> 07:39:15.073
All right.

07:39:15.153 --> 07:39:16.955
So I'm rocking the arms back.

07:39:17.236 --> 07:39:18.798
I'm getting a good torso angle.

07:39:19.138 --> 07:39:20.620
I'm jumping as far forward.

07:39:21.000 --> 07:39:28.088
Now, instead of landing on two, I'm going to land on one and I'm going to stay stiff with that foot and spring into that next bound.

07:39:28.429 --> 07:39:29.430
So I'm going to jump.

07:39:29.410 --> 07:39:39.812
I'm going to land right, and then I'm going to jump to my left, and from my left, I get straight into my penultimate step, so then it's right, left, take off.

07:39:39.832 --> 07:39:44.602
All right, so we're building a ton of explosiveness, stiffness, reactive strength.

07:39:44.963 --> 07:39:49.613
Most importantly, we're building that rhythm into the two-foot takeoff.

07:39:49.593 --> 07:39:57.009
So it would be as far as I can, right, left, right, left, take off.

07:39:57.029 --> 07:40:01.640
Off this left foot, I don't want you to think about a bound where you're getting as far as you can.

07:40:02.061 --> 07:40:09.036
I just want you to get a little bit of distance and kind of build that rhythm into that long penultimate stride.

07:40:09.573 --> 07:40:12.897
If you're doing it on the court, the further out we can get, the better.

07:40:12.937 --> 07:40:23.169
So if I'm good there and I can get up and, you know, say you can only touch the backboard, if you can get up and touch the backboard, then okay, cool, let's move it back.

07:40:23.269 --> 07:40:26.453
Let's go NBA three and then let's move it back a couple feet.

07:40:26.793 --> 07:40:32.880
If you can get back close to the circle at the half court line, that's pretty impressive, all right?

07:40:32.860 --> 07:40:38.666
I do want you to get max jump height, though, so I need you to touch as high as you can.

07:40:39.047 --> 07:40:40.749
Let's say you can already touch the rim.

07:40:41.009 --> 07:40:42.931
You need to do this with a basketball, all right?

07:40:43.191 --> 07:40:52.502
And I need you to get up there and throw down some dunks, try some different dunks that you've never worked on before, and let's try to really push ourselves to get that max jump height.

07:40:52.702 --> 07:40:54.724
So, of course, we're going to switch sides on this.

07:40:54.744 --> 07:41:01.652
So if I said three sets of two, that means two broad right, left, right,

07:41:02.425 --> 07:41:06.175
two of those and then two on the other side to complete one set.

07:41:06.395 --> 07:41:08.641
So then the other side would be just the opposite.

07:41:08.682 --> 07:41:16.823
So it would be off two, land left, right, left, right, turn stance that way, get up probably left hand.

07:41:35.467 --> 07:41:41.256
On the lateral double bound, we're focusing on our lateral power, speed, and elasticity.

07:41:41.276 --> 07:41:43.960
So we're gonna start on the right foot here.

07:41:44.300 --> 07:41:46.203
I'm gonna go right, right, left.

07:41:46.303 --> 07:41:51.531
So I'm gonna jump off my right, land on my right, push again, land on my left.

07:41:51.872 --> 07:41:57.300
And now when I land on my left, I change directions as quick as I possibly can and go to my right.

07:41:57.520 --> 07:41:58.842
So now it's gonna be left, left, right.

07:41:59.083 --> 07:42:01.106
So left, left, right, transition.

07:42:01.166 --> 07:42:03.229
Right, right, left, transition.

07:42:03.209 --> 07:42:06.572
Notice I'm using minimal knee bends.

07:42:06.592 --> 07:42:10.316
So I'm staying relatively stiff on the transition portion.

07:42:10.756 --> 07:42:14.040
I'm not going to land and sink into the joints.

07:42:14.400 --> 07:42:19.525
The quickest cuts, the quickest moves on the basketball court are done with relatively stiff joints.

07:42:19.765 --> 07:42:21.747
We hit the floor and we get out.

07:42:21.767 --> 07:42:22.768
So think about this.

07:42:23.309 --> 07:42:26.492
Visualize a game scenario while we're doing this drill.

07:42:27.072 --> 07:42:29.214
So it's right, right, left.

07:42:29.695 --> 07:42:32.878
Notice that my body is already leaning in that position.

07:42:32.858 --> 07:42:36.550
If you land in this position, we're not going to be able to cut that way.

07:42:37.072 --> 07:42:41.687
Okay, so I got to set myself up for my next cut with that body lean.

07:43:21.408 --> 07:43:25.855
So for my consecutive hurdle jumps, I'm gonna put out three hurdles.

07:43:26.196 --> 07:43:30.783
Now, I have the adjustable hurdles so that I can adjust them to different heights.

07:43:30.843 --> 07:43:34.289
It's from Champion Sports, so if you hit it, it just collapses.

07:43:34.469 --> 07:43:35.551
You don't trip over it.

07:43:36.012 --> 07:43:38.435
If you don't have these hurdles, that's completely fine.

07:43:38.756 --> 07:43:39.958
I don't really care what you have.

07:43:39.998 --> 07:43:43.163
You could have the tiny little agility hurdles

07:43:43.143 --> 07:43:49.095
or you could even put out three cones or just three shoes, whatever you want to use, it doesn't really matter.

07:43:49.495 --> 07:43:51.880
All that matters is that we have an object to jump over.

07:43:51.920 --> 07:43:55.707
At the end of the day, your max intent and your max jump height matters.

07:43:56.088 --> 07:43:59.194
The object that we're jumping over doesn't matter at all.

07:43:59.174 --> 07:44:03.800
But I want you to put out three objects and make sure that they're about six feet apart.

07:44:04.121 --> 07:44:07.125
If they're too narrow, then you're going to have a hard time with this drill.

07:44:07.486 --> 07:44:08.627
Everybody's different.

07:44:08.727 --> 07:44:10.229
Everybody has different length.

07:44:10.650 --> 07:44:14.535
And so some of you might have to go even further, kind of try it out for me.

07:44:14.916 --> 07:44:16.218
I like to go about six feet.

07:44:16.498 --> 07:44:20.163
So what I'm going to do is my first one is a double hop.

07:44:20.183 --> 07:44:22.386
So that means I jump over the first one.

07:44:22.747 --> 07:44:25.030
Now, when I land in the middle,

07:44:25.010 --> 07:44:29.056
I'm gonna go with a double hop, so I'm not reactive off the ground just yet.

07:44:29.356 --> 07:44:33.001
So I'm gonna land, and it's one, two, and get up.

07:44:33.022 --> 07:44:34.644
So you see that little baby hop?

07:44:35.064 --> 07:44:38.249
I'm landing, and then I'm jumping again, all right?

07:44:38.349 --> 07:44:40.733
I land in the center, I do the same thing.

07:44:40.833 --> 07:44:46.040
So it's land, baby hop, jump, land on the other side, all right?

07:44:46.501 --> 07:44:47.943
So that is level one.

07:44:48.303 --> 07:44:52.890
So for our first two sets, we're doing level one, double jump.

07:44:52.870 --> 07:45:04.401
Now, level two, which is for you going to be sets three and four, you're going to do a more reactive jump, but we're still not going stiff with the knees.

07:45:04.482 --> 07:45:13.110
So a lot of you have probably done more of a pogo hop type jump where it's very stiff with the knees, very little range of motion.

07:45:13.511 --> 07:45:19.296
For this one, I want you to load up pretty low, but I still want you to get off the floor fast.

07:45:19.316 --> 07:45:21.038
So we jump.

07:45:21.018 --> 07:45:24.743
We land and we load up low and get up high.

07:45:25.404 --> 07:45:26.806
Same thing on the next hurdle.

07:45:27.327 --> 07:45:29.830
We load up low, we get up high.

07:45:30.431 --> 07:45:35.878
I do want you getting off the floor fast, but I still want you getting a big range of motion.

07:45:36.259 --> 07:45:39.904
This is working what we call the slow stretch reflex.

07:45:39.884 --> 07:45:43.430
compared to the fast stretch reflex, which is going to be level three.

07:45:43.550 --> 07:45:51.344
The fast stretch reflex, now we're getting over that first hurdle and we literally just tap the floor and get off as high as we can.

07:45:51.744 --> 07:45:56.092
So it's minimal bend in the knees, we're nice and stiff with the ankles.

07:45:56.533 --> 07:46:00.379
As soon as you hit, we're reactive off the ground and bounce.

07:46:00.419 --> 07:46:03.765
It's like more of a pogo hop than a normal jump.

07:46:03.745 --> 07:46:11.596
Get over that second hurdle, same thing, pogo hop, get over that third hurdle and land nice and soft.

07:46:12.617 --> 07:46:15.642
So set one and set two, double hop.

07:46:16.042 --> 07:46:20.248
Set three and set four are land low, get high.

07:46:20.568 --> 07:46:28.960
Set five and set six are reactive, quick off the ground, minimal knee bend, more of a pogo hop type jump.

07:46:37.850 --> 07:46:45.158
So for my oscillating ankle stiffness, what I'm going to do is use a bumper plate or a nice stable step of some sort.

07:46:45.798 --> 07:46:49.402
Now if you have a Smith machine, you could do this with a Smith machine.

07:46:49.482 --> 07:46:54.588
I wouldn't do this with a regular barbell because of the stability and kind of the angle that we're in.

07:46:55.068 --> 07:46:56.930
I like to do it with dumbbells personally.

07:46:56.990 --> 07:47:01.795
So if that's the case and you want to do it with dumbbells, hold the heaviest dumbbells that you can hold.

07:47:02.556 --> 07:47:04.258
So right now I'm just using a 50.

07:47:04.778 --> 07:47:07.401
Normally I'd probably use around 75s.

07:47:07.786 --> 07:47:14.942
So what we're going to do is bring the ball of the foot up on that bumper plate and then I'm in a big split stance.

07:47:14.962 --> 07:47:17.047
This back leg is going to stay relatively straight.

07:47:17.107 --> 07:47:19.632
We can bend that knee a little bit if it's more comfortable.

07:47:20.254 --> 07:47:22.539
Now from here, I'm just oscillating.

07:47:22.980 --> 07:47:27.550
So I'm just here in my lunge and I'm just bouncing

07:47:27.530 --> 07:47:31.254
up and down, but notice my heel is not collapsing.

07:47:31.634 --> 07:47:35.598
So every time I drop down, my heel wants to collapse lower than the plate.

07:47:35.959 --> 07:47:39.583
I got to keep it stable and keep it even with the plate.

07:47:39.943 --> 07:47:42.406
So I'm just bouncing in this position.

07:47:42.566 --> 07:47:43.307
All right.

07:47:43.327 --> 07:47:48.852
We're going to feel a huge burn through the feet, through the calves, all the way up through the glutes and the quads.

07:47:49.533 --> 07:47:50.714
We are going for time.

07:47:50.814 --> 07:47:54.178
So set the timer so that you don't have to worry about counting reps.

07:47:54.158 --> 07:47:55.962
and we're just bouncing in this position.

07:47:56.003 --> 07:47:58.148
We don't want a long, slow eccentric.

07:47:58.569 --> 07:48:00.273
So don't slowly lower yourself down.

07:48:00.854 --> 07:48:02.198
Explode up, lower.

07:48:02.378 --> 07:48:03.601
Explode up, lower.

07:48:03.902 --> 07:48:08.132
And we're just oscillating in maybe a three to four inch range.

07:48:08.473 --> 07:48:11.340
We're not standing up all the way to the top.

07:48:11.320 --> 07:48:18.130
So this is great for our traditional oscillating that we've been doing, glutes, quads, hamstrings, all that.

07:48:18.230 --> 07:48:23.338
But the reason that we're doing this with an elevated foot is to work on that ankle stiffness.

07:48:23.378 --> 07:48:29.006
So when we look at an approach jump, that block foot, we need rigidity, we need stiffness.

07:48:29.547 --> 07:48:35.235
Otherwise that heel collapses and we lose all of that elastic energy through the Achilles.

07:48:35.215 --> 07:48:36.678
sprinting, same thing.

07:48:37.139 --> 07:48:47.363
If we're hitting that heel collapse, we're losing a lot of that energy, so we need proper ankle stiffness to run, to cut, and to jump to our highest potential.

07:48:47.764 --> 07:48:52.515
Let's do it.

07:48:58.722 --> 07:49:05.337
So for my hamstring chair alternating holds, I'm gonna use a box, a bench, or a chair, or even just the end of your couch.

07:49:06.039 --> 07:49:10.609
What we're gonna do is come to our back, and I wanna get 90-90.

07:49:11.190 --> 07:49:15.921
So I'm gonna put my heels up on the box or the bench or the couch,

07:49:16.120 --> 07:49:21.568
and I wanna get 90 degrees here, and I want 90 degrees here, okay?

07:49:21.588 --> 07:49:39.154
Now from that position, and if we can't get that, we might have to elevate your feet more, maybe use some pillows to elevate your feet more, or maybe you need to elevate your torso more, so maybe lay down on some pillows, mess around with that distance and that height to where we can get 90 degrees here, 90 degrees here.

07:49:39.715 --> 07:49:42.499
Now from this position, I'm just gonna bridge up

07:49:42.479 --> 07:49:44.529
and then I'm gonna come to one leg.

07:49:44.891 --> 07:49:48.810
So I'm gonna give a three count, so I'm gonna lift my left leg,

07:49:48.892 --> 07:49:52.216
One, two, three, and then I'm gonna switch.

07:49:52.597 --> 07:49:57.664
Now I'm driving my left heel down into the bench, and I'm lifting my right leg.

07:49:57.864 --> 07:49:59.746
One, two, three, okay?

07:49:59.766 --> 07:50:03.912
That counts as one rep, and I just keep alternating, all right?

07:50:03.932 --> 07:50:16.789
The key here is we're holding ourselves up with our hamstrings, so we should feel that burn back in the hamstrings, but I want you to exaggerate it and get a maximal isometric hold, so that means I'm not just

07:50:16.769 --> 07:50:25.865
putting enough tension in the hamstrings to hold myself up, I'm actually squeezing as hard as I can, and I'm pushing down into the box as hard as I can.

07:50:26.186 --> 07:50:30.834
So I'm getting a maximal hamstring hold, and then I switch.

07:50:30.854 --> 07:50:35.402
Maximal tension, push the heel as hard as you can down into the box,

07:50:35.382 --> 07:50:36.303
and then switch.

07:50:36.724 --> 07:50:44.813
So if you do these the right way, by the end of the set, you're gonna feel a huge burn in the hamstring, almost to the point where you feel like you're gonna cramp.

07:50:44.973 --> 07:50:47.796
So we're getting some good isolation back in the hamstrings.

07:50:47.816 --> 07:50:49.919
We're getting that post chain strength.

07:50:49.939 --> 07:50:58.449
A lot of times, athletes weakest link is that post chain, and a lot of athletes train their quads a lot, but their hamstrings are not quite as strong.

07:50:58.749 --> 07:51:03.395
It's a very good exercise for balancing out the hamstrings and the quads.

07:51:03.675 --> 07:51:04.596
Let's get after it.

07:51:29.753 --> 07:51:33.778
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

07:51:33.898 --> 07:51:35.700
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

07:51:35.740 --> 07:51:38.443
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

07:51:38.483 --> 07:51:45.491
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

07:51:45.511 --> 07:51:51.457
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

07:51:51.738 --> 07:51:55.542
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

07:51:55.522 --> 07:51:58.889
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

07:51:59.831 --> 07:52:03.659
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

07:52:04.220 --> 07:52:11.556
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you, when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still

07:52:12.565 --> 07:52:15.289
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

07:52:15.649 --> 07:52:22.378
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

07:52:22.939 --> 07:52:28.707
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

07:52:28.727 --> 07:52:32.372
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

07:52:34.695 --> 07:52:38.961
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

07:52:41.911 --> 07:52:43.834
and that stomach will fall, okay?

07:52:44.534 --> 07:52:47.198
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

07:52:47.939 --> 07:52:56.971
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

07:52:57.752 --> 07:53:05.522
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

07:53:05.743 --> 07:53:09.808
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

07:53:09.788 --> 07:53:38.682
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

07:53:38.932 --> 07:53:41.436
happens in the parasympathetic state.

07:53:41.977 --> 07:53:46.444
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

07:53:46.464 --> 07:54:00.045
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

07:54:01.915 --> 07:54:05.220
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

07:54:05.240 --> 07:54:09.507
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

07:54:09.807 --> 07:54:16.598
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

07:54:16.898 --> 07:54:20.384
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

07:54:20.664 --> 07:54:28.176
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

07:54:28.156 --> 07:54:32.507
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

07:54:32.928 --> 07:54:34.893
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

07:54:35.233 --> 07:54:37.439
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

07:54:37.720 --> 07:54:39.785
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

07:54:40.065 --> 07:54:42.271
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

07:54:42.592 --> 07:54:44.376
I'm just gonna lean forward.

07:54:44.356 --> 07:55:05.166
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

07:55:05.450 --> 07:55:07.412
15 seconds on the other side as well.

07:55:07.432 --> 07:55:09.575
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

07:55:09.755 --> 07:55:11.177
So that is the back of the thigh.

07:55:11.417 --> 07:55:15.622
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

07:55:15.742 --> 07:55:19.426
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

07:55:19.587 --> 07:55:22.010
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

07:55:22.350 --> 07:55:24.673
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

07:55:25.033 --> 07:55:26.595
So now look at this back foot.

07:55:26.615 --> 07:55:28.037
I'm not curling the toe under.

07:55:28.417 --> 07:55:32.622
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

07:55:32.602 --> 07:55:35.927
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

07:55:36.348 --> 07:55:41.716
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

07:55:42.016 --> 07:55:55.437
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

07:55:55.417 --> 07:55:58.544
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

07:55:58.584 --> 07:55:59.927
That's the inside of the leg.

07:56:00.408 --> 07:56:03.775
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

07:56:04.377 --> 07:56:05.058
Same thing.

07:56:05.399 --> 07:56:08.285
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

07:56:08.586 --> 07:56:11.873
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

07:56:11.853 --> 07:56:15.860
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

07:56:16.161 --> 07:56:23.815
I'm not going to lift off, and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

07:56:24.176 --> 07:56:29.666
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

07:56:30.067 --> 07:56:35.397
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

07:56:35.377 --> 07:56:40.103
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

07:56:40.503 --> 07:56:48.153
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

07:56:48.674 --> 07:56:51.457
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

07:56:51.918 --> 07:56:57.925
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

07:56:57.905 --> 07:57:01.010
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

07:57:01.050 --> 07:57:03.233
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

07:57:03.453 --> 07:57:07.118
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

07:57:07.599 --> 07:57:09.161
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

07:57:09.562 --> 07:57:12.086
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

07:57:12.166 --> 07:57:13.067
So I'm 90 there.

07:57:13.428 --> 07:57:16.212
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

07:57:16.592 --> 07:57:18.876
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

07:57:19.236 --> 07:57:23.943
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

07:57:24.283 --> 07:57:27.388
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

07:57:27.368 --> 07:57:30.293
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

07:57:30.674 --> 07:57:35.443
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

07:57:35.483 --> 07:57:36.906
All right.

07:57:36.926 --> 07:57:42.817
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

07:57:43.558 --> 07:57:47.385
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

07:57:47.365 --> 07:57:52.673
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

07:57:53.053 --> 07:57:56.278
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

07:57:56.378 --> 07:57:59.583
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

07:57:59.963 --> 07:58:09.677
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

07:58:09.961 --> 07:58:16.448
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

07:58:16.728 --> 07:58:22.915
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

07:58:23.276 --> 07:58:27.340
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

07:58:28.541 --> 07:58:32.346
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

07:58:32.706 --> 07:58:35.890
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

07:58:35.950 --> 07:58:39.934
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

07:58:42.715 --> 07:58:43.357
What's up guys?

07:58:43.517 --> 07:58:48.330
Quick minimum dose advice for day two and day four of phase six.

07:58:48.951 --> 07:58:50.796
So it's already very efficient.

07:58:50.997 --> 07:58:53.303
You honestly probably don't have to take anything out.

07:58:53.583 --> 07:58:55.789
I think we could get this done in 20 or 30 minutes.

07:58:56.591 --> 07:58:58.075
Side plank hip abduction.

07:58:58.055 --> 07:58:59.097
Great exercise.

07:58:59.117 --> 07:59:04.326
You're actually going to feel this a lot more in the hip abductors side of the hips, which is good.

07:59:04.366 --> 07:59:06.270
It's nice to get a little bit of a pump in there.

07:59:07.212 --> 07:59:11.539
You're also going to feel that in the core as well, so a little bit of full body stability there.

07:59:12.140 --> 07:59:13.583
The PRI floss.

07:59:13.985 --> 07:59:21.816
So if you're extremely flexible already, you probably don't need this, but this is also working on breathing patterns, so it is a very valuable exercise.

07:59:21.856 --> 07:59:22.938
So I'd recommend doing that.

07:59:23.399 --> 07:59:25.862
Rocking dead bug, very valuable exercise.

07:59:25.982 --> 07:59:27.465
Rotational rolling mobility.

07:59:27.745 --> 07:59:36.718
This is challenging kind of internal rotators, external rotators, core stability in a way that we haven't really done much of in the past.

07:59:36.698 --> 07:59:40.408
Even if you're already mobile, I'd recommend trying out this exercise.

07:59:40.969 --> 07:59:45.020
It's going to challenge your mobility in some different planes than we're used to.

07:59:45.060 --> 07:59:47.446
Leg lowers, great exercise.

07:59:47.506 --> 07:59:52.800
If you want to make it harder, we can go slower on the way down and build more tension through the core.

07:59:52.780 --> 07:59:54.202
lower back angels.

07:59:54.462 --> 07:59:57.285
We haven't done much erector spinae isolation.

07:59:57.325 --> 07:59:58.326
Those are the back muscles.

07:59:58.707 --> 08:00:00.249
We haven't done much isolation there.

08:00:00.689 --> 08:00:09.680
So even though this is a pretty easy exercise, at least it seems like it, you might get a pretty good burn in those erector muscles that run along the spine.

08:00:10.140 --> 08:00:11.462
So I would recommend doing this.

08:00:11.862 --> 08:00:16.948
If you have any type of back pain or you're prone to back pain, this is one that could flare it up a little bit.

08:00:16.928 --> 08:00:23.279
If that's the case and you feel any pain, skip this exercise, but it is very good for injury prevention long term.

08:00:23.599 --> 08:00:38.464
Let's do it.

08:00:40.182 --> 08:00:50.538
So for my side plank hip abduction, I'm gonna go ahead and stack my legs, 90 degree angle there with the knees, and then I'm gonna come down on that forearm.

08:00:50.558 --> 08:00:56.908
So I don't want you on the elbow, I don't want you on the wrist, I want your full forearm down on the ground.

08:00:56.888 --> 08:01:02.658
I'm going to make sure that I'm nice and flat through the body so I don't want to have a major tucked hip there.

08:01:03.079 --> 08:01:04.822
I want to be extended nice and flat.

08:01:05.323 --> 08:01:11.473
Now from there, I'm just lifting my hips off the ground and then I'm going to drive this leg at the same time.

08:01:11.854 --> 08:01:13.176
So I'm up.

08:01:13.156 --> 08:01:16.643
I'm driving that leg at the top and then I'm right back down.

08:01:16.763 --> 08:01:18.987
Bring your hip all the way back down to the ground.

08:01:19.448 --> 08:01:22.774
Torso is still off the floor but that hip will touch down.

08:01:23.275 --> 08:01:29.427
So it's here, hip touches down, your full thigh is on the ground and drive up off that.

08:01:30.875 --> 08:01:34.519
So you're gonna feel a lot of this on that leg that's down.

08:01:34.760 --> 08:01:37.743
You're gonna feel that through the lateral portion of the hip.

08:01:38.064 --> 08:01:43.510
So we're working that hip abduction, but then you're also gonna feel it through that oblique that's down as well.

08:01:43.831 --> 08:01:54.123
So if my right side is down, as I'm pushing up, I'm gonna feel it from here all the way up through that lateral aspect of the core.

08:01:54.424 --> 08:01:57.287
So if you did wanna make this a little bit more difficult,

08:01:57.267 --> 08:02:02.432
Stay in that same position and you could add a band, a mini band right above the knees.

08:02:02.932 --> 08:02:04.113
Not necessarily essential.

08:02:04.474 --> 08:02:15.004
Even our NBA players have a tough time with this because if you're getting the perfect form and you're getting the hips to light up in the areas that we want, we're training a lot of those smaller stabilizing muscles.

08:02:15.384 --> 08:02:16.885
And so this is actually pretty tough.

08:02:17.005 --> 08:02:24.152
But if this does feel way too easy and you don't feel any of a burn in that lateral aspect of the hip, then you can add the band.

08:02:24.132 --> 08:02:41.297
or if you're super advanced you could start to go with just full straight legs and then you're coming down and driving like that come down touch and drive all right but that's a pretty difficult variation most of you are going to be better off with that 90 degrees stacked legs

08:03:01.747 --> 08:03:05.033
So this mobility drill is a concept from PRI.

08:03:05.093 --> 08:03:09.620
So I want you to pay attention to your breathing patterns here, not just the stretch itself.

08:03:09.781 --> 08:03:14.188
So get a foam roller or get a pillow or get something that you can prop your knee up on.

08:03:14.769 --> 08:03:18.516
And I'm going to actively push down throughout the entire set.

08:03:18.756 --> 08:03:20.479
So I'm pushing down in this right leg.

08:03:20.779 --> 08:03:24.666
I'm bringing this left leg back a little past 90 degrees.

08:03:24.646 --> 08:03:28.874
and I'm keeping my back grounded, so I don't want a big arch back.

08:03:29.155 --> 08:03:36.469
I'm thinking about posteriorly tilting my pelvis, so that means I'm tucking my tail so that my back is nice and flat.

08:03:36.870 --> 08:03:42.621
Now from here, actively apply pressure downwards, and I'm just going to extend

08:03:42.601 --> 08:04:11.545
and come back extend and come back but I want you to pay attention to the breathing so when you go into extension I want you to breathe in so I want you to get air all the way down into your stomach when we come back into flexion I want you to breathe out and when we breathe out we're thinking about bringing our rib cage down so it's in you can either go through the nose or through the mouth I don't really care so I'm going through the nose so it's in

08:04:13.870 --> 08:04:16.013
and out, push the ribs down.

08:04:18.477 --> 08:04:25.206
All right, now with every rep, you want to try to get your knee into more and more extension, get that full range of motion.

08:04:25.467 --> 08:04:29.412
Remember to apply active pressure down through that foam roller the entire time.

08:04:29.593 --> 08:04:34.680
Then of course, once we finish the recommended reps on one side, we switch over to that other side.

08:05:02.960 --> 08:05:09.574
So for my rocking dead bug, I'm gonna use a basketball, volleyball, or any type of ball around this size.

08:05:09.674 --> 08:05:11.638
If you don't have that, that's okay.

08:05:11.658 --> 08:05:15.587
So I'm gonna pin it between my knee and my elbow, all right?

08:05:15.747 --> 08:05:19.255
Now if I don't have the ball, then I'm just gonna try to touch elbow to knee.

08:05:19.555 --> 08:05:23.263
It's actually gonna be a little bit more difficult, a little more hip range of motion.

08:05:23.243 --> 08:05:27.513
So if I have that ball, I'm going to pin it, lay your head back.

08:05:27.854 --> 08:05:30.901
I want to make sure that I'm not hyperextended in my back.

08:05:30.941 --> 08:05:36.775
So a lot of people come into this extension where if you slip your hand under there, there's a big space.

08:05:37.216 --> 08:05:40.223
I want you to think about crushing that person's hand.

08:05:40.564 --> 08:05:41.927
So push down.

08:05:41.907 --> 08:05:45.492
you gotta think about posteriorly tilting your pelvis.

08:05:45.512 --> 08:05:48.716
So that means like if you had a tail, you would tuck your tail.

08:05:49.096 --> 08:05:51.459
And when you tuck your tail, you make your back flat.

08:05:52.040 --> 08:05:58.268
So from that flat back position, I am just going to extend the opposite side.

08:05:58.328 --> 08:06:04.076
So I'm gonna extend my left hand with the left leg at the same time.

08:06:04.496 --> 08:06:08.742
Notice I'm not touching the floor, so I just come a couple inches

08:06:08.722 --> 08:06:14.689
off the ground with that left side, and I come back to that base position, now from there I rock up.

08:06:15.050 --> 08:06:23.260
So it's a rock, get the heels down to the ground, and rock back slowly, that counts as one rep, and then you go back in.

08:06:23.681 --> 08:06:34.474
Now, notice that I'm not using a bunch of momentum to rock up, I wanna start from this dead stop position, and then I just create that momentum with my core.

08:06:34.795 --> 08:06:37.598
So it's dead stop position, I'm nice and still,

08:06:39.029 --> 08:06:39.952
and rock up.

08:06:40.293 --> 08:06:45.150
So if you do that the right way, that rock up is gonna really fire up the front abs.

08:06:45.511 --> 08:06:50.929
All right, so we do the desired reps to one side and then you go to the other side and finish out.

08:07:11.632 --> 08:07:15.537
So for my rotational rolling stretch, I'm setting up in my 90-90 position.

08:07:15.978 --> 08:07:19.302
So I'm 90 degrees here and I'm 90 degrees here.

08:07:20.204 --> 08:07:28.675
Now from here, I'm going to bring my fist out and I'm just going to squeeze the fist, get a little tension, get that core a little stability.

08:07:28.775 --> 08:07:32.180
So if you're loose in this position, you're not going to be able to move.

08:07:32.500 --> 08:07:34.543
So I want you to brace that core a little bit.

08:07:34.944 --> 08:07:38.128
Now from here, I'm just rolling to this other side.

08:07:39.104 --> 08:07:41.831
and I go straight up from here.

08:07:42.353 --> 08:07:49.713
So now I'm down, roll, and I'm up, get to that full hip extension at the top.

08:07:49.733 --> 08:07:53.603
Okay, so now it's just a smooth flow, straight over up,

08:07:54.106 --> 08:07:56.189
Straight over, up.

08:07:57.351 --> 08:08:00.275
If this is too difficult, you can use your hands for assistance.

08:08:00.295 --> 08:08:07.526
So you can come down, support with your hands, support with your hands, push off the right hand and come up.

08:08:08.267 --> 08:08:12.473
Support, support, push off the left hand and come up.

08:08:12.694 --> 08:08:19.624
But if you get that down, then go to hands either straight out or you could just go hands here in front of your chest.

08:08:19.604 --> 08:08:24.174
This drill is very good for training the internal and the external rotators of the hip.

08:08:24.455 --> 08:08:26.880
We should not feel lateral stress in the knee.

08:08:27.241 --> 08:08:33.114
So some people, if they do this the wrong way, they feel more of a pressure in the knee, either the medial or the lateral aspect.

08:08:33.374 --> 08:08:35.118
We do not want any stress in the knee.

08:08:35.339 --> 08:08:39.047
We should feel like the hips are the main joint being mobilized here.

08:09:10.145 --> 08:09:15.014
So for the leg lowers, you've got to make sure that you anchor your hands to something stable.

08:09:15.034 --> 08:09:16.638
So I'm holding onto a power rack.

08:09:17.098 --> 08:09:20.906
Make sure that whatever you're holding onto is nice and stable so that that's your support.

08:09:21.246 --> 08:09:27.799
If worse comes worse and all you have is a partner, your partner could stand over you and they could just grab onto your ankles, all right?

08:09:27.779 --> 08:09:30.183
So what we're doing is negative.

08:09:30.283 --> 08:09:33.568
So we're going to go three to four seconds on the eccentric.

08:09:33.628 --> 08:09:38.296
So that means my legs are straight up and I'm going three to four seconds down.

08:09:38.576 --> 08:09:41.040
I'm trying to keep my knees as straight as possible.

08:09:41.421 --> 08:09:41.681
All right.

08:09:42.062 --> 08:09:43.945
So control down the heel.

08:09:44.005 --> 08:09:46.369
Stop a couple inches short of the floor.

08:09:46.709 --> 08:09:49.213
And then we bend our knees and bring it back.

08:09:49.193 --> 08:09:52.638
Okay, so I don't want the concentric to be the hard part.

08:09:52.959 --> 08:10:01.551
So that's why we're bending our knees, coming back, extend the knees so that our legs are nice and long, and then we go back into that lowering phase.

08:10:02.172 --> 08:10:08.121
If that is too easy, then we can keep our knees straight and go straight on the up and on the down.

08:10:08.542 --> 08:10:15.572
But I would rather you really focus on that slow, controlled, eccentric, really squeezing that core with every rep.

08:10:15.552 --> 08:10:19.118
Now where we want to feel this exercise is mostly front core.

08:10:19.239 --> 08:10:22.184
So that abdominal wall should be fired up.

08:10:22.484 --> 08:10:25.530
This is one exercise where we are going to get a pretty good burn.

08:10:25.830 --> 08:10:28.756
If you're not getting a burn, I would extend the reps a little bit.

08:10:28.796 --> 08:10:33.544
So go up a couple reps to the point where you are getting that big burn in the front core.

08:10:53.926 --> 08:11:23.629
so for my lower back angels so I'm just gonna lay flat on the floor now from here I'm gonna bring my chest up and I'm gonna bring my legs up so I don't want my knees touching the floor and I don't want my chest touching the floor okay hands straight above the head and then I'm going a circular motion down towards the hips I'm facing the palms up and then I bring it back around at that top I'm facing the palms down alright so I'm kind of making

08:11:24.605 --> 08:11:29.650
like a snow angel, but I'm staying up this whole time.

08:11:30.291 --> 08:11:42.824
And you're gonna feel a good isometric contraction in the back, so the lower back, the erector muscles in the back, and then my glutes are gonna be fired up, and you're actually gonna feel a little bit in the hamstrings as well.

08:11:43.204 --> 08:11:47.469
So that full post chain is getting a good isometric contraction here.

08:11:47.889 --> 08:11:48.570
Let's do it.

08:11:52.212 --> 08:11:52.913
What's up, guys?

08:11:52.993 --> 08:11:57.320
Quick minimum dose advice for phase six, day one and day five.

08:11:58.001 --> 08:12:00.084
This is already a very efficient workout.

08:12:00.144 --> 08:12:14.084
You really shouldn't have to cut out anything here, but if your encore volume is so high that you can't handle all of this or you just don't have the time, you're going to skip the foam roller circuit, do the dynamic warmup, skip the acceleration jumpstart.

08:12:14.545 --> 08:12:17.509
Great drill, but if you're pressed for time, that's one that we can take out.

08:12:17.930 --> 08:12:19.232
Stutter Euro drill.

08:12:19.212 --> 08:12:22.436
Great exercise, but again, if you're pressed for time, take it out.

08:12:22.857 --> 08:12:25.581
Move straight down to single leg skip bounds.

08:12:25.941 --> 08:12:27.103
Have to have that in here.

08:12:27.664 --> 08:12:36.856
Always do our 10-minute dunk freestyle session unless you're going to be playing pickup later on and you feel like you're going to be dunking afterwards.

08:12:37.137 --> 08:12:44.227
I know a lot of basketball players, after they're done playing pickup or at practice, they're competing against their friends, trying new dunks, that kind of stuff.

08:12:44.707 --> 08:12:48.072
If that's you, then you could skip this here and do that later.

08:12:48.052 --> 08:12:52.883
Just make sure you're getting at least 10 minutes worth, if not a little bit more, of dunk practice.

08:12:52.903 --> 08:12:54.386
Triple contrast cluster.

08:12:54.646 --> 08:12:59.557
This is probably the most important exercise for this phase, building that raw power.

08:13:00.058 --> 08:13:01.120
This one is huge.

08:13:01.381 --> 08:13:02.163
Never skip this.

08:13:02.944 --> 08:13:04.067
Do all four sets.

08:13:04.207 --> 08:13:06.432
Don't decrease the sets or the reps.

08:13:06.412 --> 08:13:10.380
RFESS deficit squat, this is a great exercise.

08:13:10.801 --> 08:13:14.930
We do want this exercise for pure strength and pure hypertrophy.

08:13:15.491 --> 08:13:23.467
Very good because we haven't done a ton of hypertrophy work recently, so it's great to maintain hypertrophy and build some strength with this exercise.

08:13:23.948 --> 08:13:26.614
If you had to, take it down to two sets.

08:13:26.594 --> 08:13:28.397
Ball of the foot, one leg turn.

08:13:28.518 --> 08:13:31.784
If you have to, you can skip it, but this is a great exercise.

08:13:32.064 --> 08:13:37.715
You guys have already mastered this in previous phases, so we should be pretty good by now at this exercise.

08:13:38.075 --> 08:13:40.480
Time to take it to the next level and become perfect.

08:13:40.780 --> 08:13:43.425
7-11 breathing, as always, we want to do that.

08:13:43.746 --> 08:13:47.172
Static stretch, as always, you can skip it if you don't have the time.

08:13:47.553 --> 08:13:48.214
Let's get it.

08:13:48.194 --> 08:13:49.758
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

08:13:49.899 --> 08:13:51.583
I have a long roller from Skills.

08:13:51.844 --> 08:13:58.722
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

08:13:59.083 --> 08:14:04.498
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

08:14:04.478 --> 08:14:07.585
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

08:14:07.925 --> 08:14:16.785
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

08:14:17.105 --> 08:14:23.820
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

08:14:23.800 --> 08:14:30.291
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

08:14:30.691 --> 08:14:32.895
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

08:14:32.935 --> 08:14:34.357
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

08:14:34.698 --> 08:14:43.092
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

08:14:43.472 --> 08:14:50.584
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

08:14:50.564 --> 08:14:55.242
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

08:14:55.475 --> 08:14:57.237
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

08:14:57.537 --> 08:15:00.942
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

08:15:01.042 --> 08:15:09.252
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

08:15:09.312 --> 08:15:14.838
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

08:15:14.878 --> 08:15:18.062
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

08:15:18.102 --> 08:15:25.431
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

08:15:25.411 --> 08:15:31.701
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

08:15:32.422 --> 08:15:35.407
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

08:15:35.848 --> 08:15:41.097
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

08:15:41.557 --> 08:15:48.128
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

08:15:48.108 --> 08:15:49.852
Our next one's going to be for the adductors.

08:15:49.872 --> 08:15:50.995
That's the inside of the leg.

08:15:51.175 --> 08:15:52.939
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

08:15:53.240 --> 08:16:07.011
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

08:16:08.780 --> 08:16:15.587
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

08:16:15.787 --> 08:16:19.371
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

08:16:19.652 --> 08:16:22.915
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

08:16:23.456 --> 08:16:26.159
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

08:16:26.179 --> 08:16:27.860
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

08:16:28.161 --> 08:16:32.205
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

08:16:32.185 --> 08:16:35.113
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

08:16:35.674 --> 08:16:41.570
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day.

08:16:41.871 --> 08:16:43.435
We're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

08:16:43.776 --> 08:16:46.583
We got to really start to loosen that up.

08:16:47.963 --> 08:16:52.616
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

08:16:52.877 --> 08:16:56.387
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

08:16:56.407 --> 08:17:01.601
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

08:17:01.962 --> 08:17:03.627
Everything else is extra credit.

08:17:06.695 --> 08:17:10.442
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

08:17:10.722 --> 08:17:13.307
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

08:17:13.367 --> 08:17:17.355
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

08:17:17.836 --> 08:17:24.568
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running and vice versa on that other side.

08:17:24.548 --> 08:17:52.969
all right i'm staying stiff with the feet so i'm not letting my heel collapse i want to stay on the ball of the foot and i'm just going to go about 50 of my max speed i'm not going too fast yet we're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips for the seal jacks it's basically like a jumping jack except i'm going to coordinate arms with the legs so i'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time back to neutral over with the left over with the left hand and then i'm just going to speed that up

08:17:52.949 --> 08:17:55.152
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here.

08:17:55.773 --> 08:17:59.738
Maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed.

08:17:59.798 --> 08:18:00.799
So we're not all out.

08:18:01.260 --> 08:18:02.842
We're just getting that blood flowing.

08:18:03.142 --> 08:18:04.484
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

08:18:04.684 --> 08:18:09.010
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

08:18:09.330 --> 08:18:11.173
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

08:18:11.233 --> 08:18:15.959
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees.

08:18:16.219 --> 08:18:20.485
Just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

08:18:20.985 --> 08:18:22.247
Okay, so I'm going to get down.

08:18:22.227 --> 08:18:28.508
touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

08:18:32.150 --> 08:18:36.536
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

08:18:36.737 --> 08:18:42.065
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

08:18:42.345 --> 08:18:47.573
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

08:18:47.653 --> 08:18:48.895
I'm keeping that knee in.

08:18:49.315 --> 08:18:57.347
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

08:18:57.327 --> 08:19:19.183
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

08:19:20.108 --> 08:19:23.693
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

08:19:24.134 --> 08:19:28.600
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

08:19:28.660 --> 08:19:30.924
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

08:19:31.264 --> 08:19:33.287
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

08:19:33.307 --> 08:19:33.908
That's okay.

08:19:34.249 --> 08:19:35.851
Let's just get as low as we can there.

08:19:36.312 --> 08:19:37.273
One, two, three.

08:19:37.253 --> 08:19:38.996
three and back up.

08:19:39.036 --> 08:19:41.060
So three there, three there.

08:19:41.381 --> 08:19:47.673
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

08:19:47.774 --> 08:19:51.240
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

08:19:51.280 --> 08:19:55.228
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor.

08:19:55.208 --> 08:20:00.378
Keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

08:20:00.698 --> 08:20:07.631
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

08:20:08.092 --> 08:20:11.158
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom.

08:20:11.138 --> 08:20:32.691
and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

08:20:37.733 --> 08:20:44.262
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

08:20:44.282 --> 08:20:45.724
Don't make the mistake of going here.

08:20:46.104 --> 08:20:54.175
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

08:20:54.275 --> 08:21:00.143
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

08:21:00.223 --> 08:21:04.028
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

08:21:04.008 --> 08:21:05.812
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

08:21:06.073 --> 08:21:08.017
One, two, three.

08:21:08.177 --> 08:21:11.124
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

08:21:11.325 --> 08:21:13.530
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

08:21:14.171 --> 08:21:15.895
One, two, three.

08:21:15.955 --> 08:21:17.438
Rock forward.

08:21:17.519 --> 08:21:18.541
So I'm on my toes now.

08:21:19.062 --> 08:21:21.247
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

08:21:21.327 --> 08:21:21.628
Hold.

08:21:22.269 --> 08:21:22.590
One, two.

08:21:22.570 --> 08:21:23.993
Two, three.

08:21:24.334 --> 08:21:25.055
Rock it back.

08:21:25.416 --> 08:21:27.220
Heels back down to the ground.

08:21:27.480 --> 08:21:29.124
One, two, three.

08:21:29.204 --> 08:21:30.407
I need one hand up.

08:21:30.948 --> 08:21:31.750
Two hand up.

08:21:31.970 --> 08:21:32.672
Not out here.

08:21:32.712 --> 08:21:34.175
We need it back behind the head.

08:21:34.776 --> 08:21:35.417
And stand.

08:21:35.959 --> 08:21:36.660
All right?

08:21:36.680 --> 08:21:38.063
So that's one cycle.

08:21:38.304 --> 08:21:39.426
We're going to go for three of these.

08:21:40.388 --> 08:21:41.811
So it's one, two, three.

08:21:42.145 --> 08:21:44.408
One, two, three, lean.

08:21:45.189 --> 08:21:47.452
One, two, three, heels back down.

08:21:47.512 --> 08:21:51.537
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

08:21:51.817 --> 08:22:00.147
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

08:22:00.167 --> 08:22:04.533
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

08:22:04.893 --> 08:22:11.241
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

08:22:11.221 --> 08:22:14.044
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

08:22:14.104 --> 08:22:16.386
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

08:22:16.807 --> 08:22:19.589
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

08:22:19.930 --> 08:22:21.631
I need to be flat through the hips.

08:22:21.912 --> 08:22:24.314
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

08:22:24.834 --> 08:22:29.279
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

08:22:29.639 --> 08:22:32.682
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

08:22:32.702 --> 08:22:40.570
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

08:22:40.550 --> 08:22:50.488
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

08:22:50.929 --> 08:22:59.284
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

08:23:17.287 --> 08:23:21.155
So for my acceleration jumpstart, I'm gonna start high on the toes.

08:23:21.475 --> 08:23:23.178
I'm gonna start with my right hand up.

08:23:23.198 --> 08:23:29.391
So if I'm going with my right leg forward, I start with my right hand up and my left hand down, okay?

08:23:29.871 --> 08:23:36.865
I drop eccentrically, so I drop down as fast as I can and I land in this split stance, okay?

08:23:36.845 --> 08:23:44.953
As soon as I land in this split stance, my right arm is now cocked back, my left arm is now up, and then I jump as far as I can.

08:23:45.434 --> 08:23:48.657
So it's almost like a sprint, but in a sprint it would be more of a step.

08:23:49.157 --> 08:23:57.986
Right now we're exaggerating that because I want you to learn how to apply horizontal force into the ground and cover as much distance as we can.

08:23:58.166 --> 08:24:02.751
So what I like to think about with the arms is right hand up,

08:24:02.731 --> 08:24:13.864
Switch, I like to pretend like I have sand in my hand and I'm throwing that sand as far as I can because a lot of that force comes from this arm, okay?

08:24:14.184 --> 08:24:17.488
So I don't want you to relax and lightly bring this arm up.

08:24:17.808 --> 08:24:24.396
I want you to throw that imaginary sand as far as you can as we get our body as far as we can with that step.

08:24:24.836 --> 08:24:26.979
Now, make sure that you decelerate it.

08:24:27.119 --> 08:24:30.763
So I want you to take off from that right foot

08:24:30.743 --> 08:24:41.593
Get as far as you can, land right, stick left, hold for a second or two just to show yourself that you own that position and you have that deceleration, and then just walk it back.

08:24:42.133 --> 08:24:45.757
So complete all of the reps to one side, and then I would switch it over.

08:24:45.837 --> 08:24:52.062
So now my left hand is high, drop left foot is up, and we're going the opposite way.

08:24:52.102 --> 08:24:57.707
This is a very good drill to teach that first step, to teach you how to apply force horizontally.

08:24:57.947 --> 08:24:59.929
That's going to get that first step nice and explosive.

08:25:00.169 --> 08:25:00.750
Let's do it.

08:25:00.730 --> 08:25:00.866
Bye.

08:25:15.513 --> 08:25:26.505
So for my stutter Euro drill, what I'm gonna do is go baseline all the way to the opposite free throw line, or if you're out on a track or a field, mark out 70 feet.

08:25:26.646 --> 08:25:28.888
So what we're gonna do is three stutters.

08:25:29.209 --> 08:25:37.738
So it's gonna be right baby step, left baby step, right baby step, and I pop off diagonally as far as I can.

08:25:37.798 --> 08:25:41.242
I gotta put some force in the ground to cover that distance.

08:25:41.723 --> 08:25:42.904
So it's gonna be

08:25:42.884 --> 08:25:44.154
Right, left, right.

08:25:44.436 --> 08:25:45.161
Push for distance.

08:25:45.181 --> 08:25:46.048
Right, left, right.

08:25:46.713 --> 08:25:47.680
Right, left, right.

08:25:49.786 --> 08:25:52.889
So I'm gonna end up going diagonally down the court.

08:25:52.909 --> 08:25:59.717
So make sure that you start in the corner of one baseline and then we're gonna end up in that opposite corner, all right?

08:25:59.997 --> 08:26:03.001
Then on our next rep, we walk it back and we go on the other side.

08:26:03.461 --> 08:26:08.807
Now, if you're doing this in a small space and you don't have that distance, I want you to go six each side.

08:26:09.268 --> 08:26:10.269
So right, left, right.

08:26:11.010 --> 08:26:11.630
That's one rep.

08:26:11.991 --> 08:26:12.551
Turn it around.

08:26:13.252 --> 08:26:14.173
Right, left, right.

08:26:15.217 --> 08:26:15.878
turn it around.

08:26:16.198 --> 08:26:17.220
So that would be two reps.

08:26:17.300 --> 08:26:19.182
We go six, and then we go the other way.

08:26:19.743 --> 08:26:22.126
Left, right, left, push.

08:26:22.146 --> 08:26:32.740
The footwork is very similar to a normal Euro step, except instead of just a step, I want you to exaggerate and actually cover as much distance as we can.

08:26:33.581 --> 08:26:34.322
Let's get it.

08:26:50.367 --> 08:27:00.530
For my single leg skip bounds, what I'm gonna do is essentially get as high as I can off one leg, I'm gonna land with that same leg, and then I go into a diagonal bound.

08:27:00.550 --> 08:27:04.559
So it's almost like a skip and a diagonal bound at the same time.

08:27:04.880 --> 08:27:08.207
So what I'm doing is up, land, out,

08:27:08.187 --> 08:27:09.950
up, land, out.

08:27:10.410 --> 08:27:15.598
Of course, now I got to get rhythmic and I got to get fluid and build some speed with this exercise.

08:27:15.959 --> 08:27:21.507
So if we have a court, we are going from baseline to the opposite free throw line.

08:27:21.748 --> 08:27:24.532
If you're out on a field, mark off about 70 feet.

08:27:25.013 --> 08:27:27.617
Now, if you don't have the space, it's okay.

08:27:27.637 --> 08:27:29.740
You can always just do here,

08:27:30.226 --> 08:27:33.312
here, and then just walk it back and do it again.

08:27:33.652 --> 08:27:38.601
If you don't have the space, what we're gonna do is six repetitions per set.

08:27:39.122 --> 08:27:40.445
So that means six each leg.

08:27:40.485 --> 08:27:47.357
So there and there equals one, and then we can turn it around and bring it back, all right?

08:27:47.337 --> 08:27:51.222
So six each leg, 12 total single leg jumps.

08:27:51.582 --> 08:27:54.445
Keys here is reactivity off the ground.

08:27:54.465 --> 08:27:56.628
I don't want you to slowly lower.

08:27:56.948 --> 08:28:00.773
I don't want this to be more of a power or a force exercise.

08:28:01.113 --> 08:28:05.458
I want this to be springy and I want it to be elastic off the floor.

08:28:05.839 --> 08:28:09.763
Now, when you go into that diagonal, don't start to fade on the jump.

08:28:09.803 --> 08:28:14.509
I need you to work on getting straight up, straight down, and then pushing into that next rep.

08:28:17.560 --> 08:28:35.279
Now for our freestyle dunks, I put this in there because it's so important that you get out there and just allow your body to explore different approaches, different type of dunks, different type of jumping styles without me always trying to put you in a box and trying to get you to do one thing.

08:28:35.619 --> 08:28:41.205
So I want you to set the clock for 10 minutes and I just want you to go get as many dunks as you can.

08:28:41.185 --> 08:28:43.129
Take breaks in between if you need to.

08:28:43.169 --> 08:28:45.714
I do want you to be recovered in between reps.

08:28:45.734 --> 08:28:47.778
This isn't an endurance workout.

08:28:48.138 --> 08:28:49.481
So get up off one foot.

08:28:49.521 --> 08:28:50.342
Throw it down.

08:28:50.803 --> 08:28:51.404
Walk back.

08:28:51.425 --> 08:28:53.569
Recover for 30 seconds to a minute.

08:28:53.889 --> 08:28:54.530
Come back in.

08:28:54.591 --> 08:28:55.813
Take off off two.

08:28:56.234 --> 08:28:57.456
Try to dunk left.

08:28:57.436 --> 08:29:00.500
Whatever you want to do, get creative and do it.

08:29:00.781 --> 08:29:04.806
Of course, if you can't dunk and all you can do is touch the bottom of the net, I don't care.

08:29:04.846 --> 08:29:06.108
I still want you to get creative.

08:29:06.469 --> 08:29:07.951
Come in off two and touch the net.

08:29:08.311 --> 08:29:10.915
Come from baseline, get up off one and touch the net.

08:29:11.235 --> 08:29:13.639
Try to jump off left, touch with the left.

08:29:14.640 --> 08:29:23.713
Variability, variety is the key to longevity and it's super important for building kind of well-rounded explosiveness and athleticism.

08:29:23.693 --> 08:29:28.489
And so I want you to get creative, set the clock for 10 minutes, and just freestyle dunk.

08:29:59.772 --> 08:30:04.798
So for my triple contrast cluster sets, what I'm doing is three different exercises.

08:30:05.078 --> 08:30:08.322
I'm going heavy load, moderate load, light load.

08:30:08.502 --> 08:30:10.084
All right, you're going to need a stopwatch.

08:30:10.104 --> 08:30:14.048
So what we're going to do is we're going to start with a barbell deadlift.

08:30:14.068 --> 08:30:15.630
I do want you to go heavy here.

08:30:15.750 --> 08:30:17.312
So if you have a trap bar, use that.

08:30:17.572 --> 08:30:21.897
If you don't and you just have a barbell, use the barbell, go alternating grip.

08:30:21.917 --> 08:30:22.658
We've covered

08:30:22.638 --> 08:30:37.836
the deadlift exercise in the past, so refer back to the technique on that if you're not familiar with it, or if you're still not comfortable with doing heavy deadlifts, make sure you reach out to a personal trainer or somebody that can correct your form.

08:30:37.896 --> 08:30:39.921
So of course, explode up.

08:30:39.901 --> 08:30:43.929
all the way to full hip extension, squeeze the glutes to meet the bar.

08:30:43.969 --> 08:30:48.397
So squeeze, meet that bar, and then lower back down, all right?

08:30:48.417 --> 08:30:52.285
We're not lowering down with the RDL like we have done in past phases.

08:30:52.665 --> 08:30:56.212
So in past phases, we've lowered it with an RDL to the floor.

08:30:56.593 --> 08:31:00.981
Right now, we're just reversing it the same way that we brought it up, all right?

08:31:00.961 --> 08:31:03.123
All we're doing is two repetitions.

08:31:03.384 --> 08:31:06.247
So we hit two repetitions with a heavy weight.

08:31:06.367 --> 08:31:11.032
Think about maybe 90% of your one rep max.

08:31:11.052 --> 08:31:12.013
30 seconds off.

08:31:12.273 --> 08:31:15.296
So as soon as you're done with that two reps, set the clock 30 seconds.

08:31:15.637 --> 08:31:18.860
Once that 30 seconds is up, we're in with a high toss.

08:31:19.261 --> 08:31:21.163
I'm using a 50 pound sandbag.

08:31:22.004 --> 08:31:23.085
This is from Hyperware.

08:31:23.145 --> 08:31:24.186
These are kind of expensive.

08:31:24.546 --> 08:31:26.108
You can just use a med ball.

08:31:26.088 --> 08:31:31.696
So if you have a med ball and a high ceiling, use the heaviest med ball that you got all the way up to 50 pounds.

08:31:31.716 --> 08:31:34.601
So even if it's only 15, that's okay.

08:31:35.141 --> 08:31:37.505
Anywhere from 15 to 50 pounds.

08:31:37.865 --> 08:31:41.811
Now, if you don't have these, then you're just going to do a normal dumbbell jump.

08:31:41.891 --> 08:31:48.581
So if you have this, you're going to start in that same position that we started in for our deadlift.

08:31:48.601 --> 08:31:52.708
So I'm just going to explode as hard as I can and toss it as high as I can.

08:31:52.788 --> 08:31:54.390
So it's boom.

08:31:54.978 --> 08:31:58.372
toss, let it fall, step back over it, and go again.

08:31:58.713 --> 08:32:02.288
If you don't have that or a med ball to throw overhead,

08:32:02.842 --> 08:32:04.944
then you're simply gonna do a dumbbell jump.

08:32:04.984 --> 08:32:16.337
So I'm using a 45 pounder right now, and we're just gonna go here, same position, deadlift start, and then explode as high as we can, bring it right back down, all right?

08:32:16.957 --> 08:32:20.061
Two repetitions, set the clock for 30 seconds.

08:32:20.641 --> 08:32:23.584
After that 30 seconds is up, we come to a box jump.

08:32:23.905 --> 08:32:27.769
If you don't have the box, do a regular jump and land on the floor.

08:32:27.749 --> 08:32:30.575
Only thing is I want you to focus on that eccentric speed.

08:32:30.975 --> 08:32:40.194
So start high, all the way up on the toes, hands above the head, drop as fast as you can like you're attacking the floor, and then reverse it, get up as high as you can.

08:32:40.534 --> 08:32:47.428
So it's here, attack the floor, get up, land nice and soft, and step back down.

08:32:47.611 --> 08:32:48.652
Two repetitions.

08:32:49.513 --> 08:32:53.176
Now once we're done with that, we take a full minute and a half off.

08:32:53.596 --> 08:32:54.777
That completes one set.

08:32:55.078 --> 08:32:57.700
So keep in mind this is a triple contrast.

08:32:57.720 --> 08:33:03.225
So we're going extremely heavy, and then we're going moderate, and then we're going unresisted.

08:33:03.285 --> 08:33:10.411
So we're really building up that potentiation, and by the time we get to that box jump, we should be able to get really, really high.

08:33:10.851 --> 08:33:16.116
Obviously only two reps is very low rep, so it's high intensity, it's max effort,

08:33:16.096 --> 08:33:19.660
and then we get that 30-second recovery and we go into that next one.

08:33:20.501 --> 08:33:23.644
So it's not something where we're going to feel a big burn in the legs.

08:33:24.065 --> 08:33:27.308
When people do circuit training, they assume that they should be feeling a burn.

08:33:27.729 --> 08:33:30.051
We do not want that for max explosiveness.

08:33:30.111 --> 08:33:46.069
So I just want you to stay fresh throughout the entire, whether it's three, four, or five sets, I want you to stay fresh, but I want you to feel neurally activated, like your brain and your nervous system are just firing those muscles and you're getting that explosiveness to the next level.

08:33:46.049 --> 08:33:46.550
Let's get it.

08:34:00.869 --> 08:34:05.595
Okay, so if you'd like to do rear foot elevated deficit instead of the leg press, that's completely fine.

08:34:05.916 --> 08:34:15.769
We could do these with heavy dumbbells elevated on a bumper plate, or I love to do rear foot elevated split squats on a Smith machine if you have one.

08:34:15.749 --> 08:34:23.661
The reason I like the Smith machine is because we can get max intent, max explosiveness without worrying about the stability of being on one leg.

08:34:23.921 --> 08:34:25.964
So this is way better for explosiveness.

08:34:26.125 --> 08:34:29.430
So what I'm going to do, set up my bench behind here.

08:34:29.470 --> 08:34:31.733
I know most of you guys don't have the rear foot elevated stand.

08:34:31.773 --> 08:34:32.354
That's okay.

08:34:32.755 --> 08:34:39.204
Set up your bench, get your bumper plate, and then I'm going to come down to one knee and I'm going to adjust.

08:34:39.385 --> 08:34:39.545
Okay.

08:34:39.885 --> 08:34:43.090
So I'm going to adjust so that my ankle is directly underneath the knee.

08:34:43.451 --> 08:34:44.372
Now from here,

08:34:45.635 --> 08:34:46.376
I can stand.

08:34:48.099 --> 08:34:48.840
I can lift off.

08:34:50.423 --> 08:34:56.193
Now I'm coming all the way down, full range of motion until that knee touches and explode.

08:34:56.213 --> 08:35:03.945
Now you do have to have good hip and decent ankle mobility here, so you might not be able to do it elevated on the bumper plate.

08:35:03.965 --> 08:35:05.508
If that's the case, that's okay.

08:35:05.909 --> 08:35:12.199
I'm using it so that I can get a bigger range of motion and train the muscle over a longer range of motion.

08:35:12.179 --> 08:35:17.393
So it's all the way down, control once that knee taps, explode up as fast as we can.

08:35:17.795 --> 08:35:21.505
So we're getting explosive over that big range of motion.

08:35:21.826 --> 08:35:22.628
Let's get it.

08:35:44.258 --> 08:35:49.523
So for my ball of the foot, one leg turns, I'm going to come to the ball of my foot.

08:35:49.583 --> 08:35:51.505
I cannot have my heel down on the ground.

08:35:51.885 --> 08:35:56.029
So we're decreasing that surface area, increasing the stability needs.

08:35:56.410 --> 08:35:59.733
So I'm here pointing that big foot down into the ground.

08:35:59.913 --> 08:36:01.715
First, just find your balance.

08:36:01.795 --> 08:36:05.538
Now, if you find it there, we're coming into an RDL.

08:36:05.959 --> 08:36:08.101
Try to get the torso flat to the ground.

08:36:08.441 --> 08:36:10.283
And now from here, I'm just doing turns.

08:36:10.263 --> 08:36:15.168
All right, as big as I can back to neutral and then to the other side.

08:36:15.549 --> 08:36:27.082
Now those turns might be very small like this to start, but as you get better, I want you to do full turns, full rotations up as high as we can.

08:36:27.142 --> 08:36:37.333
All right, you're going to feel the feet completely burning from the toes all the way back up through the heels, back up through the calves, lateral aspects of the calves.

08:36:37.313 --> 08:36:43.746
The lower leg and the feet are going to be firing the outside muscles and those small intrinsic muscles.

08:37:08.228 --> 08:37:12.273
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

08:37:12.393 --> 08:37:14.195
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

08:37:14.235 --> 08:37:16.939
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

08:37:16.979 --> 08:37:23.487
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

08:37:24.008 --> 08:37:29.955
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

08:37:30.235 --> 08:37:34.000
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

08:37:33.980 --> 08:37:37.866
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

08:37:38.307 --> 08:37:42.133
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

08:37:42.714 --> 08:37:50.065
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

08:37:51.057 --> 08:37:53.760
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

08:37:54.140 --> 08:38:00.847
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

08:38:01.407 --> 08:38:07.193
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

08:38:07.213 --> 08:38:10.856
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

08:38:13.179 --> 08:38:17.503
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

08:38:20.403 --> 08:38:22.325
and that stomach will fall, okay?

08:38:23.026 --> 08:38:25.670
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

08:38:26.431 --> 08:38:35.462
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

08:38:36.243 --> 08:38:44.013
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

08:38:44.253 --> 08:38:48.098
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

08:38:48.432 --> 08:39:17.173
this is doing a lot of stuff i mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay i got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

08:39:17.423 --> 08:39:19.927
happens in the parasympathetic state.

08:39:20.467 --> 08:39:24.934
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

08:39:24.954 --> 08:39:38.554
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

08:39:40.407 --> 08:39:43.692
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

08:39:43.732 --> 08:39:47.998
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

08:39:48.278 --> 08:39:55.088
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

08:39:55.389 --> 08:39:58.874
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

08:39:59.154 --> 08:40:06.685
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

08:40:06.665 --> 08:40:10.992
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

08:40:11.413 --> 08:40:13.396
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

08:40:13.416 --> 08:40:15.920
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

08:40:16.200 --> 08:40:18.264
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

08:40:18.544 --> 08:40:20.768
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

08:40:21.068 --> 08:40:22.851
I'm just gonna lean forward.

08:40:22.831 --> 08:40:43.641
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

08:40:43.942 --> 08:40:45.905
15 seconds on the other side as well.

08:40:45.925 --> 08:40:48.047
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

08:40:48.248 --> 08:40:49.649
So that is the back of the thigh.

08:40:49.910 --> 08:40:54.215
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

08:40:54.235 --> 08:40:57.920
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

08:40:58.080 --> 08:41:00.503
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

08:41:00.844 --> 08:41:03.167
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

08:41:03.527 --> 08:41:05.089
So now look at this back foot.

08:41:05.109 --> 08:41:06.511
I'm not curling the toe under.

08:41:06.912 --> 08:41:11.097
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

08:41:11.077 --> 08:41:14.422
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

08:41:14.843 --> 08:41:20.191
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

08:41:20.512 --> 08:41:33.793
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

08:41:33.773 --> 08:41:37.018
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

08:41:37.078 --> 08:41:38.401
That's the inside of the leg.

08:41:38.881 --> 08:41:42.267
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

08:41:42.848 --> 08:41:43.569
Same thing.

08:41:43.890 --> 08:41:46.774
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

08:41:47.075 --> 08:41:50.200
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

08:41:50.481 --> 08:41:54.387
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

08:41:54.667 --> 08:41:59.275
I'm not gonna lift off and I'm just gonna lay my head back and just pull

08:41:59.255 --> 08:42:18.799
this knee as far towards the chest as i can now a lot of you as you pull the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors if that's the case try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here

08:42:18.779 --> 08:42:26.656
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

08:42:27.157 --> 08:42:29.963
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

08:42:30.404 --> 08:42:36.417
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

08:42:36.397 --> 08:42:39.461
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

08:42:39.542 --> 08:42:41.685
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

08:42:41.945 --> 08:42:45.610
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

08:42:46.071 --> 08:42:47.653
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

08:42:48.034 --> 08:42:50.578
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

08:42:50.658 --> 08:42:51.559
So I'm 90 there.

08:42:51.920 --> 08:42:54.704
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

08:42:55.064 --> 08:42:57.368
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

08:42:57.728 --> 08:43:02.415
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

08:43:02.775 --> 08:43:05.880
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

08:43:05.860 --> 08:43:08.786
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

08:43:09.147 --> 08:43:13.937
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

08:43:13.957 --> 08:43:15.401
All right.

08:43:15.421 --> 08:43:21.313
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

08:43:22.055 --> 08:43:25.843
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

08:43:25.823 --> 08:43:31.173
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

08:43:31.553 --> 08:43:34.779
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

08:43:34.879 --> 08:43:38.045
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

08:43:38.446 --> 08:43:48.203
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

08:43:48.436 --> 08:43:54.943
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

08:43:55.223 --> 08:44:01.369
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

08:44:01.769 --> 08:44:05.853
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

08:44:07.015 --> 08:44:10.818
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

08:44:11.199 --> 08:44:14.382
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

08:44:14.862 --> 08:44:18.426
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

08:44:21.190 --> 08:44:21.951
What's up guys?

08:44:22.152 --> 08:44:26.500
Quick minimum dose advice for phase seven, day one and day four.

08:44:26.780 --> 08:44:29.786
So as you can tell, we actually switch our workout days.

08:44:30.186 --> 08:44:34.855
Starting in phase seven, we're actually going four days a week of lower body.

08:44:34.875 --> 08:44:36.758
We're all about progressive overload.

08:44:36.819 --> 08:44:38.101
That's how we get better.

08:44:38.141 --> 08:44:39.283
That's how we get gains.

08:44:39.263 --> 08:44:44.690
and through the last six months we progressively overloaded volume and or intensity.

08:44:45.171 --> 08:44:46.913
Now we're overloading frequency.

08:44:46.953 --> 08:44:50.417
So we're going from three lower body days to four lower body days.

08:44:50.878 --> 08:44:56.725
Now the myth that you need 48 hours to recover the muscle is not true.

08:44:57.186 --> 08:44:59.008
It is all dose dependent.

08:44:59.048 --> 08:45:06.638
So basically that means if we really beat down the lower body and we do a lot of sets per muscle group, then it's true we're gonna need

08:45:06.618 --> 08:45:09.506
at least 48 hours to recover that muscle.

08:45:09.907 --> 08:45:17.067
But if we do shorter, more efficient workouts, then it's only gonna take around 24 hours to recover and be ready to go.

08:45:17.408 --> 08:45:21.640
So you're gonna notice that our workouts are very, very short and efficient.

08:45:21.620 --> 08:45:25.528
You should be able to get these workouts done in probably 45 minutes.

08:45:25.568 --> 08:45:29.155
This plyo emphasis day, we're in and we're out.

08:45:29.535 --> 08:45:37.671
I know at the end of the workout you're going to feel like you should be doing more because you didn't get the burn that you want to, but that's exactly how it should be.

08:45:37.651 --> 08:45:42.558
And if you keep going, then you're not going to be fully recovered for tomorrow's workout.

08:45:42.958 --> 08:45:44.640
So we want just enough.

08:45:44.901 --> 08:45:45.782
Think about it like this.

08:45:46.062 --> 08:45:49.627
We don't want to chug a whole bottle of Advil to get a headache better.

08:45:49.908 --> 08:45:51.770
We want one or two at a time.

08:45:51.810 --> 08:45:54.734
So we want small doses more frequently.

08:45:55.054 --> 08:45:57.418
So that is what we're going for in this phase.

08:45:57.898 --> 08:46:02.985
That being said, we do not want to skip anything on day one or day four.

08:46:03.366 --> 08:46:04.748
Do the program as is.

08:46:05.108 --> 08:46:05.869
Let's get it.

08:46:05.849 --> 08:46:07.432
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

08:46:07.573 --> 08:46:09.256
I have a long roller from Skills.

08:46:09.537 --> 08:46:16.411
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

08:46:16.772 --> 08:46:22.103
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

08:46:22.083 --> 08:46:25.267
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

08:46:25.607 --> 08:46:34.759
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

08:46:34.779 --> 08:46:41.187
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

08:46:41.908 --> 08:46:48.276
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

08:46:48.256 --> 08:46:50.600
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

08:46:50.620 --> 08:46:52.022
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

08:46:52.362 --> 08:47:00.775
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

08:47:01.135 --> 08:47:12.913
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

08:47:13.147 --> 08:47:14.929
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

08:47:15.229 --> 08:47:18.633
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

08:47:18.733 --> 08:47:26.942
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

08:47:27.002 --> 08:47:32.528
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

08:47:32.608 --> 08:47:35.772
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

08:47:35.792 --> 08:47:43.120
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

08:47:43.100 --> 08:47:49.391
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

08:47:50.112 --> 08:47:53.098
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

08:47:53.538 --> 08:47:58.788
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

08:47:59.248 --> 08:48:05.800
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

08:48:05.780 --> 08:48:08.686
Our next one is going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

08:48:08.866 --> 08:48:10.609
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

08:48:10.930 --> 08:48:24.717
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

08:48:26.468 --> 08:48:33.276
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

08:48:33.477 --> 08:48:37.041
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

08:48:37.341 --> 08:48:40.586
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

08:48:41.146 --> 08:48:43.830
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

08:48:43.850 --> 08:48:45.552
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

08:48:45.852 --> 08:48:49.877
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

08:48:49.857 --> 08:49:04.356
and i'm going to really loosen up that fascia a lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day we're moving we're cutting we're jumping we got to really start to loosen that up

08:49:05.652 --> 08:49:10.305
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

08:49:10.566 --> 08:49:14.075
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

08:49:14.095 --> 08:49:19.289
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

08:49:19.650 --> 08:49:21.315
Everything else is extra credit.

08:49:24.383 --> 08:49:28.130
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

08:49:28.410 --> 08:49:30.995
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

08:49:31.055 --> 08:49:35.043
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

08:49:35.523 --> 08:49:42.116
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

08:49:42.476 --> 08:49:46.163
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

08:49:46.183 --> 08:49:47.826
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

08:49:47.806 --> 08:49:51.071
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

08:49:51.411 --> 08:49:52.893
I'm not going too fast yet.

08:49:53.194 --> 08:49:55.337
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

08:49:55.577 --> 08:50:00.124
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

08:50:00.144 --> 08:50:04.310
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

08:50:04.731 --> 08:50:08.296
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

08:50:08.596 --> 08:50:10.820
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

08:50:10.840 --> 08:50:13.223
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

08:50:13.203 --> 08:50:39.919
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch I'm just gonna go over with the left leg I'm gonna come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back okay so I'm gonna get down

08:50:39.899 --> 08:50:46.180
touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

08:50:49.855 --> 08:50:54.222
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

08:50:54.422 --> 08:50:59.751
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

08:51:00.032 --> 08:51:06.582
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

08:51:07.023 --> 08:51:15.036
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

08:51:15.016 --> 08:51:19.945
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

08:51:20.305 --> 08:51:25.895
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

08:51:26.577 --> 08:51:28.941
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

08:51:29.261 --> 08:51:33.890
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

08:51:34.531 --> 08:51:36.855
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

08:51:37.763 --> 08:51:41.410
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

08:51:41.831 --> 08:51:44.215
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

08:51:44.275 --> 08:51:46.279
One, two, three.

08:51:46.339 --> 08:51:48.624
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

08:51:48.944 --> 08:51:50.968
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

08:51:51.008 --> 08:51:51.610
That's okay.

08:51:51.930 --> 08:51:53.513
Let's just get as low as we can there.

08:51:53.994 --> 08:51:54.996
One, two, three.

08:51:54.976 --> 08:51:56.679
three, and back up.

08:51:56.739 --> 08:51:58.742
So three there, three there.

08:51:59.062 --> 08:52:05.352
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

08:52:05.452 --> 08:52:08.917
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

08:52:08.957 --> 08:52:12.643
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor.

08:52:13.103 --> 08:52:18.071
Keep this knee straight because I wanna get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

08:52:18.411 --> 08:52:21.456
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat.

08:52:21.436 --> 08:52:25.281
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

08:52:25.741 --> 08:52:29.746
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

08:52:30.086 --> 08:52:35.633
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

08:52:36.233 --> 08:52:50.430
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

08:52:55.405 --> 08:53:01.931
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

08:53:01.951 --> 08:53:03.413
Don't make the mistake of going here.

08:53:03.793 --> 08:53:11.861
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

08:53:11.961 --> 08:53:17.826
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

08:53:17.906 --> 08:53:21.750
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

08:53:21.730 --> 08:53:23.493
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

08:53:23.753 --> 08:53:25.697
One, two, three.

08:53:25.857 --> 08:53:28.842
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

08:53:29.003 --> 08:53:31.206
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

08:53:31.847 --> 08:53:33.570
One, two, three.

08:53:33.631 --> 08:53:35.113
Rock forward.

08:53:35.153 --> 08:53:36.255
So I'm on my toes now.

08:53:36.736 --> 08:53:38.920
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

08:53:39.000 --> 08:53:39.320
Hold.

08:53:39.941 --> 08:53:40.903
One, two, three.

08:53:41.305 --> 08:53:41.686
Three.

08:53:42.006 --> 08:53:42.708
Rock it back.

08:53:43.088 --> 08:53:44.872
Heels back down to the ground.

08:53:45.172 --> 08:53:46.776
One, two, three.

08:53:46.876 --> 08:53:48.078
I need one hand up.

08:53:48.639 --> 08:53:49.421
Two hand up.

08:53:49.661 --> 08:53:50.322
Not out here.

08:53:50.403 --> 08:53:51.865
We need it back behind the head.

08:53:52.446 --> 08:53:53.088
And stand.

08:53:53.649 --> 08:53:53.889
All right.

08:53:54.370 --> 08:53:55.733
So that's one cycle.

08:53:55.993 --> 08:53:57.095
We're going to go for three of these.

08:53:58.057 --> 08:53:59.500
So it's one, two, three.

08:53:59.817 --> 08:54:02.080
One, two, three, lean.

08:54:02.880 --> 08:54:05.143
One, two, three, heels back down.

08:54:05.203 --> 08:54:09.228
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

08:54:09.508 --> 08:54:17.837
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

08:54:17.857 --> 08:54:22.222
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

08:54:22.583 --> 08:54:28.930
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

08:54:28.910 --> 08:54:31.714
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

08:54:31.774 --> 08:54:34.097
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

08:54:34.497 --> 08:54:37.261
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

08:54:37.601 --> 08:54:39.303
I need to be flat through the hips.

08:54:39.604 --> 08:54:42.007
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

08:54:42.528 --> 08:54:46.973
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

08:54:47.334 --> 08:54:50.138
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

08:54:50.398 --> 08:54:58.208
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

08:54:58.188 --> 08:55:08.183
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

08:55:08.604 --> 08:55:16.956
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

08:55:22.994 --> 08:55:32.566
So for my lateral med ball toss, I'm basically going to push out laterally, and I'm going to change directions as fast as I can, push through the med ball, kind of like a shot put.

08:55:33.026 --> 08:55:44.040
Boom, you got your guide hand and your follow through hand, so almost like a basketball shot, except it's going to be horizontal, it's going to be with a lot more power, and this is really driven from that lower body.

08:55:44.273 --> 08:55:45.214
So it's going to be here.

08:55:45.936 --> 08:55:46.276
Boom.

08:55:46.817 --> 08:55:47.398
Transition.

08:55:47.839 --> 08:55:48.880
Push as hard as I can.

08:55:49.361 --> 08:55:52.406
Notice I didn't stay here and just turn the upper body.

08:55:52.826 --> 08:55:55.591
I pushed off and I fully turned my body.

08:55:55.891 --> 08:55:59.216
I'm getting that good rotational power and that good lateral power.

08:55:59.617 --> 08:56:04.164
A lot of times when we're playing defense and defender goes by us, it's a rotational push.

08:56:04.444 --> 08:56:05.626
It's not always lateral.

08:56:05.886 --> 08:56:06.908
It's not always linear.

08:56:07.249 --> 08:56:08.410
It's rotational.

08:56:08.671 --> 08:56:12.036
So we're working on that same thing here.

08:56:12.016 --> 08:56:27.561
remember i want you to transition out quick so it's got to be here and then immediately push out so it's bone drive out toss as hard as you can pick it up and reset so if you do not have the med ball then let's just do regular lateral bounds so it's here

08:56:27.541 --> 08:56:44.233
push high as we can as far as we can with short ground contact time that's still going to get us the same qualities as far as that lateral power we're just missing out on the rotational power but that's okay i know a lot of you guys don't have that med ball so if that's the case that's a good alternative let's get it

08:56:52.853 --> 08:56:59.981
All right, so now on this combo exercise, if you have the wall and you have a court or somewhere to sprint off, then we're gonna do the full drill.

08:57:00.281 --> 08:57:02.363
If not, it's okay, we're just gonna do half of the drill.

08:57:02.403 --> 08:57:06.568
So if you have the wall and the space, make sure the wall is concrete, it's not gonna break.

08:57:06.788 --> 08:57:10.912
So it's gonna be starting in a split stance, it's gonna be drive, so I'm pushing off both feet.

08:57:11.233 --> 08:57:21.243
I'm really gonna drive through this front foot, like it's first step explosiveness, push the ball into the wall as hard as I can, and then I'm gonna decelerate, push laterally, open up and take off.

08:57:21.744 --> 08:57:22.725
So it's gonna be here,

08:57:22.705 --> 08:57:26.387
Boom, boom, push out.

08:57:27.262 --> 08:57:29.044
So sprint out as far as you can.

08:57:29.105 --> 08:57:31.808
So if you have half court, sprint it through half court.

08:57:32.269 --> 08:57:36.935
A lot of you guys don't have that distance, so it's boom, boom, maybe sprint it out a couple steps.

08:57:37.436 --> 08:57:39.819
What matters most is that change of direction.

08:57:39.919 --> 08:57:48.111
So we want boom, sharp change of direction, toe is still pointed forward, sharp change of direction, rotationally, and get out of there.

08:57:48.131 --> 08:57:49.853
All right, I want sharp cuts.

08:57:49.873 --> 08:57:57.083
If you want to set out a cone, you can so that you have that accountability to get out over the cone and then open up and push out.

08:57:57.063 --> 08:57:59.569
Now if you don't have the med ball, pretend like you do.

08:57:59.629 --> 08:58:07.748
So it's going to be here, pretend like you got that med ball, boom, boom, open up, and push out, sprint as far as you can.

08:58:16.958 --> 08:58:25.293
So for my bound sprints, what I'm going to do, if you have a court and a half, then we're going to go a full court and a half with the bound sprint.

08:58:25.574 --> 08:58:29.100
We're going to bound six times, three each leg, get as far as we can.

08:58:29.441 --> 08:58:33.789
And then after that six, one, boom, hit the ground running, sprint it through a court and a half.

08:58:34.149 --> 08:58:36.834
Now I know a lot of you guys don't have two courts set up this way.

08:58:37.175 --> 08:58:39.178
So what you're going to do is just 54 meters.

08:58:39.218 --> 08:58:40.080
So you could

08:58:40.060 --> 08:58:45.190
Mark it out on a track or on a field or anywhere where you have 54 meters.

08:58:45.611 --> 08:58:47.574
Look, if you don't have that space, it's okay.

08:58:47.875 --> 08:58:55.369
Let's just do the bounds to half court or six times, which will get you around half court and then sprint it through the other half court, all right?

08:58:55.349 --> 08:58:57.072
So work with what you've got.

08:58:57.233 --> 08:59:02.984
But if you have the room, I'm going to start in a split stance and I'm getting out as far as I can.

08:59:03.424 --> 08:59:08.254
Of course, on my bounds, I do want to get some height with it and I got to get off the ground quick, quick, quick.

08:59:08.334 --> 08:59:14.826
So it's going to be boom, one, two, three, four, five, six,

08:59:14.806 --> 08:59:18.975
sprint it out as fast as we can through the rest of that 54 meters.

08:59:19.296 --> 08:59:28.797
Main goals of this exercise is to get as far as we can with the bounds and then to get from point A to point B as fast as we possibly can.

08:59:28.837 --> 08:59:30.560
So time yourself every session.

08:59:30.621 --> 08:59:32.224
I want you to beat that record.

08:59:40.709 --> 08:59:44.313
So for my one leg depth jump, I'm gonna go on a box.

08:59:44.333 --> 08:59:48.638
Now I wanna start with a box that's pretty low, so somewhere around mid-shin.

08:59:49.258 --> 08:59:51.481
As we get better, we can go on higher boxes.

08:59:51.541 --> 08:59:55.064
So I want box number one, and then I want box number two.

08:59:55.125 --> 08:59:59.229
Box number two is basically as high as we can jump and land in a good safe position.

08:59:59.349 --> 09:00:02.332
So if you don't have two boxes, that's okay.

09:00:02.352 --> 09:00:05.816
We can just jump and land on the floor without that second box.

09:00:05.896 --> 09:00:07.678
But what we're gonna do

09:00:07.658 --> 09:00:22.073
is step up just like a normal depth jump we're just dropping off we're not jumping off so i'm just going to drop off and i'm nice and reactive and quick off the ground now just with one leg and i jump off right and i land on right so it's just a step

09:00:23.065 --> 09:00:27.570
jump, land, stick that landing, and then step it back down.

09:00:27.790 --> 09:00:27.990
All right.

09:00:28.471 --> 09:00:32.215
I got to be springy off the ground, especially on one leg.

09:00:32.595 --> 09:00:38.842
If you really get low and you sink down into those joints, you're not going to be able to reverse it and get back out of it.

09:00:39.062 --> 09:00:44.468
So you really got to come down, hit that floor and get up nice and springy, nice and quick off the ground.

09:00:44.788 --> 09:00:48.752
Now, the way that we progress this exercise is we can just go a higher box.

09:00:48.732 --> 09:00:50.034
that we're dropping off.

09:00:50.094 --> 09:00:53.941
So I can get all the way up until that box is around knee height.

09:00:54.302 --> 09:00:59.070
I wouldn't go too much higher than knee height because it's just going to be too much stress on the landing.

09:00:59.270 --> 09:01:05.281
But the higher the box we drop off, the more eccentric force we have to absorb and redirect.

09:01:05.541 --> 09:01:10.590
So this drill is great for building that elasticity and that reactiveness off the ground.

09:01:10.931 --> 09:01:11.652
Let's get it.

09:01:31.004 --> 09:01:35.311
So for my contrast split stance jumps, what I'm gonna do is hold some light dumbbells.

09:01:35.351 --> 09:01:39.057
So I like to go with around 15 pounds and I'm just going in a split stance.

09:01:39.577 --> 09:01:42.041
I don't want a big exaggerated lunge stance.

09:01:42.061 --> 09:01:43.704
You can't be explosive from that stance.

09:01:43.744 --> 09:01:46.048
So it's a baby split right here.

09:01:46.088 --> 09:01:49.593
We're gonna jump as high as I can and I'm landing on the other side.

09:01:50.034 --> 09:01:51.035
Now I'm gonna go rapid.

09:01:51.115 --> 09:01:55.182
So I gotta get fast off the ground, reactive off the ground, get as high as I can.

09:01:55.282 --> 09:01:57.906
So it's just boom, boom.

09:01:57.886 --> 09:02:23.353
boom all right now pay attention to the rep ranges if it says five reps i'm hitting five reps each side and then i immediately go in to my assisted jumps so you want to use a band that you feel like it gives you an extra like 10 to 20 percent on your jump height we don't want so much band tension that you're flying out the roof we also don't want to set up right under a power rack because we're going to hit our head so

09:02:23.333 --> 09:02:29.380
If you're like at LA Fitness and you were in a basketball court, you could throw it over the bar behind the hoop.

09:02:29.400 --> 09:02:33.906
Or if you have a power rack, throw that band over the rack and then step back.

09:02:34.146 --> 09:02:38.712
So if that band is too long, you can kind of just wrap your hands through it a couple times.

09:02:39.373 --> 09:02:43.678
Now I'm gripping this and I'm just going to step back a little bit so that I know I'm not going to hit my head.

09:02:43.698 --> 09:02:45.580
And I'm doing the same thing.

09:02:45.620 --> 09:02:49.645
So it's... All right.

09:02:50.485 --> 09:02:53.129
So we are going to do the same amount of reps there.

09:02:53.269 --> 09:02:59.078
So if it said five reps, that means five reps each leg here, five reps each leg here.

09:02:59.679 --> 09:03:09.574
So keep in mind, normally when we do contrast training, we're splitting it up where we go like a heavy lift and then we take a minute or two or three minutes off and then we go into the plyo

09:03:09.554 --> 09:03:12.357
For this one, I want you to go directly in there.

09:03:12.377 --> 09:03:15.240
That's why we're really not gonna tire you out with those split stance jumps.

09:03:15.601 --> 09:03:20.787
It's gonna be pretty low rep, and then I want you going straight in to that over speed exercise.

09:03:21.107 --> 09:03:24.651
This is great for training the nervous system for optimal explosiveness.

09:03:24.971 --> 09:03:31.899
We're training our force capabilities there at a high speed with the light dumbbells, and then we come in here for over speed training.

09:03:32.199 --> 09:03:39.207
That's teaching the nervous system to send signals faster and activate those fast switch muscle fibers at a very high velocity.

09:03:39.187 --> 09:03:43.410
which is great for improving our encore vertical jump and our overall explosiveness.

09:03:43.732 --> 09:03:44.556
Let's get it.

09:04:08.279 --> 09:04:12.304
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

09:04:12.424 --> 09:04:14.207
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

09:04:14.267 --> 09:04:16.970
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

09:04:17.011 --> 09:04:23.519
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

09:04:24.040 --> 09:04:29.988
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

09:04:30.268 --> 09:04:34.033
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

09:04:34.013 --> 09:04:37.919
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

09:04:38.340 --> 09:04:42.226
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

09:04:42.747 --> 09:04:50.098
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

09:04:51.091 --> 09:04:54.155
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

09:04:54.175 --> 09:05:00.883
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

09:05:01.444 --> 09:05:07.231
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

09:05:07.251 --> 09:05:10.895
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

09:05:13.218 --> 09:05:17.503
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

09:05:20.437 --> 09:05:22.359
and that stomach will fall, okay?

09:05:23.060 --> 09:05:25.703
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

09:05:26.464 --> 09:05:35.494
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

09:05:36.275 --> 09:05:44.043
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

09:05:44.283 --> 09:05:48.368
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

09:05:48.348 --> 09:06:17.275
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

09:06:17.457 --> 09:06:19.960
happens in the parasympathetic state.

09:06:20.501 --> 09:06:24.625
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

09:06:24.986 --> 09:06:38.622
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

09:06:40.441 --> 09:06:43.726
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

09:06:43.766 --> 09:06:48.032
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

09:06:48.312 --> 09:06:55.122
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

09:06:55.422 --> 09:06:58.907
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

09:06:59.187 --> 09:07:06.718
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

09:07:06.698 --> 09:07:11.029
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

09:07:11.450 --> 09:07:13.415
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

09:07:13.455 --> 09:07:15.961
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

09:07:16.242 --> 09:07:18.307
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

09:07:18.587 --> 09:07:20.813
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

09:07:21.114 --> 09:07:22.918
I'm just gonna lean forward.

09:07:22.898 --> 09:07:25.586
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

09:07:25.926 --> 09:07:32.926
If you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

09:07:33.087 --> 09:07:38.241
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

09:07:39.082 --> 09:07:42.952
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

09:07:42.972 --> 09:07:45.840
15 seconds on the other side as well.

09:07:45.980 --> 09:07:48.106
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

09:07:48.306 --> 09:07:49.730
So that is the back of the thigh.

09:07:49.971 --> 09:07:54.222
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

09:07:54.202 --> 09:07:57.952
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

09:07:58.092 --> 09:08:00.519
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

09:08:00.879 --> 09:08:03.185
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

09:08:03.546 --> 09:08:05.111
So now look at this back foot.

09:08:05.151 --> 09:08:06.554
I'm not curling the toe under.

09:08:06.955 --> 09:08:11.046
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

09:08:11.026 --> 09:08:14.450
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

09:08:14.870 --> 09:08:20.236
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

09:08:20.537 --> 09:08:33.912
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

09:08:33.892 --> 09:08:37.080
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

09:08:37.120 --> 09:08:38.443
That's the inside of the leg.

09:08:38.924 --> 09:08:42.312
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

09:08:42.894 --> 09:08:43.595
Same thing.

09:08:43.916 --> 09:08:46.823
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

09:08:47.104 --> 09:08:50.432
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

09:08:50.412 --> 09:08:54.358
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

09:08:54.679 --> 09:09:02.330
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

09:09:02.691 --> 09:09:08.179
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

09:09:08.600 --> 09:09:13.888
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

09:09:13.868 --> 09:09:18.636
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

09:09:19.037 --> 09:09:26.690
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

09:09:27.191 --> 09:09:30.016
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

09:09:30.437 --> 09:09:36.467
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

09:09:36.447 --> 09:09:39.530
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

09:09:39.570 --> 09:09:41.752
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

09:09:41.972 --> 09:09:45.635
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

09:09:46.096 --> 09:09:47.677
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

09:09:48.078 --> 09:09:50.620
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

09:09:50.680 --> 09:09:51.581
So I'm 90 there.

09:09:51.941 --> 09:09:54.744
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

09:09:55.104 --> 09:09:57.386
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

09:09:57.747 --> 09:10:02.451
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

09:10:02.791 --> 09:10:05.914
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

09:10:05.894 --> 09:10:08.800
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

09:10:09.181 --> 09:10:13.970
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

09:10:13.990 --> 09:10:15.433
All right.

09:10:15.453 --> 09:10:21.345
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

09:10:22.086 --> 09:10:25.894
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

09:10:25.874 --> 09:10:31.201
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

09:10:31.581 --> 09:10:34.806
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

09:10:34.906 --> 09:10:38.070
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

09:10:38.470 --> 09:10:48.203
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

09:10:48.470 --> 09:10:50.352
So I do have a straight knee.

09:10:50.752 --> 09:10:53.234
So we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius.

09:10:53.254 --> 09:10:54.976
That's the outside calf muscle.

09:10:55.256 --> 09:11:01.402
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

09:11:01.802 --> 09:11:05.886
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

09:11:07.047 --> 09:11:10.089
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

09:11:10.130 --> 09:11:10.850
That's okay.

09:11:11.230 --> 09:11:14.413
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

09:11:14.894 --> 09:11:18.477
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

09:11:21.258 --> 09:11:21.959
What's up, guys?

09:11:22.119 --> 09:11:27.565
Quick minimum dose advice for day two and day five of phase seven.

09:11:27.606 --> 09:11:29.428
This is more of our strength day.

09:11:29.488 --> 09:11:32.331
We only have one very explosive exercise.

09:11:33.012 --> 09:11:36.576
Our nervous system should be a little bit more tired from yesterday's workout.

09:11:36.937 --> 09:11:41.122
Our muscles shouldn't be beat down, but our nervous system might be a little more tired.

09:11:41.562 --> 09:11:48.210
This exercise is a little bit more about the muscles, putting load on the muscles, and less on the nervous system.

09:11:48.190 --> 09:11:53.597
The only one that is more explosive and fast paced is the split stance clean.

09:11:54.318 --> 09:11:56.642
So we do want to keep that exercise in there.

09:11:57.162 --> 09:12:01.708
Moving down to the one leg box step down, we can add load to this.

09:12:01.748 --> 09:12:05.053
So if you want to add a vest and add dumbbells, let's do it.

09:12:05.614 --> 09:12:07.296
The Nordic curl, it's all about

09:12:07.276 --> 09:12:09.038
control on the way down.

09:12:09.158 --> 09:12:17.048
Since it's an eccentric dominant exercise, this is going to produce a lot of soreness in the hamstrings, so it doesn't feel hard.

09:12:17.349 --> 09:12:19.812
You may not get a huge burn while you're in the gym.

09:12:20.192 --> 09:12:23.236
The next day, you're going to feel it, so don't overdo these.

09:12:23.637 --> 09:12:27.642
Moving down to the rapid calf raise, it's a little bit higher rep.

09:12:27.702 --> 09:12:36.473
This is good for our elasticity through the Achilles tendon, and we should get a pretty good burn in the calf, especially up in that gastroc, the big calf muscle.

09:12:36.453 --> 09:12:38.736
As always, we're going to do the 7-Eleven breathing.

09:12:39.076 --> 09:12:41.579
If you had to, you could skip the static stretch.

09:12:41.939 --> 09:12:44.582
I don't think you really need to take out anything here.

09:12:45.263 --> 09:12:48.967
Even if you're pressed for time, this should be a very short and efficient workout.

09:12:49.247 --> 09:12:49.708
Let's get it.

09:12:50.208 --> 09:12:51.750
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

09:12:51.890 --> 09:12:53.612
I have a long roller from Skills.

09:12:53.852 --> 09:13:00.720
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

09:13:01.080 --> 09:13:06.426
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

09:13:06.406 --> 09:13:09.570
So you could either go this way or you could go vertical.

09:13:09.910 --> 09:13:18.762
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

09:13:19.082 --> 09:13:25.510
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

09:13:26.231 --> 09:13:32.599
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

09:13:32.579 --> 09:13:34.882
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

09:13:34.922 --> 09:13:36.324
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

09:13:36.665 --> 09:13:45.076
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

09:13:45.437 --> 09:13:57.253
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

09:13:57.470 --> 09:13:59.232
Now the next one we're gonna do is the calves.

09:13:59.532 --> 09:14:11.244
So we're just gonna cross over and I'm gonna push myself up so my hips are off the ground and I'm just gonna roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

09:14:11.304 --> 09:14:15.468
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

09:14:15.488 --> 09:14:20.053
And our next one we're flipping over, we're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

09:14:20.093 --> 09:14:27.460
So I'm gonna go on the side of the roller now and I'm just gonna come down into a plank position and I'm gonna roll

09:14:27.440 --> 09:14:50.106
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh so i'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee

09:14:50.086 --> 09:14:52.992
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

09:14:53.193 --> 09:14:54.936
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

09:14:55.237 --> 09:15:09.006
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

09:15:10.775 --> 09:15:17.586
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

09:15:17.786 --> 09:15:21.372
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

09:15:21.652 --> 09:15:24.917
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

09:15:25.478 --> 09:15:28.162
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

09:15:28.182 --> 09:15:29.865
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

09:15:30.165 --> 09:15:34.352
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

09:15:34.332 --> 09:15:37.098
and I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

09:15:37.660 --> 09:15:48.645
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping, we gotta really start to loosen that up.

09:15:49.958 --> 09:15:54.611
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

09:15:54.872 --> 09:15:58.402
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

09:15:58.442 --> 09:16:03.596
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

09:16:03.957 --> 09:16:05.622
Everything else is extra credit.

09:16:08.690 --> 09:16:12.437
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

09:16:12.717 --> 09:16:15.302
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

09:16:15.362 --> 09:16:19.350
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

09:16:19.851 --> 09:16:26.563
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

09:16:26.543 --> 09:16:50.087
all right i'm staying stiff with the feet so i'm not letting my heel collapse i want to stay on the ball of the foot and i'm just going to go about 50 of my max speed i'm not going too fast yet we're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips for the seal jacks it's basically like a jumping jack except i'm going to coordinate arms with the legs so i'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time back to neutral

09:16:50.067 --> 09:16:52.572
over with the left, over with the left hand.

09:16:52.872 --> 09:16:55.116
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

09:16:55.136 --> 09:16:57.160
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

09:16:57.781 --> 09:17:02.831
Maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

09:17:03.272 --> 09:17:04.875
We're just getting that blood flowing.

09:17:05.155 --> 09:17:06.658
We're gonna go for 30 seconds.

09:17:06.638 --> 09:17:11.003
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

09:17:11.323 --> 09:17:13.145
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

09:17:13.205 --> 09:17:22.455
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't wanna go to the point where we have to round our back.

09:17:22.975 --> 09:17:30.503
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

09:17:34.145 --> 09:17:36.729
All right, we're going to go five over and back.

09:17:36.989 --> 09:17:38.551
So it's 10 total touches.

09:17:38.732 --> 09:17:42.437
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch.

09:17:42.457 --> 09:17:44.060
So I'm going to pull my right foot up.

09:17:44.340 --> 09:17:48.025
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position.

09:17:48.406 --> 09:17:49.568
And I'm not out like this.

09:17:49.648 --> 09:17:50.890
I'm keeping that knee in.

09:17:51.330 --> 09:17:53.714
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight.

09:17:54.775 --> 09:17:59.342
And I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

09:17:59.322 --> 09:18:04.269
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

09:18:04.610 --> 09:18:10.198
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

09:18:10.879 --> 09:18:13.243
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

09:18:13.564 --> 09:18:18.190
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

09:18:18.831 --> 09:18:21.195
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

09:18:22.103 --> 09:18:25.691
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

09:18:26.131 --> 09:18:30.601
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

09:18:30.661 --> 09:18:32.926
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

09:18:33.266 --> 09:18:35.291
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

09:18:35.331 --> 09:18:35.912
That's okay.

09:18:36.253 --> 09:18:37.836
Let's just get as low as we can there.

09:18:38.317 --> 09:18:39.319
One, two, three.

09:18:39.299 --> 09:18:40.982
three, and back up.

09:18:41.042 --> 09:18:43.045
So three there, three there.

09:18:43.365 --> 09:18:49.675
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

09:18:49.755 --> 09:18:53.221
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

09:18:53.261 --> 09:18:57.207
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor

09:18:57.187 --> 09:19:02.357
Keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

09:19:02.697 --> 09:19:09.630
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

09:19:10.071 --> 09:19:13.137
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom.

09:19:13.117 --> 09:19:34.770
and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

09:19:39.728 --> 09:19:46.258
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

09:19:46.278 --> 09:19:47.741
Don't make the mistake of going here.

09:19:48.101 --> 09:19:56.174
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

09:19:56.274 --> 09:20:02.143
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

09:20:02.223 --> 09:20:05.989
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

09:20:05.969 --> 09:20:07.813
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

09:20:08.073 --> 09:20:10.017
One, two, three.

09:20:10.157 --> 09:20:13.103
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

09:20:13.323 --> 09:20:15.507
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

09:20:16.169 --> 09:20:17.892
One, two, three.

09:20:17.932 --> 09:20:19.435
Rock forward.

09:20:19.515 --> 09:20:20.537
So I'm on my toes now.

09:20:21.038 --> 09:20:23.262
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

09:20:23.323 --> 09:20:23.623
Hold.

09:20:24.264 --> 09:20:24.585
One, two.

09:20:24.565 --> 09:20:43.806
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're gonna go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

09:20:44.140 --> 09:20:53.532
one, two, three, lean, one, two, three, heels back down, one, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

09:20:53.832 --> 09:21:02.142
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

09:21:02.162 --> 09:21:06.528
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

09:21:06.888 --> 09:21:13.236
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

09:21:13.216 --> 09:21:16.059
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

09:21:16.099 --> 09:21:18.382
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

09:21:18.802 --> 09:21:21.585
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

09:21:21.905 --> 09:21:23.607
I need to be flat through the hips.

09:21:23.908 --> 09:21:26.310
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

09:21:26.831 --> 09:21:31.256
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

09:21:31.656 --> 09:21:34.699
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

09:21:34.719 --> 09:21:42.548
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

09:21:42.528 --> 09:21:52.491
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

09:21:52.932 --> 09:22:01.431
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

09:22:08.228 --> 09:22:12.594
So for our split stance clean, what I'm going to do is set up in a split stance.

09:22:12.835 --> 09:22:14.837
I'm going to come up on that back toe.

09:22:14.857 --> 09:22:18.603
So I'm on my left toe and that right foot is fully grounded.

09:22:19.224 --> 09:22:20.546
Slight bend in that front knee.

09:22:20.586 --> 09:22:22.108
Now from there, it's a hip hinge.

09:22:22.428 --> 09:22:27.155
So I'm sending my hips backwards until my knuckles reach around knee level.

09:22:27.616 --> 09:22:30.760
Once I hit that point, I explosively come up.

09:22:30.740 --> 09:22:35.010
The dumbbells are going to fly up from the momentum of that hip extension.

09:22:35.411 --> 09:22:39.480
So it's boom, boom, I'm driving up that left leg.

09:22:39.540 --> 09:22:48.841
So it's going to be here, boom, drive, stabilize at the top for one to two seconds, and then come back down, reset, and you're back in.

09:22:49.141 --> 09:22:50.284
So boom.

09:22:50.264 --> 09:22:52.066
drive, stabilize.

09:22:52.527 --> 09:22:56.411
I don't want you to feel like you're using your arms to curl it up.

09:22:56.431 --> 09:22:58.073
Your biceps shouldn't be getting tired here.

09:22:58.454 --> 09:23:03.139
I just want the dumbbells to come up from the momentum of that powerful hip extension.

09:23:03.459 --> 09:23:06.303
Now for the weight, start light and gradually go up.

09:23:06.663 --> 09:23:09.306
Once you get really good at this exercise, we can start going up.

09:23:09.687 --> 09:23:13.711
30 pounds in each hand, 40 pounds in each hand, 50 pounds in each hand.

09:23:14.012 --> 09:23:15.874
We've never had anybody get past 60.

09:23:16.595 --> 09:23:18.617
I don't think any of you guys will get there.

09:23:18.597 --> 09:23:22.952
because it's probably gonna get so slow that you're losing a lot of that velocity.

09:23:22.972 --> 09:23:27.848
So I still want you to stay fast and explosive, but I do want the weight to challenge you at the same time.

09:23:36.670 --> 09:23:41.155
So for my single leg box step down, I'm going to go in a box around knee height.

09:23:41.676 --> 09:23:44.319
Basically, I'm going to hold a light dumbbell.

09:23:44.359 --> 09:23:47.342
So I can go 15s in each hand at the most.

09:23:47.362 --> 09:23:52.668
So what I want you to do with the dumbbells is as we go down, I want you to kick it forward.

09:23:53.069 --> 09:23:58.375
So if you keep them down by your hip, you're not going to have the right counterbalance and you're going to fall backwards.

09:23:58.435 --> 09:24:02.199
So as you're going down, the dumbbells go higher and higher and higher.

09:24:02.540 --> 09:24:05.563
And then once that foot taps and we're on the way up, it's the opposite.

09:24:05.543 --> 09:24:09.489
we come up with the lower body and we go down with the weight.

09:24:09.529 --> 09:24:11.571
So you're using it as a counterbalance.

09:24:12.172 --> 09:24:18.141
So I want you to go on the edge of that box, make sure that box is heavy enough so you're not tipping over.

09:24:18.481 --> 09:24:23.468
If you need to, put some dumbbells on the side of that box to counterbalance.

09:24:23.448 --> 09:24:26.113
So I'm just going to drop down normal squat mechanics.

09:24:26.133 --> 09:24:28.117
I got a break from the hip first.

09:24:28.137 --> 09:24:30.782
So it's a hip hinge and then I go down with the knee.

09:24:31.143 --> 09:24:34.790
I do not want your knee going too far over your toe on this drill.

09:24:35.190 --> 09:24:36.032
So it's going to be here.

09:24:36.092 --> 09:24:40.120
Tap that heel and explode up.

09:24:40.180 --> 09:24:42.264
I do not want you to push off the ground.

09:24:42.604 --> 09:24:44.528
I just want you to tap that heel.

09:24:45.790 --> 09:24:46.572
and explode.

09:24:46.612 --> 09:24:51.381
It's gonna be two to three seconds down and then as fast as we can on the way up.

09:24:51.401 --> 09:24:54.487
So you could start maybe five, 10s or 15 pounders.

09:24:54.868 --> 09:24:58.294
If you do wanna load heavier, let's go with that vest.

09:24:58.314 --> 09:25:06.550
If it's taking you over a second to get up, then you're loaded too much because I wanna see you get up fast so that it translates to our high speed strength.

09:25:16.334 --> 09:25:22.122
So for our Nordic hamstring curls, if you have a partner, this is the easiest way to go.

09:25:22.162 --> 09:25:26.008
So your partner is going to sit behind you and hold down your ankles.

09:25:26.408 --> 09:25:29.993
Now he's got to push pretty hard because your whole body weight is going to be coming down.

09:25:30.094 --> 09:25:33.198
So if he's light, then you're going to lift him up.

09:25:33.278 --> 09:25:35.281
So he's got to put a little bit of force in there.

09:25:35.621 --> 09:25:37.484
Now I like to use a foam roller.

09:25:37.764 --> 09:25:45.936
So you put the front of your ankle on a foam roller and then you kind of dig your toes into the ground and it's just a little bit more comfortable on the ankles.

09:25:45.916 --> 09:25:52.045
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a partner, so this is how we would do it without a partner.

09:25:52.385 --> 09:25:54.769
I would set up a barbell and make sure it's nice and heavy.

09:25:55.730 --> 09:25:57.152
Make sure you got weights on both sides.

09:25:57.553 --> 09:26:03.441
If you have a rack, then put it on the inside of the rack and then you put a weighted plate on the other side so that the barbell is not going to roll.

09:26:03.842 --> 09:26:10.771
If you don't have a rack, we would use the barbell and then we would put a weight behind it and we would put another 45 pound plate in front of it.

09:26:11.092 --> 09:26:14.657
So it's still kind of trapped in there and it's not going to roll back and forth.

09:26:14.637 --> 09:26:17.767
This is optional, but I like to use this because it's a little bit softer.

09:26:17.807 --> 09:26:19.493
So I just use a pad there.

09:26:19.533 --> 09:26:23.365
And then you always need a pad

09:26:23.682 --> 09:26:53.689
for your knees all right so this is your setup now from here a lot of people go tall and they come down i if you're super advanced that's fine i would rather see a little bit of hip flexion so we're initially in just a little bit of hip flexion and then from there we're going down i want you to go basically as slow as you can or four seconds all the way down this is a lowering exercise once i get to the bottom i'm going to push back up and i'm coming all the way back up pretty fast you don't have to go slow on the way up

09:26:53.669 --> 09:27:12.100
so initial hip flexion from there I'm not bending the hips more I stay in this initial hip flexion and I slowly lower lower lower lower push off and come back up if you can get a light push off that's great a lot of you guys to start

09:27:12.080 --> 09:27:15.809
you're going to have to really push off hard to get back up.

09:27:16.110 --> 09:27:18.175
Otherwise, you're going to get stuck halfway up.

09:27:18.195 --> 09:27:29.903
So if you get extremely good at this, we can eventually either go a very light tap or there are some people who have such good strength to body weight ratios, they can just put their hands behind their back and do the whole thing.

09:27:29.883 --> 09:27:36.576
Now, you might cramp up on this exercise if you're not used to this intense of a hamstring contraction.

09:27:36.616 --> 09:27:38.319
It's a very hard hamstring contraction.

09:27:38.800 --> 09:27:41.345
So if you cramp, then stretch it out.

09:27:41.826 --> 09:27:47.057
Try to get back into it, but this might be a little bit too advanced and your hamstrings aren't quite used to it yet.

09:27:47.558 --> 09:27:51.385
So just try to make it easier or come up with an alternative if you continue to cramp.

09:27:51.365 --> 09:27:56.473
Now the other mistake that people make is they point the toes and they go from there.

09:27:56.793 --> 09:28:00.298
A lot of times your calf will start to cramp when you're in that position.

09:28:00.338 --> 09:28:04.384
So I like to go toes kind of straight down into the ground.

09:28:04.785 --> 09:28:15.060
So I'm digging into the ground and then I get a lot less in the calf and I really just feel it straight through the hamstrings, through the glutes, and then you should feel a little bit through the low back as well.

09:28:23.630 --> 09:28:31.160
So for my rapid calf raise, I could use a bumper plate or I could use a stair, step, anything that's nice and stable.

09:28:31.560 --> 09:28:36.467
Now you're going to want to hold on to something for this exercise, even if your balance is good.

09:28:36.847 --> 09:28:41.634
So I'm going to hold on to a power rack, hold on to whatever you want that keeps you nice and balanced.

09:28:41.654 --> 09:28:50.245
About half of my foot is going on the step, so I just want the ball of my foot on there, my heel is hanging off.

09:28:50.225 --> 09:29:00.438
Now, it's a normal calf raise, except we're going for max elasticity, so I want you to bounce with it, and we're still getting a full range of motion, so it's nice and rapid.

09:29:00.799 --> 09:29:07.467
So explode up to the toe, and then drop down, get your heel down as low as you can, and pop back up, all right?

09:29:07.768 --> 09:29:10.191
So it's nice and fast.

09:29:10.947 --> 09:29:15.956
and you shouldn't feel like it's a long, slow contraction like you normally would do.

09:29:15.976 --> 09:29:18.320
Now this is a higher rep exercise.

09:29:18.781 --> 09:29:33.688
If you do start to get extremely fatigued, you can always pull yourself up with the arms a little bit and kind of give yourself a little bit of assistance with your upper body because I don't want you to get so tired that you start to slow down.

09:29:33.728 --> 09:29:34.710
I want you to stay

09:29:34.690 --> 09:29:47.582
nice and rapid and bouncy because if you lose that speed and you lose that bouncy and that springiness, then we're getting away from the Achilles and we're getting more on to strictly using that calf muscle contraction.

09:29:47.903 --> 09:29:51.312
We want the perfect combination of both on this exercise.

09:29:51.713 --> 09:29:52.294
Let's do it.

09:30:18.464 --> 09:30:22.489
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

09:30:22.609 --> 09:30:24.411
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

09:30:24.451 --> 09:30:27.154
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

09:30:27.195 --> 09:30:33.722
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

09:30:34.223 --> 09:30:40.190
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

09:30:40.450 --> 09:30:44.235
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

09:30:44.215 --> 09:31:00.317
okay so i'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill all right so it's in through the nose and i want to make sure that my stomach is rising okay your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

09:31:01.276 --> 09:31:04.341
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

09:31:04.361 --> 09:31:11.091
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

09:31:11.652 --> 09:31:17.420
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

09:31:17.440 --> 09:31:21.106
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

09:31:23.409 --> 09:31:27.756
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

09:31:30.622 --> 09:31:32.545
and that stomach will fall, okay?

09:31:33.266 --> 09:31:35.889
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

09:31:36.650 --> 09:31:45.682
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

09:31:46.483 --> 09:31:54.233
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

09:31:54.474 --> 09:31:58.519
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

09:31:58.499 --> 09:32:27.392
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

09:32:27.642 --> 09:32:30.147
happens in the parasympathetic state.

09:32:30.688 --> 09:32:35.156
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

09:32:35.176 --> 09:32:48.722
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

09:32:50.626 --> 09:32:53.931
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

09:32:53.951 --> 09:32:58.197
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

09:32:58.517 --> 09:33:05.327
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

09:33:05.607 --> 09:33:09.112
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

09:33:09.372 --> 09:33:16.903
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

09:33:16.883 --> 09:33:21.235
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

09:33:21.656 --> 09:33:23.621
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

09:33:23.962 --> 09:33:26.128
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

09:33:26.429 --> 09:33:28.775
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

09:33:28.795 --> 09:33:31.001
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

09:33:31.302 --> 09:33:33.087
I'm just gonna lean forward.

09:33:33.067 --> 09:33:35.793
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

09:33:36.114 --> 09:33:43.249
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

09:33:43.269 --> 09:33:48.460
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

09:33:49.267 --> 09:33:53.156
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

09:33:53.176 --> 09:33:56.163
15 seconds on the other side as well.

09:33:56.183 --> 09:33:58.309
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

09:33:58.489 --> 09:33:59.912
So that is the back of the thigh.

09:34:00.153 --> 09:34:04.423
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

09:34:04.403 --> 09:34:08.150
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

09:34:08.290 --> 09:34:10.715
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

09:34:11.056 --> 09:34:13.380
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

09:34:13.741 --> 09:34:15.304
So now look at this back foot.

09:34:15.324 --> 09:34:16.766
I'm not curling the toe under.

09:34:17.127 --> 09:34:21.315
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

09:34:21.295 --> 09:34:24.641
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

09:34:25.061 --> 09:34:30.430
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

09:34:30.731 --> 09:34:44.113
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

09:34:44.093 --> 09:34:47.278
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

09:34:47.298 --> 09:34:48.640
That's the inside of the leg.

09:34:49.121 --> 09:34:52.487
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

09:34:53.088 --> 09:34:53.769
Same thing.

09:34:54.109 --> 09:34:56.994
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

09:34:57.295 --> 09:35:00.600
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

09:35:00.580 --> 09:35:04.567
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

09:35:04.868 --> 09:35:12.522
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

09:35:12.883 --> 09:35:18.373
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

09:35:18.794 --> 09:35:24.124
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

09:35:24.104 --> 09:35:28.830
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

09:35:29.230 --> 09:35:36.880
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

09:35:37.381 --> 09:35:40.184
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

09:35:40.625 --> 09:35:46.652
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

09:35:46.632 --> 09:35:49.735
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

09:35:49.755 --> 09:35:51.937
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

09:35:52.177 --> 09:35:55.820
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

09:35:56.301 --> 09:35:57.862
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

09:35:57.902 --> 09:36:00.785
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

09:36:00.865 --> 09:36:01.806
So I'm 90 there.

09:36:02.146 --> 09:36:04.929
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

09:36:05.289 --> 09:36:07.912
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

09:36:07.932 --> 09:36:12.636
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

09:36:12.976 --> 09:36:16.099
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

09:36:16.079 --> 09:36:19.366
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

09:36:19.386 --> 09:36:24.155
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

09:36:24.175 --> 09:36:25.638
All right.

09:36:25.658 --> 09:36:31.530
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

09:36:32.271 --> 09:36:36.079
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

09:36:36.059 --> 09:36:41.388
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

09:36:41.769 --> 09:36:44.995
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

09:36:45.095 --> 09:36:48.300
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

09:36:48.681 --> 09:36:58.438
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

09:36:58.418 --> 09:37:05.167
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

09:37:05.448 --> 09:37:11.616
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

09:37:11.997 --> 09:37:16.343
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

09:37:17.150 --> 09:37:20.277
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

09:37:20.317 --> 09:37:21.038
That's okay.

09:37:21.399 --> 09:37:24.606
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

09:37:24.646 --> 09:37:28.695
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

09:37:31.477 --> 09:37:32.178
What's up, guys?

09:37:32.358 --> 09:37:36.082
Quick minimum dose advice for day three and day six.

09:37:36.483 --> 09:37:39.366
Basically, there is no minimum dose advice for this workout.

09:37:39.526 --> 09:37:42.370
As you can see, it's already very short and efficient.

09:37:42.650 --> 09:37:44.432
We shouldn't have to skip anything here.

09:37:44.792 --> 09:37:52.882
If you're really pressed for time, you could go down to two sets, but I really recommend that you get done all three sets of everything.

09:37:53.222 --> 09:37:54.143
Let's get after it.

09:38:00.839 --> 09:38:12.483
So for my Russian Dumbbell Punch, what I'm gonna do is come to this 45 degree angle here, and I'm just gonna touch my elbow to the floor, and then I'm gonna punch across.

09:38:12.564 --> 09:38:16.131
So it's gonna be touch and punch, touch,

09:38:16.111 --> 09:38:17.092
and punch.

09:38:17.433 --> 09:38:19.336
I'm trying not to lose that position.

09:38:19.356 --> 09:38:24.483
Now, as you go down, we might move our legs a little bit, but for the most part, we want to keep that same angle.

09:38:24.764 --> 09:38:27.307
I don't want to see you flatten out and then come back.

09:38:27.588 --> 09:38:33.917
So we're just staying there and it's just a little bit of a rotation from the torso and then explode into that rotation the other way.

09:38:33.897 --> 09:38:34.719
cross punch.

09:38:35.080 --> 09:38:36.604
I want you to start light here.

09:38:36.664 --> 09:38:41.175
So start maybe 10, 15 pound dumbbell and then gradually go up.

09:38:41.235 --> 09:38:48.413
Some people get up to like 30, 35, 40 pounds, but you got to have a crazy strong core to get above the 30 pound mark.

09:38:55.413 --> 09:39:01.420
So for my band pronation supination, I'm going to sit up nice and tall and I'm just going to get some tension on this band.

09:39:01.860 --> 09:39:06.946
You can do this with a thick band or like I'm doing, I'm doubled up with a thin band and I want it around the ball of the foot.

09:39:07.386 --> 09:39:10.310
So I'm going to sit up nice and tall and I'm going to pull on it.

09:39:10.450 --> 09:39:15.395
Already I feel a stretch in the hamstrings and in my calves.

09:39:15.415 --> 09:39:18.519
Now from here, I'm just going to go into pronation.

09:39:18.539 --> 09:39:21.302
So I'm pulling with the right side.

09:39:21.721 --> 09:39:30.494
hold for three seconds, and then I switch and I pull more with the left side, and that's when I go into what we call ankle or foot supination.

09:39:31.315 --> 09:39:32.177
Hold there.

09:39:33.158 --> 09:39:36.343
So it's here, three seconds, here, three seconds.

09:39:36.744 --> 09:39:39.628
And we're gonna cycle through that five times, all right?

09:39:40.283 --> 09:39:46.698
So when I'm pulling, I'm feeling that stretch in different areas, especially down in the calf muscle.

09:39:46.978 --> 09:39:50.907
You're going to feel it on the lateral calf, and then you're going to feel it on the medial calf.

09:39:50.927 --> 09:39:55.778
You'll feel it in different parts of the hamstring as well, and you're getting a stretch through the ankles.

09:39:55.758 --> 09:40:04.999
we need proper pronation and supination, we need enough range of motion, and a lot of times that calf is limiting what we can do.

09:40:05.019 --> 09:40:14.560
A lot of us have good ankle mobility here in the sagittal plane, but here we don't quite have that range of motion and it can really limit our overall athleticism on the court.

09:40:27.601 --> 09:40:38.356
So for my rolling cross touch, what I'm going to do is I'm just going to bring my feet off the ground and I'm going to bring my hands off the ground so I'm in this hollow body position.

09:40:38.556 --> 09:40:48.110
I don't want to have an excessive curve in the lower back, so I want to think about tilting my pelvis, pretend like you have a tail, and think about tucking that tail so your back is nice and flat to the floor.

09:40:48.530 --> 09:40:50.453
And then from there, I'm just rolling.

09:40:50.433 --> 09:40:52.315
So my feet still aren't going to touch the ground.

09:40:52.355 --> 09:40:54.037
My hands are still not going to touch the ground.

09:40:54.378 --> 09:40:55.199
I'm going to roll over.

09:40:55.239 --> 09:40:56.621
I'm going to roll over again.

09:40:56.741 --> 09:41:01.106
So I end up in the same position and then I'm going to go cross body.

09:41:01.126 --> 09:41:06.313
So when I roll that way, I'm going to go left hand touches the right toe and back down.

09:41:06.553 --> 09:41:09.617
And again, left hand, right toe back down.

09:41:10.037 --> 09:41:11.839
And then I roll back to the other side.

09:41:12.240 --> 09:41:14.302
So now I'll end up in the same position.

09:41:14.683 --> 09:41:18.027
Now, once I'm back here, I go right hand left.

09:41:18.007 --> 09:41:20.351
So right hand, left toe.

09:41:20.651 --> 09:41:23.075
And then again, I roll back to the other side.

09:41:23.535 --> 09:41:32.188
So the reason that we are rolling is it is one of the most fundamental movement patterns that humans have and we lose it over time.

09:41:32.368 --> 09:41:37.416
But when we roll, we're training so many core muscles and we're training them reactively

09:41:37.396 --> 09:41:42.751
It is a training stimulus for the core that we can't get with any traditional core exercise.

09:41:43.031 --> 09:41:51.675
So it's really building up that core stability and it's building it up naturally and reflexively which is really going to carry over to all of our athletic movements.

09:41:59.013 --> 09:42:02.681
All right, so for my band straight leg raise, I'm gonna try to keep my knee pretty straight.

09:42:02.721 --> 09:42:04.565
I could have a slight bend if we need to.

09:42:04.946 --> 09:42:12.442
I'm gonna keep my foot dorsiflexed, so that means my toe back towards the head, the band is around the ball of the foot, and then I'm just going rapid with it.

09:42:13.104 --> 09:42:15.469
So let's go to a comfortable range.

09:42:15.787 --> 09:42:18.952
and then we can gradually start to raise it higher and higher.

09:42:18.992 --> 09:42:24.620
Make sure this opposite leg doesn't lift up, but this is not slow static stretching.

09:42:24.981 --> 09:42:26.163
This is very dynamic.

09:42:26.183 --> 09:42:30.369
We're working on that elasticity back in the hamstrings and in the glutes.

09:42:30.730 --> 09:42:32.192
So I'm just gonna build up

09:42:32.172 --> 09:42:36.040
that speed and get it nice and rapid there.

09:42:36.200 --> 09:42:38.064
All right, we should feel a stretch.

09:42:38.344 --> 09:42:40.068
It shouldn't be complete discomfort.

09:42:40.088 --> 09:42:42.653
So maybe like a seven out of 10 or an eight out of 10.

09:42:42.673 --> 09:42:43.915
We're getting a slight stretch.

09:42:44.216 --> 09:42:47.342
If we push it to a 10 out of 10, you're probably gonna get a strained muscle.

09:42:47.422 --> 09:42:51.851
So push slightly out of your comfort zone, but it shouldn't hurt so bad that it's unbearable.

09:42:52.232 --> 09:42:52.813
Let's do it.

09:43:21.363 --> 09:43:23.988
So for our side plank, you have several different options.

09:43:24.008 --> 09:43:38.899
So option one, we stack the feet, we get up nice and flat so I'm in one straight line, my hips are not sagging, my elbow is right underneath the shoulder, and I'm just squeezing that oblique on the inside of the core, and I'm just gonna hold in this position.

09:43:38.879 --> 09:43:55.993
now if that is too easy then you can add weight and how we add weight to a side plank is we go here right at the hip and i'm going to come up nice and flat now don't try to balance it i need to hold this weight down and i'm just going to hold in this position

09:43:55.973 --> 09:43:57.835
for the desired time, all right?

09:43:58.235 --> 09:44:05.262
Now, if that's too difficult and the stacking of the feet is too difficult, then you could go one knee bent.

09:44:05.462 --> 09:44:11.528
So I bend this knee back at 90 degrees, and then I'm straight with the other leg, and I'm getting up that way.

09:44:11.548 --> 09:44:15.552
So still working the same muscles, but that option is a lot easier.

09:44:15.993 --> 09:44:23.500
The middle option, the intermediate option, is the regular side plank with the stacked feet, and the harder version is with the plate.

09:44:26.838 --> 09:44:32.166
So for my kneeling hip mobility, I'm gonna go down on both knees, get up nice and tall.

09:44:32.466 --> 09:44:37.213
I'm gonna go out with my fists and I'm gonna squeeze my fists and put a little bit of tension in the course.

09:44:37.233 --> 09:44:40.998
You gotta kinda squeeze the core a little bit for this drill to create stability.

09:44:41.399 --> 09:44:47.067
And now from here, I'm pretending like I have this fence right here and I gotta step out over that fence.

09:44:47.327 --> 09:44:50.572
But as I do that, I don't wanna start to tilt my shoulders.

09:44:50.852 --> 09:44:56.220
I wanna keep my shoulders as flat as I can, my hips as flat as I can, and really just work

09:44:56.200 --> 09:44:57.503
on that range of motion.

09:44:57.603 --> 09:45:04.337
So it's going to be up, out, and over, and then up, out, and back.

09:45:05.359 --> 09:45:06.922
So I don't want you just going here.

09:45:06.942 --> 09:45:14.277
I want you to pretend like you've got to really step out and step up and out and around.

09:45:14.257 --> 09:45:17.364
The bigger range of motion we can get here, the better.

09:45:17.705 --> 09:45:27.146
This is working on our hip mobility, but it's also working on our hip stability, which goes a long way in improving the overall quality of our hips.

09:45:27.567 --> 09:45:32.518
Now, the reason why this is important is we need great hip mobility and stability for the vertical jump.

09:45:32.498 --> 09:45:42.088
but especially for ball handling, what separates players like Kyrie Irving is he can get down in these low positions and he can be explosive and stable in these positions.

09:45:42.108 --> 09:45:49.256
99% of people can't even get to these positions because their nervous system doesn't trust them being in those positions.

09:45:49.316 --> 09:46:02.370
It feels unsafe and that's because we don't have the quality of the hips that somebody like Kyrie Irving has, James Harden, Kemba Walker, all these guys that are very good movers, they all have very good hips,

09:46:02.350 --> 09:46:04.781
with mobility and dynamic stability.

09:46:05.042 --> 09:46:10.285
So this drill can go a long way in improving your jump, but also improving how you move on the court.

09:46:13.420 --> 09:46:14.161
What's up, guys?

09:46:14.401 --> 09:46:19.167
Quick minimum dose advice for day three and day six of phase eight.

09:46:19.928 --> 09:46:22.771
So the core is already very efficient.

09:46:22.811 --> 09:46:24.593
We shouldn't have to cut out anything there.

09:46:25.274 --> 09:46:31.121
If you're already mobile, again, I'm okay with you taking out the mobility drills.

09:46:31.642 --> 09:46:34.645
One that I want you to do is the ankle toe crawls.

09:46:34.986 --> 09:46:42.955
A lot of people have mobility, but they don't have mobility in the toes, and it limits the triple extension on their first step explosiveness.

09:46:42.935 --> 09:46:44.818
Definitely do that.

09:46:45.238 --> 09:46:49.745
You can skip the adductor stretch if your adductors are already nice and flexible.

09:46:50.506 --> 09:46:51.828
Rotational rolling mobility.

09:46:52.248 --> 09:46:56.154
Most people do struggle with this internal, external rotation of the hip.

09:46:56.595 --> 09:47:01.602
I would advise doing that, but if you're already extremely flexible, then skip it.

09:47:01.582 --> 09:47:03.266
and the band straight leg raise.

09:47:03.587 --> 09:47:06.956
If you're flexible in the hamstrings or in the post chain, skip it.

09:47:07.477 --> 09:47:13.392
But most of you guys, I think that would still be a good exercise for overall elasticity in the post chain.

09:47:13.813 --> 09:47:14.916
All right, let's get it.

09:47:22.810 --> 09:47:26.856
So for our stability ball mountain climber hold, I'm going to use a stability ball.

09:47:26.876 --> 09:47:32.845
If you don't have a stability ball, you could just use a bench or a chair or something where you could just put your hands.

09:47:33.306 --> 09:47:35.128
I'm going to use a box for my feet.

09:47:35.309 --> 09:47:36.991
If you don't have that, just go on the floor.

09:47:37.252 --> 09:47:40.396
It's completely fine, but the box is going to make it a little bit more difficult.

09:47:40.917 --> 09:47:43.060
So I'm just going to hold on to this stability ball.

09:47:43.100 --> 09:47:44.723
Now make sure your hands aren't sweaty.

09:47:45.083 --> 09:47:51.653
If your hands are sweaty, you're going to want to use a towel or something over the ball because you're going to slip off.

09:47:51.633 --> 09:47:55.264
I'm going to hold right here and then I'm going elevated.

09:47:55.565 --> 09:47:58.348
For some of you already, that's the workout right there.

09:47:58.749 --> 09:48:02.854
Make sure that your wrists are directly underneath the elbow, elbow underneath the shoulder.

09:48:03.274 --> 09:48:04.175
Squeeze that core.

09:48:04.556 --> 09:48:06.938
If that's hard enough for you, just hold for 30 seconds.

09:48:07.219 --> 09:48:08.801
But most of us, we're going to go for reps.

09:48:08.841 --> 09:48:13.706
So it's going to be drive one leg, hold, one, two, and then switch.

09:48:14.347 --> 09:48:17.391
Hold, one, two, switch.

09:48:17.831 --> 09:48:18.052
All right?

09:48:18.412 --> 09:48:21.876
So right leg, left leg, that is one rep.

09:48:21.856 --> 09:48:26.203
Make sure that you get a good hard core contraction every rep.

09:48:26.443 --> 09:48:29.367
If we relax that core, then we're going down to the floor.

09:48:29.628 --> 09:48:38.761
Now, if you have any type of wrist problems or this exercise makes your wrist sore, then let's just go to our elbows and do a regular plank on the ball.

09:48:39.082 --> 09:48:41.265
And then we got the same thing here.

09:48:41.245 --> 09:48:42.629
Hold, release.

09:48:43.371 --> 09:48:44.554
Hold, release.

09:48:44.755 --> 09:48:56.368
All right, we're gonna get the same stimulus to the core, so if you have those wrist problems or you're getting a ton of shots on the court and your wrists are a little bit tired from the amount of shots that you're getting, it's fine to just go on those elbows.

09:48:57.428 --> 09:49:01.875
So for my ankle toe crawls, what I'm going to do is get in a half kneeling position.

09:49:02.215 --> 09:49:07.383
I'm going to work on my ankle dorsiflexion by driving my knee as far as I can over the toe.

09:49:07.764 --> 09:49:10.308
And then I'm going to flow straight into my toe stretch.

09:49:10.348 --> 09:49:17.800
So I got to get this knee down to the floor while keeping my big toe and my other four toes down on the ground.

09:49:17.860 --> 09:49:19.302
So I'm here.

09:49:19.552 --> 09:49:23.461
Now look at that position that I'm in with the toe.

09:49:23.481 --> 09:49:26.549
So I'm challenging my toe mobility.

09:49:26.609 --> 09:49:33.685
So what I'm going to do is just crawl in this position and I can do anywhere from four to eight reps each leg.

09:49:33.725 --> 09:49:34.848
So I'm going to start at four.

09:49:35.470 --> 09:49:36.953
So I'm here.

09:49:37.304 --> 09:49:48.281
nice and slow, look how I crawl the hand forward, and then I ground that next foot, drive the knee over the toe, and gradually transition, all right?

09:49:48.562 --> 09:49:54.351
So I'm literally just crawling, and I'm challenging my ankle mobility,

09:49:54.331 --> 09:49:57.256
and my toe mobility as I go.

09:49:57.476 --> 09:50:00.321
So this is essential when it comes to getting shin angles.

09:50:00.341 --> 09:50:05.009
So you guys hear me talk a lot about the most explosive athletes have good shin angles.

09:50:05.330 --> 09:50:12.662
If you can get horizontal shin angles, we produce more horizontal force, which means we get somewhere faster, right?

09:50:12.682 --> 09:50:15.647
So horizontal force production is essential, but

09:50:15.627 --> 09:50:26.038
If you get here and you lock up, right, because you don't have the ankle mobility or you have the ankle mobility but you don't have the toe mobility, we can't get that shin angle.

09:50:26.358 --> 09:50:27.760
So now we have vertical shins.

09:50:28.280 --> 09:50:33.566
I don't care how much force you produce with vertical shins, you can't get horizontal.

09:50:33.986 --> 09:50:36.709
So half the battle is how much force we produce.

09:50:37.009 --> 09:50:40.673
The other half of the battle is the angle that we're producing the force.

09:50:41.033 --> 09:50:42.715
And so that's why we're training

09:50:42.695 --> 09:50:49.946
This ankle mobility, you see how I got to be able to get my knee far out over my toe, which takes dorsiflexion range of motion.

09:50:49.966 --> 09:50:54.893
But as I extend this hip, I need to have that toe range of motion.

09:50:54.954 --> 09:50:59.400
If that toe locks up, then I don't get full extension of the hip.

09:50:59.821 --> 09:51:10.457
So we need that dorsiflexion and we need that toe range of motion so that we can get these good shin angles and get full triple extension on our first step explosiveness.

09:51:10.437 --> 09:51:16.849
So we're going to do anywhere from four to eight reps, and we can do anywhere from two to four sets.

09:51:17.170 --> 09:51:19.995
Do this a couple days a week, up to three days a week.

09:51:20.255 --> 09:51:23.101
This is a good one that you can just add to your warm-ups.

09:51:23.341 --> 09:51:32.959
You can add this to your dynamic warm-ups, or if you're at home and you're bored watching TV, it's a great exercise to do just to challenge that ankle mobility and that toe mobility.

09:51:33.300 --> 09:51:33.981
Let's get it.

09:51:45.605 --> 09:51:48.691
So for my leg climbs, I'm going to go down on my back.

09:51:48.731 --> 09:51:50.854
I'm going to make sure that my back is nice and flat.

09:51:51.255 --> 09:51:53.820
I'm going to go about six inches off the ground with this left leg.

09:51:54.160 --> 09:51:58.228
And then I'm going to go straight up 90 degree angle with that right leg.

09:51:58.308 --> 09:51:59.630
Some of you will be a little less.

09:51:59.670 --> 09:52:00.271
That's okay.

09:52:00.632 --> 09:52:01.033
All right.

09:52:01.053 --> 09:52:03.938
So what I'm going to do is three grabs to the leg.

09:52:04.018 --> 09:52:07.444
So it's going to be left, right, left.

09:52:07.945 --> 09:52:08.727
All right.

09:52:08.747 --> 09:52:10.009
Left, right, left.

09:52:10.394 --> 09:52:13.647
left and I'm not really grabbing and pulling on my leg much.

09:52:14.069 --> 09:52:19.110
I'm just doing this so that I can really control that contraction from the abs.

09:52:19.411 --> 09:52:20.455
Alright, so left,

09:52:20.772 --> 09:52:23.416
Right, left, and come back down.

09:52:23.736 --> 09:52:26.721
Left, right, left, and come back down.

09:52:27.261 --> 09:52:29.124
So this is really isolating the abs.

09:52:29.404 --> 09:52:32.108
We're also getting a very good hamstring stretch.

09:52:32.549 --> 09:52:39.639
And for some of you, it'll be an opposite hip flexor stretch because when we're in that 90 degree angle, a lot of you guys like to lift that leg.

09:52:39.979 --> 09:52:46.349
So we're getting hip flexor, we're getting hamstring, and then you're gonna feel we're gonna get a huge abdominal contraction.

09:52:46.729 --> 09:52:47.370
Let's do it.

09:53:01.981 --> 09:53:07.071
So for my adductor stretch with rotation, I'm going to go straight out with one leg.

09:53:07.191 --> 09:53:08.513
I'm keeping this knee straight.

09:53:08.854 --> 09:53:11.078
I'm on the inside edge of that foot.

09:53:11.559 --> 09:53:18.252
I'm down on this right knee and then I'm going to sit my hip all the way back to my heel or as close as I can.

09:53:18.232 --> 09:53:35.363
elbows are going to be extended and then i'm just going to reach under as far as i can so the further i reach the bigger stretch i get in the inside of that left leg so i'm here reach and then rotate up as high as i can and right back into it so that's one rep

09:53:36.085 --> 09:53:38.128
That's two reps, all right?

09:53:39.009 --> 09:53:43.355
So finish the desired reps and then we're gonna switch over and do it on the other side.

09:53:43.675 --> 09:53:50.024
So the adductor muscles are one of the most commonly strained muscles in basketball and in a lot of other sports as well.

09:53:50.445 --> 09:54:00.078
It's extremely important because when we go to slide, we need enough flexibility to really get range of motion, but also not to pull those muscles.

09:54:00.418 --> 09:54:05.265
And also the adductor magnus is a hip extensor muscle, so it's important for the vertical jump

09:54:05.245 --> 09:54:09.712
It's important for any type of squatting, single leg squatting, double leg squatting.

09:54:10.153 --> 09:54:15.100
The adductors are extremely important and they're worked almost every single day.

09:54:15.180 --> 09:54:17.304
So we're always beating them down into the ground.

09:54:17.744 --> 09:54:23.473
Exercises like this are great for getting that flexibility to make sure that we don't injure these muscles.

09:54:48.192 --> 09:54:54.550
Okay, for the cable drop step rotation, I'm just going to use a cable start light and build up gradually.

09:54:54.570 --> 09:54:56.836
Or if you don't have a cable, you could use a band.

09:54:57.277 --> 09:55:02.372
I'm going to interlock my fingers over the handle and just step it out just a little bit.

09:55:02.713 --> 09:55:03.214
All right.

09:55:03.194 --> 09:55:05.660
We're gonna come into a quarter squat position.

09:55:05.721 --> 09:55:10.192
Now, to open up that way, I gotta drive off the right foot.

09:55:10.553 --> 09:55:15.907
I'm gonna open up and I'm gonna get that left foot down so that that toe is pointing that way, all right?

09:55:16.348 --> 09:55:20.258
I don't want you to go into rotation until that foot is down.

09:55:20.238 --> 09:55:26.370
So I'm here, I push into position, and then from here I can open up and explode there.

09:55:26.390 --> 09:55:34.827
Now on the way back, I'm coming halfway, hands right in front of the chest, fire up that core, two to three seconds, and then release back to the start.

09:55:35.849 --> 09:55:40.238
So it's foot down, explode, come back, hold.

09:55:40.218 --> 09:56:05.702
release alright so the reason that I want you to not just go into rotation but get the foot down first is we're then gonna work that kind of recoil core we're gonna work as the full fascia system instead of just muscular power alright so we're getting here and then we get a good recoil through the core and we get here and then coming back we get that pause and then release

09:56:05.682 --> 09:56:11.102
do the desired reps to one side, and then go back and do the desired reps to the other side.

09:56:11.503 --> 09:56:15.979
We should feel a burn through the core, especially the side in the obliques.

09:56:37.222 --> 09:56:41.146
So for my rotational rolling stretch, I'm setting up in my 90-90 position.

09:56:41.567 --> 09:56:44.931
So I'm 90 degrees here, and I'm 90 degrees here.

09:56:45.812 --> 09:56:54.261
Now from here, I'm going to bring my fist out, and I'm just going to squeeze the fist, get a little tension, get that core a little stability.

09:56:54.381 --> 09:56:57.745
So if you're loose in this position, you're not going to be able to move.

09:56:58.086 --> 09:57:00.108
So I want you to brace that core a little bit.

09:57:00.528 --> 09:57:03.752
Now from here, I'm just rolling to this other side.

09:57:04.711 --> 09:57:07.437
and I go straight up from here.

09:57:07.958 --> 09:57:15.314
So now I'm down, roll, and I'm up, get to that full hip extension at the top.

09:57:15.334 --> 09:57:19.142
Okay, so now it's just a smooth flow, straight over up,

09:57:19.696 --> 09:57:21.819
Straight over, up.

09:57:22.961 --> 09:57:25.865
If this is too difficult, you can use your hands for assistance.

09:57:25.885 --> 09:57:33.136
So you can come down, support with your hands, support with your hands, push off the right hand and come up.

09:57:33.857 --> 09:57:38.083
Support, support, push off the left hand and come up.

09:57:38.304 --> 09:57:45.214
But if you get that down, then go to hands either straight out or you could just go hands here in front of your chest.

09:57:45.194 --> 09:57:49.761
This drill is very good for training the internal and the external rotators of the hip.

09:57:50.061 --> 09:57:52.485
We should not feel lateral stress in the knee.

09:57:52.845 --> 09:57:58.694
So some people, if they do this the wrong way, they feel more of a pressure in the knee, either the medial or the lateral aspect.

09:57:58.975 --> 09:58:00.697
We do not want any stress in the knee.

09:58:00.938 --> 09:58:04.603
We should feel like the hips are the main joint being mobilized here.

09:58:24.698 --> 09:58:29.203
So for my lower back angels, so I'm just going to lay flat on the floor.

09:58:29.644 --> 09:58:33.308
Now from here, I'm going to bring my chest up and I'm going to bring my legs up.

09:58:33.328 --> 09:58:37.953
So I don't want my knees touching the floor and I don't want my chest touching the floor.

09:58:38.374 --> 09:58:44.901
Okay, hands straight above the head and then I'm going a circular motion down towards the hips.

09:58:45.081 --> 09:58:49.386
I'm facing the palms up and then I bring it back around at that top.

09:58:49.647 --> 09:58:51.689
I'm facing the palms down.

09:58:52.330 --> 09:58:54.452
All right, so I'm kind of making

09:58:55.377 --> 09:59:00.445
like a snow angel, but I'm staying up this whole time.

09:59:01.066 --> 09:59:04.953
And you're going to feel a good isometric contraction in the back.

09:59:05.314 --> 09:59:10.542
So the lower back, the erector muscles in the back, and then my glutes are going to be fired up.

09:59:10.923 --> 09:59:13.627
And you're actually going to feel a little bit in the hamstrings as well.

09:59:13.988 --> 09:59:18.275
So that full post chain is getting a good isometric contraction here.

09:59:18.696 --> 09:59:19.377
Let's do it.

09:59:28.655 --> 09:59:32.302
All right, so for my band straight leg raise, I'm gonna try to keep my knee pretty straight.

09:59:32.363 --> 09:59:34.206
I could have a slight bend if we need to.

09:59:34.567 --> 09:59:42.083
I'm gonna keep my foot dorsiflexed, so that means my toe back towards the head, the band is around the ball of the foot, and then I'm just going rapid with it.

09:59:42.744 --> 09:59:45.229
So let's go to a comfortable range.

09:59:45.412 --> 09:59:48.577
and then we can gradually start to raise it higher and higher.

09:59:48.617 --> 09:59:54.287
Make sure this opposite leg doesn't lift up, but this is not slow static stretching.

09:59:54.607 --> 09:59:55.809
This is very dynamic.

09:59:55.829 --> 09:59:59.996
We're working on that elasticity back in the hamstrings and in the glutes.

10:00:00.357 --> 10:00:01.699
So I'm just gonna build up

10:00:01.679 --> 10:00:22.422
that speed and get it nice and rapid there all right we should feel a stretch it shouldn't be complete discomfort so maybe like a 7 out of 10 or an 8 out of 10 we're getting a slight stretch if we push it to a 10 out of 10 you're probably going to get a strained muscle so push slightly out of your comfort zone but it shouldn't hurt so bad that it's unbearable let's do it

10:00:24.038 --> 10:00:25.542
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

10:00:25.702 --> 10:00:27.406
I have a long roller from Skills.

10:00:27.647 --> 10:00:34.524
You could do long or you could do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you could just get it from Walmart or Target.

10:00:34.885 --> 10:00:40.258
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

10:00:40.238 --> 10:00:59.614
so you could either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher

10:00:59.594 --> 10:01:06.465
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

10:01:06.485 --> 10:01:08.708
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

10:01:08.748 --> 10:01:10.150
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

10:01:10.491 --> 10:01:18.884
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

10:01:19.264 --> 10:01:26.395
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

10:01:26.375 --> 10:01:31.052
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

10:01:31.268 --> 10:01:33.050
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

10:01:33.350 --> 10:01:36.754
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

10:01:36.854 --> 10:01:45.064
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

10:01:45.124 --> 10:01:50.650
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

10:01:50.710 --> 10:01:53.854
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

10:01:53.914 --> 10:02:01.242
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

10:02:01.222 --> 10:02:07.513
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

10:02:08.234 --> 10:02:11.220
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

10:02:11.660 --> 10:02:16.910
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

10:02:17.370 --> 10:02:23.922
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

10:02:23.902 --> 10:02:26.808
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

10:02:26.988 --> 10:02:28.731
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

10:02:29.052 --> 10:02:42.838
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

10:02:44.590 --> 10:03:08.050
we could go bent bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll so after we're done with the foam rolling circuit i like to get out a lacrosse ball you could use a golf ball a baseball anything hard and i'm just going to really dig into that heel so i'm going to apply some pressure in there i'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot

10:03:08.030 --> 10:03:10.919
and I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

10:03:11.480 --> 10:03:19.244
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

10:03:19.585 --> 10:03:22.393
We got to really start to loosen that up.

10:03:23.774 --> 10:03:28.427
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

10:03:28.667 --> 10:03:32.197
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

10:03:32.217 --> 10:03:37.411
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

10:03:37.772 --> 10:03:39.437
Everything else is extra credit.

10:03:42.505 --> 10:03:46.250
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

10:03:46.530 --> 10:03:49.094
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

10:03:49.174 --> 10:03:53.139
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary, just in case you don't have much room.

10:03:53.640 --> 10:04:00.228
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

10:04:00.589 --> 10:04:04.254
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

10:04:04.294 --> 10:04:05.716
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

10:04:06.096 --> 10:04:09.220
And I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

10:04:09.581 --> 10:04:11.263
I'm not going too fast yet.

10:04:11.243 --> 10:04:13.448
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

10:04:13.708 --> 10:04:18.238
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

10:04:18.258 --> 10:04:22.446
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

10:04:22.847 --> 10:04:26.415
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

10:04:26.715 --> 10:04:28.960
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

10:04:28.980 --> 10:04:31.345
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

10:04:31.325 --> 10:04:56.640
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch i'm just gonna go over with the left leg i'm gonna come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

10:04:56.620 --> 10:05:04.268
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

10:05:07.960 --> 10:05:12.327
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

10:05:12.547 --> 10:05:17.856
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

10:05:18.157 --> 10:05:23.405
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

10:05:23.465 --> 10:05:24.707
I'm keeping that knee in.

10:05:25.128 --> 10:05:33.141
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

10:05:33.121 --> 10:05:54.977
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

10:05:55.885 --> 10:05:59.533
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

10:05:59.954 --> 10:06:02.360
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

10:06:02.380 --> 10:06:04.404
One, two, three.

10:06:04.464 --> 10:06:06.750
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

10:06:07.070 --> 10:06:09.095
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

10:06:09.135 --> 10:06:09.716
That's okay.

10:06:10.057 --> 10:06:11.641
Let's just get as low as we can there.

10:06:12.122 --> 10:06:13.084
One, two, three.

10:06:13.064 --> 10:06:39.561
three and back up so three there three there i'm gonna go five cycles and then i'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo i'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and i'm going down i'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because i want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat

10:06:39.541 --> 10:06:43.406
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

10:06:43.867 --> 10:06:47.872
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

10:06:48.212 --> 10:06:53.739
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

10:06:54.360 --> 10:07:08.518
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

10:07:13.527 --> 10:07:20.056
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

10:07:20.076 --> 10:07:21.538
Don't make the mistake of going here.

10:07:21.918 --> 10:07:29.969
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

10:07:30.090 --> 10:07:35.958
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

10:07:36.038 --> 10:07:39.923
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

10:07:39.903 --> 10:07:41.647
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

10:07:41.887 --> 10:07:43.831
One, two, three.

10:07:43.972 --> 10:07:46.938
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

10:07:47.118 --> 10:07:49.343
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

10:07:49.984 --> 10:07:51.688
One, two, three.

10:07:51.748 --> 10:07:53.251
Rock forward.

10:07:53.331 --> 10:07:54.373
So I'm on my toes now.

10:07:54.854 --> 10:07:57.079
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

10:07:57.119 --> 10:07:57.440
Hold.

10:07:58.081 --> 10:07:58.502
One, two.

10:07:58.482 --> 10:08:17.638
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're gonna go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

10:08:17.939 --> 10:08:27.350
one, two, three, lean, one, two, three, heels back down, one, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

10:08:27.630 --> 10:08:35.959
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

10:08:35.979 --> 10:08:40.344
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

10:08:40.705 --> 10:08:47.052
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

10:08:47.032 --> 10:08:49.836
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

10:08:49.896 --> 10:08:52.219
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

10:08:52.619 --> 10:08:55.383
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

10:08:55.723 --> 10:08:57.425
I need to be flat through the hips.

10:08:57.706 --> 10:09:00.129
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

10:09:00.650 --> 10:09:05.095
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

10:09:05.456 --> 10:09:08.260
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

10:09:08.520 --> 10:09:16.330
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

10:09:16.310 --> 10:09:18.694
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

10:09:19.756 --> 10:09:26.288
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

10:09:26.729 --> 10:09:30.876
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

10:09:30.897 --> 10:09:32.860
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

10:09:32.880 --> 10:09:35.044
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

10:09:51.342 --> 10:09:55.206
So for my one leg hurdle jumps, I'm just going to set up two hurdles.

10:09:55.547 --> 10:09:58.330
I'm going to set them up about 15 feet apart to start.

10:09:58.570 --> 10:10:02.835
That's kind of the easier version and then we're going to mix up that distance later on.

10:10:03.015 --> 10:10:05.679
So I'm going with the high hurdles from Champion Sports.

10:10:05.759 --> 10:10:06.660
You don't need these.

10:10:06.980 --> 10:10:09.943
All you need is a small hurdle, even a pair of shoes.

10:10:10.024 --> 10:10:10.985
It doesn't really matter.

10:10:11.345 --> 10:10:19.054
All that matters is that you're jumping as high as you can and that we have some sort of marker so that we can work on our timing and our steps.

10:10:19.034 --> 10:10:22.622
I'm going to jump over, so I'm going to get a running start, I don't care how far it is.

10:10:23.564 --> 10:10:28.695
Running start, jump off the left, get over that hurdle, now you're going to land on your right.

10:10:28.975 --> 10:10:32.803
So then it would be right, left, right, left, take off again.

10:10:32.823 --> 10:10:39.237
So we're always taking off with our left, and then we get over and we land with our right, okay?

10:10:39.217 --> 10:10:46.835
So, so much of the jump is timing, rhythm and being able to take short steps and then long steps.

10:10:47.256 --> 10:10:55.757
And so the easier version is where we have this set distance and it's like at least 15 feet apart so we can get those multiple steps in between.

10:10:55.737 --> 10:10:59.563
Now as you get good at this, I want you to mess around with that distance.

10:10:59.884 --> 10:11:01.086
So start to shorten it up.

10:11:01.466 --> 10:11:08.799
Maybe you put it, you know, 8 to 10 feet away and now all you can do is get a right-left jump.

10:11:09.079 --> 10:11:10.662
So I want you to split it up by days.

10:11:10.722 --> 10:11:16.311
I think the first day that you do this drill, let's just keep it long distance to where it's the most comfortable

10:11:16.291 --> 10:11:38.502
for you we can get multiple steps the next day maybe shorten it up a little bit and then the next day shorten it up even more to the point where we're getting over this first hurdle and all we can do is get right left jump all right so i want you to mix it up the more you mix up the distance the harder it's going to be for you and the more versatile we're going to be with our jumps

10:11:48.758 --> 10:11:53.749
So for my rapid crossover step, what I'm gonna do is just start here, nice and low.

10:11:54.049 --> 10:11:55.292
It's gonna be a crossover step.

10:11:55.532 --> 10:11:57.376
So right foot goes over the left.

10:11:57.777 --> 10:11:59.020
I gotta swivel the hips.

10:11:59.441 --> 10:12:02.267
So if you don't swivel the hips, it's gonna look like this.

10:12:02.387 --> 10:12:04.371
It's not gonna look very athletic.

10:12:04.351 --> 10:12:07.216
You got to be able to keep your chest relatively forward.

10:12:07.256 --> 10:12:09.940
It's going to turn a little bit and turn the hips.

10:12:10.321 --> 10:12:13.987
We got to dissociate between the hips and the torso.

10:12:14.127 --> 10:12:15.950
So it's going to be crossover step.

10:12:16.371 --> 10:12:16.711
Boom.

10:12:17.192 --> 10:12:18.254
Now I'm going to plant.

10:12:18.855 --> 10:12:20.537
Notice it's all about this shin angle.

10:12:20.618 --> 10:12:23.342
So I got to get that good steep shin angle.

10:12:23.362 --> 10:12:28.230
I'm already leaning in this direction and now it's going to be left foot crossover step.

10:12:28.290 --> 10:12:29.532
So boom.

10:12:29.731 --> 10:12:31.973
Boom, boom.

10:12:31.993 --> 10:12:34.316
All right, we're just going back and forth.

10:12:34.336 --> 10:12:39.701
It's all about how quick and how explosively we can plant and change direction.

10:12:39.721 --> 10:12:54.136
So we're getting that lateral change of direction, working on that crossover step, working on dissociating the hips, so getting a hip rotation without a big torso rotation, which is absolutely essential in all sports and especially basketball.

10:12:54.556 --> 10:12:55.317
Let's do it.

10:13:10.417 --> 10:13:16.066
So for the dumbbell power skips, we're gonna go anywhere from a 10 pound dumbbell to a 50 pound dumbbell.

10:13:16.327 --> 10:13:23.158
We've never had an athlete who needed more than 50 pounds because again, this is all about reactiveness, all about quickness off the floor.

10:13:23.519 --> 10:13:26.704
So I generally go around 25 pounds for these.

10:13:26.684 --> 10:13:29.007
So I'm gonna start high with the right foot.

10:13:29.408 --> 10:13:33.573
I'm gonna go into the floor and it's gonna be a low skip right, high skip left.

10:13:33.874 --> 10:13:37.799
So every time my left comes down and hits the floor, I get up as high as I can.

10:13:38.260 --> 10:13:40.523
So it's right, low, left, high.

10:13:40.803 --> 10:13:42.706
Right, low, left, high.

10:13:45.229 --> 10:13:46.431
So your right foot

10:13:47.170 --> 10:13:48.652
is just your recovery foot.

10:13:48.953 --> 10:13:51.236
Your left leg is really the working leg here.

10:13:51.877 --> 10:13:54.000
And then on the way back, I'm gonna switch it.

10:13:54.440 --> 10:13:55.942
So now I start left high.

10:13:56.503 --> 10:13:58.847
I'm gonna go left, low, right, get up.

10:13:59.307 --> 10:14:01.190
Left, low, right, get up.

10:14:01.851 --> 10:14:04.895
Key component here is the stiffness aspect.

10:14:05.576 --> 10:14:09.081
As I'm going into that jump, I don't want the heel to collapse.

10:14:09.101 --> 10:14:13.667
I gotta stay strong on the ball of the foot without the heel hitting the ground.

10:14:14.429 --> 10:14:15.490
On the high jump,

10:14:15.908 --> 10:14:20.356
I'm going to land at about a 45 degree angle and I'm going to push up from there.

10:14:20.857 --> 10:14:24.203
So I don't want you striding out and getting really low.

10:14:24.704 --> 10:14:31.656
I want you to produce high power with a short range of motion, just like we often have to do in a game off one foot.

10:14:42.554 --> 10:14:48.743
So for our Peterson Step Up, so the classic old school exercise, it's called a Peterson Step Up.

10:14:48.763 --> 10:14:53.970
Basically, we come down to tap that heel and we come to the forefoot.

10:14:54.291 --> 10:14:58.177
So it's here and up, here and up.

10:14:58.197 --> 10:15:03.364
We're going to add a band, so we're combining the Peterson Step Up with the TKE.

10:15:05.572 --> 10:15:16.751
So if I'm working my right foot and I'm elevated on that small box or you could use like bumper plates or something that is small that we can elevate with, if you have an inclined slant board, that's even better.

10:15:16.851 --> 10:15:18.274
So we would do that on the slant board.

10:15:18.775 --> 10:15:21.960
So I'm gonna get some good tension on here and I'm coming up.

10:15:21.940 --> 10:15:32.582
Now from here, I'm just going to come down and I can actually set that foot down a little bit so that I can focus on getting that knee out over the toe and coming to the ball of the foot.

10:15:32.862 --> 10:15:41.199
And then I'm going to push back and then at the top I'm going to really squeeze that quad and we're really going to get that VMO nice and activated.

10:15:41.279 --> 10:15:42.582
So it's here.

10:15:42.562 --> 10:15:44.344
push-up, squeeze.

10:15:44.945 --> 10:15:51.873
The exercise is slow and controlled and we should feel a very big burn in the quads.

10:15:52.134 --> 10:16:01.225
Now if you get any type of knee pain, if you have patellar tendonitis, when we get that knee out over the toe in that position, it might be a little bit too much stress.

10:16:01.265 --> 10:16:07.152
So we would skip this exercise and we would just go back to a normal supported TKE.

10:16:08.296 --> 10:16:11.745
So I would not go elevated and I would not bring this foot up.

10:16:12.005 --> 10:16:17.139
This would be my stable foot and then I would just come to the toe and bring it back and squeeze.

10:16:17.159 --> 10:16:19.645
So you're not gonna feel any pain in the knee there.

10:16:20.006 --> 10:16:21.650
It's just gonna be burn in the quad.

10:16:22.091 --> 10:16:23.976
But with that Peterson step up,

10:16:23.956 --> 10:16:34.914
If we can get an 8 or a 9 out of 10 burn in the quads with a 1 or a 2 out of 10 pain in the knee, then that's probably going to be a pretty good exercise for you.

10:16:35.315 --> 10:16:39.282
I just don't want it to be a 4 out of 10, a 5 out of 10 pain in the knee.

10:16:39.622 --> 10:16:42.948
Then we know we're putting excess stress on that patellar tendon.

10:16:43.168 --> 10:16:44.390
All right, let's train hard.

10:16:44.771 --> 10:16:46.654
Most importantly, we want to train smart.

10:16:47.075 --> 10:16:47.736
Let's get it.

10:17:28.081 --> 10:17:32.127
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

10:17:32.227 --> 10:17:34.030
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

10:17:34.070 --> 10:17:36.794
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

10:17:36.814 --> 10:17:43.343
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

10:17:43.864 --> 10:17:49.812
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

10:17:50.073 --> 10:17:53.818
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

10:17:53.798 --> 10:17:57.726
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

10:17:58.167 --> 10:18:01.995
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

10:18:02.556 --> 10:18:09.951
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

10:18:10.910 --> 10:18:13.974
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

10:18:13.994 --> 10:18:20.724
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

10:18:21.265 --> 10:18:27.033
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

10:18:27.053 --> 10:18:30.719
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

10:18:33.022 --> 10:18:37.288
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

10:18:40.255 --> 10:18:42.157
and that stomach will fall, okay?

10:18:42.878 --> 10:18:45.521
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

10:18:46.282 --> 10:18:55.291
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

10:18:56.092 --> 10:19:04.081
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

10:19:04.101 --> 10:19:08.085
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

10:19:08.268 --> 10:19:37.026
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

10:19:37.259 --> 10:19:39.764
happens in the parasympathetic state.

10:19:40.305 --> 10:19:44.453
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

10:19:44.794 --> 10:19:58.440
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

10:20:00.209 --> 10:20:03.513
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

10:20:03.533 --> 10:20:07.819
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

10:20:08.099 --> 10:20:14.908
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

10:20:15.208 --> 10:20:18.693
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

10:20:18.973 --> 10:20:26.503
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

10:20:26.483 --> 10:20:30.815
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

10:20:31.236 --> 10:20:33.221
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

10:20:33.542 --> 10:20:35.748
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

10:20:36.029 --> 10:20:38.095
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

10:20:38.375 --> 10:20:40.601
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

10:20:40.882 --> 10:20:42.687
I'm just gonna lean forward.

10:20:42.667 --> 10:21:03.443
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

10:21:03.760 --> 10:21:05.603
15 seconds on the other side as well.

10:21:05.743 --> 10:21:08.047
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

10:21:08.067 --> 10:21:09.489
So that is the back of the thigh.

10:21:09.729 --> 10:21:14.036
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

10:21:14.056 --> 10:21:17.741
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

10:21:17.902 --> 10:21:20.325
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

10:21:20.666 --> 10:21:22.989
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

10:21:23.350 --> 10:21:24.892
So now look at this back foot.

10:21:24.932 --> 10:21:26.595
I'm not curling the toe under.

10:21:26.575 --> 10:21:30.419
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

10:21:30.979 --> 10:21:34.243
And I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

10:21:34.663 --> 10:21:40.008
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

10:21:40.329 --> 10:21:53.562
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

10:21:53.542 --> 10:21:56.870
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

10:21:56.890 --> 10:21:58.234
That's the inside of the leg.

10:21:58.715 --> 10:22:02.083
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

10:22:02.685 --> 10:22:03.366
Same thing.

10:22:03.707 --> 10:22:06.594
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

10:22:06.895 --> 10:22:10.183
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

10:22:10.163 --> 10:22:14.169
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

10:22:14.469 --> 10:22:22.080
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

10:22:22.481 --> 10:22:27.949
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

10:22:28.369 --> 10:22:33.657
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

10:22:33.637 --> 10:22:38.424
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

10:22:38.825 --> 10:22:46.477
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

10:22:46.978 --> 10:22:49.782
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

10:22:50.223 --> 10:22:56.252
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

10:22:56.232 --> 10:22:59.275
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

10:22:59.375 --> 10:23:01.517
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

10:23:01.758 --> 10:23:05.421
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

10:23:05.882 --> 10:23:07.463
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

10:23:07.844 --> 10:23:10.406
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

10:23:10.467 --> 10:23:11.367
So I'm 90 there.

10:23:11.728 --> 10:23:14.531
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

10:23:14.871 --> 10:23:17.173
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

10:23:17.534 --> 10:23:22.239
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

10:23:22.579 --> 10:23:25.682
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward

10:23:25.662 --> 10:23:28.607
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

10:23:28.968 --> 10:23:33.737
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

10:23:33.777 --> 10:23:35.220
All right.

10:23:35.240 --> 10:23:41.111
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

10:23:41.872 --> 10:23:45.679
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

10:23:45.659 --> 10:23:50.989
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

10:23:51.370 --> 10:23:54.596
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

10:23:54.696 --> 10:23:57.862
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

10:23:58.263 --> 10:24:08.022
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

10:24:08.002 --> 10:24:14.757
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

10:24:15.038 --> 10:24:21.212
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

10:24:21.613 --> 10:24:25.943
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

10:24:26.733 --> 10:24:29.862
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

10:24:29.902 --> 10:24:30.644
That's okay.

10:24:31.005 --> 10:24:34.194
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

10:24:34.234 --> 10:24:38.245
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

10:24:40.942 --> 10:24:46.369
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase eight, day two and day five.

10:24:46.690 --> 10:24:48.912
So this is our lower body strength day.

10:24:50.234 --> 10:24:55.041
If we are in a rush, we can skip our foam roller circuit, do the dynamic warm up.

10:24:55.061 --> 10:24:58.886
We can skip the Peterson step up, great exercise.

10:24:59.126 --> 10:25:03.051
If you have any type of knee problems or you're prone to patellar tendonitis,

10:25:03.031 --> 10:25:08.759
you could revert back to the TKEs, which I showed in the video.

10:25:08.899 --> 10:25:16.469
The Peterson step up is a little higher stress to the knees, but that's also good for injury prevention if your knees can handle that stress.

10:25:17.811 --> 10:25:18.652
Hang high pull.

10:25:19.072 --> 10:25:21.816
Of course, we got to keep this exercise in there.

10:25:21.796 --> 10:25:30.801
If you want to throw in a med ball high toss, a sandbag high toss, or something like that as an alternative, that's fine.

10:25:31.282 --> 10:25:34.250
All that matters is that we're getting powerful triple extension.

10:25:34.832 --> 10:25:37.780
The high pull gets us that, but there's other options there.

10:25:37.760 --> 10:25:42.286
One leg RDL swing, definitely keep this exercise in.

10:25:42.806 --> 10:25:47.432
Rear foot elevated split squat, this is our meat and potatoes heavy strength lift for the day.

10:25:48.033 --> 10:25:55.241
You'll notice it's only three reps, so we're really after high intensity, max intent, and loading up heavy.

10:25:56.163 --> 10:25:58.045
Nordic curl, do not skip that.

10:25:58.065 --> 10:26:00.928
7-11 breathing, always do it.

10:26:01.509 --> 10:26:05.314
Static stretch, if you're already flexible, we can skip that exercise.

10:26:05.634 --> 10:26:06.315
Let's get it.

10:26:06.295 --> 10:26:22.633
we're going to get through a foam roller circuit i have a long roller from skills you could do long or you could do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from skills online or you could just get it from walmart or target so the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll

10:26:22.613 --> 10:26:41.989
so you could either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher

10:26:41.969 --> 10:26:48.496
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

10:26:48.857 --> 10:26:51.099
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

10:26:51.119 --> 10:26:52.521
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

10:26:52.861 --> 10:27:01.251
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

10:27:01.631 --> 10:27:08.719
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

10:27:08.699 --> 10:27:13.444
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

10:27:13.643 --> 10:27:15.425
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

10:27:15.725 --> 10:27:19.129
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

10:27:19.209 --> 10:27:27.439
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

10:27:27.499 --> 10:27:33.025
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

10:27:33.085 --> 10:27:36.269
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

10:27:36.289 --> 10:27:43.617
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

10:27:43.597 --> 10:27:49.888
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

10:27:50.609 --> 10:27:53.595
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

10:27:54.035 --> 10:27:59.285
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

10:27:59.745 --> 10:28:06.297
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

10:28:06.277 --> 10:28:09.184
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

10:28:09.384 --> 10:28:11.108
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

10:28:11.429 --> 10:28:25.180
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

10:28:26.965 --> 10:28:33.775
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

10:28:33.976 --> 10:28:37.521
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

10:28:37.842 --> 10:28:41.107
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

10:28:41.647 --> 10:28:44.352
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

10:28:44.392 --> 10:28:46.034
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

10:28:46.355 --> 10:28:50.161
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

10:28:50.621 --> 10:28:53.766
And I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

10:28:53.746 --> 10:29:01.593
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

10:29:01.934 --> 10:29:04.636
We got to really start to loosen that up.

10:29:06.218 --> 10:29:10.802
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

10:29:11.042 --> 10:29:14.565
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

10:29:15.306 --> 10:29:19.770
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

10:29:20.130 --> 10:29:21.812
Everything else is extra credit.

10:29:24.863 --> 10:29:48.323
the first thing we're going to do for our dynamic warm-up is stationary skips now for all of these you can take them on the move if you want for right now i'm just going to do them stationary just in case you don't have much room so we're just going up with the right up with the left just like we're running and vice versa on that other side all right i'm staying stiff with the feet so i'm not letting my heel collapse i want to stay on the ball of the foot

10:29:48.303 --> 10:29:51.568
And I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

10:29:51.908 --> 10:29:53.370
I'm not going too fast yet.

10:29:53.691 --> 10:29:55.834
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

10:29:56.074 --> 10:30:00.621
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

10:30:00.641 --> 10:30:04.807
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

10:30:05.228 --> 10:30:08.793
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

10:30:09.093 --> 10:30:10.876
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

10:30:11.236 --> 10:30:13.720
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here.

10:30:13.700 --> 10:30:38.982
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch I'm just gonna go over with the left leg I'm gonna come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

10:30:38.962 --> 10:30:46.710
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

10:30:50.352 --> 10:30:54.699
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

10:30:54.919 --> 10:31:00.248
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

10:31:00.529 --> 10:31:07.079
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

10:31:07.500 --> 10:31:15.533
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

10:31:15.513 --> 10:31:20.442
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

10:31:20.782 --> 10:31:26.392
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

10:31:27.074 --> 10:31:29.438
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

10:31:29.758 --> 10:31:34.387
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

10:31:35.028 --> 10:31:37.352
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

10:31:38.260 --> 10:31:41.909
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

10:31:42.330 --> 10:31:44.716
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

10:31:44.756 --> 10:31:46.781
One, two, three.

10:31:46.841 --> 10:31:49.127
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

10:31:49.447 --> 10:31:51.472
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

10:31:51.492 --> 10:31:52.114
That's okay.

10:31:52.435 --> 10:31:54.019
Let's just get as low as we can there.

10:31:54.500 --> 10:31:55.442
One, two, three.

10:31:55.422 --> 10:32:07.655
Three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side Five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo.

10:32:07.675 --> 10:32:21.970
I'm going lateral with one skip So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor Keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

10:32:21.950 --> 10:32:25.775
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

10:32:26.236 --> 10:32:30.241
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

10:32:30.582 --> 10:32:36.110
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

10:32:36.730 --> 10:32:50.910
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

10:32:55.902 --> 10:33:02.432
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

10:33:02.452 --> 10:33:03.935
Don't make the mistake of going here.

10:33:04.295 --> 10:33:12.348
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

10:33:12.468 --> 10:33:18.337
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

10:33:18.417 --> 10:33:22.163
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

10:33:22.143 --> 10:33:24.007
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

10:33:24.247 --> 10:33:26.191
One, two, three.

10:33:26.351 --> 10:33:29.317
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

10:33:29.498 --> 10:33:31.702
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

10:33:32.344 --> 10:33:34.067
One, two, three.

10:33:34.127 --> 10:33:35.610
Rock forward.

10:33:35.650 --> 10:33:36.752
So I'm on my toes now.

10:33:37.233 --> 10:33:39.418
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

10:33:39.498 --> 10:33:39.819
Hold.

10:33:40.440 --> 10:33:41.502
One, two, three.

10:33:41.482 --> 10:33:42.183
Three.

10:33:42.504 --> 10:33:43.226
Rock it back.

10:33:43.586 --> 10:33:45.370
Heels back down to the ground.

10:33:45.651 --> 10:33:47.274
One, two, three.

10:33:47.374 --> 10:33:48.577
I need one hand up.

10:33:49.138 --> 10:33:49.919
Two hand up.

10:33:50.160 --> 10:33:50.821
Not out here.

10:33:50.881 --> 10:33:52.364
We need it back behind the head.

10:33:52.946 --> 10:33:53.587
And stand.

10:33:54.128 --> 10:33:54.389
Alright.

10:33:54.849 --> 10:33:56.232
So that's one cycle.

10:33:56.493 --> 10:33:57.595
We're going to go for three of these.

10:33:58.557 --> 10:33:59.980
So it's one, two, three.

10:34:00.331 --> 10:34:29.393
one two three lean one two three heels back down one two three one up two up stand all right if you cannot get down in that deep position then let's just go as low as we can right here and then on the lean it's just gonna look like this all right and then we're gonna come back hopefully parallel to the floor one up two up stand we just got to get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower better range of motion so

10:34:29.373 --> 10:34:32.216
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

10:34:32.276 --> 10:34:34.579
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

10:34:34.979 --> 10:34:37.782
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

10:34:38.103 --> 10:34:39.804
I need to be flat through the hips.

10:34:40.085 --> 10:34:42.487
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

10:34:43.008 --> 10:34:47.453
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

10:34:47.833 --> 10:34:50.876
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

10:34:50.896 --> 10:34:58.705
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

10:34:58.685 --> 10:35:01.050
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

10:35:02.132 --> 10:35:08.645
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

10:35:09.106 --> 10:35:13.254
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

10:35:13.274 --> 10:35:15.238
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

10:35:15.258 --> 10:35:19.005
So we've got to get them revved up for our session.

10:35:26.528 --> 10:35:32.736
So for our Peterson Step Up, so the classic old school exercise, it's called a Peterson Step Up.

10:35:32.756 --> 10:35:37.963
Basically, we come down to tap that heel and we come to the forefoot.

10:35:38.283 --> 10:35:42.168
So it's here and up, here and up.

10:35:42.188 --> 10:35:47.355
We're going to add a band, so we're combining the Peterson Step Up with the TKE.

10:35:49.563 --> 10:36:00.763
So if I'm working my right foot and I'm elevated on that small box or you could use like bumper plates or something that is small that we can elevate with, if you have an inclined slant board, that's even better.

10:36:00.863 --> 10:36:02.266
So we would do that on the slant board.

10:36:02.766 --> 10:36:05.952
So I'm gonna get some good tension on here and I'm coming up.

10:36:05.932 --> 10:36:16.573
Now from here, I'm just gonna come down and I can actually set that foot down a little bit so that I can focus on getting that knee out over the toe and coming to the ball of the foot.

10:36:16.854 --> 10:36:25.190
And then I'm gonna push back and then at the top, I'm gonna really squeeze that quad and we're really gonna get that VMO nice and activated.

10:36:25.270 --> 10:36:26.573
So it's here.

10:36:26.553 --> 10:36:28.335
push up, squeeze.

10:36:28.956 --> 10:36:35.863
The exercise is slow and controlled and we should feel a very big burn in the quads.

10:36:36.124 --> 10:36:45.234
Now if you get any type of knee pain, if you have patellar tendonitis, when we get that knee out over the toe in that position, it might be a little bit too much stress.

10:36:45.254 --> 10:36:51.160
So we would skip this exercise and we would just go back to a normal supported TKE.

10:36:52.304 --> 10:36:55.973
So I would not go elevated and I would not bring this foot up.

10:36:55.993 --> 10:37:01.106
This would be my stable foot and then I would just come to the toe and bring it back and squeeze.

10:37:01.147 --> 10:37:03.673
So you're not gonna feel any pain in the knee there.

10:37:03.994 --> 10:37:05.638
It's just gonna be burn in the quad.

10:37:06.099 --> 10:37:07.984
But with that Peterson step up,

10:37:07.964 --> 10:37:18.901
If we can get an 8 or a 9 out of 10 burn in the quads with a 1 or a 2 out of 10 pain in the knee, then that's probably going to be a pretty good exercise for you.

10:37:19.321 --> 10:37:23.267
I just don't want it to be a 4 out of 10, a 5 out of 10 pain in the knee.

10:37:23.628 --> 10:37:26.953
Then we know we're putting excess stress on that patellar tendon.

10:37:27.153 --> 10:37:28.375
All right, let's train hard.

10:37:28.756 --> 10:37:30.618
Most importantly, we want to train smart.

10:37:31.039 --> 10:37:31.660
Let's get it.

10:37:44.262 --> 10:37:48.368
So for my hang high pull, we can either use a trap bar or a barbell.

10:37:48.388 --> 10:37:49.851
I would prefer a trap bar.

10:37:50.411 --> 10:37:52.615
But if you don't have a trap bar, we could use a barbell.

10:37:52.655 --> 10:37:54.538
So I'm going to go with a hook grip.

10:37:54.798 --> 10:38:00.106
So that means I'm bringing my thumb around the bar and then I'm bringing my finger over that thumb.

10:38:00.287 --> 10:38:02.991
Or some people like to go two fingers over that thumb.

10:38:03.271 --> 10:38:05.014
That way it doesn't slip out of our hands.

10:38:05.435 --> 10:38:10.202
So with my arm length, I like to go right around the seams.

10:38:10.182 --> 10:38:34.975
on the barbell now a lot of you have longer arms you might want to go a little bit wider that's okay so I'm just going to deadlift it up all right now this is my base position notice I have a slight bend in the knee from there think about our RDL that we've been doing where we shift the hip backwards so I don't go down by bending my knees and if you do that by bending your knees you're actually going to

10:38:34.955 --> 10:38:36.860
feel a little pain in your back.

10:38:36.880 --> 10:38:41.511
So I want you to keep that initial knee bend, and then from there it's just hips back.

10:38:41.532 --> 10:38:43.396
So I'm hinging from the hips.

10:38:43.777 --> 10:38:51.276
Once that bar gets right above knee height, I'm exploding as hard as I can, and I'm just throwing this bar upwards.

10:38:51.931 --> 10:38:53.114
like that, okay?

10:38:53.434 --> 10:38:56.300
So I wanna end in this position.

10:38:56.741 --> 10:39:02.915
Now, it's not a lift where we do the hinge, we come here, and then we lift with the upper body.

10:39:03.416 --> 10:39:11.273
All of that momentum is coming from the lower body, transferring through the core into the upper body, so I need it to be fast and explosive.

10:39:11.253 --> 10:39:38.543
so we're here and then boom finish high you finish on the toes like you're going to take off for a jump but your feet aren't going to actually leave the ground okay this is a advanced exercise now if you've been Olympic lifting you're probably familiar with brushing that bar so you can kind of hit it on the top of the thigh and pop up the majority of us we don't need to worry about that right now because that's going to take a long time to learn and it's going to hurt your thighs for a while in the process so

10:39:38.523 --> 10:39:45.795
I'd rather you just think about getting to that good RDL position, that good hinge position, and then just exploding as hard as we can.

10:39:46.355 --> 10:39:53.046
Only thing, if you're not going to brush and you're not going to hit the top of the thigh, I don't want you to bring the barbell out here.

10:39:53.086 --> 10:39:58.415
I still need it to be on one line all the way up, close to the body.

10:39:58.655 --> 10:40:02.822
If we get it out away from the body, we're probably going to feel it more in the lower back.

10:40:03.122 --> 10:40:04.885
We're going to decrease our power output.

10:40:04.865 --> 10:40:06.587
Now, there's a couple different options here.

10:40:07.168 --> 10:40:15.017
After we finish, we can either just drop the barbell if you're in a gym that allows that and you have bumper plates, or you can actually catch it.

10:40:15.297 --> 10:40:22.125
So catching it is higher stress to the body, but it's also good because you get a little eccentric work on the catch, so it's not a bad thing.

10:40:22.525 --> 10:40:29.353
Personally, since I have the bumper plates, I prefer to rip as hard as I can, drop it, and then pick it back up and go from there.

10:40:29.333 --> 10:40:43.201
Okay, now if you have a trap bar, it's going to be the same thing, except slight bend in the knees, hinge from the hips, go all the way until your knuckles are even with your knee, and then it's going to explode from there all the way up to the toes.

10:40:43.783 --> 10:40:46.448
Toss that barbell up.

10:40:46.664 --> 10:40:49.748
On the barbell, we would normally finish here.

10:40:50.289 --> 10:40:52.331
With the trap bar, we finish here.

10:40:52.712 --> 10:40:56.157
So the elbows go backwards instead of out to the side.

10:40:57.298 --> 10:41:13.139
Now, if these are too advanced for you because this is several years of strength training before you should really be doing explosive hang high pulls, if that's the case, I want you to go hinge and explode to the toes.

10:41:13.539 --> 10:41:14.781
So it's just hinge here.

10:41:15.419 --> 10:41:20.728
explode to the toes, and then back to that slight bend in the knee, hinge, boom, explode to the toes.

10:41:21.069 --> 10:41:25.116
You're really thinking about pulling the hips through, squeezing the glutes.

10:41:25.517 --> 10:41:30.625
This is going to set a good foundation and really build towards that hang high pull.

10:41:30.886 --> 10:41:32.128
All right, let's get it.

10:41:50.705 --> 10:41:58.197
So for my one leg RDL swing, what I'm going to do is hold two dumbbells by my side, come to an RDL position.

10:41:58.258 --> 10:42:04.067
So that's one leg, slight bend in the knee, of course, from there it's a hip hinge, we're not going down with the knee.

10:42:04.748 --> 10:42:13.122
Once I get down to that bottom position, which right now is going to be hands past the knee, so the dumbbells are going to be about mid-shin for most of us.

10:42:13.303 --> 10:42:16.628
If you're a little more tight, it's okay to just go down to your knees.

10:42:16.608 --> 10:42:22.119
So I'm gonna get down to that position and then I'm gonna aggressively swing as fast as I can.

10:42:22.139 --> 10:42:27.309
I'm gonna keep my elbow straight and I'm gonna swing the dumbbells up over my head.

10:42:27.650 --> 10:42:35.726
I'm gonna bring it down while I'm still on one leg to challenge our stability and then bring that other foot down, bring the dumbbells back down, okay?

10:42:36.107 --> 10:42:38.211
So it's slight bend.

10:42:38.191 --> 10:43:02.545
RDL as fast as we can explode up to that top position do not think about doing an RDL and then a lift with the arms the arms are just swinging from the momentum of that aggressive explosive hip extension okay so it's here swing them stabilize at the top bring it down come all the way down

10:43:02.525 --> 10:43:10.018
Now, this is a very advanced exercise, so if you don't have the stability to do it on one leg, I'd rather you come to a split stance.

10:43:10.138 --> 10:43:15.667
So, if you did it in a split stance, we would be here in a little baby split.

10:43:16.288 --> 10:43:21.597
My feet have to be pretty narrow, I can't be very wide, otherwise I'm going to hit the dumbbell on my leg.

10:43:22.235 --> 10:43:23.437
Here, baby split.

10:43:23.497 --> 10:43:25.120
From there, it's a hip hinge.

10:43:25.501 --> 10:43:27.064
Notice I'm on the ball of the foot.

10:43:27.124 --> 10:43:31.552
I'm not on my heel on that back leg, but I am on my heel on the front leg.

10:43:31.612 --> 10:43:35.018
So it's gonna be here and then boom, explode.

10:43:35.479 --> 10:43:37.483
Come back down and reset.

10:43:38.004 --> 10:43:38.725
Boom.

10:43:38.705 --> 10:43:41.789
explode, come back down, reset.

10:43:42.450 --> 10:43:48.598
Now, if you're gonna do that single leg RDL, start very light, start five or 10 pound dumbbells in each hand.

10:43:49.359 --> 10:43:51.622
Once you're good to go, then you can start to go up.

10:43:51.963 --> 10:43:54.586
Most of our athletes don't get past 20 pounds.

10:43:54.606 --> 10:43:57.270
20 pounds is a pretty good weight.

10:43:57.250 --> 10:44:03.819
as far as really just utilizing that hip extension velocity, hamstring and glute, instead of using the arms.

10:44:04.140 --> 10:44:09.067
If you start to get too heavy, you're going to feel like you're really working your arms to lift, alright?

10:44:09.448 --> 10:44:18.060
So, start light, gradually go up, let's get it.

10:44:24.875 --> 10:44:30.663
So for our rear foot elevated split squat, if you have a Smith machine, I advise that we use that.

10:44:30.883 --> 10:44:38.393
It's one of the best things that we can do for the RFPSS simply because we have enough stability to where we can really get max intent.

10:44:38.433 --> 10:44:43.039
We can load up the weight and get explosive without worrying about the balance factor.

10:44:43.339 --> 10:44:47.845
So it's one of the best ways to build pure strength and power in the rear foot elevated.

10:44:47.825 --> 10:44:52.096
If you don't have a Smith machine, I would rather not go barbell.

10:44:52.457 --> 10:44:54.122
I would rather just go to dumbbells.

10:44:54.142 --> 10:44:57.150
So we're gonna do the same thing, just with dumbbells as heavy as you can.

10:44:57.410 --> 10:44:59.877
So for this rear foot elevated,

10:45:00.802 --> 10:45:03.626
We are going to go balanced glute and quad.

10:45:03.686 --> 10:45:06.870
So sometimes we go ankle underneath the knee.

10:45:06.890 --> 10:45:08.092
That's going to be more glute.

10:45:08.112 --> 10:45:17.845
Right now at that bottom position, when I got my rear foot up and I'm down on my knee, I want my knee directly over the toe.

10:45:18.205 --> 10:45:19.407
So not beyond the toe.

10:45:19.527 --> 10:45:21.770
I just want it right over that toe.

10:45:22.130 --> 10:45:24.193
So that's the position that we're going to work with.

10:45:25.793 --> 10:45:31.842
Of course, we would always start with just the bar, and then we would gradually load up heavier and heavier if we're feeling good.

10:45:32.863 --> 10:45:38.712
So, unrack it, and then it's control down, tap that knee, explode up as hard as I can.

10:45:38.752 --> 10:45:43.479
Now, if I'm loaded heavy, of course I'm not gonna be able to get up very fast.

10:45:43.759 --> 10:45:55.176
It should be taking between half a second and a second to get from the bottom all the way to the top, but we have the intent to move that bar with a fast speed.

10:45:55.156 --> 10:45:59.240
Okay, so we have two options for unracking that bar.

10:45:59.341 --> 10:46:06.268
So the first option is what I just described where we get that position from there and then we go up and we unrack.

10:46:06.608 --> 10:46:20.083
The other option is just to mark out maybe with a piece of tape or a weight where our toe will be and then I can lift up with two and then I can get that toe right where that piece of tape is and then I can go back from there.

10:46:20.063 --> 10:46:25.048
With the Smith machine, we have the luxury of doing that since we have some stability, we don't have to worry about the balance as much.

10:46:25.469 --> 10:46:29.714
So I can go here, and then I can work from there.

10:46:30.374 --> 10:46:35.300
Now, if you have the dumbbells, you don't have that luxury, so I would rather see you start from the bottom up.

10:46:35.860 --> 10:46:43.048
I would rather see the dumbbells be on the floor, and then we set up in our position, grab the dumbbells, and then we go from there.

10:46:43.428 --> 10:46:44.890
All right, let's get after it.

10:46:55.973 --> 10:47:01.759
So for our Nordic hamstring curls, if you have a partner, this is the easiest way to go.

10:47:01.799 --> 10:47:05.642
So your partner is going to sit behind you and hold down your ankles.

10:47:06.043 --> 10:47:09.626
Now he's got to push pretty hard because your whole body weight is going to be coming down.

10:47:09.706 --> 10:47:12.830
So if he's light, then you're going to lift him up.

10:47:12.910 --> 10:47:14.912
So he's got to put a little bit of force in there.

10:47:15.252 --> 10:47:17.114
Now I like to use a foam roller.

10:47:17.394 --> 10:47:22.159
So you put the front of your ankle on a foam roller, and then you kind of dig your toes into the ground.

10:47:22.539 --> 10:47:25.542
And it's just a little bit more comfortable on the ankles.

10:47:25.522 --> 10:47:28.125
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a partner.

10:47:28.726 --> 10:47:31.670
So this is how we would do it without a partner.

10:47:32.031 --> 10:47:34.414
I would set up a barbell and make sure it's nice and heavy.

10:47:34.834 --> 10:47:36.777
Make sure you got weights on both sides.

10:47:37.197 --> 10:47:43.065
If you have a rack, then put it on the inside of the rack and then you put a weighted plate on the other side so that the barbell is not going to roll.

10:47:43.486 --> 10:47:50.395
If you don't have a rack, we would use the barbell and then we would put a weight behind it and we would put another 45 pound plate in front of it.

10:47:50.735 --> 10:47:54.280
So it's still kind of trapped in there and it's not going to roll back and forth.

10:47:54.260 --> 10:48:03.004
this is optional but i like to use this because it's a little bit softer so i just use a pad there and then you always need a pad

10:48:03.322 --> 10:48:33.328
for your knees all right so this is your setup now from here a lot of people go tall and they come down i if you're super advanced that's fine i would rather see a little bit of hip flexion so we're initially in just a little bit of hip flexion and then from there we're going down i want you to go basically as slow as you can or four seconds all the way down this is a lowering exercise once i get to the bottom i'm going to push back up and i'm coming all the way back up pretty fast you don't have to go slow on the way up

10:48:33.308 --> 10:48:51.722
so initial hip flexion from there i'm not bending the hips more i stay in this initial hip flexion and i slowly lower lower lower lower push off and come back up if you can get a light push off that's great a lot of you guys to start

10:48:51.702 --> 10:48:57.555
you're going to have to really push off hard to get back up, otherwise you're going to get stuck halfway up.

10:48:57.816 --> 10:49:09.762
So if you get extremely good at this, we can eventually either go a very light tap or there's some people who have such good strength to body weight ratios, they can just put their hands behind their back and do the whole thing.

10:49:09.742 --> 10:49:16.210
Now, you might cramp up on this exercise if you're not used to this intense of a hamstring contraction.

10:49:16.250 --> 10:49:17.952
It's a very hard hamstring contraction.

10:49:18.432 --> 10:49:20.975
So if you cramp, then stretch it out.

10:49:21.456 --> 10:49:26.662
Try to get back into it, but this might be a little bit too advanced and your hamstrings aren't quite used to it yet.

10:49:27.183 --> 10:49:31.007
So just try to make it easier or come up with an alternative if you continue to cramp.

10:49:30.987 --> 10:49:36.113
Now the other mistake that people make is they point the toes and they go from there.

10:49:36.434 --> 10:49:39.918
A lot of times your calf will start to cramp when you're in that position.

10:49:39.958 --> 10:49:43.983
So I like to go toes kind of straight down into the ground.

10:49:44.424 --> 10:49:54.717
So I'm digging into the ground and then I get a lot less in the calf and I really just feel it straight through the hamstrings, through the glutes and then you should feel a little bit through the low back as well.

10:50:18.558 --> 10:50:22.583
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

10:50:22.703 --> 10:50:24.506
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

10:50:24.546 --> 10:50:27.249
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

10:50:27.290 --> 10:50:33.798
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

10:50:34.319 --> 10:50:40.267
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

10:50:40.547 --> 10:50:44.312
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

10:50:44.292 --> 10:50:48.201
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

10:50:48.622 --> 10:50:52.973
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

10:50:53.033 --> 10:51:00.411
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

10:51:01.370 --> 10:51:04.435
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

10:51:04.455 --> 10:51:11.186
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

10:51:11.546 --> 10:51:17.516
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

10:51:17.536 --> 10:51:21.182
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

10:51:23.505 --> 10:51:27.732
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

10:51:30.715 --> 10:51:32.638
and that stomach will fall, okay?

10:51:33.359 --> 10:51:35.983
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

10:51:36.744 --> 10:51:45.778
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

10:51:46.560 --> 10:51:54.552
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

10:51:54.572 --> 10:51:58.478
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

10:51:58.728 --> 10:52:27.486
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

10:52:27.736 --> 10:52:30.240
happens in the parasympathetic state.

10:52:30.962 --> 10:52:34.909
So if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

10:52:35.270 --> 10:52:48.934
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

10:52:50.686 --> 10:52:53.991
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

10:52:54.011 --> 10:52:58.277
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

10:52:58.577 --> 10:53:05.367
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

10:53:05.667 --> 10:53:09.152
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

10:53:09.432 --> 10:53:16.963
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

10:53:16.943 --> 10:53:21.274
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

10:53:21.695 --> 10:53:23.660
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

10:53:24.020 --> 10:53:26.206
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

10:53:26.487 --> 10:53:28.572
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

10:53:28.852 --> 10:53:31.058
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

10:53:31.359 --> 10:53:33.163
I'm just gonna lean forward.

10:53:33.143 --> 10:53:53.886
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

10:53:54.237 --> 10:53:56.080
15 seconds on the other side as well.

10:53:56.220 --> 10:53:58.504
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

10:53:58.524 --> 10:53:59.966
So that is the back of the thigh.

10:54:00.186 --> 10:54:04.393
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

10:54:04.513 --> 10:54:08.218
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

10:54:08.359 --> 10:54:10.782
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

10:54:11.143 --> 10:54:13.466
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

10:54:13.807 --> 10:54:15.389
So now look at this back foot.

10:54:15.409 --> 10:54:17.072
I'm not curling the toe under.

10:54:17.052 --> 10:54:20.877
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

10:54:21.458 --> 10:54:24.702
And I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

10:54:25.122 --> 10:54:30.469
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

10:54:30.790 --> 10:54:44.207
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

10:54:44.187 --> 10:54:47.334
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

10:54:47.374 --> 10:54:48.697
That's the inside of the leg.

10:54:49.178 --> 10:54:52.545
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

10:54:53.147 --> 10:54:53.848
Same thing.

10:54:54.169 --> 10:54:57.075
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

10:54:57.356 --> 10:55:00.643
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

10:55:00.623 --> 10:55:04.608
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

10:55:04.928 --> 10:55:12.577
I'm not going to lift off, and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

10:55:12.937 --> 10:55:18.423
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

10:55:18.844 --> 10:55:24.150
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

10:55:24.130 --> 10:55:28.879
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

10:55:29.280 --> 10:55:36.934
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

10:55:37.455 --> 10:55:40.240
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

10:55:40.701 --> 10:55:46.712
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

10:55:46.692 --> 10:55:49.775
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

10:55:49.815 --> 10:55:51.997
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

10:55:52.237 --> 10:55:55.880
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

10:55:56.361 --> 10:55:57.922
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

10:55:58.323 --> 10:56:00.845
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

10:56:00.925 --> 10:56:01.866
So I'm 90 there.

10:56:02.186 --> 10:56:04.969
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

10:56:05.349 --> 10:56:07.631
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

10:56:07.992 --> 10:56:12.676
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

10:56:13.036 --> 10:56:16.159
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

10:56:16.139 --> 10:56:19.426
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

10:56:19.446 --> 10:56:24.215
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

10:56:24.255 --> 10:56:25.658
All right.

10:56:25.698 --> 10:56:31.590
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

10:56:32.331 --> 10:56:36.139
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

10:56:36.119 --> 10:56:41.448
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

10:56:41.829 --> 10:56:45.055
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

10:56:45.155 --> 10:56:48.340
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

10:56:48.741 --> 10:56:58.498
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

10:56:58.478 --> 10:57:05.234
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

10:57:05.514 --> 10:57:11.689
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

10:57:12.070 --> 10:57:16.420
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

10:57:17.210 --> 10:57:20.338
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

10:57:20.378 --> 10:57:21.099
That's okay.

10:57:21.460 --> 10:57:24.668
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

10:57:24.708 --> 10:57:28.738
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

10:57:31.418 --> 10:57:36.528
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase nine, day one and day four.

10:57:37.290 --> 10:57:42.400
So always do our dynamic warm up if you want to skip that foam roller circuit you can.

10:57:42.961 --> 10:57:49.073
If we have to skip anything today, just skip the one-two push out and the hip swivel back pedal.

10:57:49.053 --> 10:57:53.643
So take out the agility and do everything else on today's program.

10:57:54.024 --> 10:57:58.815
Of course, we want to do those agility drills, but if you're really short on time, cut those two out.

10:57:59.757 --> 10:58:07.855
We are on this six day a week program, so four days of lower body, which means by nature, our workouts need to be short and more efficient.

10:58:07.835 --> 10:58:15.249
If we're in the gym for longer than an hour with this workout, you're doing too much, you should probably be cutting out some of these drills.

10:58:15.750 --> 10:58:29.996
This entire workout should probably only take you around 45 minutes, so you should be able to get in and get a good high intensity session and get out and start your recovery process because we gotta recover for tomorrow's lower body workout.

10:58:30.357 --> 10:58:31.058
Let's do it.

10:58:31.038 --> 10:58:32.621
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

10:58:32.761 --> 10:58:34.464
I have a long roller from Skills.

10:58:34.725 --> 10:58:41.597
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

10:58:41.958 --> 10:58:47.308
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

10:58:47.288 --> 10:58:50.453
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

10:58:50.793 --> 10:58:59.948
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

10:58:59.968 --> 10:59:06.698
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

10:59:06.678 --> 10:59:13.185
or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

10:59:13.566 --> 10:59:15.768
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

10:59:15.808 --> 10:59:17.210
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

10:59:17.550 --> 10:59:25.960
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

10:59:26.320 --> 10:59:33.428
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

10:59:33.408 --> 10:59:38.119
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

10:59:38.335 --> 10:59:40.117
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

10:59:40.418 --> 10:59:43.822
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

10:59:43.902 --> 10:59:52.132
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

10:59:52.192 --> 10:59:57.719
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

10:59:57.779 --> 11:00:00.923
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

11:00:00.983 --> 11:00:08.292
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

11:00:08.272 --> 11:00:14.582
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

11:00:15.303 --> 11:00:18.288
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

11:00:18.729 --> 11:00:23.977
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

11:00:24.438 --> 11:00:30.988
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

11:00:30.968 --> 11:00:33.875
Our next one's going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

11:00:34.055 --> 11:00:35.819
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

11:00:36.120 --> 11:00:49.872
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

11:00:51.657 --> 11:00:58.467
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

11:00:58.668 --> 11:01:02.233
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

11:01:02.534 --> 11:01:05.779
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

11:01:06.339 --> 11:01:09.044
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

11:01:09.064 --> 11:01:10.726
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

11:01:11.047 --> 11:01:14.873
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

11:01:15.313 --> 11:01:18.458
And I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

11:01:18.438 --> 11:01:26.290
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

11:01:26.631 --> 11:01:29.335
We got to really start to loosen that up.

11:01:30.917 --> 11:01:35.504
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

11:01:35.745 --> 11:01:39.270
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

11:01:40.011 --> 11:01:44.478
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

11:01:44.838 --> 11:01:46.521
Everything else is extra credit.

11:01:49.572 --> 11:01:53.317
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

11:01:53.598 --> 11:01:56.161
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

11:01:56.241 --> 11:02:00.207
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

11:02:00.708 --> 11:02:07.317
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

11:02:07.657 --> 11:02:11.343
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

11:02:11.363 --> 11:02:13.065
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

11:02:13.045 --> 11:02:16.248
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

11:02:16.609 --> 11:02:18.050
I'm not going too fast yet.

11:02:18.371 --> 11:02:20.753
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

11:02:20.773 --> 11:02:25.318
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

11:02:25.338 --> 11:02:33.447
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

11:02:33.787 --> 11:02:36.009
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

11:02:36.029 --> 11:02:38.412
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

11:02:38.392 --> 11:02:42.617
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed.

11:02:42.677 --> 11:02:43.679
So we're not all out.

11:02:44.139 --> 11:02:45.721
We're just getting that blood flowing.

11:02:46.022 --> 11:02:47.363
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

11:02:47.564 --> 11:02:51.889
For the over under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

11:02:52.209 --> 11:02:54.032
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

11:02:54.092 --> 11:03:00.180
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid shin or even to the knees just as low as we can.

11:03:00.240 --> 11:03:03.724
But we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

11:03:03.704 --> 11:03:11.419
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

11:03:15.027 --> 11:03:19.394
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

11:03:19.594 --> 11:03:24.922
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

11:03:25.222 --> 11:03:30.470
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

11:03:30.530 --> 11:03:31.772
I'm keeping that knee in.

11:03:32.193 --> 11:03:40.225
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

11:03:40.205 --> 11:03:45.134
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

11:03:45.494 --> 11:03:51.084
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

11:03:51.766 --> 11:03:54.130
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

11:03:54.450 --> 11:03:59.079
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

11:03:59.720 --> 11:04:02.044
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

11:04:02.969 --> 11:04:06.595
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

11:04:07.016 --> 11:04:11.463
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

11:04:11.523 --> 11:04:13.807
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

11:04:14.127 --> 11:04:16.151
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

11:04:16.191 --> 11:04:16.792
That's okay.

11:04:17.112 --> 11:04:18.715
Let's just get as low as we can there.

11:04:19.175 --> 11:04:20.117
One, two, three.

11:04:20.097 --> 11:04:46.577
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

11:04:46.557 --> 11:05:15.535
and i'm getting nice and low touch the floor and i'm popping out to that other side all right so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

11:05:20.594 --> 11:05:27.122
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

11:05:27.142 --> 11:05:28.604
Don't make the mistake of going here.

11:05:28.984 --> 11:05:37.035
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

11:05:37.155 --> 11:05:43.022
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

11:05:43.102 --> 11:05:47.007
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

11:05:46.987 --> 11:06:05.502
it's gonna be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one

11:06:05.482 --> 11:06:24.672
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

11:06:25.006 --> 11:06:42.559
one two three lean one two three heels back down one two three one up two up stand all right if you cannot get down in that deep position then let's just go as low as we can right here and then on the lean it's just going to look like this

11:06:42.539 --> 11:06:47.388
All right, and then we're going to come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up stand.

11:06:47.748 --> 11:06:54.079
We just got to get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

11:06:54.099 --> 11:06:56.924
So for the hip bridge, it's going to be a very basic hip bridge.

11:06:56.985 --> 11:06:59.309
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

11:06:59.689 --> 11:07:02.474
So we're going to get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

11:07:02.815 --> 11:07:04.518
I need to be flat through the hips.

11:07:04.818 --> 11:07:07.483
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

11:07:07.463 --> 11:07:12.132
and now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

11:07:12.513 --> 11:07:26.181
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult, but if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers.

11:07:26.752 --> 11:07:33.363
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

11:07:33.784 --> 11:07:37.931
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

11:07:37.951 --> 11:07:39.914
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

11:07:39.934 --> 11:07:42.178
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

11:07:53.228 --> 11:07:57.155
So for our one-two push out, I just like to go on the lane line.

11:07:57.475 --> 11:08:01.241
So we're looking at about 18 feet from baseline to the free throw line.

11:08:01.462 --> 11:08:05.429
If you're not on a court, just mark out about 18 to 20 feet and let's work with that.

11:08:05.549 --> 11:08:09.555
So if I'm going to my left, I need to lead with my left leg.

11:08:09.896 --> 11:08:17.008
So I go over the line or if you have like rings or agility ladder or something, it's here, here,

11:08:16.988 --> 11:08:17.950
here, here.

11:08:18.291 --> 11:08:20.597
So we're pushing over the line and we're pushing back.

11:08:20.958 --> 11:08:23.544
So it's left, right, left, right.

11:08:23.584 --> 11:08:27.133
But when that right foot hits, now we push laterally as far as we can.

11:08:27.574 --> 11:08:30.080
So it's left, right, left, right.

11:08:30.701 --> 11:08:33.107
Land, left, right, left, right.

11:08:33.087 --> 11:08:34.489
left, right, left, right.

11:08:34.810 --> 11:08:40.178
Alright, you're pushing as far as you can with that right foot.

11:08:40.779 --> 11:08:43.984
Now once I get down to that side, I would bring it back the other way.

11:08:44.024 --> 11:08:51.957
So then it would be the other way, right, left, right, left, right, left, push, right, left, right, left,

11:08:51.937 --> 11:09:16.257
right left right left alright so there's a lot of rhythm working on that pure quickness most importantly we're working on that punch out so boom punching when we slam the floor like that we really involve the tendons instead of just the muscles so I don't want it to be get the foot here and then a soft push out I want you to slam the floor and push out aggressively let's do it

11:09:32.943 --> 11:09:41.278
So for my bound sprints, what I'm going to do, if you have a court and a half, then we're going to go a full court and a half with the bound sprint.

11:09:41.559 --> 11:09:45.085
We're going to bound six times, three each leg, get as far as we can.

11:09:45.426 --> 11:09:50.194
And then after that six, one, boom, hit the ground running, sprint it through a court and a half.

11:09:50.214 --> 11:09:52.839
Now I know a lot of you guys don't have two courts set up this way.

11:09:53.160 --> 11:09:55.163
So what you're going to do is just 54 meters.

11:09:55.224 --> 11:09:56.065
So you could

11:09:56.045 --> 11:10:01.180
Mark it out on a track or on a field or anywhere where you have 54 meters.

11:10:01.621 --> 11:10:03.587
Look, if you don't have that space, it's okay.

11:10:03.868 --> 11:10:11.269
Let's just do the bounds to half court or six times, which will get you around half court and then sprint it through the other half court, all right?

11:10:11.249 --> 11:10:19.005
So, work with what you've got, but if you have the room, I'm going to start in a split stance, and I'm getting out as far as I can.

11:10:19.426 --> 11:10:24.256
Of course, on my bounds, I do want to get some height with it, and I got to get off the ground quick, quick, quick.

11:10:24.336 --> 11:10:30.810
So, it's going to be boom, one, two, three, four, five, six,

11:10:30.790 --> 11:10:35.279
sprint it out as fast as we can through the rest of that 54 meters.

11:10:35.299 --> 11:10:44.798
Main goals of this exercise is to get as far as we can with the bounds and then to get from point A to point B as fast as we possibly can.

11:10:44.838 --> 11:10:46.582
So time yourself every session.

11:10:46.602 --> 11:10:48.225
I want you to beat that record.

11:10:56.558 --> 11:11:00.445
So for my lateral bound with dumbbell punch, I'm going to go with the light dumbbell.

11:11:00.485 --> 11:11:03.691
Most of you will go five, maybe up to 10 pounds.

11:11:04.112 --> 11:11:06.256
So I'm just going to go a lateral bound.

11:11:06.316 --> 11:11:09.201
So you guys know the regular lateral bound.

11:11:09.582 --> 11:11:11.405
But now I'm going to go opposite.

11:11:11.465 --> 11:11:17.436
So as I push out and as I land on that left leg, I'm punching across.

11:11:17.416 --> 11:11:34.952
with this dumbbell and i'm trying to do it pretty forcefully so the harder i punch with the dumbbell the more force i got going down in the ground the more i have to absorb with this left leg and then the more i have to redirect and push in that direction so you'll notice with the dumbbell it's a punch

11:11:34.932 --> 11:11:36.013
and then it's a pull.

11:11:36.354 --> 11:11:45.065
So a punch, and then I pull it to the side of my chest, and now all that additional force for absorption and redirection is on that left leg.

11:11:45.105 --> 11:11:46.486
So I'll give you a slow motion here.

11:11:46.546 --> 11:11:53.074
It's bam, push, absorb, push, absorb, push, absorb, push.

11:11:54.116 --> 11:11:58.341
Notice I'm not just going with the arm, I'm actually dipping the shoulder.

11:11:58.321 --> 11:12:02.026
So I'm getting a little bit of rotational power in there.

11:12:02.066 --> 11:12:06.212
So it's rotation, drive out, rotation, drive out.

11:12:06.592 --> 11:12:09.837
Now this exercise takes a lot of coordination and a lot of timing.

11:12:09.857 --> 11:12:16.866
If we can't get that down with the dumbbell, it's okay to just go with no weights and just use your hand and do it in the same way.

11:12:17.227 --> 11:12:19.570
That reach is still beneficial because

11:12:19.550 --> 11:12:24.157
We got to be able to stabilize that knee when our upper body is rotating, right?

11:12:24.177 --> 11:12:26.360
Because that's where a lot of injuries happen on the court.

11:12:26.841 --> 11:12:35.573
It's not when we're in this perfect posture, it's when we're in these positions and we're rotating with our upper body and we have rotational forces.

11:12:35.613 --> 11:12:37.116
That's when a lot of knee injuries happen.

11:12:37.536 --> 11:12:44.967
So we got to get used to being able to absorb and explode while our upper body is moving in space.

11:12:55.544 --> 11:12:59.452
So for our box drop med ball toss, we're going to hold a med ball.

11:12:59.512 --> 11:13:00.915
Now work with what you've got.

11:13:01.075 --> 11:13:04.763
So I like to go 20 up to about 30 pounds here.

11:13:05.685 --> 11:13:09.753
If you have lighter, that's okay because we are getting a drop from the box.

11:13:09.774 --> 11:13:12.439
So we're getting some good eccentric loading.

11:13:12.419 --> 11:13:15.866
I like to go in a box around knee height or just below knee height.

11:13:16.227 --> 11:13:19.954
Now, if you're not as advanced, go in a smaller box with a lighter weight.

11:13:20.195 --> 11:13:22.659
If you're more advanced, we can start to raise that box up.

11:13:22.960 --> 11:13:26.968
I wouldn't go too much higher than knee height for this exercise though.

11:13:28.062 --> 11:13:31.728
So all I'm gonna do is just hold it here, right in front of my belly button.

11:13:32.088 --> 11:13:33.350
I'm gonna drop down.

11:13:33.791 --> 11:13:41.964
As soon as my feet land, I end up arms extended, and then I gotta transition out of the bottom as fast as I can, toss as high as I can.

11:13:42.365 --> 11:13:43.827
So it's drop,

11:13:43.958 --> 11:14:10.084
load up toss as high as we can we will get a little bit of a jump naturally but I don't want you to consciously think about jumping so you might come to your toes and leave the ground just a couple inches all I want you to really think about is transitioning from eccentric to concentric as fast as you can and tossing this ball as high as you possibly can every rep compete with yourself try to get it higher and higher and higher

11:14:10.064 --> 11:14:14.950
Now, of course, anytime we're throwing something overhead, we got to make sure that we get out of the way.

11:14:15.051 --> 11:14:18.595
So don't be distracted and throw it up and have it come down and hit you in the head.

11:14:18.976 --> 11:14:19.877
It's very dangerous.

11:14:19.897 --> 11:14:25.384
So throw it up, step out of the way, keep your eye on it the whole time so that you can see it drop.

11:14:25.685 --> 11:14:32.173
Make sure nobody else is in the way because it could be very dangerous if you're throwing this in a super crowded place.

11:14:32.153 --> 11:14:37.219
If you're in a gym that's way too crowded to do this, I want you to just jump with it.

11:14:37.539 --> 11:14:42.765
So we're just going to hold a light medicine ball right by the chest, or you can even hug it.

11:14:43.126 --> 11:14:49.533
Sometimes I like to hug it here, cross the arms, and then just drop, land, and jump.

11:14:49.974 --> 11:14:54.840
So that's your option if you're in a place that's too crowded to actually throw it overhead.

11:14:55.260 --> 11:14:56.001
Let's do it.

11:14:59.035 --> 11:15:16.739
Now for our freestyle dunks, I put this in there because it's so important that you get out there and just allow your body to explore different approaches, different type of dunks, different type of jumping styles without me always trying to put you in a box and trying to get you to do one thing.

11:15:17.079 --> 11:15:22.647
So I want you to set the clock for 10 minutes and I just want you to go get as many dunks as you can.

11:15:22.627 --> 11:15:24.590
Take breaks in between if you need to.

11:15:24.630 --> 11:15:27.175
I do want you to be recovered in between reps.

11:15:27.195 --> 11:15:29.238
This isn't an endurance workout.

11:15:29.599 --> 11:15:30.921
So get up off one foot.

11:15:30.981 --> 11:15:31.802
Throw it down.

11:15:32.263 --> 11:15:32.844
Walk back.

11:15:32.884 --> 11:15:35.008
Recover for 30 seconds to a minute.

11:15:35.348 --> 11:15:35.989
Come back in.

11:15:36.129 --> 11:15:37.251
Take off off two.

11:15:37.692 --> 11:15:38.914
Try to dunk left.

11:15:38.894 --> 11:15:41.978
Whatever you want to do, get creative and do it.

11:15:42.219 --> 11:15:46.265
Of course, if you can't dunk and all you can do is touch the bottom of the net, I don't care.

11:15:46.305 --> 11:15:47.547
I still want you to get creative.

11:15:47.927 --> 11:15:49.409
Come in off two and touch the net.

11:15:49.770 --> 11:15:52.654
Come from baseline, get up off one and touch the net.

11:15:52.674 --> 11:15:55.077
Try to jump off left, touch with the left.

11:15:56.099 --> 11:16:05.172
Variability, variety is the key to longevity and it's super important for building kind of well-rounded explosiveness and athleticism.

11:16:05.152 --> 11:16:09.947
and so I want you to get creative, set the clock for 10 minutes, and just freestyle dunk.

11:16:35.122 --> 11:16:39.167
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

11:16:39.287 --> 11:16:41.069
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

11:16:41.129 --> 11:16:43.832
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

11:16:43.873 --> 11:16:50.380
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

11:16:50.901 --> 11:16:56.848
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

11:16:57.128 --> 11:17:00.893
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

11:17:00.873 --> 11:17:04.759
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

11:17:05.200 --> 11:17:09.546
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

11:17:09.607 --> 11:17:16.958
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

11:17:17.950 --> 11:17:20.673
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

11:17:21.034 --> 11:17:27.742
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

11:17:28.303 --> 11:17:34.090
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

11:17:34.110 --> 11:17:37.775
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

11:17:40.058 --> 11:17:44.363
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

11:17:47.296 --> 11:17:49.198
and that stomach will fall, okay?

11:17:49.919 --> 11:17:52.562
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

11:17:53.323 --> 11:18:03.013
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

11:18:03.134 --> 11:18:10.902
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

11:18:11.142 --> 11:18:15.227
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

11:18:15.207 --> 11:18:44.067
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I gotta get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

11:18:44.317 --> 11:18:46.822
happens in the parasympathetic state.

11:18:47.363 --> 11:18:51.491
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

11:18:51.852 --> 11:19:05.498
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

11:19:07.283 --> 11:19:10.588
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

11:19:10.608 --> 11:19:14.894
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

11:19:15.174 --> 11:19:21.984
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

11:19:22.285 --> 11:19:25.770
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's OK.

11:19:26.050 --> 11:19:33.561
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

11:19:33.541 --> 11:19:37.892
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

11:19:38.313 --> 11:19:40.298
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

11:19:40.318 --> 11:19:42.804
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

11:19:43.105 --> 11:19:45.430
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

11:19:45.450 --> 11:19:47.676
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

11:19:47.957 --> 11:19:49.761
I'm just gonna lean forward.

11:19:49.741 --> 11:20:10.483
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

11:20:10.835 --> 11:20:12.798
15 seconds on the other side as well.

11:20:12.818 --> 11:20:14.941
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

11:20:15.142 --> 11:20:16.564
So that is the back of the thigh.

11:20:16.804 --> 11:20:21.111
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

11:20:21.131 --> 11:20:24.816
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

11:20:24.977 --> 11:20:27.400
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

11:20:27.741 --> 11:20:30.064
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

11:20:30.425 --> 11:20:31.987
So now look at this back foot.

11:20:32.007 --> 11:20:33.670
I'm not curling the toe under.

11:20:33.650 --> 11:20:37.496
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

11:20:38.057 --> 11:20:41.322
And I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

11:20:41.742 --> 11:20:47.070
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

11:20:47.411 --> 11:21:00.771
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

11:21:00.751 --> 11:21:03.940
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

11:21:03.980 --> 11:21:05.303
That's the inside of the leg.

11:21:05.784 --> 11:21:09.173
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

11:21:09.755 --> 11:21:10.457
Same thing.

11:21:10.778 --> 11:21:13.685
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

11:21:13.966 --> 11:21:17.275
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

11:21:17.255 --> 11:21:21.240
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

11:21:21.520 --> 11:21:29.170
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

11:21:29.551 --> 11:21:35.038
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

11:21:35.438 --> 11:21:40.765
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

11:21:40.745 --> 11:21:45.490
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

11:21:45.891 --> 11:21:53.539
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

11:21:54.040 --> 11:21:56.843
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

11:21:57.283 --> 11:22:03.310
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

11:22:03.290 --> 11:22:06.353
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

11:22:06.433 --> 11:22:08.595
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

11:22:08.835 --> 11:22:12.478
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

11:22:12.959 --> 11:22:14.540
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

11:22:14.560 --> 11:22:17.463
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

11:22:17.523 --> 11:22:18.464
So I'm 90 there.

11:22:18.804 --> 11:22:21.587
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

11:22:21.947 --> 11:22:24.249
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

11:22:24.610 --> 11:22:29.294
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

11:22:29.634 --> 11:22:32.757
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

11:22:32.737 --> 11:22:35.682
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

11:22:36.042 --> 11:22:40.810
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

11:22:40.850 --> 11:22:42.293
All right.

11:22:42.313 --> 11:22:48.182
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

11:22:48.924 --> 11:22:52.770
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

11:22:52.750 --> 11:22:58.058
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

11:22:58.439 --> 11:23:01.664
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

11:23:01.764 --> 11:23:04.929
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

11:23:05.330 --> 11:23:15.045
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

11:23:15.346 --> 11:23:21.832
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

11:23:22.113 --> 11:23:28.259
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

11:23:28.659 --> 11:23:32.743
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

11:23:33.925 --> 11:23:37.708
A lot of us aren't gonna be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

11:23:38.089 --> 11:23:41.272
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

11:23:41.752 --> 11:23:45.336
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

11:23:47.999 --> 11:23:53.245
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for phase nine, day two and day five.

11:23:53.566 --> 11:23:57.310
So this is our lower body strength day, already very efficient.

11:23:58.872 --> 11:24:03.357
Definitely skip the foam roller circuit if you're short on time.

11:24:03.798 --> 11:24:06.201
Always do the lateral step core stiffness.

11:24:06.681 --> 11:24:09.545
All of these next exercises we have to do.

11:24:10.085 --> 11:24:15.812
If you have to skip one of the strength exercises, you could skip the band stability ball hamstring curl.

11:24:15.792 --> 11:24:17.014
Love that exercise.

11:24:17.314 --> 11:24:19.137
But if you're short on time, cut that out.

11:24:19.577 --> 11:24:23.683
And then if you're short on time, cut out the rear foot elevated foot pops.

11:24:24.324 --> 11:24:26.207
So that one is a lower priority.

11:24:26.688 --> 11:24:32.636
Very great for building strength in the ankle and in the foot and building that functional stiffness that we need.

11:24:33.217 --> 11:24:35.721
But if you're short on time, cut that out.

11:24:35.741 --> 11:24:39.005
And then of course, if you're short on time, cut out that static stretch.

11:24:39.386 --> 11:24:40.247
Let's do it.

11:24:40.362 --> 11:24:41.906
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

11:24:42.046 --> 11:24:43.730
I have a long roller from Skills.

11:24:43.991 --> 11:24:50.868
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

11:24:51.229 --> 11:24:56.582
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

11:24:56.562 --> 11:25:22.738
so you could either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well

11:25:22.718 --> 11:25:25.041
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

11:25:25.081 --> 11:25:26.483
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

11:25:26.824 --> 11:25:35.216
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

11:25:35.596 --> 11:25:47.393
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

11:25:47.626 --> 11:25:49.388
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

11:25:49.688 --> 11:25:53.091
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

11:25:53.192 --> 11:26:01.400
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

11:26:01.460 --> 11:26:06.985
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

11:26:07.025 --> 11:26:10.229
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

11:26:10.249 --> 11:26:17.616
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

11:26:17.596 --> 11:26:40.262
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh so i'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee

11:26:40.242 --> 11:26:43.148
Our next one is going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

11:26:43.329 --> 11:26:45.092
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

11:26:45.393 --> 11:26:59.162
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

11:27:00.931 --> 11:27:07.742
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

11:27:07.942 --> 11:27:11.508
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

11:27:11.809 --> 11:27:15.074
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

11:27:15.615 --> 11:27:18.319
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

11:27:18.339 --> 11:27:20.022
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

11:27:20.323 --> 11:27:24.289
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

11:27:24.269 --> 11:27:27.254
and I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

11:27:27.815 --> 11:27:38.734
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping, we gotta really start to loosen that up.

11:27:40.115 --> 11:27:44.768
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

11:27:45.029 --> 11:27:48.538
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

11:27:48.558 --> 11:27:53.752
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

11:27:54.113 --> 11:27:55.778
Everything else is extra credit.

11:27:58.846 --> 11:28:02.592
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

11:28:02.873 --> 11:28:05.457
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

11:28:05.517 --> 11:28:09.484
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary, just in case you don't have much room.

11:28:09.985 --> 11:28:16.596
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

11:28:16.936 --> 11:28:20.622
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

11:28:20.642 --> 11:28:22.305
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

11:28:22.285 --> 11:28:25.530
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

11:28:25.871 --> 11:28:27.353
I'm not going too fast yet.

11:28:27.653 --> 11:28:29.797
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

11:28:30.057 --> 11:28:34.584
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

11:28:34.604 --> 11:28:38.791
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

11:28:39.192 --> 11:28:42.757
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

11:28:43.058 --> 11:28:45.301
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

11:28:45.321 --> 11:28:47.685
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

11:28:47.665 --> 11:28:52.974
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed, so we're not all out.

11:28:53.415 --> 11:28:54.997
We're just getting that blood flowing.

11:28:55.298 --> 11:28:56.640
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

11:28:56.840 --> 11:29:01.167
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

11:29:01.508 --> 11:29:03.291
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

11:29:03.371 --> 11:29:12.947
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

11:29:12.927 --> 11:29:20.642
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

11:29:24.318 --> 11:29:28.705
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

11:29:28.885 --> 11:29:34.214
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

11:29:34.494 --> 11:29:39.722
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

11:29:39.803 --> 11:29:41.045
I'm keeping that knee in.

11:29:41.465 --> 11:29:49.498
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

11:29:49.478 --> 11:30:11.335
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

11:30:12.243 --> 11:30:15.868
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

11:30:16.289 --> 11:30:20.735
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

11:30:20.795 --> 11:30:23.079
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

11:30:23.399 --> 11:30:25.422
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

11:30:25.462 --> 11:30:26.063
That's okay.

11:30:26.384 --> 11:30:27.986
Let's just get as low as we can there.

11:30:28.467 --> 11:30:29.408
One, two, three.

11:30:29.388 --> 11:30:50.063
Three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that

11:30:50.043 --> 11:30:52.506
stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

11:30:52.827 --> 11:30:59.776
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

11:31:00.217 --> 11:31:04.202
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

11:31:04.562 --> 11:31:10.330
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

11:31:10.310 --> 11:31:24.859
Same thing with the skip and now I land I turn this foot forward I pivot I'm here knee a couple inches off the floor I'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

11:31:29.885 --> 11:31:36.393
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

11:31:36.413 --> 11:31:37.875
Don't make the mistake of going here.

11:31:38.256 --> 11:31:46.326
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

11:31:46.426 --> 11:31:52.293
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

11:31:52.373 --> 11:31:56.078
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

11:31:56.345 --> 11:32:15.619
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one two

11:32:15.599 --> 11:32:19.368
Three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground.

11:32:19.628 --> 11:32:21.272
One, two, three.

11:32:21.352 --> 11:32:24.821
I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here.

11:32:24.861 --> 11:32:27.567
We need it back behind the head and stand.

11:32:28.108 --> 11:32:28.349
All right.

11:32:28.830 --> 11:32:30.213
So that's one cycle.

11:32:30.474 --> 11:32:31.576
We're going to go for three of these.

11:32:32.539 --> 11:32:33.962
So it's one, two, three.

11:32:34.297 --> 11:33:03.358
one two three lean one two three heels back down one two three one up two up stand all right if you cannot get down in that deep position then let's just go as low as we can right here and then on the lean it's just gonna look like this all right and then we're gonna come back hopefully parallel to the floor one up two up stand we just got to get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower better range of motion

11:33:03.338 --> 11:33:06.181
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

11:33:06.241 --> 11:33:08.564
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

11:33:08.965 --> 11:33:11.748
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

11:33:12.068 --> 11:33:13.770
I need to be flat through the hips.

11:33:14.051 --> 11:33:16.474
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

11:33:16.994 --> 11:33:21.440
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

11:33:21.800 --> 11:33:24.603
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

11:33:24.864 --> 11:33:32.653
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

11:33:32.633 --> 11:33:35.037
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

11:33:36.098 --> 11:33:42.648
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

11:33:43.069 --> 11:33:47.216
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

11:33:47.236 --> 11:33:49.199
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

11:33:49.219 --> 11:33:51.402
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

11:34:00.258 --> 11:34:06.009
So for my lateral step core stiffness, what I'm gonna do is use a cable machine.

11:34:06.370 --> 11:34:08.975
I'm gonna get that cable set at around hip height.

11:34:09.356 --> 11:34:15.347
Now if you don't have a cable machine, you could just do these with a band, get it around something stable, around hip height.

11:34:16.238 --> 11:34:19.965
Either one will work, but I like to use a cable machine because we can load up a little heavier.

11:34:20.066 --> 11:34:22.490
So of course, as always, we start very light.

11:34:22.851 --> 11:34:25.015
Once we feel comfortable, we gradually go up.

11:34:25.316 --> 11:34:28.041
I like to go around 80, 90, up to 100 pounds.

11:34:28.101 --> 11:34:30.827
So I'm going to go feet pretty close together.

11:34:31.127 --> 11:34:33.732
So I'm maybe hip width, shoulder width apart here.

11:34:33.772 --> 11:34:36.498
Already I should feel like it's really pulling me in.

11:34:36.939 --> 11:34:38.702
Now I'm just going to take a little step.

11:34:38.682 --> 11:35:02.952
with this left foot and as soon as that foot hits i'm landing with that 90 degree knee angle and then i'm keeping my core stiff and i'm pushing out of it all right so so many of you what you're going to do is you're going to step and then you're going to have this torso collapse you got to keep in mind a lot of the battle on lateral movements right if i'm changing direction is that core stiffness

11:35:02.932 --> 11:35:10.945
So if I get here and I go to change directions and I deform from the core, we lose milliseconds on that change of direction.

11:35:11.345 --> 11:35:13.849
So what I want you to focus on is keeping that stiff core.

11:35:14.150 --> 11:35:19.619
As soon as that foot hits, I do not collapse at all and I can just push right back out.

11:35:21.061 --> 11:35:27.471
Alright, so it's feet together, it's here, short ground contact time and just push up out of it.

11:35:28.345 --> 11:35:30.752
It's got to be fast, it's got to be rapid.

11:35:30.973 --> 11:35:37.714
We are going to feel a burn in the leg, but we should also feel it in the core, especially in that outside oblique.

11:35:50.620 --> 11:35:57.088
So for our eccentric overloaded one leg RDL, what I'm going to do is just deadlift the weight up.

11:35:57.168 --> 11:36:02.195
So I want to choose a weight that I couldn't normally do single leg deadlifts with.

11:36:02.275 --> 11:36:07.141
So I'm just going to do bilateral, so double leg on the way up, a normal deadlift.

11:36:07.422 --> 11:36:10.165
This part should be the easy part.

11:36:10.145 --> 11:36:12.651
Now from here, I'm just coming to one leg.

11:36:13.031 --> 11:36:14.855
All right, so slight bend in the knee.

11:36:14.915 --> 11:36:16.699
We don't need to be completely stiff with it.

11:36:17.180 --> 11:36:18.102
Slight bend in the knee.

11:36:18.463 --> 11:36:19.726
From there, it's just a hip hinge.

11:36:19.806 --> 11:36:21.310
I'm not bending my knee anymore.

11:36:21.370 --> 11:36:25.639
And I'm coming down slow about three.

11:36:25.619 --> 11:36:27.941
seconds and just put it down on the floor.

11:36:28.281 --> 11:36:37.990
Now if you don't have that range of motion you could always use bumper plates or some sort of blocks and we could put it down on the blocks and then pick it back up with two feet.

11:36:38.050 --> 11:36:46.398
So then again I'm coming two feet back to that right foot slight bend in that knee and nice and slow three seconds on the way down.

11:36:46.998 --> 11:36:55.626
So the key here is if you choose a weight that you could easily just do normal RDL's with that's not actually overloading the eccentric.

11:36:55.606 --> 11:37:00.532
So we have to choose a weight that we normally wouldn't be able to just rep out RDLs.

11:37:00.853 --> 11:37:03.796
Now, some people balance is the limiting factor.

11:37:03.976 --> 11:37:20.277
So if balance is the limiting factor and it's not necessarily strength, I would rather see you just go in a split stance, so a slight split, and keep all the weight on this front leg just like we would in a single leg RDL, but keep this back foot down for some support.

11:37:20.597 --> 11:37:25.123
So I'm just gonna come down on this back toe so I can deadlift it up.

11:37:25.660 --> 11:37:28.164
And I can just put that foot down a little bit for support.

11:37:28.264 --> 11:37:29.987
I am not putting my weight back.

11:37:30.408 --> 11:37:35.256
All my weight is still on that front leg and I'm doing the same thing.

11:37:35.276 --> 11:37:36.819
Alright, so that version is great.

11:37:36.879 --> 11:37:40.845
It'll actually allow us to use a little bit more weight and we can be on balance.

11:37:41.066 --> 11:37:48.478
But some of you guys are more advanced to where you can really load up heavy and get a good single leg RDL without balance being the limiting factor.

11:37:48.458 --> 11:37:54.124
Now keep in mind with that single leg RDL it is a hip hinge so I'm pushing my butt back back back.

11:37:54.484 --> 11:37:56.186
I'm keeping my back completely flat.

11:37:56.466 --> 11:37:58.488
I do not want to have any type of rounded spine.

11:37:59.189 --> 11:38:02.912
Also with that back leg I can keep that knee relaxed a little bit.

11:38:02.953 --> 11:38:07.577
Sometimes people try to go straight with that leg and it might limit the overall range of motion.

11:38:07.938 --> 11:38:13.403
I'd rather see you have sort of a relaxed leg where we're almost straight but we do have a little bend in the knee.

11:38:13.383 --> 11:38:15.927
And then I want to kick this heel nice and high.

11:38:16.347 --> 11:38:20.474
So as I go down, that leg is getting kicked up, up, up.

11:38:20.494 --> 11:38:23.618
So that thigh should really be parallel with your torso.

11:38:23.899 --> 11:38:27.324
So as you go down, I don't want that thigh to hang out down here.

11:38:27.444 --> 11:38:29.807
I want it to be up in that position.

11:38:30.108 --> 11:38:33.653
And we're thinking about driving that heel up towards the ceiling.

11:38:46.897 --> 11:38:53.346
So for our TKE rear foot elevated split squat, I'm gonna pull up a bench and I'm gonna go dumbbells on the floor.

11:38:53.767 --> 11:38:55.730
Now, if you don't have the bench, that's okay.

11:38:56.050 --> 11:38:59.055
We can always just make this into a reverse lunge instead.

11:38:59.415 --> 11:39:02.279
But if you do have the bench, we want something just below knee height.

11:39:02.520 --> 11:39:03.802
All right, so I'm gonna get in the band.

11:39:03.882 --> 11:39:06.666
Now I'm gonna make sure that it's right behind the knee.

11:39:06.786 --> 11:39:10.351
Typically a thicker band does better here, a thicker, stronger band.

11:39:10.591 --> 11:39:12.474
But if you just have the thin bands, that's okay.

11:39:12.494 --> 11:39:15.098
Just step back further to get more tension.

11:39:15.078 --> 11:39:21.492
So when I'm in this setup position with my rear foot elevated, I should already feel a good pull here.

11:39:21.833 --> 11:39:26.623
So I have to really activate my quad, my VMO, just to stay stable in this position, right?

11:39:26.663 --> 11:39:29.510
If it's too loose, then there's no point in even having that band.

11:39:30.111 --> 11:39:34.200
So get that perfect amount of tension and then come down to the floor.

11:39:34.180 --> 11:39:38.045
pick up our dumbbells and now it's a rear foot elevated split squat.

11:39:38.085 --> 11:39:41.750
So I'm going all the way down and all the way back up.

11:39:41.930 --> 11:39:47.097
Now at that bottom position, I do want the knee just over the toe.

11:39:47.438 --> 11:39:52.925
So this isn't our typical rear foot elevated where our knee is going to stay back behind the toe.

11:39:52.945 --> 11:39:58.973
We are going to get a little bit of a forward shin angle so that knee is going to end up just in front of the toe.

11:39:58.953 --> 11:40:06.885
And now we're really going to make it quad dominant because we have this band pulling at the top, so we're getting extra VMO activation.

11:40:06.905 --> 11:40:08.647
That's that small teardrop muscle.

11:40:11.091 --> 11:40:16.118
So at the top I get a good squeeze in the quad, especially that teardrop muscle.

11:40:18.072 --> 11:40:21.856
and I'm going through my normal rear foot elevated, all right?

11:40:22.297 --> 11:40:26.021
So you probably won't feel much in the glutes or the hamstrings.

11:40:26.041 --> 11:40:28.123
You're gonna feel everything in the quad.

11:40:28.724 --> 11:40:37.053
On this exercise, we're trying to bulletproof the quads, which means bulletproof in the knees, getting the knees nice and resilient with this exercise.

11:40:37.373 --> 11:40:39.316
So if you didn't have the bench, that's okay.

11:40:39.336 --> 11:40:41.638
We could just do a reverse lunge.

11:40:41.939 --> 11:40:44.161
So I'm gonna do the same exact thing.

11:40:44.541 --> 11:40:46.003
I'm gonna step back,

11:40:45.983 --> 11:40:47.165
and I'm getting back up.

11:40:47.185 --> 11:40:50.390
I'm going to squeeze up at that top position.

11:40:50.771 --> 11:40:53.876
As always, start body weight, gradually go up.

11:40:53.896 --> 11:40:58.744
We do want to load as heavy as we possibly can once the form is down and everything is right.

11:40:59.205 --> 11:41:05.095
Just because it's a TKE quad focus exercise doesn't mean that this is something where we're just going to go light.

11:41:05.476 --> 11:41:09.683
We do want to progressively overload and get as heavy as we can with those dumbbells.

11:41:10.104 --> 11:41:10.945
Let's get it.

11:41:20.999 --> 11:41:41.185
so for my band stability ball hamstring curl if we have some thin bands we can put one around a power rack or something nice and stable i actually like to link together two of them if you don't have this band i'm going to show you the alternative at the end of this video so if we have it i'm going to put my feet up on the stability ball i'm going to get

11:41:42.042 --> 11:41:44.607
the band right around my midfoot.

11:41:44.647 --> 11:41:49.336
Now, I got to stay in dorsiflexion, so I got to keep my toes back towards my head.

11:41:49.716 --> 11:41:55.727
And the good thing about this is we're getting a lot of that anterior tibialis work, so that muscle right along the shin.

11:41:56.088 --> 11:41:57.731
We're going to really work that muscle here.

11:41:58.192 --> 11:42:00.536
So I want to start with some light tension on the band.

11:42:00.576 --> 11:42:02.199
I don't want it to be completely slack.

11:42:02.640 --> 11:42:03.662
So I'm here.

11:42:04.925 --> 11:42:13.339
Now I'm gonna bridge up nice and flat like a bridge, squeeze the glutes, pull the toes back towards the head, and then it's just a hamstring curl.

11:42:13.599 --> 11:42:20.571
So I'm being resisted on the way back and I'm controlling on the eccentric.

11:42:22.272 --> 11:42:24.758
So we should feel a big burn in the hamstrings.

11:42:24.978 --> 11:42:31.373
We will also feel a little bit of a burn in the calves, but especially on the front of that shin, on those shin muscles.

11:42:31.633 --> 11:42:37.767
Now, if you do not have the band, then version one is just a regular Swiss ball curl.

11:42:38.557 --> 11:42:42.182
We can really work the same way there, just without the band.

11:42:42.682 --> 11:42:44.785
But a lot of you guys, that'll be a little bit too easy.

11:42:44.825 --> 11:42:52.435
So you would come to one leg and I would bridge up and I would go single leg curls.

11:42:54.118 --> 11:42:59.164
Now, if that one is a little too hard, but the double leg is a little too easy, I could go supported.

11:42:59.204 --> 11:43:00.466
So I just put that foot down.

11:43:00.847 --> 11:43:03.250
I do not have my weight on that left leg.

11:43:03.310 --> 11:43:07.956
My weight is still all on that right leg, but it's just for a little bit of support.

11:43:08.341 --> 11:43:09.864
and then I'm working there.

11:43:12.389 --> 11:43:18.420
Make sure on all these variations, we keep the hips up flat like a bridge the entire time.

11:43:18.760 --> 11:43:24.972
If that hip starts to sag, then we get some good work in the hamstrings, but we take all that tension off of the glutes.

11:43:25.313 --> 11:43:26.535
All right, let's get it.

11:43:35.324 --> 11:43:41.832
So for our rear foot elevated foot pops, we're gonna take a big step forward, normal rear foot elevated position.

11:43:42.092 --> 11:43:44.095
If you wanna curl the toe, that's fine.

11:43:44.435 --> 11:43:47.939
I like to point the toe so the top of my foot is down on that bench.

11:43:47.959 --> 11:43:52.905
Now from here, I want pretty much a vertical shin, so I don't wanna have a steep shin angle here.

11:43:53.586 --> 11:43:57.791
Vertical shin, I'm gonna come to the ball of my foot, okay?

11:43:58.031 --> 11:44:01.095
You could do this barefoot or you could do it in shoes.

11:44:01.075 --> 11:44:07.521
Heel is going to be off the ground, and then from there, I'm at like maybe 60 to 90 degree knee angle.

11:44:07.861 --> 11:44:16.769
From there, I'm just popping that foot up and down as quick as I can, and I can never let that heel touch the ground.

11:44:17.129 --> 11:44:19.632
You're going to feel a huge burn through the calves.

11:44:19.932 --> 11:44:22.514
We're co-contracting all of those ankle muscles.

11:44:22.855 --> 11:44:31.082
Those intrinsic foot muscles are getting fired up, so we're really training that entire lower leg for strength, stability, and stiffness.

11:44:31.062 --> 11:44:33.245
Now, pay attention to the rep range.

11:44:33.285 --> 11:44:36.971
I do not want you to count reps, so this exercise is for time.

11:44:37.391 --> 11:44:42.539
Set the timer or have a friend time you and get in as many ground contacts as you can.

11:44:42.860 --> 11:44:46.886
Get that foot moving as many as we can in that time frame.

11:44:47.186 --> 11:44:47.907
Let's do it.

11:45:11.798 --> 11:45:15.824
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

11:45:15.944 --> 11:45:17.747
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

11:45:17.787 --> 11:45:20.491
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

11:45:20.531 --> 11:45:27.061
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

11:45:27.581 --> 11:45:33.530
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

11:45:33.790 --> 11:45:37.536
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

11:45:37.516 --> 11:45:41.446
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

11:45:41.887 --> 11:45:45.696
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

11:45:46.277 --> 11:45:53.635
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

11:45:54.627 --> 11:45:57.690
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

11:45:57.711 --> 11:46:04.438
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

11:46:04.979 --> 11:46:10.745
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

11:46:10.765 --> 11:46:14.429
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

11:46:16.732 --> 11:46:21.057
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

11:46:23.973 --> 11:46:25.876
and that stomach will fall, okay?

11:46:26.597 --> 11:46:29.261
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

11:46:30.002 --> 11:46:38.996
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

11:46:39.817 --> 11:46:47.568
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

11:46:47.829 --> 11:46:51.955
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

11:46:51.935 --> 11:47:20.727
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

11:47:20.977 --> 11:47:23.481
happens in the parasympathetic state.

11:47:24.063 --> 11:47:28.170
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

11:47:28.511 --> 11:47:42.175
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

11:47:43.960 --> 11:47:47.265
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

11:47:47.285 --> 11:47:51.551
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

11:47:51.851 --> 11:47:58.661
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

11:47:58.942 --> 11:48:02.447
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

11:48:02.727 --> 11:48:10.238
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

11:48:10.218 --> 11:48:14.570
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

11:48:14.991 --> 11:48:16.956
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

11:48:16.996 --> 11:48:19.463
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

11:48:19.763 --> 11:48:22.110
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

11:48:22.130 --> 11:48:24.356
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

11:48:24.636 --> 11:48:26.421
I'm just gonna lean forward.

11:48:26.401 --> 11:48:29.128
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

11:48:29.449 --> 11:48:36.469
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

11:48:36.629 --> 11:48:41.743
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

11:48:42.601 --> 11:48:46.511
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

11:48:46.531 --> 11:48:49.498
15 seconds on the other side as well.

11:48:49.518 --> 11:48:51.623
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

11:48:51.824 --> 11:48:53.247
So that is the back of the thigh.

11:48:53.488 --> 11:48:57.758
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

11:48:57.738 --> 11:49:01.488
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

11:49:01.628 --> 11:49:04.055
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

11:49:04.395 --> 11:49:06.721
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

11:49:06.782 --> 11:49:08.626
So now look at this back foot.

11:49:08.667 --> 11:49:10.110
I'm not curling the toe under.

11:49:10.471 --> 11:49:14.582
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

11:49:14.562 --> 11:49:17.986
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

11:49:18.406 --> 11:49:23.752
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

11:49:24.072 --> 11:49:37.347
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

11:49:37.327 --> 11:49:40.591
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

11:49:40.631 --> 11:49:41.973
That's the inside of the leg.

11:49:42.453 --> 11:49:45.817
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

11:49:46.418 --> 11:49:47.099
Same thing.

11:49:47.439 --> 11:49:50.323
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

11:49:50.623 --> 11:49:53.767
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

11:49:54.047 --> 11:49:57.912
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

11:49:58.212 --> 11:50:02.878
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

11:50:02.858 --> 11:50:05.843
this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

11:50:06.204 --> 11:50:11.693
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

11:50:12.114 --> 11:50:22.352
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

11:50:22.332 --> 11:50:30.208
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

11:50:30.729 --> 11:50:33.514
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

11:50:33.955 --> 11:50:39.987
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

11:50:39.967 --> 11:50:43.031
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

11:50:43.112 --> 11:50:45.275
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

11:50:45.515 --> 11:50:49.160
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

11:50:49.641 --> 11:50:51.223
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

11:50:51.604 --> 11:50:54.128
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

11:50:54.208 --> 11:50:55.149
So I'm 90 there.

11:50:55.490 --> 11:50:58.274
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

11:50:58.634 --> 11:51:00.918
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

11:51:01.278 --> 11:51:05.985
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

11:51:06.325 --> 11:51:09.450
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

11:51:09.430 --> 11:51:12.354
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

11:51:12.714 --> 11:51:17.481
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

11:51:17.521 --> 11:51:18.963
All right.

11:51:18.983 --> 11:51:24.851
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

11:51:25.592 --> 11:51:29.397
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

11:51:29.377 --> 11:51:34.723
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

11:51:35.103 --> 11:51:38.327
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

11:51:38.427 --> 11:51:41.631
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

11:51:42.011 --> 11:51:51.722
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

11:51:52.023 --> 11:51:58.510
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

11:51:58.770 --> 11:52:04.937
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

11:52:05.338 --> 11:52:09.422
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

11:52:10.583 --> 11:52:14.388
A lot of us aren't gonna be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

11:52:14.748 --> 11:52:17.952
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

11:52:17.992 --> 11:52:21.996
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

11:52:24.659 --> 11:52:25.300
What's up, guys?

11:52:25.440 --> 11:52:29.905
Quick minimum dose advice for phase nine, day three and day six.

11:52:29.925 --> 11:52:31.967
So this is our core and mobility day.

11:52:32.568 --> 11:52:35.671
As you can see, don't need to cut out anything.

11:52:35.892 --> 11:52:38.355
Very short, very efficient workout.

11:52:38.955 --> 11:52:43.641
Since we're at six days a week, we can't have these long core and mobility workouts.

11:52:44.381 --> 11:52:53.772
We would rather just get in, get it done in 20, 30 minutes, and get out of the gym because we need to recover for the next day.

11:52:53.752 --> 11:52:59.244
If you have to cut anything back, just cut from four sets down to three sets, all right?

11:52:59.945 --> 11:53:02.010
But don't cut out any exercises.

11:53:02.130 --> 11:53:04.575
All of these exercises are absolutely essential.

11:53:04.876 --> 11:53:05.517
Let's get it.

11:53:14.778 --> 11:53:18.383
So for our leg raises, you're gonna go on a Roman chair if you have it.

11:53:18.563 --> 11:53:21.647
If not, then you can just hang on a normal pull-up bar.

11:53:21.987 --> 11:53:23.389
Either one will work just fine.

11:53:23.449 --> 11:53:26.653
So if I'm in the Roman chair, I'm gonna get set up here.

11:53:26.673 --> 11:53:30.038
Now, you guys are used to doing a full knee raise.

11:53:30.358 --> 11:53:33.262
That bottom half is really more hip flexors.

11:53:33.722 --> 11:53:35.925
The top half, from here,

11:53:35.905 --> 11:53:42.054
to here to as high as I can get that knee is going to be abs, especially those lower abdominals.

11:53:42.115 --> 11:53:46.441
So what I got to think about is a posterior pelvic tilt.

11:53:47.262 --> 11:53:51.589
So if this is an anterior pelvic tilt, posterior is the opposite.

11:53:51.689 --> 11:53:52.891
Think about like I have a tail.

11:53:53.412 --> 11:53:54.994
Think about tucking that tail.

11:53:55.495 --> 11:54:01.104
That motion of posterior pelvic tilt is what's going to really activate those abdominals.

11:54:01.164 --> 11:54:03.347
So I'm here.

11:54:03.563 --> 11:54:11.774
and I got to posteriorly tilt, think about getting my knees as high as I can, and then back down halfway.

11:54:12.355 --> 11:54:18.464
So that is how we actually train the abs instead of just working the hip flexors.

11:54:18.724 --> 11:54:27.276
So for our leg raises, you can either go in a Roman chair like this if you have it, if not, then we're just gonna hang from a regular pull-up bar.

11:54:32.285 --> 11:54:36.730
So for my ankle toe crawls, what I'm going to do is get in a half kneeling position.

11:54:37.071 --> 11:54:42.237
I'm going to work on my ankle dorsiflexion by driving my knee as far as I can over the toe.

11:54:42.638 --> 11:54:45.181
And then I'm going to flow straight into my toe stretch.

11:54:45.201 --> 11:54:52.670
So I got to get this knee down to the floor while keeping my big toe and my other four toes down on the ground.

11:54:52.710 --> 11:54:54.192
So I'm here.

11:54:54.408 --> 11:54:58.337
Now look at that position that I'm in with the toe.

11:54:58.357 --> 11:55:01.405
So I'm challenging my toe mobility.

11:55:01.465 --> 11:55:08.561
So what I'm going to do is just crawl in this position and I can do anywhere from four to eight reps each leg.

11:55:08.581 --> 11:55:09.704
So I'm going to start at four.

11:55:10.346 --> 11:55:11.809
So I'm here.

11:55:12.160 --> 11:55:23.137
nice and slow, look how I crawl the hand forward, and then I ground that next foot, drive the knee over the toe, and gradually transition, all right?

11:55:23.418 --> 11:55:29.207
So I'm literally just crawling, and I'm challenging my ankle mobility,

11:55:29.187 --> 11:55:32.132
and my toe mobility as I go.

11:55:32.352 --> 11:55:35.177
So this is essential when it comes to getting shin angles.

11:55:35.197 --> 11:55:40.186
So you guys hear me talk a lot about the most explosive athletes have good shin angles.

11:55:40.206 --> 11:55:47.518
If you can get horizontal shin angles, we produce more horizontal force, which means we get somewhere faster, right?

11:55:47.558 --> 11:55:50.503
So horizontal force production is essential, but

11:55:50.483 --> 11:56:00.874
If you get here and you lock up, right, because you don't have the ankle mobility or you have the ankle mobility but you don't have the toe mobility, we can't get that shin angle.

11:56:01.214 --> 11:56:02.636
So now we have vertical shins.

11:56:03.136 --> 11:56:08.442
I don't care how much force you produce with vertical shins, you can't get horizontal.

11:56:08.842 --> 11:56:11.845
So half the battle is how much force we produce.

11:56:11.865 --> 11:56:15.549
The other half of the battle is the angle that we're producing the force.

11:56:15.909 --> 11:56:17.571
And so that's why we're training

11:56:17.551 --> 11:56:24.338
This ankle mobility, you see how I got to be able to get my knee far out over my toe, which takes dorsiflexion range of motion.

11:56:24.838 --> 11:56:29.743
But as I extend this hip, I need to have that toe range of motion.

11:56:29.803 --> 11:56:34.287
If that toe locks up, then I don't get full extension of the hip.

11:56:34.668 --> 11:56:45.398
So we need that dorsiflexion and we need that toe range of motion so that we can get these good shin angles and get full triple extension on our first step explosiveness.

11:56:45.378 --> 11:56:51.709
So we're going to do anywhere from four to eight reps, and we can do anywhere from two to four sets.

11:56:52.029 --> 11:56:54.854
Do this a couple days a week, up to three days a week.

11:56:55.114 --> 11:56:57.959
This is a good one that you can just add to your warm-ups.

11:56:58.199 --> 11:57:07.815
You could add this to your dynamic warm-ups, or if you're at home and you're bored watching TV, it's a great exercise to do just to challenge that ankle mobility and that toe mobility.

11:57:08.156 --> 11:57:08.837
Let's get it.

11:57:14.487 --> 11:57:18.640
So for our lateral oblique crunch, there's a lot of different ways we can do this.

11:57:18.660 --> 11:57:25.763
If you have something to hold down your feet, or you have a partner to hold your feet down, then just go in a stacked position.

11:57:26.418 --> 11:57:28.641
and we can just go on a BOSU ball.

11:57:28.661 --> 11:57:34.370
So you'd put your hip directly on the center of that BOSU ball, and then I would come fully extended.

11:57:34.730 --> 11:57:41.340
So you could either cross your arms or just keep your arms here, or you could bring one arm here and one arm across the chest.

11:57:41.660 --> 11:57:43.263
I like to do that option a lot of times.

11:57:43.303 --> 11:57:52.296
So in this position now, I have my whole torso hanging over the BOSU, and I'm just gonna come into a little lateral crunch, all right?

11:57:52.316 --> 11:57:54.619
So it's a small range motion.

11:57:54.599 --> 11:58:00.548
but I'm really getting a good workout on that top oblique, so my left oblique is fired up right now.

11:58:00.928 --> 11:58:04.814
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a BOSU ball, so there's some other options.

11:58:05.275 --> 11:58:14.548
We could go on a bench and get our torso off the bench, have a partner hold down our legs and do the same thing, or we could go on a hyperextension machine

11:58:16.469 --> 11:58:20.176
and come in that way so that our feet are anchored.

11:58:20.798 --> 11:58:26.088
And same thing, my body is now hanging off so I can do the same exact thing.

11:58:26.649 --> 11:58:33.623
Actually, in this position, I could even hold a dumbbell if that's too easy and still get that good oblique work.

11:58:34.044 --> 11:58:38.292
Now, if none of those setups work for you, then just go to a cable.

11:58:41.528 --> 11:58:46.895
Bring it down to the lowest setting and we can just do a side crunch like this.

11:58:47.456 --> 11:58:49.779
So any of those options work.

11:58:49.799 --> 11:58:54.284
All we're looking for is a big workout, a big burn in those side obliques.

11:58:54.605 --> 11:58:58.390
If the rep range is too easy for you, then go ahead and add reps.

11:58:58.410 --> 11:59:02.075
We want to go pretty much to a maximal burn on this exercise.

11:59:02.415 --> 11:59:03.216
Let's do it.

11:59:16.375 --> 11:59:21.765
So for my single leg supported rotations, what I'm going to do is step in front of a bench.

11:59:22.005 --> 11:59:28.657
If you don't have a bench, you can actually put your back foot on a wall and get the sole flat to the wall just for a little added stability.

11:59:29.077 --> 11:59:32.864
Now if your stability is very good, you could just do this on one leg.

11:59:33.125 --> 11:59:39.075
But for most of you guys, I want you doing it assisted and we can actually get a little more mobility if it is assisted.

11:59:39.055 --> 11:59:40.416
So take a big step forward.

11:59:40.677 --> 11:59:42.078
I'm going to throw my back foot up.

11:59:42.158 --> 11:59:47.503
Instead of pointing the toe like I normally would for a rear foot elevated, I'm curling that toe.

11:59:48.183 --> 11:59:54.609
If you have a band, pull that band tight and get it up above the head or any type of light dowel would work.

11:59:55.310 --> 12:00:05.940
Slight bend in that front knee and now I hinge from the hips and from here I'm just rotating as far as I can and then back to neutral.

12:00:05.920 --> 12:00:35.860
and as far as i can the other way and back to neutral okay i'm going to feel that stretch all through the hips lateral portion of the hips medial portion of the hips my upper back and my shoulder my thoracic spine i'm getting a lot of really good mobility so we should feel this a little bit throughout the entire body we're really mobilizing the entire body and getting some stability at the same time let's do it

12:00:46.403 --> 12:00:57.119
So for my rolling cross touch, what I'm going to do is I'm just going to bring my feet off the ground and I'm going to bring my hands off the ground so I'm in this hollow body position.

12:00:57.360 --> 12:01:06.894
I don't want to have an excessive curve in the lower back, so I want to think about tilting my pelvis, pretend like you have a tail, and think about tucking that tail so your back is nice and flat to the floor.

12:01:07.315 --> 12:01:09.238
And then from there, I'm just rolling.

12:01:09.218 --> 12:01:11.100
So my feet still aren't going to touch the ground.

12:01:11.140 --> 12:01:12.822
My hands are still not going to touch the ground.

12:01:13.162 --> 12:01:13.983
I'm going to roll over.

12:01:14.023 --> 12:01:15.405
I'm going to roll over again.

12:01:15.525 --> 12:01:19.890
So I end up in the same position and then I'm going to go cross body.

12:01:19.910 --> 12:01:25.096
So when I roll that way, I'm going to go left hand touches the right toe and back down.

12:01:25.336 --> 12:01:28.400
And again, left hand, right toe back down.

12:01:28.820 --> 12:01:30.602
And then I roll back to the other side.

12:01:31.022 --> 12:01:33.085
So now I'll end up in the same position.

12:01:33.465 --> 12:01:36.829
Now, once I'm back here, I go right hand left.

12:01:36.809 --> 12:01:39.154
So right hand, left toe.

12:01:39.435 --> 12:01:41.880
And then again, I roll back to the other side.

12:01:42.341 --> 12:01:50.979
So the reason that we are rolling is it is one of the most fundamental movement patterns that humans have and we lose it over time.

12:01:51.180 --> 12:01:56.050
But when we roll, we're training so many core muscles and we're training them reactively.

12:01:56.030 --> 12:02:01.541
It is a training stimulus for the core that we can't get with any traditional core exercise.

12:02:01.821 --> 12:02:10.478
So it's really building up that core stability and it's building it up naturally and reflexively which is really going to carry over to all of our athletic movements.

12:02:25.038 --> 12:02:28.465
For the hip flexor tilt, I'm just gonna use a box of bench or a chair.

12:02:29.186 --> 12:02:34.015
I'm gonna get my back foot up so the top of my foot is on the bench.

12:02:34.055 --> 12:02:35.638
I'm not gonna go with my toe on the bench.

12:02:36.039 --> 12:02:38.584
Top of the foot on the bench, I'm just gonna come down.

12:02:38.905 --> 12:02:43.333
Now I wanna get my heel touching my hips or as close as I can get to that.

12:02:43.313 --> 12:02:45.316
If you have a pad, that'd be better.

12:02:45.356 --> 12:02:46.658
I wouldn't do this on hardwood.

12:02:46.838 --> 12:02:48.521
Make sure you got a nice, soft surface.

12:02:49.022 --> 12:02:49.783
So I'm touching.

12:02:50.183 --> 12:02:54.049
I'm in a straight line from my knee all the way up through my shoulders.

12:02:54.590 --> 12:02:55.752
And I'm just going to sit here.

12:02:55.892 --> 12:02:57.454
First, I'm going to get in a good position.

12:02:57.955 --> 12:02:59.918
This ankle should be under that knee.

12:03:00.559 --> 12:03:04.365
I should already feel a little bit of a stretch in that hip flexor, a little bit in the quad as well.

12:03:04.786 --> 12:03:07.289
Now from here, I'm getting nice and wide.

12:03:07.309 --> 12:03:08.471
I'm not going to lean forward.

12:03:08.812 --> 12:03:10.975
And I'm going arms out.

12:03:10.955 --> 12:03:22.686
and together now at the top with straight elbows not bent elbows at the top I'm going to lean towards the lead leg so that's to my right as far as I can

12:03:23.408 --> 12:03:27.076
hold for five, and then come right back, okay?

12:03:27.096 --> 12:03:36.156
And then we're hands down, all the way up, hands together, nice and tall, elongate through the spine, and lean towards that lead leg.

12:03:36.196 --> 12:03:42.430
Three, two, one, back down, and we're back through.

12:03:45.548 --> 12:03:46.229
What's up, guys?

12:03:46.349 --> 12:03:51.137
Quick minimum dose advice for phase 10, day one and day four.

12:03:51.397 --> 12:03:53.100
So this is our lower body strength day.

12:03:53.501 --> 12:03:56.706
As you can see, already very short and efficient.

12:03:56.746 --> 12:03:59.150
We probably shouldn't have to cut anything out here.

12:03:59.531 --> 12:04:03.577
Now, just because it is short and efficient doesn't mean that it's easy.

12:04:03.898 --> 12:04:05.440
It is very high intensity.

12:04:05.500 --> 12:04:08.485
So these rear foot elevated split squats.

12:04:08.465 --> 12:04:11.189
Alright, six sets of two.

12:04:11.669 --> 12:04:16.977
So you might see two and think that it's easy, but two means that, of course, we're max intensity.

12:04:17.497 --> 12:04:22.785
So we're gonna be loaded up, very heavy, and six sets is quite a lot.

12:04:22.825 --> 12:04:25.288
Make sure you're taking plenty of rest time in between.

12:04:25.348 --> 12:04:28.212
We want full recovery.

12:04:28.192 --> 12:04:46.977
Now if you have any type of knee problems, patellar tendonitis, anything like that, that might be too intense, so you could go back to another hip dominant exercise, a deadlift or something else, but we still want to get that high intensity just in a pain-free zone, so find an exercise that you're pain-free.

12:04:46.957 --> 12:04:50.285
Full range of motion RDL, so four sets of five.

12:04:50.687 --> 12:04:52.752
This is not gonna be maximally loaded.

12:04:52.832 --> 12:04:55.800
We're more so going for full range of motion.

12:04:56.441 --> 12:05:00.231
So for some of you guys, that's gonna be a normal RDL, just full range.

12:05:00.492 --> 12:05:03.058
For some of you, we're actually gonna elevate on a box

12:05:03.038 --> 12:05:09.388
or a bumper plate, and we're getting that max range, I want you guys to start to strengthen in that end range.

12:05:09.629 --> 12:05:17.441
That's where most injuries happen, is at our near end range of that muscle length, so that's why we're going full range of motion there.

12:05:18.823 --> 12:05:22.189
The mini band ankle inversion, eversion, definitely don't skip that.

12:05:22.529 --> 12:05:29.721
One leg functional calf raise, if you have to skip one, this is the one to take out, but definitely try to leave it in, it's a very good exercise.

12:05:29.853 --> 12:05:31.376
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

12:05:31.516 --> 12:05:33.219
I have a long roller from Skills.

12:05:33.479 --> 12:05:40.350
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

12:05:40.711 --> 12:05:46.140
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

12:05:46.120 --> 12:05:49.223
So you can either go this way or you could go vertical.

12:05:49.564 --> 12:05:58.714
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

12:05:58.734 --> 12:06:05.141
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

12:06:05.862 --> 12:06:12.229
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

12:06:12.209 --> 12:06:14.552
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

12:06:14.572 --> 12:06:15.974
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

12:06:16.314 --> 12:06:24.705
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

12:06:25.085 --> 12:06:36.900
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

12:06:37.100 --> 12:06:38.882
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

12:06:39.182 --> 12:06:42.585
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

12:06:42.665 --> 12:06:50.892
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

12:06:50.952 --> 12:06:54.716
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

12:06:55.116 --> 12:06:56.477
And our next one we're flipping over.

12:06:56.538 --> 12:06:59.680
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

12:06:59.740 --> 12:07:07.107
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll.

12:07:07.087 --> 12:07:29.753
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh so i'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee

12:07:29.733 --> 12:07:31.497
Our next one is going to be for the adductors.

12:07:31.517 --> 12:07:32.639
That's the inside of the leg.

12:07:32.820 --> 12:07:34.563
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

12:07:34.884 --> 12:07:48.653
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside of the top of the knee all the way up through the groin area.

12:07:50.422 --> 12:07:57.232
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

12:07:57.432 --> 12:08:00.998
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

12:08:01.298 --> 12:08:04.563
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

12:08:05.104 --> 12:08:07.808
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

12:08:07.848 --> 12:08:09.490
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

12:08:09.811 --> 12:08:13.637
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

12:08:14.077 --> 12:08:17.222
And I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

12:08:17.202 --> 12:08:25.049
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

12:08:25.390 --> 12:08:28.092
We got to really start to loosen that up.

12:08:29.694 --> 12:08:34.258
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

12:08:34.498 --> 12:08:38.001
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

12:08:38.762 --> 12:08:43.206
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

12:08:43.586 --> 12:08:45.268
Everything else is extra credit.

12:08:48.337 --> 12:08:52.083
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

12:08:52.363 --> 12:08:54.947
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

12:08:55.007 --> 12:08:58.973
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

12:08:59.474 --> 12:09:06.244
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

12:09:06.224 --> 12:09:10.069
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

12:09:10.109 --> 12:09:11.491
I want to stay on the ball of the foot.

12:09:11.911 --> 12:09:15.035
And I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

12:09:15.376 --> 12:09:16.837
I'm not going too fast yet.

12:09:17.118 --> 12:09:19.321
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

12:09:19.541 --> 12:09:24.087
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

12:09:24.107 --> 12:09:28.272
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

12:09:28.693 --> 12:09:32.217
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

12:09:32.557 --> 12:09:34.560
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

12:09:34.540 --> 12:09:36.803
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

12:09:37.424 --> 12:09:42.450
Maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

12:09:42.911 --> 12:09:44.492
We're just getting that blood flowing.

12:09:44.773 --> 12:09:46.135
We're gonna go for 30 seconds.

12:09:46.295 --> 12:09:50.640
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

12:09:50.981 --> 12:09:52.803
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

12:09:52.843 --> 12:09:57.589
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees.

12:09:57.849 --> 12:10:02.455
Just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

12:10:02.435 --> 12:10:10.150
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

12:10:13.792 --> 12:10:18.158
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

12:10:18.379 --> 12:10:23.707
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

12:10:23.987 --> 12:10:29.215
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

12:10:29.295 --> 12:10:30.537
I'm keeping that knee in.

12:10:30.957 --> 12:10:38.989
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

12:10:38.969 --> 12:11:00.825
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

12:11:01.717 --> 12:11:05.364
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

12:11:05.785 --> 12:11:08.169
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

12:11:08.209 --> 12:11:10.233
One, two, three.

12:11:10.293 --> 12:11:12.577
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

12:11:12.898 --> 12:11:14.922
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

12:11:14.962 --> 12:11:15.543
That's okay.

12:11:15.883 --> 12:11:17.466
Let's just get as low as we can there.

12:11:17.947 --> 12:11:18.949
One, two, three.

12:11:18.929 --> 12:11:20.633
three, and back up.

12:11:20.693 --> 12:11:22.697
So three there, three there.

12:11:23.018 --> 12:11:29.311
I'm gonna go five cycles, and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

12:11:29.411 --> 12:11:32.879
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

12:11:32.919 --> 12:11:36.607
So it's one skip lateral, and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor.

12:11:37.068 --> 12:11:39.553
Keep this knee straight because I wanna get that

12:11:39.533 --> 12:11:59.787
stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat and i'm getting nice and low touch the floor and i'm popping out to that other side all right so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor

12:11:59.767 --> 12:12:14.434
Same thing with the skip and now I land I turn this foot forward I pivot I'm here knee a couple inches off the floor I'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

12:12:19.358 --> 12:12:25.885
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

12:12:25.905 --> 12:12:27.366
Don't make the mistake of going here.

12:12:27.747 --> 12:12:35.795
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

12:12:35.915 --> 12:12:41.781
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

12:12:41.861 --> 12:12:45.585
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

12:12:45.818 --> 12:13:04.249
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one

12:13:04.229 --> 12:13:23.453
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

12:13:23.787 --> 12:13:52.849
one two three lean one two three heels back down one two three one up two up stand all right if you cannot get down in that deep position then let's just go as low as we can right here and then on the lean it's just gonna look like this all right and then we're gonna come back hopefully parallel to the floor one up two up stand we just got to get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower better range of motion so

12:13:52.829 --> 12:13:55.672
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

12:13:55.732 --> 12:13:58.054
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

12:13:58.434 --> 12:14:01.236
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

12:14:01.557 --> 12:14:03.258
I need to be flat through the hips.

12:14:03.538 --> 12:14:05.941
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

12:14:06.481 --> 12:14:10.905
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

12:14:11.285 --> 12:14:14.068
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

12:14:14.348 --> 12:14:22.195
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

12:14:22.175 --> 12:14:24.520
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

12:14:25.582 --> 12:14:32.537
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

12:14:32.557 --> 12:14:36.705
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

12:14:36.725 --> 12:14:38.469
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

12:14:38.709 --> 12:14:42.377
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

12:14:49.748 --> 12:14:55.534
So for our rear foot elevated split squat, if you have a Smith machine, I advise that we use that.

12:14:55.734 --> 12:15:03.261
It's one of the best things that we can do for the RFPSS simply because we have enough stability to where we can really get max intent.

12:15:03.301 --> 12:15:07.906
We can load up the weight and get explosive without worrying about the balance factor.

12:15:08.206 --> 12:15:12.711
So it's one of the best ways to build pure strength and power in the rear foot elevated.

12:15:12.731 --> 12:15:17.055
If you don't have a Smith machine, I would rather not go barbell.

12:15:17.035 --> 12:15:18.944
I would rather just go to dumbbells.

12:15:18.964 --> 12:15:22.018
So we're going to do the same thing, just with dumbbells as heavy as you can.

12:15:22.279 --> 12:15:24.750
So for this rear foot elevated,

12:15:25.675 --> 12:15:28.498
We are going to go balanced glute and quad.

12:15:28.538 --> 12:15:31.722
So sometimes we go ankle underneath the knee.

12:15:31.762 --> 12:15:32.703
That's going to be more glute.

12:15:32.983 --> 12:15:42.714
Right now at that bottom position, when I got my rear foot up and I'm down on my knee, I want my knee directly over the toe.

12:15:43.074 --> 12:15:44.276
So not beyond the toe.

12:15:44.396 --> 12:15:46.638
I just want it right over that toe.

12:15:46.999 --> 12:15:49.101
So that's the position that we're going to work with.

12:15:50.667 --> 12:15:56.715
Of course, we would always start with just the bar, and then we would gradually load up heavier and heavier if we're feeling good.

12:15:57.737 --> 12:16:03.585
So unrack it, and then it's control down, tap that knee, explode up as hard as I can.

12:16:03.625 --> 12:16:08.351
Now if I'm loaded heavy, of course I'm not gonna be able to get up very fast.

12:16:08.631 --> 12:16:20.067
It should be taking between half a second and a second to get from the bottom all the way to the top, but we have the intent to move that bar with a fast speed.

12:16:20.047 --> 12:16:24.112
Okay, so we have two options for unracking that bar.

12:16:24.212 --> 12:16:31.140
So the first option is what I just described where we get that position from there and then we go up and we unrack.

12:16:31.481 --> 12:16:44.957
The other option is just to mark out maybe with a piece of tape or a weight where our toe will be and then I can lift up with two and then I can get that toe right where that piece of tape is and then I can go back from there.

12:16:44.937 --> 12:16:49.924
With the Smith machine, we have the luxury of doing that since we have some stability, we don't have to worry about the balance as much.

12:16:50.325 --> 12:16:54.571
So I can go here, and then I can work from there.

12:16:55.252 --> 12:17:00.179
Now, if you have the dumbbells, you don't have that luxury, so I would rather see you start from the bottom up.

12:17:00.740 --> 12:17:07.930
I would rather see the dumbbells be on the floor, and then we set up in our position, grab the dumbbells, and then we go from there.

12:17:08.291 --> 12:17:09.713
All right, let's get after it.

12:17:25.201 --> 12:17:32.618
So for our full range of motion RDLs, what I'm going to do is I can either use a barbell or if you have a trap bar, that's even better.

12:17:33.199 --> 12:17:35.123
So I'm going to show you guys with the barbell.

12:17:35.143 --> 12:17:39.393
So I would go with my alternating grip just outside hip width.

12:17:39.613 --> 12:17:43.041
So for my first rep, I want to do a normal deadlift up.

12:17:43.628 --> 12:17:47.555
Now from here, for an RDL, we want a slight bend in the knee.

12:17:47.875 --> 12:17:50.379
From there, I am not bending my knees anymore.

12:17:50.439 --> 12:17:51.561
From there, it's just a hip hinge.

12:17:51.902 --> 12:17:56.770
So I'm essentially just pushing my hips backwards as I go into this RDL.

12:17:56.790 --> 12:18:01.778
So I'm bringing the bar close down my body, all right?

12:18:02.199 --> 12:18:07.628
Full range all the way, and back up, okay?

12:18:08.435 --> 12:18:15.044
Notice that I was able to get to the floor, so I'm probably not at my maximal range of motion.

12:18:15.705 --> 12:18:22.995
For a lot of you guys, the majority of you guys, you are full range and you won't quite be able to touch the floor, so that's fine.

12:18:23.516 --> 12:18:26.821
But for somebody who has that range of motion, we actually wanna go elevated.

12:18:27.241 --> 12:18:30.927
So first progression, I would just stand on one bumper plate.

12:18:30.947 --> 12:18:37.716
For me, I've been doing these a while, so I'm gonna actually stand on two bumper plates, make sure we're nice and stable there.

12:18:39.198 --> 12:18:41.342
And then, as always, I'm going to deadlift it up.

12:18:43.487 --> 12:18:45.150
Same thing, same form.

12:18:47.114 --> 12:18:47.676
RDL.

12:18:48.056 --> 12:18:53.728
Now notice I can get a lot lower to the point where I can't quite reach the floor.

12:18:54.309 --> 12:18:55.692
Alright, so it's the same form.

12:18:56.334 --> 12:18:58.438
I'm going to get a huge stretch.

12:18:58.823 --> 12:19:02.270
in the hamstrings at that bottom position, all right?

12:19:02.430 --> 12:19:11.388
Now, common mistakes is people start getting competitive and start pushing for more range of motion, and to do that, they start rounding their back.

12:19:11.488 --> 12:19:13.111
We do not wanna round the back.

12:19:13.412 --> 12:19:18.722
I wanna keep spinal integrity, so I want to keep that neutral spine

12:19:18.702 --> 12:19:22.690
where I can keep my back completely straight all the way through.

12:19:22.750 --> 12:19:26.097
We're getting all of that range of motion just through the hips.

12:19:26.779 --> 12:19:31.328
Now because of the added range of motion, you're going to want to start very light.

12:19:31.408 --> 12:19:37.521
So start with the barbell, maybe go up to 25s on each side and then 35s on each side.

12:19:37.501 --> 12:19:41.668
Most of you guys aren't going to need much more than 45 pounds on each side.

12:19:41.768 --> 12:19:43.111
Some of you guys are super strong.

12:19:43.491 --> 12:19:44.994
You can progress past that point.

12:19:45.515 --> 12:19:47.519
Majority of you guys are going to be fine with this.

12:19:47.839 --> 12:19:55.653
Keep in mind, this is not an exercise that creates a huge burn, but the next day you're going to be very sore.

12:19:56.795 --> 12:20:01.483
This exercise is known for causing delayed onset muscle soreness.

12:20:01.463 --> 12:20:11.115
Two days after you're probably still going to be pretty sore from this because that eccentric is so long and slow and the range of motion is so big.

12:20:11.376 --> 12:20:15.281
We're getting a lot of those microscopic tears in the muscle fibers.

12:20:15.301 --> 12:20:18.284
So great exercise but it's going to cause a lot of soreness.

12:20:18.785 --> 12:20:22.370
So do not load up too heavy your first session.

12:20:22.390 --> 12:20:24.332
Let's make gradual improvements.

12:20:35.146 --> 12:20:46.487
So for our miniband inversion, eversion, what I'm gonna do is get this miniband sort of around the ball of the foot or around midfoot, and I'm just gonna space it out so I have a little bit of tension there.

12:20:46.828 --> 12:20:53.661
Now if your miniband is super light and you don't have a stronger one, then you're gonna go even further out to get more tension on it.

12:20:54.687 --> 12:20:56.348
So for me, I got pretty good tension.

12:20:56.689 --> 12:21:00.572
So I'm going maybe a little more narrow than shoulders width apart.

12:21:00.993 --> 12:21:04.556
Now from here, most of you guys are used to the windshield wipers.

12:21:04.996 --> 12:21:07.218
So that's abduction adduction.

12:21:07.438 --> 12:21:12.583
But what we want to do is actually turn the soles of the feet in and then the soles of the feet out.

12:21:12.963 --> 12:21:15.065
It's going to be a small range of motion.

12:21:15.386 --> 12:21:17.007
But what I mean is this.

12:21:17.047 --> 12:21:24.694
So I'm dropping the soles of the feet in and then I'm pushing the soles of the feet out as far as I can.

12:21:24.674 --> 12:21:49.129
out and with that inwards movement naturally you're gonna get some adduction so the toes are gonna point towards each other so it is sort of like your typical windshield wiper but we want to think about this movement instead of this movement so what we're doing with this exercise we're really strengthening up the ankles we're strengthening up the sides of the shins now think about how you sprain an ankle with an inversion sprain

12:21:49.109 --> 12:21:51.231
that foot is coming to the inside.

12:21:51.551 --> 12:21:57.256
So that's what we want to get here is we want to get there and then we're pushing against resistance to get it back.

12:21:57.576 --> 12:22:08.566
So we're strengthening up all of those, the muscles on that lateral aspect of the ankle, the foot, the shin, so that we can really help reduce the risk of sprained ankles.

12:22:08.866 --> 12:22:15.372
But also it's good because when we go to jump, we need good co-contraction around the ankle so that we're nice and stable.

12:22:15.672 --> 12:22:17.934
This is a major key for one foot jumping.

12:22:18.314 --> 12:22:19.015
Let's do it.

12:22:31.246 --> 12:22:35.712
So for the one leg functional calf raise, you can either do this barefoot or you could use shoes.

12:22:36.073 --> 12:22:39.137
Ideally, if you're in a good situation, go barefoot.

12:22:39.197 --> 12:22:42.241
So find either a stair, a step, or a weight.

12:22:42.602 --> 12:22:45.326
Make sure that weight is heavy so that it doesn't fly up at you.

12:22:45.967 --> 12:22:47.749
And you're gonna go ball of the foot on.

12:22:47.789 --> 12:22:51.955
So about half of your foot is gonna be on that edge

12:22:52.812 --> 12:22:54.634
You're going to hold on to something for support.

12:22:54.955 --> 12:22:57.298
I don't want you trying this without support just yet.

12:22:58.079 --> 12:22:59.741
We're going to stand on one leg.

12:22:59.762 --> 12:23:04.748
Instead of a normal calf raise where it's just the ankle, we're going to get the knee and the hip involved.

12:23:05.049 --> 12:23:06.691
So it's almost like a quarter squat.

12:23:06.751 --> 12:23:11.057
So like a partial squat, and then you finish with the calf raise.

12:23:11.498 --> 12:23:12.359
Okay, so it's here.

12:23:13.100 --> 12:23:14.502
Finish high with the calf raise.

12:23:14.522 --> 12:23:16.424
You've got to explode up on your toes.

12:23:16.865 --> 12:23:21.351
So it's squat, press, squat, press, squat, press.

12:23:21.331 --> 12:23:26.100
Notice this other leg is driving, just like in a single leg vertical jump.

12:23:26.120 --> 12:23:29.346
So we're back with that left leg, drive with the left leg.

12:23:29.587 --> 12:23:31.891
Back with the left leg, drive with the left leg.

12:23:32.633 --> 12:23:35.378
Notice I'm focusing on stiffness here with this foot.

12:23:35.638 --> 12:23:38.484
So as I go down, I'm not letting it collapse.

12:23:38.504 --> 12:23:41.770
I'm keeping it completely even with that ledge.

12:23:42.745 --> 12:24:11.250
keep it even keep it stiff and then at the top drive up as high as we can okay so sometimes it is good to isolate with normal calf raises but this is a much more functional way of doing it and this teaches our glutes and our quads and our hamstrings to send energy down through the calves and then down through the feet to reach the floor to actually produce power that we need in the vertical jump so normally this exercise should just be body weight especially for phase one but some of our pros uh

12:24:11.230 --> 12:24:14.058
More of our advanced athletes, we will go weighted vest.

12:24:14.078 --> 12:24:18.451
You could go 10, 15, 20 pound weighted vest, but still hold on for support.

12:24:18.691 --> 12:24:22.843
Not necessary, but if you are advanced, you can use that weighted vest up front.

12:24:47.208 --> 12:24:51.232
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

12:24:51.373 --> 12:24:53.154
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

12:24:53.215 --> 12:24:55.917
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

12:24:55.958 --> 12:25:02.465
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

12:25:02.985 --> 12:25:09.192
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

12:25:09.212 --> 12:25:12.996
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

12:25:12.976 --> 12:25:16.843
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

12:25:17.284 --> 12:25:21.653
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

12:25:21.693 --> 12:25:29.027
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

12:25:30.037 --> 12:25:32.741
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

12:25:33.121 --> 12:25:39.850
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

12:25:40.391 --> 12:25:46.178
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

12:25:46.198 --> 12:25:49.863
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

12:25:52.146 --> 12:25:56.432
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

12:25:59.365 --> 12:26:01.288
and that stomach will fall, okay?

12:26:02.009 --> 12:26:04.653
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

12:26:05.414 --> 12:26:14.447
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

12:26:15.228 --> 12:26:23.220
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

12:26:23.240 --> 12:26:27.246
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

12:26:27.226 --> 12:26:56.187
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

12:26:56.403 --> 12:26:58.908
happens in the parasympathetic state.

12:26:59.449 --> 12:27:03.557
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

12:27:03.938 --> 12:27:17.584
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

12:27:19.336 --> 12:27:22.641
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

12:27:22.681 --> 12:27:26.947
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

12:27:27.227 --> 12:27:34.037
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

12:27:34.337 --> 12:27:37.822
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

12:27:38.102 --> 12:27:45.613
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

12:27:45.593 --> 12:27:49.944
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

12:27:50.365 --> 12:27:52.330
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

12:27:52.670 --> 12:27:54.876
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

12:27:55.157 --> 12:27:57.222
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

12:27:57.502 --> 12:27:59.728
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

12:28:00.029 --> 12:28:01.813
I'm just gonna lean forward.

12:28:01.793 --> 12:28:04.498
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

12:28:04.838 --> 12:28:11.831
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

12:28:12.011 --> 12:28:16.840
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

12:28:18.142 --> 12:28:22.570
And I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

12:28:22.887 --> 12:28:24.749
15 seconds on the other side as well.

12:28:24.869 --> 12:28:27.012
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

12:28:27.192 --> 12:28:28.634
So that is the back of the thigh.

12:28:28.854 --> 12:28:33.059
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

12:28:33.179 --> 12:28:36.863
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

12:28:37.023 --> 12:28:39.446
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

12:28:39.786 --> 12:28:42.109
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

12:28:42.469 --> 12:28:44.031
So now look at this back foot.

12:28:44.051 --> 12:28:45.473
I'm not curling the toe under.

12:28:45.853 --> 12:28:49.958
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

12:28:49.938 --> 12:28:53.363
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

12:28:53.783 --> 12:28:59.151
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

12:28:59.451 --> 12:29:12.790
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

12:29:12.770 --> 12:29:15.997
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

12:29:16.037 --> 12:29:17.360
That's the inside of the leg.

12:29:17.841 --> 12:29:21.209
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

12:29:21.810 --> 12:29:22.512
Same thing.

12:29:22.833 --> 12:29:25.739
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

12:29:26.020 --> 12:29:29.327
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

12:29:29.307 --> 12:29:33.272
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

12:29:33.592 --> 12:29:41.242
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

12:29:41.603 --> 12:29:47.090
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

12:29:47.510 --> 12:29:52.817
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

12:29:52.797 --> 12:29:57.546
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

12:29:57.947 --> 12:30:05.601
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

12:30:06.102 --> 12:30:08.907
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

12:30:09.348 --> 12:30:15.379
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

12:30:15.359 --> 12:30:18.444
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

12:30:18.484 --> 12:30:20.667
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

12:30:20.887 --> 12:30:24.553
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

12:30:25.013 --> 12:30:26.596
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

12:30:26.996 --> 12:30:29.540
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

12:30:29.600 --> 12:30:30.502
So I'm 90 there.

12:30:30.862 --> 12:30:33.666
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

12:30:34.027 --> 12:30:36.310
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

12:30:36.671 --> 12:30:41.358
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

12:30:41.718 --> 12:30:44.843
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

12:30:44.823 --> 12:30:47.729
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

12:30:48.110 --> 12:30:52.880
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

12:30:52.920 --> 12:30:54.344
All right.

12:30:54.364 --> 12:31:00.256
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

12:31:00.998 --> 12:31:04.806
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

12:31:04.786 --> 12:31:10.116
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

12:31:10.497 --> 12:31:13.723
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

12:31:13.823 --> 12:31:17.009
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

12:31:17.390 --> 12:31:27.148
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

12:31:27.128 --> 12:31:34.164
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

12:31:34.185 --> 12:31:40.339
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

12:31:40.740 --> 12:31:45.070
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

12:31:45.877 --> 12:31:49.005
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

12:31:49.046 --> 12:31:49.767
That's okay.

12:31:50.128 --> 12:31:53.317
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

12:31:53.377 --> 12:31:57.388
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

12:32:00.203 --> 12:32:05.289
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for day two and day five of phase 10.

12:32:05.369 --> 12:32:07.392
So this is our plyo and agility day.

12:32:07.812 --> 12:32:17.083
Now keep in mind, since we are back to back, right, yesterday we had our lower body strength workout, if you have knee problems, you're gonna wanna space this by a day.

12:32:17.424 --> 12:32:25.113
You're probably gonna wanna go down to three days a week, so you would do your strength Monday, do this workout Wednesday, and then do your strength again Friday.

12:32:25.273 --> 12:32:26.835
And then the next week you could flip it.

12:32:26.815 --> 12:32:31.021
So you can do this workout Monday and Friday and the strength workout Wednesday.

12:32:32.183 --> 12:32:35.768
For most of us, we're good to go and we're highly trained by this point.

12:32:35.808 --> 12:32:40.335
We're used to this higher frequency, the more days per week, and so we should be fine.

12:32:40.415 --> 12:32:47.185
But if you're waking up and your joints are sore, your knees are sore, then you might want to push this back a day.

12:32:47.305 --> 12:32:50.410
Do not plow through the program.

12:32:50.390 --> 12:32:52.994
Music freestyle agility, do not skip this.

12:32:53.094 --> 12:32:54.776
I really want you guys to hit that.

12:32:55.197 --> 12:33:00.745
If you're gonna skip anything, it would be the lateral med ball toss and the med ball acceleration change of direction.

12:33:01.226 --> 12:33:10.819
Great exercises, but I understand it takes time and it takes a wall and a med ball, so some of you guys aren't gonna have the resources, so you can skip those if you have to.

12:33:11.340 --> 12:33:12.702
High box depth jump.

12:33:12.682 --> 12:33:14.986
This is the potent stimulus for the day.

12:33:15.446 --> 12:33:22.558
This is gonna lead to probably our best vertical jump gains, but also with that comes higher stress.

12:33:22.918 --> 12:33:26.104
So a high box depth jump is higher stress to the knees.

12:33:26.144 --> 12:33:33.255
So if you're prone to knee injuries or patellar tendonitis, this is one that we're gonna have to scale back and listen to your body.

12:33:33.235 --> 12:33:35.218
Rear foot elevated stiffness jump.

12:33:35.578 --> 12:33:38.242
If you have the time and the energy, let's definitely do this.

12:33:38.642 --> 12:33:41.306
Great exercise for building that elasticity.

12:33:42.127 --> 12:33:44.230
If you don't have the time, it's okay to skip that.

12:33:44.250 --> 12:33:46.914
20 minute dunk session, have to do it.

12:33:47.354 --> 12:33:53.202
So the mandatory ones for this day is the music freestyle agility.

12:33:53.182 --> 12:33:58.186
the high box depth jump as long as your knees are healthy, and the 20-minute dunk session.

12:33:58.526 --> 12:34:01.189
If you get those three things, we're getting everything that we need.

12:34:01.689 --> 12:34:06.493
These other ones are just extra credit that I advise you get if you do have the time.

12:34:06.873 --> 12:34:08.415
So we're gonna get through a foam roller circuit.

12:34:08.535 --> 12:34:10.256
I have a long roller from Skills.

12:34:10.496 --> 12:34:17.382
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

12:34:17.723 --> 12:34:23.087
So the first muscle that we're gonna focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

12:34:23.067 --> 12:34:26.231
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

12:34:26.571 --> 12:34:35.402
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

12:34:35.723 --> 12:34:42.150
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

12:34:42.871 --> 12:34:49.159
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

12:34:49.139 --> 12:34:51.543
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

12:34:51.563 --> 12:34:52.986
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

12:34:53.326 --> 12:35:01.700
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

12:35:02.101 --> 12:35:13.881
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

12:35:14.114 --> 12:35:15.876
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

12:35:16.176 --> 12:35:19.600
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

12:35:19.680 --> 12:35:27.889
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

12:35:27.969 --> 12:35:31.713
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

12:35:32.134 --> 12:35:33.495
And our next one we're flipping over.

12:35:33.555 --> 12:35:36.699
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

12:35:36.759 --> 12:35:44.087
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

12:35:44.067 --> 12:35:50.355
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

12:35:51.076 --> 12:35:54.060
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

12:35:54.501 --> 12:35:59.748
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

12:36:00.208 --> 12:36:06.717
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

12:36:06.697 --> 12:36:08.501
Our next one is going to be for the adductors.

12:36:08.541 --> 12:36:09.644
That's the inside of the leg.

12:36:09.824 --> 12:36:11.588
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

12:36:11.889 --> 12:36:25.701
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside of the top of the knee all the way up through the groin area.

12:36:27.419 --> 12:36:34.248
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

12:36:34.429 --> 12:36:38.014
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

12:36:38.294 --> 12:36:41.558
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

12:36:42.119 --> 12:36:44.803
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

12:36:44.823 --> 12:36:46.505
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

12:36:46.806 --> 12:36:50.811
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

12:36:50.791 --> 12:36:53.757
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

12:36:54.319 --> 12:37:00.211
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day.

12:37:00.512 --> 12:37:02.075
We're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

12:37:02.416 --> 12:37:05.222
We got to really start to loosen that up.

12:37:06.603 --> 12:37:11.256
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

12:37:11.517 --> 12:37:15.046
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

12:37:15.086 --> 12:37:20.240
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

12:37:20.601 --> 12:37:22.266
Everything else is extra credit.

12:37:25.351 --> 12:37:29.337
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

12:37:29.357 --> 12:37:31.961
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

12:37:32.021 --> 12:37:35.987
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

12:37:36.488 --> 12:37:43.078
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

12:37:43.419 --> 12:37:47.104
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

12:37:47.144 --> 12:37:48.827
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

12:37:48.807 --> 12:37:52.032
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

12:37:52.373 --> 12:37:53.836
I'm not going too fast yet.

12:37:54.136 --> 12:37:56.300
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

12:37:56.540 --> 12:38:11.806
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs, so I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand, and then I'm just gonna speed that up.

12:38:11.826 --> 12:38:14.190
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

12:38:14.170 --> 12:38:39.469
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed so we're not all out we're just getting that blood flowing we're gonna go for 30 seconds for the over under hamstring stretch i'm just gonna go over with the left leg i'm gonna come down touch the toe now if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back then just go mid-shin or even to the knees just as low as we can but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back

12:38:39.449 --> 12:38:47.164
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

12:38:50.806 --> 12:38:55.172
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

12:38:55.373 --> 12:39:00.721
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

12:39:00.981 --> 12:39:07.551
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

12:39:07.971 --> 12:39:16.003
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

12:39:15.983 --> 12:39:20.912
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

12:39:21.253 --> 12:39:26.863
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

12:39:27.544 --> 12:39:29.888
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

12:39:30.229 --> 12:39:34.838
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

12:39:35.479 --> 12:39:37.823
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

12:39:38.748 --> 12:39:42.353
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

12:39:42.794 --> 12:39:47.240
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

12:39:47.300 --> 12:39:49.564
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

12:39:49.904 --> 12:39:51.907
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

12:39:51.967 --> 12:39:52.548
That's okay.

12:39:52.889 --> 12:39:54.471
Let's just get as low as we can there.

12:39:54.952 --> 12:39:55.913
One, two, three.

12:39:55.893 --> 12:39:57.636
three and back up.

12:39:57.696 --> 12:39:59.719
So three there, three there.

12:40:00.020 --> 12:40:04.247
I'm going to go five cycles and then I'm going to switch to the other side.

12:40:04.788 --> 12:40:06.331
Five and five, same thing.

12:40:06.411 --> 12:40:09.877
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

12:40:09.917 --> 12:40:12.361
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down.

12:40:12.421 --> 12:40:13.603
I'm trying to touch this floor.

12:40:14.064 --> 12:40:16.568
Keep this knee straight because I want to get that

12:40:16.548 --> 12:40:19.012
stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

12:40:19.333 --> 12:40:26.245
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat, and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

12:40:26.706 --> 12:40:30.713
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

12:40:31.074 --> 12:40:36.784
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

12:40:36.764 --> 12:40:51.330
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

12:40:56.373 --> 12:41:02.900
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

12:41:02.920 --> 12:41:04.382
Don't make the mistake of going here.

12:41:04.742 --> 12:41:12.810
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

12:41:12.911 --> 12:41:18.797
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

12:41:18.857 --> 12:41:22.701
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

12:41:22.681 --> 12:41:24.464
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

12:41:24.724 --> 12:41:26.667
One, two, three.

12:41:26.807 --> 12:41:29.771
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

12:41:29.952 --> 12:41:32.175
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

12:41:32.195 --> 12:41:34.518
One, two, three.

12:41:34.578 --> 12:41:36.060
Rock forward.

12:41:36.161 --> 12:41:37.202
So I'm on my toes now.

12:41:37.683 --> 12:41:39.906
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

12:41:39.946 --> 12:41:40.267
Hold.

12:41:40.907 --> 12:41:41.989
One, two, three.

12:41:41.969 --> 12:41:42.630
Three.

12:41:42.971 --> 12:41:43.693
Rock it back.

12:41:44.054 --> 12:41:45.858
Heels back down to the ground.

12:41:46.118 --> 12:41:47.742
One, two, three.

12:41:47.842 --> 12:41:49.045
I need one hand up.

12:41:49.606 --> 12:41:50.388
Two hand up.

12:41:50.608 --> 12:41:51.290
Not out here.

12:41:51.350 --> 12:41:52.833
We need it back behind the head.

12:41:53.414 --> 12:41:54.036
And stand.

12:41:54.597 --> 12:41:54.858
Alright.

12:41:55.319 --> 12:41:56.682
So that's one cycle.

12:41:56.962 --> 12:41:58.065
We're going to go for three of these.

12:41:59.027 --> 12:42:00.450
So it's one, two, three.

12:42:00.785 --> 12:42:03.048
One, two, three, lean.

12:42:03.829 --> 12:42:06.091
One, two, three, heels back down.

12:42:06.151 --> 12:42:10.196
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

12:42:10.477 --> 12:42:18.787
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

12:42:18.807 --> 12:42:23.172
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

12:42:23.532 --> 12:42:29.880
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

12:42:29.860 --> 12:42:32.683
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

12:42:32.743 --> 12:42:35.046
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

12:42:35.446 --> 12:42:38.249
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

12:42:38.570 --> 12:42:40.251
I need to be flat through the hips.

12:42:40.552 --> 12:42:42.934
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

12:42:43.475 --> 12:42:47.900
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

12:42:48.300 --> 12:42:51.083
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

12:42:51.363 --> 12:42:59.192
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

12:42:59.172 --> 12:43:01.516
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

12:43:02.598 --> 12:43:09.151
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

12:43:09.571 --> 12:43:13.719
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

12:43:13.739 --> 12:43:15.703
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

12:43:15.723 --> 12:43:17.907
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

12:43:17.887 --> 12:43:26.063
So for my music freestyle agility, this drill is a lot of fun and it's very different from traditional training.

12:43:26.163 --> 12:43:38.528
So what I talk about a lot in the past, you've heard me say rhythm is everything, contract relax is everything for athleticism, for agility, for change of direction.

12:43:38.508 --> 12:43:46.882
There's probably no better way to develop this than actually going to a beat, getting loose, freestyling to your favorite song.

12:43:47.022 --> 12:43:53.212
So what we're going to do is, if you're a basketball player, I like to just pretend like I have a basketball.

12:43:53.232 --> 12:43:54.674
We're going to try to go to the beat.

12:43:54.995 --> 12:43:56.878
Now, you create your own rules.

12:43:56.918 --> 12:43:58.581
You've got your own rhythm.

12:43:58.601 --> 12:43:59.883
You figure it out yourself.

12:44:00.183 --> 12:44:05.612
But I want you to pick a song that's like three or four minutes long, and we're going to freestyle to that whole thing.

12:44:05.592 --> 12:44:09.320
You can have slow sections where we relax and go smooth.

12:44:09.621 --> 12:44:11.064
You can have fast sections.

12:44:11.465 --> 12:44:22.230
You can challenge your foot patterns in ways that you might not even challenge it in a game, but we're just building that general ability to get our feet to listen to our brain in any scenario.

12:44:22.310 --> 12:44:24.495
So I'm going to give you a quick example.

12:44:25.049 --> 12:44:26.794
I hope you can hear the music a little bit.

12:44:27.236 --> 12:44:31.067
I like to use instrumentals, so I'm just going to use an instrumental.

12:44:31.388 --> 12:44:39.312
I'm going three straight minutes, and I'm just going to get loose, get creative.

12:45:26.272 --> 12:45:28.018
You guys get the point.

12:45:28.420 --> 12:45:29.122
Get creative.

12:45:29.183 --> 12:45:29.965
Do whatever you want.

12:45:29.985 --> 12:45:31.531
That's what athleticism is all about.

12:45:31.812 --> 12:45:35.867
Contract, relax, rhythm, fluidity, intuition.

12:45:36.148 --> 12:45:37.131
This drill builds it all.

12:45:37.453 --> 12:45:38.155
Let's get it.

12:45:45.897 --> 12:45:55.457
So for my lateral med ball toss, I'm basically going to push out laterally, and I'm going to change directions as fast as I can, push through the med ball, kind of like a shot put.

12:45:55.938 --> 12:46:06.960
Boom, you got your guide hand and your follow through hand, so almost like a basketball shot, except it's going to be horizontal, it's going to be with a lot more power, and this is really driven from that lower body.

12:46:07.177 --> 12:46:08.098
So it's going to be here.

12:46:08.839 --> 12:46:09.680
Boom.

12:46:09.700 --> 12:46:10.721
Transition.

12:46:10.742 --> 12:46:11.783
Push as hard as I can.

12:46:12.243 --> 12:46:15.267
Notice I didn't stay here and just turn the upper body.

12:46:15.708 --> 12:46:18.451
I pushed off and I fully turned my body.

12:46:18.772 --> 12:46:22.096
I'm getting that good rotational power and that good lateral power.

12:46:22.116 --> 12:46:27.302
A lot of times when we're playing defense and defender goes by us, it's a rotational push.

12:46:27.322 --> 12:46:28.744
It's not always lateral.

12:46:28.764 --> 12:46:29.786
It's not always linear.

12:46:30.146 --> 12:46:31.267
It's rotational.

12:46:31.548 --> 12:46:33.791
So we're working on that same thing here.

12:46:33.851 --> 12:46:34.872
Now

12:46:34.852 --> 12:46:50.548
remember i want you to transition out quick so it's got to be here and then immediately push out so it's bone drive out toss as hard as you can pick it up and reset so if you do not have the med ball then let's just do regular lateral bounds so it's here

12:46:50.528 --> 12:46:55.256
push high as we can, as far as we can with short ground contact time.

12:46:55.557 --> 12:46:58.462
That's still going to get us the same qualities as far as that lateral power.

12:46:58.742 --> 12:47:01.828
We're just missing out on the rotational power, but that's okay.

12:47:01.848 --> 12:47:04.072
I know a lot of you guys don't have that med ball.

12:47:04.092 --> 12:47:06.155
So if that's the case, that's a good alternative.

12:47:06.556 --> 12:47:07.137
Let's get it.

12:47:15.723 --> 12:47:22.869
All right, so now on this combo exercise, if you have the wall and you have a court or somewhere to sprint off, then we're going to do the full drill.

12:47:23.169 --> 12:47:24.030
If not, it's okay.

12:47:24.050 --> 12:47:25.251
We're just going to do half of the drill.

12:47:25.291 --> 12:47:28.774
So if you have the wall and the space, make sure the wall is concrete.

12:47:28.814 --> 12:47:29.454
It's not going to break.

12:47:29.675 --> 12:47:31.556
So it's going to be starting in a split stance.

12:47:31.576 --> 12:47:32.517
It's going to be drive.

12:47:32.537 --> 12:47:33.798
So I'm pushing off both feet.

12:47:34.118 --> 12:47:40.244
I'm really going to drive through this front foot like it's first step explosiveness, push the ball into the wall as hard as I can.

12:47:40.284 --> 12:47:44.147
And then I'm going to decelerate, push laterally, open up and take off.

12:47:44.627 --> 12:47:45.628
So it's going to be here.

12:47:45.608 --> 12:47:49.324
Boom, boom, push out.

12:47:50.165 --> 12:47:54.671
So sprint out as far as you can, so if you have half court, sprint it through half court.

12:47:55.152 --> 12:47:59.818
A lot of you guys don't have that distance, so it's boom, boom, maybe sprint it out a couple steps.

12:48:00.339 --> 12:48:02.722
What matters most is that change of direction.

12:48:02.822 --> 12:48:11.234
So we want boom, sharp change of direction, toe is still pointed forward, sharp change of direction, rotationally, and get out of there, all right?

12:48:11.254 --> 12:48:12.315
I want sharp cuts.

12:48:12.776 --> 12:48:19.986
If you want to set out a cone, you can, so that you have that accountability to get out over the cone and then open up and push out.

12:48:19.966 --> 12:48:22.453
Now if you don't have the med ball, pretend like you do.

12:48:22.533 --> 12:48:30.634
So it's going to be here, pretend like you got that med ball, boom, boom, open up and push out, sprint as far as you can.

12:48:41.228 --> 12:48:48.158
So for my high box death jumps, I'm going to set up with a box probably a little higher than what I'm used to.

12:48:48.198 --> 12:48:50.061
So I'm going to start with a 30-inch box.

12:48:50.101 --> 12:48:53.265
Now, this is a very, very intense exercise.

12:48:53.626 --> 12:49:02.899
It is a potent stimulus, so incredible for a vertical jump, but also higher risk for our knees if you're not quite ready to absorb all this force.

12:49:02.879 --> 12:49:05.582
How you actually choose a box is you would monitor.

12:49:05.682 --> 12:49:11.728
So I know a lot of you guys don't have a just jump mat or something to calculate your vertical, but you can eyeball it or set up a phone.

12:49:12.669 --> 12:49:16.413
Basically, what is your vertical jumping and landing?

12:49:16.473 --> 12:49:19.256
Let's say it's 28 inches, right?

12:49:19.616 --> 12:49:25.282
If I drop off a six inch box, it should still be around 28 inches or maybe actually a little bit higher.

12:49:25.342 --> 12:49:27.925
For most of you guys, it'll be around 28 inches.

12:49:27.905 --> 12:49:45.053
all right then we go up to say 12 inch box same thing and then we go up and we go up and we go you're gonna find a point where your vertical starts to go down the higher the drop gets and that shows you that you're not actually absorbing all of that force

12:49:45.033 --> 12:49:50.521
So at that point where you drop from 28 to 27, we know that we went a little bit too high, right?

12:49:50.882 --> 12:49:57.892
But for me, I can get around the same vertical at a 30 inch box because I can absorb that much force.

12:49:57.932 --> 12:49:59.474
So that's where I'm going to train here.

12:49:59.734 --> 12:50:03.840
So what I'm going to do is just dangle one foot off and I'm just dropping down.

12:50:04.141 --> 12:50:05.703
I'm going to get a normal range of motion.

12:50:05.743 --> 12:50:08.707
I'm not trying to stay as stiff as possible on these jumps.

12:50:09.068 --> 12:50:10.450
So I'm dropping.

12:50:10.430 --> 12:50:13.774
Boom, get up as high as I possibly can, all right?

12:50:14.174 --> 12:50:15.055
Stick that landing.

12:50:15.396 --> 12:50:21.243
Now, I don't want you to make this into a stiff leg depth jump where we're going for pier speed off the ground.

12:50:21.563 --> 12:50:25.328
I want you to land and load up like we normally would in a vertical jump.

12:50:26.749 --> 12:50:28.471
The goal is max jump height.

12:50:28.972 --> 12:50:33.337
Sure, we do want to be quick off the ground, but the number one priority is max jump height.

12:50:33.497 --> 12:50:35.440
So you do not have to switch feet.

12:50:35.580 --> 12:50:38.243
You can drop off with the same foot every time.

12:50:38.223 --> 12:50:41.386
What matters is that you're landing with both feet at the same time.

12:50:41.426 --> 12:50:43.408
We are not landing one, two.

12:50:43.809 --> 12:50:53.558
Now, if you're dropping off a box where you feel joint pain, you feel knee pain or back pain, ankle pain, then you're jumping off a box that is way too high.

12:50:53.838 --> 12:50:54.799
So you want to go lower.

12:50:55.120 --> 12:51:07.572
This should be a pain-free exercise where we're still getting a big vertical, but we are challenging how much force we're absorbing by going off a box that's a little higher than we're used to.

12:51:19.145 --> 12:51:24.934
So for our rear foot elevated split squat, if you have a Smith machine, I advise that we use that.

12:51:25.134 --> 12:51:32.666
It's one of the best things that we can do for the RFESS simply because we have enough stability to where we can really get max intent.

12:51:32.726 --> 12:51:37.313
We can load up the weight and get explosive without worrying about the balance factor.

12:51:37.613 --> 12:51:41.860
So it's one of the best ways to build pure strength and power in the rear foot elevated.

12:51:42.140 --> 12:51:46.587
If you don't have a Smith machine, I would rather not go barbell.

12:51:46.567 --> 12:51:48.377
I would rather just go to dumbbells.

12:51:48.417 --> 12:51:51.453
So we're going to do the same thing, just with dumbbells as heavy as you can.

12:51:51.694 --> 12:51:54.147
So for this rear foot elevated,

12:51:55.072 --> 12:51:57.895
We are going to go balanced glute and quad.

12:51:57.955 --> 12:52:01.139
So sometimes we go ankle underneath the knee.

12:52:01.179 --> 12:52:02.361
That's going to be more glute.

12:52:02.381 --> 12:52:12.112
Right now at that bottom position, when I got my rear foot up and I'm down on my knee, I want my knee directly over the toe.

12:52:12.473 --> 12:52:13.654
So not beyond the toe.

12:52:13.794 --> 12:52:16.057
I just want it right over that toe.

12:52:16.398 --> 12:52:18.480
So that's the position that we're going to work with.

12:52:20.063 --> 12:52:26.111
Of course we would always start with just the bar and then we would gradually load up heavier and heavier if we're feeling good.

12:52:27.133 --> 12:52:32.981
So unrack it and then it's control down, tap that knee, explode up as hard as I can.

12:52:33.021 --> 12:52:37.747
Now if I'm loaded heavy, of course I'm not going to be able to get up very fast.

12:52:38.027 --> 12:52:49.463
It should be taking between half a second and a second to get from the bottom all the way to the top, but we have the intent to move that bar with a fast speed.

12:52:49.443 --> 12:52:53.530
Okay, so we have two options for unracking that bar.

12:52:53.610 --> 12:53:00.541
So the first option is what I just described where we get that position from there and then we go up and we unrack.

12:53:00.901 --> 12:53:14.303
The other option is just to mark out maybe with a piece of tape or a weight where our toe will be and then I can lift up with two and then I can get that toe right where that piece of tape is and then I can go back from there.

12:53:14.283 --> 12:53:19.332
With the Smith machine, we have the luxury of doing that since we have some stability, we don't have to worry about the balance as much.

12:53:19.732 --> 12:53:23.960
So I can go here, and then I can work from there.

12:53:24.661 --> 12:53:29.590
Now, if you have the dumbbells, you don't have that luxury, so I would rather see you start from the bottom up.

12:53:30.130 --> 12:53:37.323
I would rather see the dumbbells be on the floor, and then we set up in our position, grab the dumbbells, and then we go from there.

12:53:37.704 --> 12:53:39.126
All right, let's get after it.

12:53:41.975 --> 12:53:59.661
Now for our freestyle dunks, I put this in there because it's so important that you get out there and just allow your body to explore different approaches, different type of dunks, different type of jumping styles without me always trying to put you in a box and trying to get you to do one thing.

12:54:00.001 --> 12:54:05.569
So I want you to set the clock for 10 minutes and I just want you to go get as many dunks as you can.

12:54:05.549 --> 12:54:07.492
Take breaks in between if you need to.

12:54:07.552 --> 12:54:10.097
I do want you to be recovered in between reps.

12:54:10.117 --> 12:54:12.160
This isn't an endurance workout.

12:54:12.521 --> 12:54:13.863
So get up off one foot.

12:54:13.883 --> 12:54:14.705
Throw it down.

12:54:15.166 --> 12:54:15.747
Walk back.

12:54:15.807 --> 12:54:17.950
Recover for 30 seconds to a minute.

12:54:18.251 --> 12:54:18.912
Come back in.

12:54:19.052 --> 12:54:20.154
Take off off two.

12:54:20.615 --> 12:54:21.837
Try to dunk left.

12:54:21.817 --> 12:54:25.121
Whatever you want to do, get creative and do it.

12:54:25.141 --> 12:54:29.167
Of course, if you can't dunk and all you can do is touch the bottom of the net, I don't care.

12:54:29.227 --> 12:54:30.468
I still want you to get creative.

12:54:30.829 --> 12:54:32.351
Come in off two and touch the net.

12:54:32.671 --> 12:54:35.575
Come from baseline, get up off one and touch the net.

12:54:35.595 --> 12:54:38.018
Try to jump off left, touch with the left.

12:54:39.019 --> 12:54:48.111
Variability, variety is the key to longevity and it's super important for building kind of well-rounded explosiveness and athleticism.

12:54:48.091 --> 12:54:52.853
And so I want you to get creative, set the clock for 10 minutes, and just freestyle dunk.

12:55:18.044 --> 12:55:22.069
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

12:55:22.189 --> 12:55:23.991
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

12:55:24.031 --> 12:55:26.734
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

12:55:26.775 --> 12:55:33.783
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

12:55:33.803 --> 12:55:39.770
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

12:55:40.030 --> 12:55:43.815
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

12:55:43.795 --> 12:55:47.180
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

12:55:48.102 --> 12:55:51.948
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

12:55:52.509 --> 12:55:59.880
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

12:56:00.873 --> 12:56:03.937
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

12:56:03.957 --> 12:56:10.666
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

12:56:11.227 --> 12:56:15.893
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in.

12:56:17.014 --> 12:56:20.679
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

12:56:22.982 --> 12:56:27.268
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

12:56:30.218 --> 12:56:32.121
and that stomach will fall, okay?

12:56:32.842 --> 12:56:35.506
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

12:56:36.247 --> 12:56:45.260
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

12:56:46.061 --> 12:56:53.813
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

12:56:54.073 --> 12:56:58.099
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

12:56:58.079 --> 12:57:26.972
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

12:57:27.239 --> 12:57:29.722
happens in the parasympathetic state.

12:57:30.263 --> 12:57:34.409
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

12:57:34.749 --> 12:57:48.387
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state, and then I can really start to aid my recovery, and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

12:57:50.172 --> 12:57:53.477
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

12:57:53.497 --> 12:57:57.783
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

12:57:58.063 --> 12:58:04.873
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

12:58:05.174 --> 12:58:08.659
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

12:58:08.939 --> 12:58:16.450
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

12:58:16.430 --> 12:58:20.782
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

12:58:21.203 --> 12:58:23.168
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

12:58:23.208 --> 12:58:25.675
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

12:58:25.995 --> 12:58:28.322
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

12:58:28.342 --> 12:58:30.568
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

12:58:30.848 --> 12:58:32.633
I'm just gonna lean forward.

12:58:32.613 --> 12:58:53.406
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

12:58:53.723 --> 12:58:55.565
15 seconds on the other side as well.

12:58:55.705 --> 12:58:57.828
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

12:58:58.028 --> 12:58:59.450
So that is the back of the thigh.

12:58:59.670 --> 12:59:03.895
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

12:59:04.015 --> 12:59:07.699
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

12:59:07.839 --> 12:59:10.282
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

12:59:10.622 --> 12:59:12.945
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

12:59:13.305 --> 12:59:14.867
So now look at this back foot.

12:59:14.887 --> 12:59:16.289
I'm not curling the toe under.

12:59:16.689 --> 12:59:20.794
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

12:59:20.774 --> 12:59:24.201
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

12:59:24.622 --> 12:59:29.992
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

12:59:30.293 --> 12:59:38.749
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down

12:59:38.729 --> 12:59:43.377
to the elbows, and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

12:59:43.798 --> 12:59:48.206
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

12:59:48.687 --> 12:59:53.355
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side, same thing.

12:59:53.676 --> 12:59:56.541
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

12:59:56.861 --> 13:00:00.147
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

13:00:00.127 --> 13:00:04.133
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

13:00:04.433 --> 13:00:12.064
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

13:00:12.445 --> 13:00:17.913
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

13:00:18.333 --> 13:00:23.721
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

13:00:23.701 --> 13:00:28.369
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

13:00:28.769 --> 13:00:36.422
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

13:00:36.943 --> 13:00:39.727
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

13:00:40.188 --> 13:00:46.198
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

13:00:46.178 --> 13:00:49.241
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

13:00:49.321 --> 13:00:51.483
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

13:00:51.723 --> 13:00:55.366
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

13:00:55.847 --> 13:00:57.428
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

13:00:57.809 --> 13:01:00.351
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

13:01:00.431 --> 13:01:01.352
So I'm 90 there.

13:01:01.692 --> 13:01:04.475
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

13:01:04.835 --> 13:01:07.137
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

13:01:07.498 --> 13:01:12.182
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

13:01:12.542 --> 13:01:15.645
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

13:01:15.625 --> 13:01:18.912
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

13:01:18.932 --> 13:01:23.701
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

13:01:23.741 --> 13:01:25.184
All right.

13:01:25.204 --> 13:01:31.076
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

13:01:31.817 --> 13:01:35.625
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

13:01:35.605 --> 13:01:40.935
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

13:01:41.315 --> 13:01:44.561
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

13:01:44.661 --> 13:01:47.847
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

13:01:48.228 --> 13:01:57.985
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

13:01:57.965 --> 13:02:04.699
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

13:02:05.000 --> 13:02:11.193
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

13:02:11.554 --> 13:02:15.923
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

13:02:16.696 --> 13:02:19.826
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

13:02:19.866 --> 13:02:20.608
That's okay.

13:02:20.969 --> 13:02:24.159
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

13:02:24.199 --> 13:02:28.191
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

13:02:30.955 --> 13:02:31.496
What's up, guys?

13:02:31.616 --> 13:02:35.922
Quick minimum dose advice for day three and day six of phase 10.

13:02:37.223 --> 13:02:38.785
Very short workout already.

13:02:39.707 --> 13:02:41.669
Elevated stir the pot, gotta do it.

13:02:42.290 --> 13:02:44.212
Band straight leg raise, gotta do it.

13:02:44.253 --> 13:02:45.274
Stability hip.

13:02:45.294 --> 13:02:54.726
Well, if your hamstrings are extremely flexible and you're getting more than 90 or 100 degrees on your active straight leg raise, make sure you look at the videos tab for that video.

13:02:55.447 --> 13:02:57.109
If you're getting that, then you can skip this.

13:02:57.390 --> 13:02:59.072
Otherwise, I think you should get it.

13:02:59.052 --> 13:03:02.987
Stability ball hip twist, that's a new one you guys are going to like.

13:03:03.067 --> 13:03:07.223
Standing hip cars, definitely do that.

13:03:07.439 --> 13:03:10.243
Side plank row, gotta do that.

13:03:10.624 --> 13:03:17.834
Ankle toe crawls, gotta do that, unless you have crazy ankle mobility, ankle dorsiflexion mobility.

13:03:17.854 --> 13:03:22.541
If you have crazy mobility, you can skip that one, but most of us will definitely do that.

13:03:22.902 --> 13:03:30.072
Now, if you wanna add in some stuff, again, with these core and mobility days, if you don't have enough mobility,

13:03:30.052 --> 13:03:33.997
Add in some mobility drills from the previous phases, that's completely okay.

13:03:34.297 --> 13:03:36.239
You can even add in some core exercises.

13:03:36.299 --> 13:03:45.450
If you don't feel like your core is too tired, keep in mind we don't wanna go crazy because even training our core is demanding on the nervous system.

13:03:45.730 --> 13:03:55.061
And since we have four days a week of lower body, plus you're mixing in your upper body, our nervous system is gonna be fighting an uphill battle.

13:03:55.221 --> 13:03:58.725
So you don't wanna go crazy on your core.

13:03:58.705 --> 13:04:04.199
But you can add in another exercise or maybe two exercises if you really feel like you need it.

13:04:04.521 --> 13:04:05.104
Let's do it.

13:04:16.188 --> 13:04:20.453
So for our elevated stir the pot rockers, what we're going to do is to stir the pot.

13:04:20.834 --> 13:04:23.757
We're going elevated to make this a little bit more difficult.

13:04:23.837 --> 13:04:26.941
So you can either do this on a box or up on a bench.

13:04:27.321 --> 13:04:29.964
Essentially, we want to be flat at the top.

13:04:29.984 --> 13:04:32.387
So if that bench is too high, you're going to be angled down.

13:04:32.708 --> 13:04:35.511
If the bench is too low, you're going to be angled up.

13:04:35.531 --> 13:04:37.213
So we want to be pretty much flat.

13:04:38.254 --> 13:04:40.056
I'm going to curl my toes on that bench.

13:04:40.417 --> 13:04:44.001
I'm going elbows directly underneath the shoulders.

13:04:45.432 --> 13:04:47.356
and stabilize in this position.

13:04:47.397 --> 13:04:52.849
Once we're good, it's going to be circles, big controlled circles.

13:04:53.952 --> 13:04:55.716
All right, I'm going to go look at the rep range.

13:04:55.796 --> 13:04:57.019
Let's say I have eight.

13:04:57.039 --> 13:05:03.995
It's going to be eight that way, eight circles the other way, and then it's going to be eight front back.

13:05:04.015 --> 13:05:04.656
All right.

13:05:04.636 --> 13:05:11.787
The tendency here is for people to start to sag the hips and you'll start feeling it more in the erectors and those back muscles.

13:05:12.107 --> 13:05:13.589
We do not want to feel this in our back.

13:05:13.609 --> 13:05:15.292
We want to feel it front core.

13:05:15.312 --> 13:05:16.634
We want to feel it obliques.

13:05:16.994 --> 13:05:21.121
All right, got to keep the hips up flat the entire set.

13:05:21.141 --> 13:05:25.367
The other common mistake is people get sloppy and out of control on the circles.

13:05:25.808 --> 13:05:30.154
I need you to stay tight and get your full controlled circles.

13:05:30.134 --> 13:05:39.013
Now, if you're super sweaty, make sure that you use a towel over the ball or a shirt over the ball so that you get a good grip with your forearms.

13:05:39.033 --> 13:05:42.481
You don't want to be slipping off the ball because I've seen that happen a lot.

13:05:42.661 --> 13:05:48.273
Now, for some of you guys, that's going to be too advanced and you're not going to be stable enough and it's going to be extremely sloppy.

13:05:48.594 --> 13:05:49.476
If that's the case,

13:05:49.456 --> 13:06:01.390
then take away the bend and go back to your regular stir the pot all right we got to master this one before we get to that one so make sure you're training according to your level let's do it

13:06:09.503 --> 13:06:13.189
Alright, so for my band straight leg raise, I'm going to try to keep my knee pretty straight.

13:06:13.209 --> 13:06:15.032
I could have a slight bend if we need to.

13:06:15.432 --> 13:06:22.924
I'm going to keep my foot dorsiflexed, so that means my toe back towards the head, the band is around the ball of the foot, and then I'm just going rapid with it.

13:06:23.585 --> 13:06:25.909
So let's go to a comfortable range.

13:06:26.260 --> 13:06:29.448
and then we can gradually start to raise it higher and higher.

13:06:29.488 --> 13:06:35.123
Make sure this opposite leg doesn't lift up, but this is not slow static stretching.

13:06:35.484 --> 13:06:36.667
This is very dynamic.

13:06:36.687 --> 13:06:40.878
We're working on that elasticity back in the hamstrings and in the glutes.

13:06:41.219 --> 13:06:42.582
So I'm just gonna build up.

13:06:42.562 --> 13:07:03.270
that speed and get it nice and rapid there all right we should feel a stretch it shouldn't be complete discomfort so maybe like a 7 out of 10 or an 8 out of 10 we're getting a slight stretch if we push it to a 10 out of 10 you're probably going to get a strained muscle so push slightly out of your comfort zone but it shouldn't hurt so bad that it's unbearable let's do it

13:07:11.401 --> 13:07:18.990
Alright, for our stability ball hip twists, what I'm going to do is come on a bench, or you could go on a box, but I like to use a bench.

13:07:19.251 --> 13:07:25.839
I'm going to make sure that my hips are fully supported on the bench, and then I'm going to go with the stability ball right in between my feet.

13:07:26.219 --> 13:07:27.381
Now I'm just going to lay back.

13:07:27.681 --> 13:07:31.346
I want to make sure that my back is flat, so I'm not in that big arch.

13:07:31.606 --> 13:07:36.632
So think about posteriorly tilting your pelvis, tuck your tail so we're flat to the bench.

13:07:36.612 --> 13:07:47.099
I'm going to hold on right here for a little extra support, lay my head back, and all I'm going to do is just turn right over left and then left over right.

13:07:47.520 --> 13:07:49.605
All right, so it's just a hip swivel essentially.

13:07:50.046 --> 13:07:52.292
I'm firing up my core.

13:07:52.272 --> 13:07:56.323
and I'm also gonna train those hip flexors in that position.

13:07:56.463 --> 13:08:06.029
So if you have the hip range of motion to actually get your right foot completely over the left, do it, and then the left foot completely over the right, most of you that will feel unnatural.

13:08:06.069 --> 13:08:08.475
So you'll just go right here,

13:08:08.455 --> 13:08:09.136
to right here.

13:08:09.737 --> 13:08:12.941
But work with whatever range of motion that you have.

13:08:13.302 --> 13:08:15.344
Now, if you don't have a stability ball, that's okay.

13:08:15.364 --> 13:08:18.168
We're going to do the same exact motion without the stability ball.

13:08:18.228 --> 13:08:23.896
So you're just here, feet up, and you're making that same range of motion.

13:08:24.857 --> 13:08:29.484
Now, because you're taking away the weight of the stability ball, it'll be a little bit easier.

13:08:29.804 --> 13:08:31.847
So you'll want to increase the reps a little bit.

13:08:31.827 --> 13:08:36.572
Essentially, we want to get like a 9 out of 10 or a 10 out of 10 burn in the core.

13:08:36.852 --> 13:08:38.935
So we're pretty much going towards failure.

13:08:39.235 --> 13:08:47.303
You're going to push to the point either way, with or without the stability ball, push to the point where you get a 9 or a 10 out of 10 burn in the front abs.

13:08:47.764 --> 13:08:52.169
But if we feel it more in the lower back, then you went a little bit too far.

13:08:52.389 --> 13:08:54.291
So we want no pain in the back.

13:08:54.331 --> 13:08:59.016
We want all that burn in the abs and maybe even a little bit down into the hip flexor.

13:08:59.436 --> 13:09:00.197
Let's do it.

13:09:21.136 --> 13:09:26.242
So for our standing hip CARs, CAR stands for Controlled Articular Rotation.

13:09:26.542 --> 13:09:30.367
So this is a FRC concept, Functional Range Conditioning.

13:09:30.407 --> 13:09:40.499
So this is getting us nice and mobile, but building stability at these short and long muscle lengths, which prevents injuries, helps us get more explosive.

13:09:40.519 --> 13:09:44.764
So it's a little bit different in your normal type of stretching.

13:09:44.744 --> 13:09:49.010
Rule number one, we've got to create tension through the body, tension through the upper body.

13:09:49.070 --> 13:09:53.196
So I need you to squeeze the fist and keep a tight core in this whole drill.

13:09:54.057 --> 13:10:00.947
From here, I'm just going to lift up as high as I can with that right knee.

13:10:00.967 --> 13:10:06.715
And then from there, instead of a normal hip circle where we're down like that, I'm going to turn the foot outwards.

13:10:07.457 --> 13:10:11.983
So I'm going to turn the foot out, and then I'm going to kick it around

13:10:11.963 --> 13:10:27.745
and bring it down and then i'm going to reverse that so now i'm going to kick back as far as i can and then i'm bringing it all the way back around so i like to pretend like there's a really high fence right here and i gotta step over that imaginary fence

13:10:27.725 --> 13:10:28.666
and then bring it down.

13:10:28.967 --> 13:10:31.210
Some of you, balance is going to be an issue here.

13:10:31.230 --> 13:10:37.438
It's OK if you want to hold on to something, hold on to two poles or hold on to a power rack, whatever you've got to do.

13:10:37.698 --> 13:10:42.384
All that matters here is that we're getting the full range of motion through that hip joint.

13:10:42.545 --> 13:10:46.169
So I don't want to leave any range of motion left in the tank.

13:10:46.190 --> 13:10:57.124
I want you to really get every single range of motion so that we can build that stability at those ranges and really free up your hips for more movement.

13:11:10.351 --> 13:11:35.886
for my side plank row i'm going to use a cable if you don't have a cable we can always use a band here i'm going to make sure that that cable is on the lower setting so that we're nice and flat with this cable and it's not aiming up and it's not aiming down so i'm going to get into my side plank position on that forearm i'm going to make sure that i'm grounded from the wrist all the way through the elbow and i'm just going to stack the feet nice and flat

13:11:35.866 --> 13:11:39.871
I'm going to be nice and flat from toes all the way up to my head.

13:11:40.472 --> 13:11:42.835
I'm not sinking, I'm not hiking the hips up.

13:11:43.236 --> 13:11:46.460
And then from here, it's just a regular row.

13:11:47.822 --> 13:11:53.109
So I'm going to feel that a lot in the obliques, especially the one on the underside.

13:11:53.209 --> 13:11:57.375
So if I'm on my right side, that right oblique side of the core is going to be fired up.

13:11:57.856 --> 13:12:02.582
If that is too difficult, we can always stack the feet so you could always

13:12:02.562 --> 13:12:08.445
come to more of a 90 degree position and stack the legs that way.

13:12:08.787 --> 13:12:12.943
Alright, so that's just a little bit of a easier version.

13:12:13.447 --> 13:12:39.943
but i think most of you by this time can go ahead and get into that full stack position so we're flat all the way from the feet through the back of the head get up nice and high but i do think that most of you guys are probably ready to go to that flat position with the straight legs so try that out start with a lightweight as always and gradually go up we do want to go as heavy as we can as long as we're maintaining that posture

13:12:39.923 --> 13:12:44.635
If we're going so heavy that we're starting to go into rotation to pull, that's too heavy.

13:12:44.915 --> 13:12:52.414
This is more of an anti-rotation, anti-lateral flexion exercise, so I want you to stay stable while your arm moves.

13:12:55.769 --> 13:13:00.235
So for my ankle toe crawls, what I'm going to do is get in a half kneeling position.

13:13:00.576 --> 13:13:05.723
I'm going to work on my ankle dorsiflexion by driving my knee as far as I can over the toe.

13:13:06.123 --> 13:13:08.647
And then I'm going to flow straight into my toe stretch.

13:13:08.687 --> 13:13:16.137
So I got to get this knee down to the floor while keeping my big toe and my other four toes down on the ground.

13:13:16.217 --> 13:13:17.659
So I'm here.

13:13:17.909 --> 13:13:21.759
Now look at that position that I'm in with the toe.

13:13:21.779 --> 13:13:24.907
So I'm challenging my toe mobility.

13:13:24.947 --> 13:13:32.065
So what I'm going to do is just crawl in this position and I can do anywhere from four to eight reps each leg.

13:13:32.085 --> 13:13:33.208
So I'm going to start at four.

13:13:33.829 --> 13:13:35.293
So I'm here.

13:13:35.662 --> 13:13:46.639
nice and slow, look how I crawl the hand forward, and then I ground that next foot, drive the knee over the toe, and gradually transition, alright?

13:13:46.919 --> 13:13:52.708
So I'm literally just crawling, and I'm challenging my ankle mobility,

13:13:52.688 --> 13:13:55.613
and my toe mobility as I go.

13:13:55.833 --> 13:13:58.678
So this is essential when it comes to getting shin angles.

13:13:58.698 --> 13:14:03.347
So you guys hear me talk a lot about the most explosive athletes have good shin angles.

13:14:03.687 --> 13:14:10.980
If you can get horizontal shin angles, we produce more horizontal force, which means we get somewhere faster, right?

13:14:11.040 --> 13:14:14.005
So horizontal force production is essential, but

13:14:13.985 --> 13:14:24.395
If you get here and you lock up, right, because you don't have the ankle mobility or you have the ankle mobility but you don't have the toe mobility, we can't get that shin angle.

13:14:24.696 --> 13:14:26.097
So now we have vertical shins.

13:14:26.638 --> 13:14:31.923
I don't care how much force you produce with vertical shins, you can't get horizontal.

13:14:32.343 --> 13:14:35.066
So half the battle is how much force we produce.

13:14:35.346 --> 13:14:39.370
The other half of the battle is the angle that we're producing the force.

13:14:39.390 --> 13:14:41.072
And so that's why we're training

13:14:41.052 --> 13:14:47.839
This ankle mobility, you see how I got to be able to get my knee far out over my toe, which takes dorsiflexion range of motion.

13:14:48.320 --> 13:14:53.245
But as I extend this hip, I need to have that toe range of motion.

13:14:53.285 --> 13:14:57.770
If that toe locks up, then I don't get full extension of the hip.

13:14:58.170 --> 13:15:08.882
So we need that dorsiflexion and we need that toe range of motion so that we can get these good shin angles and get full triple extension on our first step explosiveness.

13:15:08.862 --> 13:15:15.510
So we're gonna do anywhere from four to eight reps, and we can do anywhere from two to four sets.

13:15:15.530 --> 13:15:18.313
Do this a couple days a week, up to three days a week.

13:15:18.594 --> 13:15:21.437
This is a good one that you can just add to your warmups.

13:15:21.677 --> 13:15:31.289
You could add this to your dynamic warmups, or if you're at home and you're bored watching TV, it's a great exercise to do just to challenge that ankle mobility and that toe mobility.

13:15:31.629 --> 13:15:32.490
Let's get it.

13:15:33.027 --> 13:15:38.242
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for day one and day four of phase 11.

13:15:38.302 --> 13:15:40.869
So this is our high intensity day.

13:15:41.451 --> 13:15:43.797
So for our high intensity day,

13:15:44.030 --> 13:15:45.673
We're already pretty efficient.

13:15:46.174 --> 13:15:49.240
If we want to take anything out, let's just cut back on sets.

13:15:49.721 --> 13:15:56.313
So, for example, instead of four sets, you could just cut back and do two sets, but still try to get through all the exercises.

13:15:56.774 --> 13:16:04.328
If you do need to cut out any exercises, it would be the band accelerations and then, of course, the foam roller circuit.

13:16:04.569 --> 13:16:05.751
That is optional.

13:16:05.731 --> 13:16:30.439
and the static stretch that is completely optional everything else we absolutely have to do the high box depth jumps obviously if you have any type of joint pain this is one that we're going to skip if you're in season this would be one that we're going to skip because the eccentric component is too intense but if you're off season and your body's feeling good then definitely get that one in

13:16:30.689 --> 13:16:32.213
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

13:16:32.373 --> 13:16:34.078
I have a long roller from Skills.

13:16:34.319 --> 13:16:41.197
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

13:16:41.558 --> 13:16:46.892
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

13:16:46.872 --> 13:16:50.057
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

13:16:50.397 --> 13:16:59.231
So if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

13:16:59.571 --> 13:17:06.021
Now always if we want more pressure we can cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

13:17:06.702 --> 13:17:13.032
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

13:17:13.012 --> 13:17:15.395
So the next one, we're going to move down the posterior chain.

13:17:15.415 --> 13:17:16.816
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

13:17:17.157 --> 13:17:25.526
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

13:17:25.927 --> 13:17:32.774
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

13:17:33.135 --> 13:17:37.720
And then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

13:17:37.953 --> 13:17:39.715
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

13:17:40.015 --> 13:17:43.439
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

13:17:43.519 --> 13:17:51.728
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

13:17:51.788 --> 13:17:57.314
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

13:17:57.394 --> 13:18:00.538
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

13:18:00.578 --> 13:18:07.926
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

13:18:07.906 --> 13:18:14.198
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

13:18:14.899 --> 13:18:30.589
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

13:18:30.569 --> 13:18:33.475
Our next one is going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

13:18:33.676 --> 13:18:35.419
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

13:18:35.720 --> 13:18:49.489
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

13:18:51.258 --> 13:18:58.088
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

13:18:58.268 --> 13:19:01.854
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

13:19:02.134 --> 13:19:05.399
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

13:19:05.940 --> 13:19:08.644
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

13:19:08.664 --> 13:19:10.347
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

13:19:10.647 --> 13:19:14.473
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

13:19:14.913 --> 13:19:18.058
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

13:19:18.038 --> 13:19:25.890
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

13:19:26.231 --> 13:19:28.915
We got to really start to loosen that up.

13:19:30.538 --> 13:19:35.105
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

13:19:35.365 --> 13:19:38.891
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

13:19:39.612 --> 13:19:44.079
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

13:19:44.439 --> 13:19:46.122
Everything else is extra credit.

13:19:49.173 --> 13:19:52.919
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

13:19:53.200 --> 13:19:55.784
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

13:19:55.844 --> 13:19:59.811
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

13:20:00.312 --> 13:20:07.063
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

13:20:07.043 --> 13:20:10.907
I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

13:20:10.947 --> 13:20:15.873
I want to stay on the ball of the foot, and I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

13:20:16.214 --> 13:20:17.655
I'm not going too fast yet.

13:20:17.956 --> 13:20:20.358
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

13:20:20.378 --> 13:20:30.570
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs, so I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time, back to neutral.

13:20:30.550 --> 13:20:33.037
over with the left, over with the left hand.

13:20:33.357 --> 13:20:35.483
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

13:20:35.503 --> 13:20:42.241
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here, maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed.

13:20:42.301 --> 13:20:43.324
So we're not all out.

13:20:43.745 --> 13:20:45.370
We're just getting that blood flowing.

13:20:45.650 --> 13:20:47.074
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

13:20:47.054 --> 13:20:51.459
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

13:20:51.800 --> 13:20:53.622
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

13:20:53.682 --> 13:21:02.953
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't wanna go to the point where we have to round our back.

13:21:03.453 --> 13:21:10.902
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

13:21:14.645 --> 13:21:17.209
All right, we're gonna go five over and back.

13:21:17.469 --> 13:21:19.032
So it's 10 total touches.

13:21:19.212 --> 13:21:22.918
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch.

13:21:22.938 --> 13:21:24.541
So I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

13:21:24.821 --> 13:21:30.049
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position and I'm not out like this.

13:21:30.150 --> 13:21:31.372
I'm keeping that knee in.

13:21:31.812 --> 13:21:39.825
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can.

13:21:39.805 --> 13:21:44.754
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

13:21:45.095 --> 13:21:50.685
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

13:21:51.366 --> 13:21:53.731
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

13:21:54.051 --> 13:21:58.680
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

13:21:59.321 --> 13:22:01.645
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

13:22:02.570 --> 13:22:06.197
I'm gonna go left leg up outside this left hand.

13:22:06.618 --> 13:22:09.002
Now from here, I'm just gonna rotate up and hold.

13:22:09.062 --> 13:22:11.066
One, two, three.

13:22:11.126 --> 13:22:13.410
Now I'm gonna go elbow down towards the floor.

13:22:13.731 --> 13:22:16.396
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground, that's okay.

13:22:16.716 --> 13:22:18.319
Let's just get as low as we can there.

13:22:18.800 --> 13:22:19.802
One, two, three.

13:22:19.782 --> 13:22:21.464
three, and back up.

13:22:21.524 --> 13:22:23.547
So three there, three there.

13:22:23.567 --> 13:22:30.156
I'm gonna go five cycles, and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

13:22:30.237 --> 13:22:33.681
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

13:22:33.741 --> 13:22:37.707
So it's one skip lateral, and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor,

13:22:37.687 --> 13:22:53.620
keep this knee straight because i want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat and i'm getting nice and low touch the floor and i'm popping out to that other side all right so let's just hold about one second at the bottom

13:22:53.600 --> 13:23:15.253
and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor same thing with the skip and now i land i turn this foot forward i pivot i'm here knee a couple inches off the floor i'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

13:23:20.212 --> 13:23:26.700
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

13:23:26.740 --> 13:23:28.202
Don't make the mistake of going here.

13:23:28.583 --> 13:23:36.653
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

13:23:36.753 --> 13:23:42.621
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

13:23:42.701 --> 13:23:46.506
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

13:23:46.486 --> 13:23:48.289
It's going to be a hamstring stretch.

13:23:48.529 --> 13:23:50.492
One, two, three.

13:23:50.653 --> 13:23:53.597
Keep the hands down and come into a deep squat.

13:23:53.657 --> 13:23:56.001
Try to stay back on your heels if you can.

13:23:56.642 --> 13:23:58.365
One, two, three.

13:23:58.405 --> 13:23:59.907
Rock forward.

13:23:59.987 --> 13:24:01.009
So I'm on my toes now.

13:24:01.530 --> 13:24:03.713
Drive the knees out with the elbows.

13:24:03.793 --> 13:24:04.114
Hold.

13:24:04.735 --> 13:24:05.035
One, two.

13:24:05.015 --> 13:24:24.289
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

13:24:24.623 --> 13:24:26.886
One, two, three, lean.

13:24:27.667 --> 13:24:29.930
One, two, three, heels back down.

13:24:29.990 --> 13:24:34.015
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

13:24:34.295 --> 13:24:39.081
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here.

13:24:39.461 --> 13:24:42.625
And then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

13:24:42.645 --> 13:24:47.011
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

13:24:47.371 --> 13:24:53.719
We just gotta get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

13:24:53.699 --> 13:24:56.522
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

13:24:56.582 --> 13:24:58.864
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

13:24:59.285 --> 13:25:02.067
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

13:25:02.388 --> 13:25:04.089
I need to be flat through the hips.

13:25:04.390 --> 13:25:06.792
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

13:25:07.312 --> 13:25:11.737
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

13:25:12.117 --> 13:25:14.920
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

13:25:15.180 --> 13:25:23.048
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

13:25:23.028 --> 13:25:32.943
and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers, and we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

13:25:33.403 --> 13:25:41.796
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

13:25:53.892 --> 13:25:58.480
So for our banded accelerations, I'm going to get a bench and I'm going to get a band.

13:25:58.800 --> 13:26:01.906
I like to hook two bands together, but all you really need is one band.

13:26:02.367 --> 13:26:14.107
We want a pretty low angle on that, so hook it around something nice and stable, bottom of a power rack, or have a friend hold, but make sure that he's maybe sitting down so that you get that low angle.

13:26:14.487 --> 13:26:17.553
Now, I want to walk it out to get some good band tension.

13:26:17.593 --> 13:26:19.596
I should feel like it's pulling me back.

13:26:19.576 --> 13:26:20.978
Then I'm leaning forward on the bench.

13:26:21.038 --> 13:26:23.061
Of course, make sure this bench is nice and stable.

13:26:23.342 --> 13:26:25.906
You could use a box or anything just to rest your hands on.

13:26:26.847 --> 13:26:27.488
So I'm here.

13:26:28.170 --> 13:26:29.632
Now I'm going in a split stance.

13:26:29.652 --> 13:26:35.100
So notice how I'm in a pretty good position for maximal acceleration right now.

13:26:35.661 --> 13:26:42.753
Now from here, I'm just going to drive as hard as I can off this right leg and I'm pushing that left knee high.

13:26:43.053 --> 13:26:44.976
So I'm driving the knee high.

13:26:44.956 --> 13:27:11.740
towards the chest if you're too close you're going to notice that your knee hits the bench we want to be far enough away to where we end up right here and I'm pulling that toe up towards my head and then I release and I want to build up a little bit of an eccentric so we actually get pretty rapid with it so it's a pause at the top and then I'm back down and I boom explode quick it's all about that fast transition all right so it's here

13:27:12.344 --> 13:27:38.368
drive hold we're squeezing this glue pull that toe up towards the head release go release go we're getting explosive with it now i want to make sure that at the top of that rep i'm flat all the way through back of my head you could draw a straight line down to my heel a lot of you guys when you get low in this uh this 45 degree angle

13:27:38.348 --> 13:27:58.821
you have tucked hip so you're here if we have tucked hip and we have hip flexion we're not getting a hundred percent out of our glutes most powerful muscle in the body most important muscle for acceleration first step explosiveness we got to be able to get that full hip extension all right so

13:27:58.801 --> 13:28:06.419
take a video or have a friend tell you where you're at, but we should be completely straight at the top of every rep.

13:28:06.499 --> 13:28:13.735
Now, if you don't have the setup, you don't have the band or something to anchor to or the bench, but you do have a sled, it's a great alternative.

13:28:13.755 --> 13:28:17.063
So get in a sled and drive at that low angle.

13:28:17.043 --> 13:28:22.834
Work on short accelerations, so maybe 15 to 20 yards, as explosive as you can.

13:28:23.255 --> 13:28:24.998
Load up that sled nice and heavy.

13:28:25.419 --> 13:28:32.151
I do like this variation because we get more of that eccentric, whereas the sled we only get concentric.

13:28:32.572 --> 13:28:35.878
But if you don't have this setup, a sled is a great option as well.

13:28:47.232 --> 13:28:52.803
So for my lateral bound dumbbell punch level two, we're gonna take this to the next level.

13:28:52.843 --> 13:28:54.687
So now I'm going to elevate it on a box.

13:28:54.787 --> 13:28:56.912
Now, maybe mid-shin height.

13:28:57.012 --> 13:28:58.796
We don't want anywhere near knee height.

13:28:58.816 --> 13:28:59.697
That's gonna be too much.

13:29:00.018 --> 13:29:02.443
So what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna bound out.

13:29:02.463 --> 13:29:04.407
I'm not going max distance.

13:29:04.427 --> 13:29:05.950
So think about...

13:29:05.930 --> 13:29:12.276
If that's no distance and my furthest push out is max distance, I want maybe 50% of that.

13:29:13.417 --> 13:29:16.179
So I'm holding my light dumbbell by the side.

13:29:16.419 --> 13:29:17.821
My palm is facing that way.

13:29:18.301 --> 13:29:19.262
I'm going to bound out.

13:29:19.642 --> 13:29:25.668
And now when I land with that left foot, at that same time, I'm punching across.

13:29:25.688 --> 13:29:27.990
So notice it is upper body rotation.

13:29:28.310 --> 13:29:35.937
I'm dropping this shoulder and then I'm ripping back and I'm getting back onto the box as I rip diagonally.

13:29:35.917 --> 13:29:41.475
Alright, so we're here, boom, and push back, stabilize at the top.

13:29:41.917 --> 13:29:43.783
Push, get back.

13:29:44.185 --> 13:29:47.069
push, get back, all right?

13:29:47.089 --> 13:29:55.259
The harder I punch with the dumbbell, the more force I have to absorb with this left leg and redirect with this left leg.

13:29:55.639 --> 13:30:01.587
Now, I don't want this to be something where you're going through the motions and you're just punching with the dumbbell.

13:30:01.967 --> 13:30:04.831
It has to be an upper body rotation.

13:30:05.171 --> 13:30:10.218
So, one thing that this really translates well to is a single leg dunk.

13:30:10.238 --> 13:30:12.741
So if you think about a single leg dunk,

13:30:12.839 --> 13:30:15.743
Step in, we go with that cross swing.

13:30:15.763 --> 13:30:23.274
But when you go with that cross swing, if you watch the best one foot dunkers, it's not just with the ball, it's with the opposite shoulder.

13:30:23.795 --> 13:30:28.221
So they're loading up that core and then it's a full body effort.

13:30:28.582 --> 13:30:31.326
It's lower body and it's this upper body rip.

13:30:31.766 --> 13:30:36.433
We get a lot of force from that diagonal upper body rip, right?

13:30:36.413 --> 13:30:47.670
We're training the exact same thing here where we're absorbing and we're loading, we're coiling up, and then we uncoil when we rip back onto the box.

13:30:47.690 --> 13:30:57.284
It is the same exact motion and it's going to really translate to that single leg jump with those specific mechanics.

13:30:57.765 --> 13:30:58.506
Let's get it.

13:31:11.699 --> 13:31:17.669
So for our hang clean pull, when we set up with that barbell, we're not going to be stiff leg.

13:31:17.689 --> 13:31:20.754
Think about the position we set up for an RDL.

13:31:21.335 --> 13:31:29.168
Slight bend in the knees, from there it's a hip hinge to get that barbell down right above the knees before we explode.

13:31:29.148 --> 13:31:34.314
We do not want to make this into a squat pattern where we go down by bending the knees.

13:31:35.075 --> 13:31:39.479
We have that initial bend, but from there the knees don't bend anymore.

13:31:39.499 --> 13:31:43.063
It's just butt back back back and then explode up with it.

13:31:43.304 --> 13:31:45.206
Basically what we're doing is a hook grip.

13:31:45.466 --> 13:31:49.771
So I'm going around with the thumb and then I'm holding on to that with my fingers.

13:31:50.171 --> 13:31:54.396
I'm going a little bit wider than shoulder width apart with that grip.

13:31:55.810 --> 13:31:56.791
and I'm gonna pull up.

13:31:56.831 --> 13:31:58.673
Now, I'm showing this with a light weight.

13:31:58.733 --> 13:32:01.777
Of course, we're gonna go as heavy as we can with good form.

13:32:02.137 --> 13:32:04.240
Start light and then gradually go up.

13:32:04.780 --> 13:32:08.024
So I want high power in short ranges of motion.

13:32:08.084 --> 13:32:10.287
So I don't wanna go past the knees.

13:32:10.687 --> 13:32:18.076
I'm just gonna get right above the knees with that barbell and then I'm gonna explode up and I'm gonna come to full hip extension.

13:32:18.436 --> 13:32:23.202
I'm coming all the way to the toes and then I'm shrugging it up at the top.

13:32:23.182 --> 13:32:25.785
Now if your elbows bend a little bit, that's okay.

13:32:25.845 --> 13:32:29.869
So if we kind of come into like a half, remember our high pull where we're here?

13:32:30.270 --> 13:32:36.196
If we come into like half of that and that bar comes up and the elbows bend a little bit, that's okay.

13:32:36.557 --> 13:32:41.182
But this weight should be heavy enough to where we couldn't complete a full high pull.

13:32:41.442 --> 13:32:48.110
Now if that barbell is light enough to where you can high pull it and get it to your mid chest, that means it's too light, we gotta go up.

13:32:48.350 --> 13:32:52.935
We're working more on that strength side of things and less on the speed side.

13:32:52.915 --> 13:32:59.824
Now common mistakes here, common mistake number one is people come into this and then they get into hyper extension.

13:33:00.145 --> 13:33:01.547
I don't want you to lean back.

13:33:01.867 --> 13:33:04.270
I want you to pretend like you're being measured at the top.

13:33:04.631 --> 13:33:08.296
So if you're six foot, I want you to be 6'5 up at the top.

13:33:08.336 --> 13:33:11.360
We can't be 6'5 if we're leaning back like this.

13:33:11.681 --> 13:33:14.224
So at the top, I want you to be tall with your head.

13:33:14.544 --> 13:33:17.168
I want you to be tall with your shoulders.

13:33:17.148 --> 13:33:26.464
Alright, now we're not going to hold that shrug position, you're going to explode with it and then we're going to land in a safe position and then reset and go again.

13:33:26.885 --> 13:33:35.159
I don't want this to be rapid so it's not going to be constant, you're going to go and then you're going to stop, you're going to reset and then go again.

13:33:35.219 --> 13:33:41.029
Another common mistake is getting the barbell too far out in front, so a lot of times

13:33:41.009 --> 13:33:44.696
You go here and then the barbell comes up on this line.

13:33:45.357 --> 13:33:50.848
The further away that barbell gets from our body, the more stress we're going to have on our lower back.

13:33:50.868 --> 13:33:53.674
So we want to think about keeping it close to the body.

13:33:53.834 --> 13:33:58.042
Advanced weightlifters will actually brush the thigh and pop off into that.

13:33:58.403 --> 13:34:00.587
Now if you're not advanced and you're not

13:34:00.567 --> 13:34:15.606
with this stuff you might not quite get that thigh brush that's okay as long as you're keeping it very close to where we're either making contact with the thigh or we're a half an inch away from the thigh I just don't want that barbell getting out in front of your body and

13:34:15.586 --> 13:34:18.551
And the final common mistake here is timing issues.

13:34:18.611 --> 13:34:25.503
So we want to make sure that we're finishing with the hips and we're finishing with the ankles at the same time.

13:34:25.543 --> 13:34:30.292
I don't want you to come to full hip extension and then extend from the feet.

13:34:30.312 --> 13:34:35.721
Think about a vertical jump, we're finishing with the ankles and that final little hip snap

13:34:35.701 --> 13:35:00.595
at the same time all right so i don't want it to be ankles and then hip and i don't want it to be hip and then ankles it's got to be very fluid finished with the hips and the ankles at the same time now one more thing if you're experienced using straps you might as well use straps on this exercise because as you get heavier and heavier eventually your grip strength might become the limiting factor

13:35:00.575 --> 13:35:07.041
We do not want our grip to be the limiting factor, so if you're experienced using straps, go ahead and use them.

13:35:27.288 --> 13:35:30.093
So for the assisted jump, there's a lot of different ways to do this.

13:35:30.494 --> 13:35:35.643
The way that I like to do it is I just like to use a power rack and I just loop a band through there.

13:35:35.723 --> 13:35:40.832
So I loop it through, make sure that we're nice and stable there, and then I just step back a little bit.

13:35:40.872 --> 13:35:42.935
Now, you've got to be safe with this.

13:35:42.995 --> 13:35:50.308
I don't want you using a band that has so much tension that when you jump, you're being pulled forward because you could easily hit your head on the rack.

13:35:50.288 --> 13:36:02.906
I want you to use a band where you can come down with your hands right about chin level, step back a little bit, and then you're able to just jump straight up and down without getting pulled in towards a rack, alright?

13:36:03.327 --> 13:36:07.673
Some people, if you're at like a LA Fitness where they have a hoop attached to the wall,

13:36:07.653 --> 13:36:14.427
You can always loop a band around the pole that attaches the hoop to the wall, and you can just go right underneath and explode.

13:36:14.607 --> 13:36:15.850
There's a lot of different ways to do it.

13:36:15.930 --> 13:36:23.485
The key to assisted jumps, it's an overspeed exercise, so I'm teaching my nervous system to activate muscle fibers faster.

13:36:23.465 --> 13:36:31.753
But if there's no force involved because the band has too much tension and it's doing all the work to pull me up, then this isn't beneficial at all.

13:36:32.114 --> 13:36:35.857
And so I recommend the 10 to 20% range.

13:36:35.937 --> 13:36:39.481
So I'm increasing my vertical by 10 to 20%, okay?

13:36:40.061 --> 13:36:48.730
So if I have a standing 30 inch vertical, and let's just say I was measuring on like a just jump mat or some sort of device like an app,

13:36:48.710 --> 13:36:53.798
If I jump with this, I don't wanna be all of a sudden at 40 inches.

13:36:54.098 --> 13:36:59.506
I wanna be anywhere from 33 to maybe 36 or 37 inches.

13:36:59.726 --> 13:37:06.176
Now, I don't need you to test, but you can kind of just eyeball it and just feel like, hey, maybe I'm getting an extra five inches on this.

13:37:06.496 --> 13:37:14.989
That means that you still have to apply force, but because you've deloaded your body weight a little bit with the band, then you're able to apply that force faster.

13:37:15.009 --> 13:37:17.873
Now, because a major goal of this phase

13:37:17.853 --> 13:37:47.860
eccentric and transitioning out of the bottom I do want you to think about attacking the floor so I need you to drop as fast as we can I don't want you to slowly lower or let gravity take you down I want you to think about starting high and attack the floor get up and then reset attack the floor get up reset okay and again we do not want to get pulled forward every rep on these I want to be able to step back and go straight up straight down without this band

13:37:47.840 --> 13:37:49.311
pulling me too far in.

13:38:02.639 --> 13:38:06.783
So for our banded quarter squats, we're going to hook up our bands.

13:38:06.843 --> 13:38:14.491
Now, I have really thick band tension, so I hook it up to the pins because it's too tight to actually hook it up to the bottom.

13:38:14.511 --> 13:38:20.597
YouTube, if you don't know how to set up the bands the right way for accommodating resistance, search on YouTube.

13:38:20.657 --> 13:38:22.599
There's hundreds of videos on different setups.

13:38:22.999 --> 13:38:30.807
If you have thinner bands, of course, you can hook it around, so loop it around the bottom, hook it to the inside, put the weights on the outside.

13:38:30.787 --> 13:38:34.471
So mess around with it a little bit if your bands are too light.

13:38:34.811 --> 13:38:38.715
Sometimes you can double loop it and get a little bit more band tension.

13:38:38.775 --> 13:38:41.939
So what we're going for is a quarter squat position.

13:38:42.399 --> 13:38:44.201
So start off obviously light.

13:38:44.461 --> 13:38:50.608
This is an exercise that we're going to load up heavy, but I want most of the load to be through that band tension at the top.

13:38:50.948 --> 13:38:54.412
So we're essentially going to get down to about 90 degree knee angle.

13:38:54.492 --> 13:38:56.093
Now that's not a half squat.

13:38:56.414 --> 13:38:59.697
Our normal half squat would be about parallel to the floor.

13:38:59.677 --> 13:39:08.468
I just want you right here at 90 degrees so it's more of a quarter squat position and then we explode up as hard as we can to the toes.

13:39:08.888 --> 13:39:15.977
Now, if we explode up and we feel like we can leave the ground, the weight is too light or the band tension is too light.

13:39:16.237 --> 13:39:16.437
Alright?

13:39:16.717 --> 13:39:22.404
That band tension is going to be light at the bottom and then it's going to be heavy at the top.

13:39:22.705 --> 13:39:24.327
So it's accommodating resistance.

13:39:24.347 --> 13:39:29.633
We're getting more resistance as we go up and that's what it's all about for the vertical jump.

13:39:29.613 --> 13:39:37.951
Now, if you don't have bands or you don't have a good power rack setup where we can loop it to the rack and be safe, we could go chains.

13:39:38.131 --> 13:39:46.249
If we have chains that build up on the floor at that bottom position and then at the top, they're off the floor, then that's still accommodating resistance.

13:39:46.269 --> 13:39:48.634
We still have more resistance at the top.

13:39:48.614 --> 13:40:12.698
and if we don't have bands or chains it's okay let's just use weights let's load up nice and heavy and let's just rep out that that same uh quarter squat range of motion now it's not going to be as beneficial because we don't have more resistance at the top but hey let's work with what we got so of course if we're going to be doing banded squats i'm assuming that you've already mastered your normal back squats if not

13:40:12.678 --> 13:40:16.303
go back and let's just work on form, working on your back squats.

13:40:16.323 --> 13:40:24.956
But assuming that you're advanced, we're gonna step into that barbell and I gotta make sure that when I unrack, these bands aren't set up too far back.

13:40:25.297 --> 13:40:29.824
Because if they are too far back, I'm gonna unrack and it's gonna pull me backwards, right?

13:40:29.844 --> 13:40:34.030
I also don't want it too far forward because then I unrack and it pulls me forward.

13:40:34.390 --> 13:40:42.162
So notice that they're about even with my feet or a little bit behind my feet when I step in that barbell.

13:40:42.547 --> 13:40:49.534
So notice they're right behind my heels and then I can lift up and it doesn't pull me back too much.

13:40:50.015 --> 13:40:57.222
I can just walk back so that my ankle and my midfoot is aligned with that line of pull with the band.

13:40:58.163 --> 13:41:00.325
Now from there I'm just dropping down.

13:41:00.385 --> 13:41:07.693
I'm not going to control down slow but I'm also not going to let it pull me down too rapid because then it's a little too dangerous.

13:41:07.673 --> 13:41:17.732
So we're just gonna go down at a normal speed and then at that bottom position, notice the band is light there and then I explode up and at the top it gets heavier.

13:41:18.073 --> 13:41:21.018
I'm coming all the way up to my toes and then I'm gonna reset.

13:41:21.319 --> 13:41:24.585
So it's here, explode, reset.

13:41:24.565 --> 13:41:26.849
Drop, explode, reset.

13:41:27.109 --> 13:41:29.773
Now obviously this weight is a little bit too light for me.

13:41:29.833 --> 13:41:34.120
I would be loading up much heavier, but keep in mind we do still want to get up fast.

13:41:34.220 --> 13:41:41.732
If you're getting towards your last couple reps of the set and you're really slow and you're grinding it out, then that's more on the strength side.

13:41:41.993 --> 13:41:44.076
We want to be in that strength speed

13:41:44.056 --> 13:41:48.062
where it is heavy, but we're still accelerating it very fast.

13:41:48.082 --> 13:41:56.775
So if you feel like you're having huge velocity drop-offs, go down and wait so that we can maintain that peak velocity throughout the whole set.

13:41:57.216 --> 13:42:05.288
If you were tracking, we would basically be going until we drop off like more than 10 or 15% and then we would stop the set, alright?

13:42:05.268 --> 13:42:13.529
So if you feel like you get good velocity and then your next couple reps you're kind of getting stuck in quicksand, well then it's probably a little bit too heavy.

13:42:13.810 --> 13:42:14.492
Let's go down.

13:42:14.772 --> 13:42:17.479
Let's maintain that good explosive power.

13:42:17.880 --> 13:42:18.602
Let's get it.

13:42:30.884 --> 13:42:37.795
So for my high box death jumps, I'm going to set up with a box probably a little higher than what I'm used to.

13:42:37.835 --> 13:42:39.678
So I'm going to start with a 30-inch box.

13:42:39.758 --> 13:42:42.883
Now, this is a very, very intense exercise.

13:42:43.284 --> 13:42:51.677
It is a potent stimulus, so incredible for a vertical jump, but also higher risk for our knees if you're not quite ready to absorb all this force.

13:42:51.657 --> 13:42:55.252
So how you actually choose a box is you would monitor.

13:42:55.332 --> 13:43:02.140
So I know a lot of you guys don't have a just jump mat or something to calculate your vertical, but you can eyeball it or set up a phone.

13:43:02.221 --> 13:43:06.048
Basically, what is your vertical jumping and landing?

13:43:06.108 --> 13:43:08.873
Let's say it's 28 inches, right?

13:43:09.274 --> 13:43:14.904
If I drop off a six-inch box, it should still be around 28 inches or maybe actually a little bit higher.

13:43:14.984 --> 13:43:18.391
For most of you guys, it'll be around 28 inches, all right?

13:43:18.411 --> 13:43:20.975
Then we go up to, say, a 12-inch box, same thing.

13:43:21.376 --> 13:43:23.520
And then we go up and we go up and we go up.

13:43:23.500 --> 13:43:30.489
you're going to find a point where your vertical starts to go down the higher the drop gets.

13:43:30.509 --> 13:43:34.854
And that shows you that you're not actually absorbing all of that force.

13:43:34.874 --> 13:43:40.201
So at that point where you drop from 28 to 27, we know that we went a little bit too high, right?

13:43:40.541 --> 13:43:47.570
But for me, I can get around the same vertical at a 30 inch box because I can absorb that much force.

13:43:47.590 --> 13:43:49.272
So that's where I'm going to train here.

13:43:49.252 --> 13:43:53.461
So what I'm going to do is just dangle one foot off and I'm just dropping down.

13:43:53.761 --> 13:43:55.325
I'm going to get a normal range of motion.

13:43:55.365 --> 13:43:58.331
I'm not trying to stay as stiff as possible on these jumps.

13:43:58.692 --> 13:44:03.442
So I'm dropping, boom, get up as high as I possibly can, all right?

13:44:03.843 --> 13:44:04.965
Stick that landing.

13:44:04.945 --> 13:44:10.875
Now, I don't want you to make this into a stiff leg depth jump where we're going for pier speed off the ground.

13:44:11.215 --> 13:44:14.961
I want you to land and load up like we normally would in a vertical jump.

13:44:16.424 --> 13:44:18.106
The goal is max jump height.

13:44:18.607 --> 13:44:22.994
Sure, we do want to be quick off the ground, but the number one priority is max jump height.

13:44:23.154 --> 13:44:25.098
So you do not have to switch feet.

13:44:25.218 --> 13:44:27.882
You can drop off with the same foot every time.

13:44:27.862 --> 13:44:31.025
What matters is that you're landing with both feet at the same time.

13:44:31.065 --> 13:44:33.047
We are not landing one, two.

13:44:33.467 --> 13:44:43.177
Now, if you're dropping off a box where you feel joint pain, you feel knee pain or back pain, ankle pain, then you're jumping off a box that is way too high.

13:44:43.497 --> 13:44:44.458
So you want to go lower.

13:44:44.758 --> 13:44:57.210
This should be a pain-free exercise where we're still getting a big vertical, but we are challenging how much force we're absorbing by going off a box that's a little higher than we're used to.

13:45:22.334 --> 13:45:26.338
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

13:45:26.479 --> 13:45:28.300
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

13:45:28.321 --> 13:45:31.043
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

13:45:31.063 --> 13:45:37.591
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

13:45:38.111 --> 13:45:44.058
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

13:45:44.318 --> 13:45:48.122
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

13:45:48.102 --> 13:46:04.204
okay so i'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill all right so it's in through the nose and i want to make sure that my stomach is rising okay your chest will naturally rise a little bit but most of you when you think of deep breathing what happens is your belly stays still

13:46:05.146 --> 13:46:07.869
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

13:46:08.229 --> 13:46:14.957
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

13:46:15.517 --> 13:46:21.284
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

13:46:21.304 --> 13:46:24.968
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

13:46:27.270 --> 13:46:31.575
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

13:46:34.492 --> 13:46:36.414
and that stomach will fall, okay?

13:46:37.135 --> 13:46:39.778
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

13:46:40.519 --> 13:46:49.549
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

13:46:50.350 --> 13:46:58.318
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

13:46:58.338 --> 13:47:02.423
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

13:47:02.403 --> 13:47:31.363
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

13:47:31.495 --> 13:47:34.019
happens in the parasympathetic state.

13:47:34.560 --> 13:47:38.707
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

13:47:39.047 --> 13:47:52.609
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

13:47:54.496 --> 13:47:57.801
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

13:47:57.821 --> 13:48:02.087
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

13:48:02.387 --> 13:48:09.197
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

13:48:09.497 --> 13:48:12.982
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

13:48:13.262 --> 13:48:20.773
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

13:48:20.753 --> 13:48:25.104
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

13:48:25.525 --> 13:48:27.490
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

13:48:27.530 --> 13:48:30.016
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

13:48:30.317 --> 13:48:32.642
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

13:48:32.662 --> 13:48:34.888
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

13:48:35.169 --> 13:48:36.973
I'm just gonna lean forward.

13:48:36.953 --> 13:48:57.780
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

13:48:58.030 --> 13:49:00.012
15 seconds on the other side as well.

13:49:00.032 --> 13:49:02.314
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

13:49:02.334 --> 13:49:03.775
So that is the back of the thigh.

13:49:03.996 --> 13:49:08.199
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

13:49:08.319 --> 13:49:12.023
For our adductor stretch, I'm just going to go one leg out.

13:49:12.163 --> 13:49:14.585
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

13:49:14.946 --> 13:49:17.248
I'm going to sit back onto my heel.

13:49:17.608 --> 13:49:19.190
So now look at this back foot.

13:49:19.210 --> 13:49:20.611
I'm not curling the toe under.

13:49:20.991 --> 13:49:25.135
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

13:49:25.115 --> 13:49:28.520
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

13:49:28.940 --> 13:49:34.308
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

13:49:34.608 --> 13:49:47.967
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

13:49:47.947 --> 13:49:51.111
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

13:49:51.171 --> 13:49:52.513
That's the inside of the leg.

13:49:52.994 --> 13:49:56.358
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

13:49:56.959 --> 13:49:57.660
Same thing.

13:49:57.980 --> 13:50:00.864
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

13:50:01.164 --> 13:50:04.288
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

13:50:04.589 --> 13:50:08.454
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

13:50:08.754 --> 13:50:13.380
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

13:50:13.360 --> 13:50:40.971
this knee as far towards the chest as i can now a lot of you as you pull the tendency is to rise here because we're tightening the hip flexors if that's the case try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here but this is a glute stretch for this left leg but at the same time for a lot of you this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side

13:50:40.951 --> 13:50:44.035
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

13:50:44.476 --> 13:50:50.585
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

13:50:50.605 --> 13:50:53.589
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

13:50:53.649 --> 13:50:55.832
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

13:50:56.052 --> 13:50:59.938
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

13:50:59.918 --> 13:51:01.720
So I'm just gonna go on one knee.

13:51:02.121 --> 13:51:04.664
This knee is gonna go 90 degrees.

13:51:04.744 --> 13:51:05.666
So I'm 90 there.

13:51:06.006 --> 13:51:08.810
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

13:51:09.150 --> 13:51:11.453
Now from here, I just gotta get up nice and tall.

13:51:11.814 --> 13:51:16.500
Some of you are already gonna feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

13:51:16.860 --> 13:51:19.864
Now from there, I'm just gonna lean forward.

13:51:20.104 --> 13:51:23.269
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

13:51:23.289 --> 13:51:28.255
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm gonna hold.

13:51:29.062 --> 13:51:35.371
All right, so I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

13:51:36.132 --> 13:51:39.878
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

13:51:39.998 --> 13:51:45.285
For my calf stretch, I'm basically gonna do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

13:51:45.666 --> 13:51:48.891
So I'm here, I'm gonna push back like this.

13:51:48.991 --> 13:51:50.513
I'm gonna straighten out my arms.

13:51:50.493 --> 13:51:52.135
and now I'm just going to cross over.

13:51:52.175 --> 13:52:02.150
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

13:52:02.651 --> 13:52:09.060
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

13:52:09.340 --> 13:52:15.489
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

13:52:15.890 --> 13:52:20.196
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

13:52:21.020 --> 13:52:24.149
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

13:52:24.189 --> 13:52:24.911
That's okay.

13:52:25.272 --> 13:52:28.481
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

13:52:28.521 --> 13:52:32.532
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

13:52:35.178 --> 13:52:35.879
What's up, guys?

13:52:36.019 --> 13:52:40.605
Quick minimum dose advice for day two and day five of phase 11.

13:52:40.686 --> 13:52:42.909
So this is a very easy day.

13:52:43.690 --> 13:52:49.297
The intense day, the day one and day four are pretty high intensity on the nervous system.

13:52:49.638 --> 13:52:54.485
So on this day, we couldn't handle any more nervous system intense work.

13:52:54.925 --> 13:52:57.208
And so you'll notice that it's very submaximal stuff.

13:52:57.228 --> 13:52:59.311
We got our submaximal bounds.

13:52:59.331 --> 13:53:03.497
This is good for the tendons, but you should not be overexerting yourself.

13:53:03.477 --> 13:53:11.867
Now, the hamstring walkouts, we'll get a good pump in the hamstrings, we'll get some strength, but it's not an extremely intense exercise.

13:53:12.505 --> 13:53:15.869
The side plank hip abduction, we're getting some good hip work.

13:53:16.149 --> 13:53:17.631
It's good for injury prevention.

13:53:18.151 --> 13:53:28.743
And then the four-way ankle walks, this is very good for the front of the shin, the anterior tibialis, our calves, the lateral aspects of our shin and our ankle muscles.

13:53:29.164 --> 13:53:32.167
So this is a good overall injury reduction day.

13:53:32.588 --> 13:53:39.896
It's also good because we're going to get a little bit of a pump with these isolation exercises, which goes a long way for our overall recovery.

13:53:40.236 --> 13:53:40.977
Let's get it.

13:53:40.957 --> 13:53:42.621
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

13:53:42.761 --> 13:53:44.444
I have a long roller from Skills.

13:53:44.725 --> 13:53:51.600
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

13:53:51.961 --> 13:53:57.312
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

13:53:57.292 --> 13:54:16.667
so you can either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher

13:54:16.647 --> 13:54:23.157
Or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

13:54:23.558 --> 13:54:25.781
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

13:54:25.801 --> 13:54:27.223
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

13:54:27.564 --> 13:54:35.977
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

13:54:36.337 --> 13:54:43.448
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings.

13:54:43.428 --> 13:54:48.105
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

13:54:48.338 --> 13:54:50.120
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

13:54:50.400 --> 13:54:53.824
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

13:54:53.904 --> 13:55:02.114
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

13:55:02.194 --> 13:55:07.720
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

13:55:07.780 --> 13:55:10.924
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

13:55:10.984 --> 13:55:18.312
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

13:55:18.292 --> 13:55:24.583
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

13:55:25.304 --> 13:55:28.290
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

13:55:28.730 --> 13:55:33.980
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

13:55:34.440 --> 13:55:40.992
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

13:55:40.972 --> 13:55:44.039
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

13:55:44.059 --> 13:55:45.783
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

13:55:46.124 --> 13:55:59.875
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

13:56:01.660 --> 13:56:08.470
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

13:56:08.671 --> 13:56:12.216
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

13:56:12.517 --> 13:56:15.802
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

13:56:16.342 --> 13:56:19.027
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

13:56:19.047 --> 13:56:20.729
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

13:56:21.030 --> 13:56:24.856
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

13:56:25.316 --> 13:56:28.461
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

13:56:28.441 --> 13:56:36.268
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

13:56:36.609 --> 13:56:39.331
We got to really start to loosen that up.

13:56:40.913 --> 13:56:45.497
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

13:56:45.737 --> 13:56:49.260
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

13:56:49.300 --> 13:56:54.465
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

13:56:54.825 --> 13:56:56.507
Everything else is extra credit.

13:56:59.575 --> 13:57:03.320
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

13:57:03.601 --> 13:57:06.164
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

13:57:06.244 --> 13:57:10.210
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

13:57:10.711 --> 13:57:17.300
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

13:57:17.660 --> 13:57:21.326
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

13:57:21.366 --> 13:57:23.068
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

13:57:23.048 --> 13:57:26.231
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

13:57:26.592 --> 13:57:28.073
I'm not going too fast yet.

13:57:28.374 --> 13:57:30.516
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

13:57:30.776 --> 13:57:35.301
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

13:57:35.321 --> 13:57:39.506
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

13:57:39.906 --> 13:57:43.470
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

13:57:43.770 --> 13:57:46.012
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

13:57:46.032 --> 13:57:48.415
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

13:57:48.395 --> 13:57:53.684
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

13:57:54.145 --> 13:57:55.727
We're just getting that blood flowing.

13:57:56.028 --> 13:57:57.370
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

13:57:57.570 --> 13:58:01.877
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

13:58:02.218 --> 13:58:04.021
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

13:58:04.101 --> 13:58:13.677
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

13:58:13.657 --> 13:58:21.388
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

13:58:25.030 --> 13:58:29.397
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

13:58:29.597 --> 13:58:34.946
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

13:58:35.227 --> 13:58:40.455
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

13:58:40.535 --> 13:58:41.777
I'm keeping that knee in.

13:58:42.198 --> 13:58:50.211
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

13:58:50.191 --> 13:58:55.140
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

13:58:55.480 --> 13:59:01.070
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

13:59:01.752 --> 13:59:04.116
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

13:59:04.456 --> 13:59:09.065
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

13:59:09.706 --> 13:59:12.030
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

13:59:12.955 --> 13:59:16.604
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

13:59:17.025 --> 13:59:21.476
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold one, two, three.

13:59:21.536 --> 13:59:23.822
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

13:59:24.142 --> 13:59:26.147
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

13:59:26.187 --> 13:59:26.789
That's okay.

13:59:27.130 --> 13:59:28.714
Let's just get as low as we can there.

13:59:29.195 --> 13:59:30.137
One, two, three.

13:59:30.117 --> 13:59:56.580
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat

13:59:56.560 --> 14:00:00.464
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

14:00:00.925 --> 14:00:04.929
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

14:00:05.290 --> 14:00:10.816
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

14:00:11.416 --> 14:00:25.572
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

14:00:30.597 --> 14:00:37.125
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

14:00:37.145 --> 14:00:38.627
Don't make the mistake of going here.

14:00:38.967 --> 14:00:47.038
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

14:00:47.158 --> 14:00:53.045
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

14:00:53.105 --> 14:00:57.010
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

14:00:56.990 --> 14:01:15.488
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one

14:01:15.468 --> 14:01:34.675
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

14:01:35.009 --> 14:01:37.272
One, two, three, lean.

14:01:38.053 --> 14:01:40.336
One, two, three, heels back down.

14:01:40.376 --> 14:01:44.401
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

14:01:44.701 --> 14:01:53.011
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

14:01:53.051 --> 14:01:57.397
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

14:01:57.757 --> 14:02:04.105
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

14:02:04.085 --> 14:02:06.910
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

14:02:06.970 --> 14:02:09.295
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

14:02:09.675 --> 14:02:12.481
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

14:02:12.801 --> 14:02:14.504
I need to be flat through the hips.

14:02:14.785 --> 14:02:17.190
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

14:02:17.731 --> 14:02:22.159
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

14:02:22.540 --> 14:02:25.485
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

14:02:25.465 --> 14:02:35.759
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction, and I'm gonna activate a lot of muscle fibers.

14:02:36.821 --> 14:02:43.390
And we're just gonna hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze, and then relax.

14:02:43.810 --> 14:02:52.182
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up, it's the most powerful muscle in the body, so we gotta get them revved up for our session.

14:03:47.191 --> 14:03:57.552
So for my side plank hip abduction, I'm gonna go ahead and stack my legs, 90 degree angle there with the knees, and then I'm gonna come down on that forearm.

14:03:57.572 --> 14:04:03.985
So I don't want you on the elbow, I don't want you on the wrist, I want your full forearm down on the ground.

14:04:03.965 --> 14:04:09.678
I'm going to make sure that I'm nice and flat through the body so I don't want to have a major tucked hip there.

14:04:10.079 --> 14:04:11.843
I want to be extended nice and flat.

14:04:12.344 --> 14:04:18.498
Now from there, I'm just lifting my hips off the ground and then I'm going to drive this leg at the same time.

14:04:18.879 --> 14:04:20.202
So I'm up.

14:04:20.182 --> 14:04:23.649
I'm driving that leg at the top and then I'm right back down.

14:04:23.769 --> 14:04:26.034
Bring your hip all the way back down to the ground.

14:04:26.455 --> 14:04:29.802
Torso is still off the floor but that hip will touch down.

14:04:30.283 --> 14:04:36.435
So it's here, hip touches down, your full thigh is on the ground and drive up off that.

14:04:37.900 --> 14:04:41.525
So you're gonna feel a lot of this on that leg that's down.

14:04:41.785 --> 14:04:45.049
You're gonna feel that through the lateral portion of the hip.

14:04:45.069 --> 14:04:50.496
So we're working that hip abduction, but then you're also gonna feel it through that oblique that's down as well.

14:04:50.856 --> 14:05:01.129
So if my right side is down, as I'm pushing up, I'm gonna feel it from here all the way up through that lateral aspect of the core.

14:05:01.449 --> 14:05:04.313
So if you did wanna make this a little bit more difficult,

14:05:04.293 --> 14:05:09.419
Stay in that same position and you could add a band, a mini band right above the knees.

14:05:09.940 --> 14:05:11.121
Not necessarily essential.

14:05:11.481 --> 14:05:22.013
Even our NBA players have a tough time with this because if you're getting the perfect form and you're getting the hips to light up in the areas that we want, we're training a lot of those smaller stabilizing muscles.

14:05:22.414 --> 14:05:23.916
And so this is actually pretty tough.

14:05:24.036 --> 14:05:31.144
But if this does feel way too easy and you don't feel any of a burn in that lateral aspect of the hip, then you can add the band.

14:05:31.124 --> 14:06:00.102
or if you're super advanced you could start to go with just full straight legs and then you're coming down and driving like that come down touch and drive all right but that's a pretty difficult variation most of you are going to be better off with that 90 degrees stacked legs here's the four-way ankle with a resistive band

14:06:00.537 --> 14:06:05.483
For ankle eversion, take a roll or a rolled up towel and place it underneath your leg.

14:06:05.944 --> 14:06:11.751
Then take the band and place it around the ball of your foot with the knot on the inside.

14:06:11.792 --> 14:06:16.237
Then wrap it around the other foot and turn away.

14:06:16.297 --> 14:06:20.723
Try to use just movement at your ankle and not your whole leg.

14:06:29.748 --> 14:06:36.821
For ankle inversion, take the resistive band and wrap it around the ball of your foot with the knot on the outside.

14:06:37.262 --> 14:06:43.673
Then cross it over your other leg and wrap the band around your foot, turning outwards.

14:06:52.782 --> 14:07:00.373
For ankle plantar flexion with the resistive band, take a roll or a rolled up towel and place it underneath your legs so you'll have movement in your foot.

14:07:00.774 --> 14:07:04.099
Then take the band and put it just at the ball of your foot.

14:07:07.464 --> 14:07:13.413
Give yourself some resistance, and then push down as far as you comfortably can, and come back up.

14:07:13.433 --> 14:07:14.655
Nice and controlled.

14:07:25.469 --> 14:07:32.783
For ankle dorsiflexion with a resistive band, take a roll or a rolled up towel and place it underneath your legs so you have movement at your ankle.

14:07:33.204 --> 14:07:47.190
Then take the band and wrap it around a sturdy table, putting the band around the ball of your foot, and then holding the band nice and tight, and then pulling up towards you, and controlling it coming back down.

14:07:56.857 --> 14:07:57.518
What's up, guys?

14:07:57.618 --> 14:08:01.967
Quick minimum dose advice for day three and day six of phase 11.

14:08:02.828 --> 14:08:06.135
Very simple core workout, nice and efficient.

14:08:06.596 --> 14:08:12.026
Obviously, if you have perfect mobility already, skip the mobility exercises.

14:08:12.106 --> 14:08:15.152
We don't necessarily have to continue to build more.

14:08:15.132 --> 14:08:18.338
Do all of the core exercises.

14:08:18.618 --> 14:08:21.603
It's already very short and efficient, only three exercises.

14:08:22.124 --> 14:08:27.935
If you want to add in some extra stuff, if you have the time and the energy, go ahead and add some extra core work.

14:08:28.255 --> 14:08:32.282
And then as always, you could add a third core day if you want to.

14:08:32.323 --> 14:08:37.812
So on that off day, which would be Sunday, you can absolutely add in a third core day.

14:08:38.173 --> 14:08:38.794
Let's get it.

14:08:51.683 --> 14:08:56.934
So for our barbell rollout, we're going to get a normal barbell and put a couple plates on each side.

14:08:56.974 --> 14:09:03.347
Now you could go with the smaller 10 pound plates or if you have bumper plates that probably works even better.

14:09:03.708 --> 14:09:07.175
Now if you don't have the barbell, you could go with a normal ab roller.

14:09:07.436 --> 14:09:10.662
And if you don't have either, then you're just going to do hand walkouts.

14:09:10.682 --> 14:09:12.426
So I'll show you that at the end.

14:09:12.406 --> 14:09:16.015
So if you have the barbell, I like to go pretty wide grip.

14:09:16.055 --> 14:09:17.960
I get my pinky here on the seams.

14:09:18.621 --> 14:09:25.258
And then from there, I'm going to go all the way out and I'm trying to get full extension at the bottom.

14:09:25.519 --> 14:09:26.922
So all the way out.

14:09:27.796 --> 14:09:30.720
Full extension and then roll it back up.

14:09:30.820 --> 14:09:33.765
I want to feel this exercise in the front core.

14:09:33.865 --> 14:09:37.050
So I want to feel this in my abs and not in my lower back.

14:09:37.390 --> 14:09:43.920
A lot of people don't have the core strength to do this exercise and they feel it more in their lower back.

14:09:44.020 --> 14:09:48.707
So I want to make sure that at the bottom you are not hyper extending.

14:09:48.687 --> 14:10:01.988
if anything i want you to go out halfway and keep that integrity in the spine so we keep that neutral spine and we're not hyper extended all right so the progression would be out

14:10:02.339 --> 14:10:05.123
to here and back up, right?

14:10:05.143 --> 14:10:10.710
And then if you get better, then we can go out a little further and back up.

14:10:10.770 --> 14:10:18.640
And if that spine remains neutral and you feel it in the front core, we can keep going lower and lower until we get to the point where we're all the way fully extended.

14:10:18.920 --> 14:10:30.135
Now, keep in mind, this is an exercise where you will feel a burn during the set, but the next day, the soreness is pretty high compared to most ab exercises.

14:10:30.115 --> 14:10:36.001
This one produces a fair amount of soreness because there is a long, slow eccentric.

14:10:36.061 --> 14:10:46.712
So be cautious, don't go overboard on this and don't add too many sets on this beyond what's recommended because remember, you probably will feel that soreness tomorrow.

14:10:46.892 --> 14:10:51.657
All right, now if you don't have the barbell and you don't have an ab roller, you can just do hand walkouts.

14:10:52.178 --> 14:10:59.245
So I'm just gonna go down here, neutral position, and I'm just gonna walk out my hands as far as I can.

14:10:59.225 --> 14:11:01.111
and then creep them back up.

14:11:02.115 --> 14:11:09.741
Notice I'm only going a couple inches at a time and I want to get as far out as I can and then inch it back up.

14:11:28.368 --> 14:11:36.150
If you don't have the ankle mobility to keep your heels down and sit in that bottom position, then I want you to grab the weight

14:11:36.788 --> 14:11:39.711
and I want you to come into a deep squat without the heels on the ground.

14:11:40.151 --> 14:11:50.782
So I would just come forward, my knees are over the toes, and now from here, elbows are resting on the knees, and I'm just trying to sit the heels down as low as I can.

14:11:51.563 --> 14:11:53.445
If you can get the heels down, great.

14:11:53.765 --> 14:12:01.733
Then we're going to just rock side to side, trying to drive that knee right over the middle of my foot.

14:12:01.713 --> 14:12:30.387
and we're just gonna rock there now if you can't get your heels down just get as far as you can all right so i'm pushing my heels as close as i can but they are off the ground now from here i'm just gonna rock side to side all right and we're still gonna get ankle mobility even though the heels aren't on the ground and week after week we can start to get the heels closer and closer until we're able to really sit with a flat foot and rock side to side and get that good ankle mobility

14:12:52.372 --> 14:13:01.013
So for our split stance anti-rotation ABCs, what I'm gonna do is capital letters A through J. Now I want you in a split stance.

14:13:01.414 --> 14:13:08.170
So think about if you were gonna drop your feet, that is the position, that's our base position that we're working with here because

14:13:08.825 --> 14:13:17.356
When we drop to that position and get into our drive, we always take hand checks, we always take hits to one side of our body and a lot of us fly into rotation.

14:13:17.696 --> 14:13:23.243
We have to have that anti-rotation core strength so that we can push through those situations.

14:13:23.323 --> 14:13:29.871
But too often we get good anti-rotation in that square stance and we never get it in that actual split stance.

14:13:29.851 --> 14:13:47.421
What I'm gonna do is get to that position, nice and low, I gotta brace my core here, nice and strong, my breath is normal, and then I'm gonna press right out in front of my chest, and I'm gonna start that ABC, so capital letter A, capital B,

14:13:48.532 --> 14:13:54.206
capital C, A through J, and then I switch it and I do it the other way.

14:13:54.707 --> 14:14:01.664
Make sure that the inside foot, so the foot that's closer to the cable machine, is back.

14:14:02.005 --> 14:14:05.072
And then when we switch, that left foot is back.

14:14:05.052 --> 14:14:11.159
Now, if you don't have the cable machine, of course you can just use a band hooked up to a power rack or something nice and stable.

14:14:11.460 --> 14:14:18.908
We're going to get very similar effects there, but if you can, we would want to use the cable and of course start light and gradually go up.

14:14:19.169 --> 14:14:21.111
We do want to get as heavy as we can here.

14:14:21.391 --> 14:14:33.085
The heavier we get, the more we're going to get stable in that split stance position, fire up that core, get nice and strong and stable so that we can be dominant once we hit the court or the field.

14:14:48.522 --> 14:15:01.001
So for our hip flexor box, what I'm going to do, easiest version, is sit up nice and tall, plant your hands into the ground, get up, and then it's just going to be raise, bring it out, and bring it down.

14:15:01.021 --> 14:15:04.486
We're trying to make a big box, full range of motion.

14:15:04.746 --> 14:15:12.758
We're loading that hip flexor, so we're building some strength at those vulnerable angles that our hip flexor doesn't like to move at.

14:15:13.160 --> 14:15:18.008
boom, over and down, up, over and down.

14:15:19.010 --> 14:15:29.768
Now once you've got that down, let's squeeze the hands, let's sit up nice and tall because now we actually build a little bit more stability through the core and now we can really start to train

14:15:30.018 --> 14:15:30.699
those hips.

14:15:31.179 --> 14:15:34.643
So same thing, we're pushing that range of motion.

14:15:34.964 --> 14:15:41.952
Remember that hip flexor is operating in a really shortened position and so it's going to feel like you're almost going to cramp.

14:15:41.972 --> 14:15:45.176
That's kind of the feeling that we want to have here, that's completely normal.

14:15:45.436 --> 14:15:50.683
Now if you are cramping, you might be pushing it a little too far, but you want to feel like you're almost going to cramp.

14:15:51.003 --> 14:15:56.970
Alright, now common mistake is we're here and then to get the leg up, we lean back like this.

14:15:56.950 --> 14:16:15.827
Remember, we're trying to challenge just that hip structure right now and we're trying to build that stability and that motor control so that our brain starts to trust it because if you have tight hip flexors, chances are good you actually have weak hip flexors and the brain is tightening it up because it feels like it's in danger.

14:16:16.087 --> 14:16:19.450
So we might actually need less stretching and more strengthening.

14:16:19.731 --> 14:16:26.637
This is an exercise where you might feel the burn but this is actually going in a positive direction towards better hip mobility.

14:16:48.875 --> 14:16:54.446
For my stability ball roll up, I can either use a stability ball or I could just use a basketball.

14:16:54.927 --> 14:17:02.101
I need this foot, my right foot off the ground about six inches, this leg straight up in the air and point the toe.

14:17:02.461 --> 14:17:04.205
If I can have it at 90 degrees, great.

14:17:04.525 --> 14:17:07.491
Most of you won't, it'll be a little bit lower.

14:17:07.471 --> 14:17:09.153
And you're just going to pin opposite.

14:17:09.233 --> 14:17:11.416
So left leg high, right hand.

14:17:11.797 --> 14:17:18.847
And now I'm just going to gradually move it up about six inches with each hand until it reaches the toe.

14:17:19.267 --> 14:17:23.833
And then I bring it back down until I'm all the way back down to the ground with my head.

14:17:24.194 --> 14:17:29.361
All right, so it's here all the way up to the toe and right back down.

14:17:33.897 --> 14:17:41.107
Alright, once I finish the desired reps with that left leg, right hand, I switch and I go right leg, left hand.

14:17:41.127 --> 14:17:46.875
Alright, we're going to feel a big burn in that front core, anterior abdominal muscles get fired up.

14:17:46.935 --> 14:17:55.247
This is a good one to actually get your front core nice and ripped if your body fat is low enough, your abs are going to start to pop out a little bit with this exercise.

14:18:07.040 --> 14:18:12.708
So for our 90-90 loaded mobility, I'm gonna go 90-90 shin box.

14:18:12.728 --> 14:18:18.356
So I'm 90 degrees here, I'm 90 degrees here, and I'm 90 degrees here, all right?

14:18:18.376 --> 14:18:21.781
I'm on my right glute, so that means I'm gonna go to my right side to start.

14:18:21.821 --> 14:18:32.035
So I need my chest in line with this knee, so I'm not here, I'm squaring up chest to right knee, and then I'm just bringing chest down towards the knee.

14:18:32.015 --> 14:18:34.899
Now, normally that would be a good static stretch.

14:18:34.959 --> 14:18:38.224
I feel it in the glutes, the external rotators, all that good stuff.

14:18:38.604 --> 14:18:42.550
But the brain wants load to make serious long-term changes.

14:18:42.590 --> 14:18:51.863
So from here, instead of relying on my hands and just holding a static stretch, I'm going to contract that glue and contract the external rotators.

14:18:51.843 --> 14:18:56.155
and push myself up and then slowly lower myself back down.

14:18:56.656 --> 14:18:58.561
So I'm keeping the glute on tension.

14:18:58.662 --> 14:19:02.312
I'm keeping the hip structure on tension the entire time.

14:19:02.392 --> 14:19:06.122
So the better we get at it with our palms down,

14:19:06.440 --> 14:19:11.691
the more we can start to get towards our fingertips and I'm barely pushing into the floor.

14:19:11.851 --> 14:19:19.046
I'm really putting almost all of my weight on this leg and I'm just bouncing up and down in that position.

14:19:19.988 --> 14:19:20.228
All right.

14:19:20.549 --> 14:19:22.052
So you're going to feel a stretch.

14:19:22.032 --> 14:19:42.454
in the outside of your hip but you're also going to feel like it's strengthening at the same time your nervous system is going to start trusting those positions that's a vulnerable position and so once the brain knows that it has some motor control and some strength in those weird angles it's going to start to trust it more and more long term that's pretty good for injury prevention

14:19:42.434 --> 14:19:44.758
Now how the reps work is by time.

14:19:44.838 --> 14:19:52.552
So if it says 20 seconds, that would mean that I get to this bottom position and then the clock starts and I'm working for 20 seconds.

14:19:52.732 --> 14:19:59.545
As that set goes on, I'm trying to go lighter and lighter with the hands so that I'm really loading the hip.

14:19:59.565 --> 14:20:03.632
After our 20 seconds, we would switch over and hit it to the other side.

14:20:06.515 --> 14:20:11.761
What's up guys, minimum dose advice for phase 12, already very efficient.

14:20:12.102 --> 14:20:17.628
Notice our Monday and our Thursday are slightly different, almost the same workout.

14:20:17.909 --> 14:20:21.853
These are higher intensity lower body days, but they are slightly different.

14:20:22.033 --> 14:20:26.659
For day one, we need to do the overcoming ankle isometrics.

14:20:26.819 --> 14:20:29.282
Very, very, very important.

14:20:29.262 --> 14:20:31.905
A lot of people get confused on the sets and reps here.

14:20:32.906 --> 14:20:35.069
One set is a three second hold.

14:20:35.470 --> 14:20:40.556
So I hold right leg, push as hard as you can, hold left leg, push as hard as you can.

14:20:40.616 --> 14:20:41.537
That's one set.

14:20:41.977 --> 14:20:44.641
Then you rest 30 seconds and then you go back in.

14:20:44.721 --> 14:20:46.263
So it's six total sets there.

14:20:47.804 --> 14:20:52.670
If you have to skip one, skip the drop lateral box jump.

14:20:52.690 --> 14:20:57.396
Love this, but if you're pressed for time, that would be one that we could skip over.

14:20:57.376 --> 14:21:25.392
got to do our split stance half squat this is our lower body meat and potatoes for the day so do not skip this if you have patellar tendonitis or knee pain this is one that you would want to skip and instead do like an rdl or a high pole or something that's more hip dominant a heavy single leg bridge got to do this this is our heavy post chain for the day band resisted line hops if you're pressed for time skip this but i do love this exercise

14:21:25.372 --> 14:21:27.937
As always, do the 7-Eleven breathing.

14:21:28.137 --> 14:21:30.261
Skip the static stretch if you have to.

14:21:30.281 --> 14:21:32.465
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

14:21:32.605 --> 14:21:34.288
I have a long roller from Skills.

14:21:34.548 --> 14:21:41.441
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

14:21:41.801 --> 14:21:47.211
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

14:21:47.191 --> 14:21:50.274
So you can either go this way or you could go vertical.

14:21:50.635 --> 14:21:59.765
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

14:21:59.785 --> 14:22:06.212
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

14:22:06.933 --> 14:22:13.300
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

14:22:13.280 --> 14:22:15.624
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

14:22:15.644 --> 14:22:17.046
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

14:22:17.386 --> 14:22:25.779
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

14:22:26.159 --> 14:22:37.937
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

14:22:38.170 --> 14:22:39.952
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

14:22:40.253 --> 14:22:43.657
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

14:22:43.737 --> 14:22:51.967
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

14:22:52.027 --> 14:22:57.554
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles and our next one we're flipping over.

14:22:57.614 --> 14:23:00.758
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

14:23:00.818 --> 14:23:08.127
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

14:23:08.107 --> 14:23:14.419
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

14:23:15.140 --> 14:23:18.126
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

14:23:18.567 --> 14:23:23.817
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

14:23:24.278 --> 14:23:30.790
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

14:23:30.770 --> 14:23:33.895
Our next one's going to be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

14:23:33.915 --> 14:23:35.618
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

14:23:35.938 --> 14:23:49.740
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

14:23:51.492 --> 14:23:58.302
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

14:23:58.503 --> 14:24:02.048
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

14:24:02.368 --> 14:24:05.633
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

14:24:06.174 --> 14:24:08.858
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

14:24:08.878 --> 14:24:10.561
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

14:24:10.881 --> 14:24:14.867
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

14:24:14.847 --> 14:24:17.816
and I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

14:24:18.377 --> 14:24:29.329
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping, we gotta really start to loosen that up.

14:24:30.676 --> 14:24:35.325
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

14:24:35.565 --> 14:24:39.092
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

14:24:39.112 --> 14:24:44.301
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

14:24:44.662 --> 14:24:46.305
Everything else is extra credit.

14:24:49.407 --> 14:24:53.153
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

14:24:53.433 --> 14:24:55.997
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

14:24:56.077 --> 14:25:00.043
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

14:25:00.544 --> 14:25:07.154
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

14:25:07.495 --> 14:25:11.160
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

14:25:11.200 --> 14:25:12.883
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

14:25:12.863 --> 14:25:16.088
and I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

14:25:16.429 --> 14:25:17.911
I'm not going too fast yet.

14:25:18.211 --> 14:25:20.355
We're gonna go for 30 seconds on the skips.

14:25:20.615 --> 14:25:25.142
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm gonna coordinate arms with the legs.

14:25:25.162 --> 14:25:29.349
So I'm gonna go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

14:25:29.750 --> 14:25:33.315
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

14:25:33.616 --> 14:25:35.859
And then I'm just gonna speed that up.

14:25:35.879 --> 14:25:38.263
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

14:25:38.243 --> 14:25:43.528
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

14:25:43.969 --> 14:25:45.550
We're just getting that blood flowing.

14:25:45.851 --> 14:25:47.192
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

14:25:47.392 --> 14:25:51.716
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

14:25:52.057 --> 14:25:53.838
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

14:25:53.919 --> 14:26:03.508
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

14:26:03.488 --> 14:26:11.237
OK, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way and I'm going the other side.

14:26:14.879 --> 14:26:19.224
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

14:26:19.444 --> 14:26:24.770
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

14:26:25.050 --> 14:26:31.597
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this, I'm keeping that knee in.

14:26:32.017 --> 14:26:40.026
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can,

14:26:40.006 --> 14:26:44.973
So I'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back.

14:26:45.313 --> 14:26:50.901
I'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and I'm getting this hamstring a good stretch.

14:26:51.582 --> 14:26:53.945
And I'm also working on my balance at the same time.

14:26:54.286 --> 14:26:58.892
I'm going to go five leans and then I'm going to switch over five leans on the other side.

14:26:59.533 --> 14:27:01.896
For the hip opener, we're going to go push up position.

14:27:02.804 --> 14:27:06.429
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

14:27:06.850 --> 14:27:09.233
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

14:27:09.293 --> 14:27:11.296
One, two, three.

14:27:11.356 --> 14:27:13.640
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

14:27:13.960 --> 14:27:15.983
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

14:27:16.023 --> 14:27:16.624
That's okay.

14:27:16.945 --> 14:27:18.547
Let's just get as low as we can there.

14:27:19.008 --> 14:27:19.969
One, two, three.

14:27:19.949 --> 14:27:40.607
three and back up so three there three there I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side five and five same thing for the adductor hip flexor combo I'm going lateral with one skip so it's one skip lateral and I'm going down I'm trying to touch this floor keep this knee straight because I want to get that

14:27:40.587 --> 14:28:00.857
stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat and i'm getting nice and low touch the floor and i'm popping out to that other side all right so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor

14:28:00.837 --> 14:28:13.560
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back.

14:28:14.041 --> 14:28:15.403
And it's to the other side.

14:28:20.429 --> 14:28:26.957
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

14:28:26.977 --> 14:28:28.459
Don't make the mistake of going here.

14:28:28.819 --> 14:28:36.870
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

14:28:36.990 --> 14:28:42.857
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

14:28:42.937 --> 14:28:46.842
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

14:28:46.822 --> 14:29:06.028
it's gonna be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so I'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one two three

14:29:06.008 --> 14:29:06.689
Three.

14:29:07.030 --> 14:29:07.731
Rock it back.

14:29:08.112 --> 14:29:09.895
Heels back down to the ground.

14:29:09.955 --> 14:29:11.799
One, two, three.

14:29:11.899 --> 14:29:13.101
I need one hand up.

14:29:13.662 --> 14:29:14.444
Two hand up.

14:29:14.684 --> 14:29:15.346
Not out here.

14:29:15.406 --> 14:29:16.889
We need it back behind the head.

14:29:17.470 --> 14:29:18.111
And stand.

14:29:18.692 --> 14:29:18.912
Alright.

14:29:19.373 --> 14:29:20.736
So that's one cycle.

14:29:21.016 --> 14:29:22.118
We're going to go for three of these.

14:29:23.080 --> 14:29:24.523
So it's one, two, three.

14:29:24.841 --> 14:29:27.124
One, two, three, lean.

14:29:27.905 --> 14:29:30.168
One, two, three, heels back down.

14:29:30.228 --> 14:29:34.253
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

14:29:34.533 --> 14:29:39.299
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here.

14:29:39.699 --> 14:29:42.863
And then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

14:29:42.883 --> 14:29:47.249
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

14:29:47.609 --> 14:29:53.937
We just gotta get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

14:29:53.917 --> 14:29:56.740
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

14:29:56.800 --> 14:29:59.102
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

14:29:59.522 --> 14:30:02.305
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

14:30:02.625 --> 14:30:04.327
I need to be flat through the hips.

14:30:04.607 --> 14:30:07.030
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

14:30:07.550 --> 14:30:11.974
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

14:30:12.355 --> 14:30:15.137
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

14:30:15.418 --> 14:30:23.265
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction

14:30:23.245 --> 14:30:25.588
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

14:30:26.649 --> 14:30:33.598
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

14:30:33.618 --> 14:30:37.763
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

14:30:37.783 --> 14:30:39.525
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

14:30:39.786 --> 14:30:43.330
So we've got to get them revved up for our session.

14:30:54.413 --> 14:30:59.679
So for our overcoming ankle isometrics, what we're gonna do is set up the pins.

14:31:00.020 --> 14:31:03.323
Now, if you don't have the pins, sometimes the U-hooks can actually work.

14:31:03.544 --> 14:31:07.088
You can go underneath there with the barbell, depends how it's set up.

14:31:07.448 --> 14:31:10.632
For me, I like to set up the pins around chin height.

14:31:10.732 --> 14:31:18.662
If you wanna have it a little bit higher, you could, but you can't have them too low, otherwise our angle is gonna be too low on this exercise.

14:31:18.742 --> 14:31:23.307
So I'm setting up the barbell here, and then I'm just gonna step under,

14:31:23.287 --> 14:31:24.790
We want no weight on the bar.

14:31:25.451 --> 14:31:30.040
And then basically I'm just driving as hard as I can into the pins.

14:31:30.742 --> 14:31:32.024
So I'm going to come to one foot.

14:31:32.385 --> 14:31:34.669
I'm going to bring my heel off the ground.

14:31:35.050 --> 14:31:41.302
I'm not maximally plantar flex like that, but I'm just on the ball of the foot and the heel is off the ground.

14:31:41.643 --> 14:31:45.210
And then from there, I'm just driving this other leg high.

14:31:45.190 --> 14:31:48.959
and I'm going to push as hard as I can into these pins.

14:31:49.360 --> 14:31:54.392
Notice I have a slight bend in this right knee, but it's a very minimal knee bend.

14:31:54.673 --> 14:32:01.630
So I'm driving through the knee and through that ankle to push as hard as I can into these pins.

14:32:01.610 --> 14:32:04.113
Now pay attention to the rep range.

14:32:04.394 --> 14:32:09.240
It's going to be very short because we're going for maximal force into the pins.

14:32:09.620 --> 14:32:15.828
As soon as we start to decrease that maximal force and fatigue kicks in, we're stopping the set.

14:32:16.168 --> 14:32:22.977
So we're going for quality, high force, short sets, full recovery, and then we go back in.

14:32:22.957 --> 14:32:37.097
Now, if you don't have a rack that has the pins or U-hooks that you could put the barbell underneath, then what you're going to do is go up against a wall or go up against a rack or something stable and do the same thing.

14:32:37.157 --> 14:32:43.186
So now we're just going at an angle and I'm just pushing in to the rack as hard as I possibly can.

14:32:43.867 --> 14:32:50.837
So not as good, but we are still getting similar angles and we're still getting an overcoming isometric.

14:32:50.817 --> 14:32:59.812
If you can't do either of those, we could always go on a leg press and go ball of the foot on, heel off, and then hold a weight that's as heavy as we can handle.

14:33:00.473 --> 14:33:06.363
If you go on the leg press, I wouldn't pull the pins because that weight is so heavy, we wouldn't want it to come down on us.

14:33:06.403 --> 14:33:12.353
So I'd keep the pins there and then I would just lift off just a little bit and hold that position.

14:33:12.333 --> 14:33:18.441
So with this exercise, this is one of the best things that we can do for building that functional ankle stiffness.

14:33:18.801 --> 14:33:31.758
So when it comes to sprint speed, when it comes to change of direction, one of the most important factors is being able to have that stiff ankle so that when we land, we don't deform and waste valuable ground contact time.

14:33:32.078 --> 14:33:42.171
When we build strength in this exercise, it can really start to translate over to the court and to the field with stiffer, more quick cuts through the ankles.

14:33:54.740 --> 14:34:00.190
So for our drop lateral box jump, what we're gonna do is first just stand on a box.

14:34:00.250 --> 14:34:01.893
I want it below knee height.

14:34:02.113 --> 14:34:08.705
If you're not extremely advanced with your single leg landings from high heights, then I just want you to go a really low box, all right?

14:34:08.845 --> 14:34:16.900
For me or for a lot of advanced athletes who can't absorb this much force on one leg, then we go a box about knee height or just below.

14:34:16.880 --> 14:34:24.692
So notice that I have the other box about four feet forward in front of the box that I'm dropping off and about three to four feet to the side.

14:34:25.012 --> 14:34:26.495
It's going to be different for everybody.

14:34:26.515 --> 14:34:28.337
You're going to have to play around with it a little bit.

14:34:28.618 --> 14:34:31.763
Okay, so if that box is to the left of me, I'm dropping off.

14:34:31.863 --> 14:34:35.949
I'm reactive off the ground with this left, boom, push.

14:34:35.969 --> 14:34:41.838
Now I do want to keep my shoulders over my hips or pointed in that direction.

14:34:41.998 --> 14:34:45.283
So I'm setting up my change of direction instead of

14:34:45.263 --> 14:35:08.807
deforming from the core and coming here on the change of direction like a lot of us do and makes our change of direction significantly slower so i'm trying to change directions as quick as i possibly can i'm pushing out and now to double right so now it's right land right push land on two and get up so it's right right land on two and get up

14:35:08.787 --> 14:35:14.873
Notice now when we're landing on two, I'm landing with the feet at the same time, and I gotta decelerate myself.

14:35:14.913 --> 14:35:18.577
A lot of you guys are gonna jump, and your momentum is gonna take you over here.

14:35:19.038 --> 14:35:23.623
We gotta work on stopping on a dime laterally and getting straight up on this box.

14:35:24.103 --> 14:35:31.391
Now, I want you to jump on a box that challenges you, but remember, we always wanna land in this quarter squat position.

14:35:31.751 --> 14:35:38.338
If I'm landing with my thigh below parallel, that box is too high, you need to go down with that box.

14:35:38.318 --> 14:35:47.507
Now, I know some of you guys won't have two boxes, but as long as you have one box or a bench or something that we can drop off, we don't necessarily need this box.

14:35:47.868 --> 14:35:50.710
I could set up a little hurdle and work on jumping over that.

14:35:51.471 --> 14:35:54.074
Or if I'm on the court, I could just jump and touch the rim.

14:35:54.494 --> 14:36:00.620
So I could drop off, boom, do my double lateral, and then get up and touch the rim or touch the backboard.

14:36:00.821 --> 14:36:01.942
That's just as good.

14:36:02.442 --> 14:36:04.725
So, of course, we got to do this to both sides.

14:36:05.105 --> 14:36:07.127
So if it says, let's say, three reps,

14:36:07.107 --> 14:36:13.433
That means I do my three reps to this side and then I take this box and I slide it over to the other side.

14:36:13.714 --> 14:36:16.003
And now I have three reps to the other side.

14:36:34.394 --> 14:36:38.801
So for our split stance half squat, what we're gonna do is step in.

14:36:38.921 --> 14:36:41.846
I do not want an elongated split.

14:36:42.166 --> 14:36:45.451
We're gonna go with a short, compact split stance.

14:36:45.471 --> 14:36:48.736
This is more of a sport specific split stance squat.

14:36:49.177 --> 14:36:50.479
So when I come in,

14:36:51.809 --> 14:36:57.417
I'm just going to step back and I'm going to get this left toe in line with my right heel.

14:36:58.158 --> 14:37:05.888
Then from there, I'm just coming down until this right thigh is parallel to the floor and then explode up.

14:37:05.948 --> 14:37:16.483
You'll notice that we are getting a little bit of ankle mobility in this back leg and we do get quite a lot of knee flexion, so we're training that knee in that knee over toe position.

14:37:16.463 --> 14:37:20.829
We're not taking it overboard because not all of our weight is on this back leg.

14:37:21.089 --> 14:37:23.933
We want more of our weight towards this front leg.

14:37:24.253 --> 14:37:27.097
So this front leg we're thinking of as the working leg.

14:37:27.558 --> 14:37:31.944
We get down to parallel and then explode out of the bottom.

14:37:32.304 --> 14:37:35.629
Now, this is a traditional strength exercise.

14:37:36.009 --> 14:37:38.733
So we're not necessarily going for maximal speed.

14:37:39.354 --> 14:37:44.901
I do want you to explode up fast, but the weight should be heavy enough to where you actually move pretty slow.

14:37:44.881 --> 14:37:52.954
So from that bottom position to the top position, so bottom thigh parallel to top position, I want it to take about one full second.

14:37:53.294 --> 14:37:58.502
So you're exploding as fast as we can, but it takes you maybe close to a second no longer.

14:37:58.943 --> 14:38:01.026
If you're taking longer than a second, then it's too heavy.

14:38:01.347 --> 14:38:04.932
If you're taking less than like half a second, it might be too light.

14:38:05.253 --> 14:38:08.658
I do want to build some good traditional strength here.

14:38:08.698 --> 14:38:11.783
So load it up as long as you have perfect form.

14:38:11.763 --> 14:38:17.355
And as always, we're going to start light and then gradually go up once we get comfortable with the exercise.

14:38:17.536 --> 14:38:20.663
Now keep in mind, you are going to have more of an upright torso.

14:38:20.703 --> 14:38:22.206
So look at the bottom position.

14:38:22.547 --> 14:38:29.161
I have a slight lean, but it's nowhere near what I would have in a deadlift or even a regular barbell back squat.

14:38:29.181 --> 14:38:30.885
You have more of a lean.

14:38:30.865 --> 14:38:39.237
So this, because it's more knee dominant and quad dominant, you're going to keep more of an upright torso in that bottom position.

14:38:39.537 --> 14:38:44.865
Now, if you're somebody who is prone to knee pain, this exercise is probably not for you.

14:38:45.505 --> 14:38:53.076
You could try to go very light and work through the range slow because it is good to put some stress on those quads and strengthen them up.

14:38:53.056 --> 14:39:01.971
But if you have like patellar tendonitis or any other knee problems, you're going to want to skip this exercise and instead do a hip dominant exercise.

14:39:02.011 --> 14:39:10.666
So go back to your RDLs or you could do like a split stance trap bar RDL or split stance dumbbell RDL.

14:39:11.046 --> 14:39:17.477
But I don't want you to stress your knees too much by trying to go into this movement because it is very knee dominant.

14:39:26.620 --> 14:39:30.904
So for our heavy single leg hip bridge, I'm going to go on a box or bench.

14:39:31.365 --> 14:39:32.486
Make sure it's nice and stable.

14:39:32.506 --> 14:39:35.249
If you're going on a bench, put weights behind it so it doesn't slide.

14:39:35.870 --> 14:39:37.792
Now, I want you to start light.

14:39:37.892 --> 14:39:40.795
You maybe start with a 10 or 25 pound plate on you.

14:39:41.175 --> 14:39:43.478
And we're just holding in that single leg position.

14:39:43.538 --> 14:39:45.480
Now, most of us will go about 45.

14:39:45.780 --> 14:39:49.083
If you can load heavier, then go ahead and stack them up.

14:39:49.143 --> 14:39:52.927
We can go another 45 on top, another 45 on top.

14:39:52.967 --> 14:39:56.391
Nobody actually usually gets to that level.

14:39:56.371 --> 14:40:01.718
Go as heavy as we can, as long as we can hold in that perfect single leg bridge position.

14:40:01.778 --> 14:40:08.247
Now, if you are going to stack plates, you need friends to be able to put them on and to hold them.

14:40:08.608 --> 14:40:13.835
So I want you to get in that position and then I want your friends to stack them on top.

14:40:14.115 --> 14:40:17.620
If you don't have friends to stack, put them on your thighs like this.

14:40:18.595 --> 14:40:45.868
get into position as long as you just have one it's not going to slide too much and then bring it here so that center is right over your belly button then make sure your shoulder blades are all the way on the box I can go down with my elbows here and then I'm just extending one leg and it's a hold now make sure that the ankle is about underneath the knee so I don't want it too far here and I don't want it too close to you here so ankle underneath the knee

14:40:45.848 --> 14:40:47.171
and then it's a hold.

14:40:47.231 --> 14:40:53.707
Now if you have friends to stack, have them stack one on top of the other, but they need to hold them in position.

14:40:54.169 --> 14:40:57.477
And when you're ready to get out of it, they need to be the ones that take it off.

14:40:57.537 --> 14:41:02.068
I don't want you to have to throw it off for you to get out of that exercise.

14:41:02.048 --> 14:41:05.215
Now the other option would be to load up with a barbell.

14:41:05.295 --> 14:41:10.967
So if you have a thick pad, you could use the pad, load up with a barbell, and hold in that position.

14:41:11.328 --> 14:41:15.557
However you want to load that's most comfortable and safe for you, you do that.

14:41:15.857 --> 14:41:21.289
But we do want to load up pretty heavy and get that maximal isometric contraction in the glutes.

14:41:33.588 --> 14:41:40.119
So for our band-resisted line hops, I'm going to hook up the band to a power rack or something nice and stable.

14:41:40.179 --> 14:41:43.444
I like to use two thin bands and tie them together.

14:41:43.825 --> 14:41:47.992
Just make sure you don't have the super thick bands like this because that's going to be too much tension.

14:41:48.392 --> 14:41:51.377
If you don't have the bands, then let's just do this without the resistance.

14:41:51.417 --> 14:41:51.918
That's fine.

14:41:53.180 --> 14:41:54.803
So I'm going to step in.

14:41:55.998 --> 14:42:21.786
i'm going to start at the point where i just have a little bit of tension notice that it's pulling directly behind so i want to even with about hip height now from here you could either have a line or i'm just using an imaginary line and i'm going on one foot and i'm going as quick as i possibly can over and back all the way out until i can't go any further and then i'm going to bring it back so on the way back we're actually getting a little more deceleration so

14:42:21.766 --> 14:42:38.982
it's here quick as we can over and back over and back staying on the ball of the foot now i have maximal tension and now i bring it back now it's pulling me so the tendencies to make big jumps you got to keep them small and precise so it's here

14:42:39.350 --> 14:42:44.578
working on deceleration through the calf, through the Achilles.

14:42:44.758 --> 14:42:48.865
So one rep is all the way down and all the way back.

14:42:49.145 --> 14:42:51.449
And then of course we do the reps to each side.

14:42:51.709 --> 14:42:56.236
When it comes to building raw quickness, this is one of the best drills that we can do.

14:42:56.657 --> 14:42:57.358
Let's get it.

14:43:21.723 --> 14:43:25.768
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

14:43:25.888 --> 14:43:27.670
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

14:43:27.730 --> 14:43:30.433
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

14:43:30.474 --> 14:43:36.981
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

14:43:37.502 --> 14:43:43.449
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

14:43:43.729 --> 14:43:47.494
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

14:43:47.474 --> 14:43:50.860
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

14:43:51.802 --> 14:43:56.149
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

14:43:56.209 --> 14:44:03.542
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

14:44:04.552 --> 14:44:30.964
your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate i mean we are not actually getting all of that air all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs so i want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in okay so we're here in deep through the nose let the stomach rise seven seconds once you finish that seven seconds it's out through the nose

14:44:33.897 --> 14:44:36.500
and that stomach will fall, okay?

14:44:36.520 --> 14:44:39.163
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

14:44:39.924 --> 14:44:48.934
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

14:44:49.735 --> 14:44:57.484
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

14:44:57.745 --> 14:45:01.609
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

14:45:01.927 --> 14:45:30.668
this is doing a lot of stuff i mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay i got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

14:45:30.935 --> 14:45:33.420
happens in the parasympathetic state.

14:45:34.141 --> 14:45:38.429
So if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

14:45:38.449 --> 14:45:52.015
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we want to do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

14:45:53.902 --> 14:45:57.187
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

14:45:57.207 --> 14:46:01.494
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

14:46:01.774 --> 14:46:08.584
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

14:46:08.885 --> 14:46:12.370
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

14:46:12.651 --> 14:46:20.162
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

14:46:20.142 --> 14:46:24.494
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

14:46:24.915 --> 14:46:26.900
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

14:46:26.920 --> 14:46:29.427
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

14:46:29.707 --> 14:46:32.034
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

14:46:32.054 --> 14:46:34.280
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

14:46:34.560 --> 14:46:36.345
I'm just gonna lean forward.

14:46:36.325 --> 14:46:51.702
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back

14:46:52.542 --> 14:46:56.411
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

14:46:56.431 --> 14:46:59.277
15 seconds on the other side as well.

14:46:59.437 --> 14:47:01.562
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

14:47:01.743 --> 14:47:03.166
So that is the back of the thigh.

14:47:03.426 --> 14:47:07.716
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

14:47:07.696 --> 14:47:11.404
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

14:47:11.564 --> 14:47:13.989
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

14:47:14.330 --> 14:47:16.635
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

14:47:17.015 --> 14:47:18.579
So now look at this back foot.

14:47:18.599 --> 14:47:20.002
I'm not curling the toe under.

14:47:20.402 --> 14:47:24.591
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

14:47:24.571 --> 14:47:27.916
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

14:47:28.337 --> 14:47:33.985
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

14:47:34.005 --> 14:47:47.406
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

14:47:47.386 --> 14:47:50.531
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

14:47:50.571 --> 14:47:51.893
That's the inside of the leg.

14:47:52.374 --> 14:47:55.759
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

14:47:56.360 --> 14:47:57.061
Same thing.

14:47:57.382 --> 14:48:00.266
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

14:48:00.567 --> 14:48:03.692
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

14:48:03.972 --> 14:48:07.878
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

14:48:08.159 --> 14:48:12.786
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

14:48:12.766 --> 14:48:40.461
this knee as far towards the chest as i can now a lot of you as you pull the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors if that's the case try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here but this is a glute stretch for this left leg but at the same time for a lot of you this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side

14:48:40.441 --> 14:48:43.427
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

14:48:43.868 --> 14:48:49.999
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

14:48:50.019 --> 14:48:53.025
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

14:48:53.065 --> 14:48:55.229
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

14:48:55.450 --> 14:48:59.277
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

14:48:59.257 --> 14:49:01.120
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

14:49:01.160 --> 14:49:04.044
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

14:49:04.124 --> 14:49:05.065
So I'm 90 there.

14:49:05.405 --> 14:49:08.189
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

14:49:08.550 --> 14:49:10.833
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

14:49:11.193 --> 14:49:15.900
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

14:49:16.260 --> 14:49:19.225
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

14:49:19.505 --> 14:49:22.649
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

14:49:22.669 --> 14:49:27.576
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

14:49:28.467 --> 14:49:34.778
All right, so I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

14:49:35.519 --> 14:49:39.265
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

14:49:39.385 --> 14:49:44.694
For my calf stretch, I'm basically gonna do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

14:49:45.055 --> 14:49:48.280
So I'm here, I'm gonna push back like this.

14:49:48.381 --> 14:49:49.783
I'm gonna straighten out my arms.

14:49:49.763 --> 14:49:51.525
And now I'm just going to cross over.

14:49:51.926 --> 14:50:01.538
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

14:50:01.598 --> 14:50:03.821
So I do have a straight knee.

14:50:04.222 --> 14:50:06.725
So we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius.

14:50:06.765 --> 14:50:08.447
That's the outside calf muscle.

14:50:08.728 --> 14:50:14.876
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

14:50:15.276 --> 14:50:19.602
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

14:50:20.425 --> 14:50:23.534
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

14:50:23.574 --> 14:50:24.316
That's okay.

14:50:24.677 --> 14:50:27.866
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

14:50:27.906 --> 14:50:31.937
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

14:50:34.651 --> 14:50:37.976
So quick minimum dose advice for day two and day five.

14:50:38.416 --> 14:50:41.581
This is our lower intensity, lower body day.

14:50:42.101 --> 14:50:43.804
Lower intensity doesn't mean it's easy.

14:50:44.004 --> 14:50:47.409
It just means from a nervous system level, it's not as high intensity.

14:50:47.429 --> 14:50:48.590
We're not going as heavy.

14:50:49.031 --> 14:50:52.055
If we want to skip our 90-90 loaded mobility, that's fine.

14:50:52.276 --> 14:50:54.338
If your hip mobility is really good, we don't need it.

14:50:55.019 --> 14:50:59.766
Around the world cross drives love this exercise, but if you're pressed for time, skip that.

14:51:00.287 --> 14:51:04.152
The most important exercises for today's workout

14:51:04.132 --> 14:51:06.978
slanted isometric holds, and the Nordic curls.

14:51:07.379 --> 14:51:08.441
Never skip those two.

14:51:09.623 --> 14:51:13.511
Rapid calf raise, if you have the time to do that, great exercise.

14:51:13.932 --> 14:51:17.940
Do the 7-Eleven breathing, skip the static stretch if you're pressed for time.

14:51:18.261 --> 14:51:19.002
Let's get it.

14:51:19.032 --> 14:51:20.575
So we're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

14:51:20.715 --> 14:51:22.418
I have a long roller from Skills.

14:51:22.678 --> 14:51:29.550
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

14:51:29.911 --> 14:51:35.320
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

14:51:35.300 --> 14:51:54.675
so you can either go this way or you could go vertical so if you go this way we're just going to lean over to one side and then i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute now always if we want more pressure we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher

14:51:54.655 --> 14:52:21.388
or we could go vertical here and then we could lean over and get the glute horizontally as well so the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain we're going to focus on the hamstrings so i'm just going to sit on the roller and i'm going to cross over this foot and i'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee so i'm just going to start in the center and then i'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so i can get the outside of my hamstrings

14:52:21.368 --> 14:52:26.097
and then I'm gonna rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

14:52:26.296 --> 14:52:28.058
Now the next one we're gonna do is the calves.

14:52:28.358 --> 14:52:40.070
So we're just gonna cross over and I'm gonna push myself up so my hips are off the ground and I'm just gonna roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

14:52:40.150 --> 14:52:44.294
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

14:52:44.314 --> 14:52:48.879
And our next one we're flipping over, we're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

14:52:48.939 --> 14:52:56.286
So I'm gonna go on the side of the roller now and I'm just gonna come down into a plank position and I'm gonna roll

14:52:56.266 --> 14:53:02.538
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

14:53:03.259 --> 14:53:06.245
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

14:53:06.686 --> 14:53:11.936
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

14:53:12.397 --> 14:53:18.949
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

14:53:18.929 --> 14:53:21.835
Our next one's gonna be for the adductors, that's the inside of the leg.

14:53:22.016 --> 14:53:23.739
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

14:53:24.060 --> 14:53:37.849
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside, the top of the knee, all the way up through the groin area.

14:53:39.618 --> 14:53:46.449
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

14:53:46.630 --> 14:53:50.195
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

14:53:50.476 --> 14:53:53.761
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

14:53:54.302 --> 14:53:57.007
And I'm just going to really dig into that heel.

14:53:57.027 --> 14:53:58.689
So I'm going to apply some pressure in there.

14:53:58.990 --> 14:54:02.816
I'm going to go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

14:54:03.257 --> 14:54:06.402
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

14:54:06.382 --> 14:54:14.229
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

14:54:14.570 --> 14:54:17.292
We got to really start to loosen that up.

14:54:18.874 --> 14:54:23.438
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

14:54:23.698 --> 14:54:27.221
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

14:54:27.241 --> 14:54:32.426
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

14:54:32.786 --> 14:54:34.448
Everything else is extra credit.

14:54:37.516 --> 14:54:41.282
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

14:54:41.542 --> 14:54:44.146
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

14:54:44.206 --> 14:54:48.152
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

14:54:48.673 --> 14:54:55.243
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

14:54:55.604 --> 14:54:59.309
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

14:54:59.329 --> 14:55:00.712
I wanna stay on the ball of the foot.

14:55:01.112 --> 14:55:04.257
And I'm just gonna go about 50% of my max speed.

14:55:04.598 --> 14:55:06.240
I'm not going too fast yet.

14:55:06.220 --> 14:55:08.704
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

14:55:08.724 --> 14:55:13.271
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

14:55:13.291 --> 14:55:17.458
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

14:55:17.879 --> 14:55:21.424
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

14:55:21.725 --> 14:55:23.848
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

14:55:23.868 --> 14:55:26.372
I'm going to get a good little rhythm going here.

14:55:26.352 --> 14:55:31.641
maybe somewhere between 30 and 50 percent of your max speed, so we're not all out.

14:55:32.082 --> 14:55:33.684
We're just getting that blood flowing.

14:55:33.965 --> 14:55:35.327
We're going to go for 30 seconds.

14:55:35.487 --> 14:55:39.854
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just going to go over with the left leg.

14:55:40.175 --> 14:55:41.998
I'm going to come down, touch the toe.

14:55:42.058 --> 14:55:51.634
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees, just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

14:55:51.614 --> 14:55:59.312
Okay, so I'm going to get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

14:56:02.988 --> 14:56:07.355
All right, we're gonna go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

14:56:07.555 --> 14:56:12.904
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just gonna go into a quad stretch, so I'm gonna pull my right foot up.

14:56:13.164 --> 14:56:18.413
I wanna make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

14:56:18.493 --> 14:56:19.735
I'm keeping that knee in.

14:56:20.155 --> 14:56:28.168
Now from there, I'm gonna keep the left knee straight, and I'm just gonna go into a lean as low as I can.

14:56:28.148 --> 14:56:50.005
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

14:56:50.913 --> 14:56:54.520
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

14:56:54.961 --> 14:56:57.346
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

14:56:57.406 --> 14:56:59.430
One, two, three.

14:56:59.490 --> 14:57:01.755
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

14:57:02.096 --> 14:57:04.120
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

14:57:04.160 --> 14:57:04.741
That's okay.

14:57:05.082 --> 14:57:06.685
Let's just get as low as we can there.

14:57:07.146 --> 14:57:08.128
One, two, three.

14:57:08.108 --> 14:57:09.791
three, and back up.

14:57:09.871 --> 14:57:11.874
So three there, three there.

14:57:12.195 --> 14:57:18.505
I'm gonna go five cycles and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

14:57:18.585 --> 14:57:22.051
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

14:57:22.091 --> 14:57:25.777
So it's one skip lateral and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor.

14:57:26.258 --> 14:57:31.226
Keep this knee straight because I wanna get that stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor.

14:57:31.547 --> 14:57:34.572
I'm going back with the hip like I would in a normal squat.

14:57:34.552 --> 14:57:38.416
and I'm getting nice and low, touch the floor, and I'm popping out to that other side.

14:57:38.876 --> 14:57:42.880
All right, so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out.

14:57:43.240 --> 14:57:48.766
I need five of these down and back, so it's 10 total, and then I immediately go into a hip flexor.

14:57:49.386 --> 14:58:03.580
Same thing with the skip, and now I land, I turn this foot forward, I pivot, I'm here, knee a couple inches off the floor, I'm gonna rotate, hold one, and then come back, and it's to the other side.

14:58:08.555 --> 14:58:15.084
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

14:58:15.104 --> 14:58:16.567
Don't make the mistake of going here.

14:58:16.927 --> 14:58:24.999
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

14:58:25.099 --> 14:58:30.987
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance, so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

14:58:31.067 --> 14:58:34.933
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

14:58:35.015 --> 14:58:53.378
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one

14:58:53.358 --> 14:59:12.632
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

14:59:12.967 --> 14:59:15.230
One, two, three, lean.

14:59:16.011 --> 14:59:18.273
One, two, three, heels back down.

14:59:18.333 --> 14:59:22.378
One, two, three, one up, two up, stand, all right?

14:59:22.659 --> 14:59:30.949
If you cannot get down in that deep position, then let's just go as low as we can right here, and then on the lean, it's just gonna look like this, all right?

14:59:30.989 --> 14:59:35.354
And then we're gonna come back, hopefully parallel to the floor, one up, two up, stand.

14:59:35.714 --> 14:59:42.062
We just gotta get as low as we can, and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower, better range of motion.

14:59:42.042 --> 14:59:44.866
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

14:59:44.926 --> 14:59:47.249
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

14:59:47.630 --> 14:59:50.434
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

14:59:50.754 --> 14:59:52.437
I need to be flat through the hips.

14:59:52.737 --> 14:59:55.120
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

14:59:55.661 --> 15:00:00.087
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

15:00:00.488 --> 15:00:03.392
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

15:00:03.372 --> 15:00:13.727
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm going to get a really good isometric contraction and I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

15:00:14.789 --> 15:00:21.318
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

15:00:21.759 --> 15:00:25.905
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

15:00:25.925 --> 15:00:27.888
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

15:00:27.908 --> 15:00:30.291
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

15:00:39.282 --> 15:00:44.969
So for our 90-90 loaded mobility, I'm gonna go 90-90 shin box.

15:00:44.989 --> 15:00:50.615
So I'm 90 degrees here, I'm 90 degrees here, and I'm 90 degrees here, all right?

15:00:50.635 --> 15:00:54.019
I'm on my right glute, so that means I'm gonna go to my right side to start.

15:00:54.059 --> 15:01:04.311
So I need my chest in line with this knee, so I'm not here, I'm squaring up chest to right knee, and then I'm just bringing chest down towards the knee.

15:01:04.291 --> 15:01:07.155
Now, normally that would be a good static stretch.

15:01:07.216 --> 15:01:10.481
I feel it in the glutes, the external rotators, all that good stuff.

15:01:10.861 --> 15:01:14.807
But the brain wants load to make serious long-term changes.

15:01:14.848 --> 15:01:24.122
So from here, instead of relying on my hands and just holding a static stretch, I'm going to contract that glue and contract the external rotators.

15:01:24.102 --> 15:01:38.517
and push myself up and then slowly lower myself back down so i'm keeping the glute on tension i'm keeping that the hip structure on tension the entire time so the better we get at it with our palms down

15:01:38.699 --> 15:01:43.954
the more we can start to get towards our fingertips and I'm barely pushing into the floor.

15:01:44.114 --> 15:01:51.294
I'm really putting almost all of my weight on this leg and I'm just bouncing up and down in that position.

15:01:52.217 --> 15:01:52.498
All right.

15:01:52.839 --> 15:01:54.463
So you're going to feel a stretch.

15:01:54.443 --> 15:01:58.730
in the outside of your hip, but you're also gonna feel like it's strengthening at the same time.

15:01:59.091 --> 15:02:01.795
Your nervous system is gonna start trusting those positions.

15:02:02.056 --> 15:02:03.598
That's a vulnerable position.

15:02:03.959 --> 15:02:11.812
And so once the brain knows that it has some motor control and some strength in those weird angles, it's gonna start to trust it more and more.

15:02:12.293 --> 15:02:14.697
Long term, that's pretty good for injury prevention.

15:02:14.677 --> 15:02:17.000
Now how the reps work is by time.

15:02:17.101 --> 15:02:24.832
So if it says 20 seconds, that would mean that I get to this bottom position and then the clock starts and I'm working for 20 seconds.

15:02:24.992 --> 15:02:31.401
As that set goes on, I'm trying to go lighter and lighter with the hands so that I'm really loading the hip.

15:02:31.822 --> 15:02:35.908
After our 20 seconds, we would switch over and hit it to the other side.

15:02:54.991 --> 15:03:00.619
So for our around the world cross drives, what I'm going to do, if you can go barefoot, great.

15:03:00.659 --> 15:03:02.683
If you can go socks off, even better.

15:03:03.143 --> 15:03:05.066
If you're in a busy gym, just go socks on.

15:03:05.827 --> 15:03:07.850
Or if you can't take your shoes off at all, that's fine.

15:03:07.890 --> 15:03:08.692
Just do it in your shoes.

15:03:08.752 --> 15:03:10.434
So I'm going to stand on one leg.

15:03:11.035 --> 15:03:13.479
A lot of this is foot stability and ankle stability.

15:03:13.679 --> 15:03:15.902
We're going to take that to the next level with this exercise.

15:03:16.483 --> 15:03:17.725
So I'm on one leg.

15:03:17.705 --> 15:03:18.607
I'm gonna go with the plate.

15:03:19.028 --> 15:03:31.217
I want you to start with a 10 pound plate and then you can eventually get up to that 25 or maybe even the 35 but I haven't seen people go further than a 35 pound plate and do it with great form.

15:03:31.738 --> 15:03:33.402
So I'm gonna hold the plate

15:03:33.382 --> 15:03:52.605
right in front of my chest i'm going to be balanced here on my right leg if i'm balancing on my right leg i start to my right side okay that's very important now when that plate goes around my head i'm challenging that single leg stability because i now have a weight shift so i'm bringing it all the way around my head

15:03:52.585 --> 15:03:56.169
bring it to here and then I'm bringing it outside my hip.

15:03:56.469 --> 15:04:05.118
So outside this right hip and I go into a partial RDL and then I drive out of it diagonally and then I bring it right back down to my chest.

15:04:05.458 --> 15:04:09.402
So notice what we have here, that cross rip that we work on a lot.

15:04:09.642 --> 15:04:14.207
Remember if I have the basketball here and I'm getting that cross rip, it's the same thing.

15:04:14.267 --> 15:04:15.629
We're challenging that stability.

15:04:15.669 --> 15:04:19.993
So I'm here and then I drive out of it

15:04:19.973 --> 15:04:21.715
bring it back, and then I'm right back in.

15:04:22.075 --> 15:04:23.957
I'm always going to the same side.

15:04:24.258 --> 15:04:31.986
So I'm going back, right side, around, right hip, drive diagonally left, back to chest, okay?

15:04:32.006 --> 15:04:35.029
Once I finish the reps there, then I go to the other side.

15:04:35.450 --> 15:04:48.023
And as you already know, now left foot means I start left around, I bring it to left hip, RDL, I drive diagonally to that right side, and I bring it back to my chest.

15:04:56.710 --> 15:05:06.744
So for our slanted isometric holds, what I'm gonna do, I could go on a slant board or I could just use like a dumbbell or I could even just use a bumper plate.

15:05:06.984 --> 15:05:10.670
But I like to put the bumper plate down to hold the dumbbell in place.

15:05:11.030 --> 15:05:15.536
I would put the dumbbell there and then I can put my heel up on that dumbbell.

15:05:15.817 --> 15:05:19.542
So the ball of my foot and toes is gonna be the only thing on the floor.

15:05:19.923 --> 15:05:23.848
So now I get this angle where we're getting a lot of it to the quad.

15:05:23.828 --> 15:05:42.883
the key here is we're going to do an isometric hold the goal is to improve the strength the stiffness of that patellar tendon now if you feel pain then you went a little too much all right so of course just start body weight in this position we want to get to a similar angle that we take off from

15:05:42.863 --> 15:05:46.489
So somewhere between a 60 degree, 90 degree knee angle.

15:05:47.310 --> 15:05:53.240
So I'm going to look at that angle from here and then I'm just going to come up and I'm going to lift this back leg.

15:05:53.581 --> 15:05:55.705
Notice I'm not bringing the back foot up.

15:05:55.725 --> 15:06:01.935
I'm going to leave this back foot back right here and I'm just going to hold for time.

15:06:01.915 --> 15:06:04.602
Already I should start to feel a burn in the quads.

15:06:04.862 --> 15:06:14.968
The whole goal is to get like an 8, a 9, or a 10 out of 10 burn in the quads with a 0 or up to a 1 out of 10 pain in the knee.

15:06:14.988 --> 15:06:16.572
So we really shouldn't feel anything in the knee.

15:06:16.832 --> 15:06:19.519
We should feel everything in the quad.

15:06:19.499 --> 15:06:23.586
Of course, if that's easy, we're going to start to load up and hold dumbbells in the hands.

15:06:23.667 --> 15:06:27.433
We get guys holding 50, 60, 70 pound dumbbells by the side.

15:06:27.814 --> 15:06:31.461
So I'll hold 50s right now.

15:06:31.661 --> 15:06:33.685
That's probably around the weight I would normally use.

15:06:36.069 --> 15:06:39.375
And I'm just staying nice and stable in this position.

15:06:39.395 --> 15:06:41.980
I do not want my knees to be shaking.

15:06:42.584 --> 15:06:45.668
so it's going to really build some good isometric strength in the quads.

15:06:45.989 --> 15:07:03.533
More importantly, it's going to build some health in that tendon, so it's going to get that tendon stronger, more stiff, so that it's really able to handle high forces, and it's not going to be constantly breaking down when we're out on the court beating it down over and over again, or in our plyometrics, which is also very demanding on the tendons.

15:07:03.553 --> 15:07:08.440
Let's do it.

15:07:15.608 --> 15:07:21.416
So for our Nordic hamstring curls, if you have a partner, this is the easiest way to go.

15:07:21.436 --> 15:07:25.301
So your partner is going to sit behind you and hold down your ankles.

15:07:25.681 --> 15:07:29.286
Now he's got to push pretty hard because your whole body weight is going to be coming down.

15:07:29.366 --> 15:07:32.490
So if he's light, then you're going to lift him up.

15:07:32.550 --> 15:07:34.553
So he's got to put a little bit of force in there.

15:07:34.913 --> 15:07:36.776
Now I like to use a foam roller.

15:07:37.056 --> 15:07:41.843
So you put the front of your ankle on a foam roller, and then you kind of dig your toes into the ground.

15:07:42.203 --> 15:07:45.227
And it's just a little bit more comfortable on the ankles.

15:07:45.207 --> 15:07:47.769
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a partner.

15:07:48.370 --> 15:07:51.312
So this is how we would do it without a partner.

15:07:51.673 --> 15:07:54.055
I would set up a barbell and make sure it's nice and heavy.

15:07:54.475 --> 15:07:56.417
Make sure you got weights on both sides.

15:07:56.837 --> 15:08:02.702
If you have a rack, then put it on the inside of the rack and then you put a weighted plate on the other side so that the barbell is not going to roll.

15:08:03.123 --> 15:08:10.029
If you don't have a rack, we would use the barbell and then we would put a weight behind it and we would put another 45 pound plate in front of it.

15:08:10.369 --> 15:08:13.932
So it's still kind of trapped in there and it's not going to roll back and forth.

15:08:13.912 --> 15:08:17.044
This is optional, but I like to use this because it's a little bit softer.

15:08:17.386 --> 15:08:18.771
So I just use a pad there.

15:08:18.812 --> 15:08:22.707
And then you always need a pad

15:08:22.957 --> 15:08:52.963
for your knees all right so this is your setup now from here a lot of people go tall and they come down i if you're super advanced that's fine i would rather see a little bit of hip flexion so we're initially in just a little bit of hip flexion and then from there we're going down i want you to go basically as slow as you can or four seconds all the way down this is a lowering exercise once i get to the bottom i'm going to push back up and i'm coming all the way back up pretty fast you don't have to go slow on the way up

15:08:52.943 --> 15:09:11.374
so initial hip flexion from there I'm not bending the hips more I stay in this initial hip flexion and I slowly lower lower lower lower push off and come back up if you can get a light push off that's great a lot of you guys to start

15:09:11.354 --> 15:09:15.381
you're going to have to really push off hard to get back up.

15:09:15.401 --> 15:09:17.184
Otherwise, you're going to get stuck halfway up.

15:09:17.464 --> 15:09:29.464
So if you get extremely good at this, we can eventually either go a very light tap or there are some people who have such good strength to body weight ratios, they can just put their hands behind their back and do the whole thing.

15:09:29.444 --> 15:09:35.853
Now, you might cramp up on this exercise if you're not used to this intense of a hamstring contraction.

15:09:35.913 --> 15:09:37.595
It's a very hard hamstring contraction.

15:09:38.076 --> 15:09:40.599
So if you cramp, then stretch it out.

15:09:41.100 --> 15:09:46.327
Try to get back into it, but this might be a little bit too advanced and your hamstrings aren't quite used to it yet.

15:09:46.828 --> 15:09:50.693
So just try to make it easier or come up with an alternative if you continue to cramp.

15:09:50.673 --> 15:09:55.741
Now the other mistake that people make is they point the toes and they go from there.

15:09:56.082 --> 15:09:59.568
A lot of times your calf will start to cramp when you're in that position.

15:09:59.608 --> 15:10:03.634
So I like to go toes kind of straight down into the ground.

15:10:04.075 --> 15:10:14.352
So I'm digging into the ground and then I get a lot less in the calf and I really just feel it straight through the hamstrings, through the glutes, and then you should feel a little bit through the low back as well.

15:10:22.904 --> 15:10:30.453
So for my rapid calf raise, I could use a bumper plate or I could use a stair, step, anything that's nice and stable.

15:10:30.834 --> 15:10:35.759
Now you're going to want to hold on to something for this exercise, even if your balance is good.

15:10:36.140 --> 15:10:40.405
So I'm going to hold on to a power rack, hold on to whatever you want that keeps you nice and balanced.

15:10:40.946 --> 15:10:49.536
About half of my foot is going on the step, so I just want the ball of my foot on there, my heel is hanging off.

15:10:49.516 --> 15:10:59.738
Now, it's a normal calf raise, except we're going for max elasticity, so I want you to bounce with it, and we're still getting a full range of motion, so it's nice and rapid.

15:11:00.099 --> 15:11:06.152
So explode up to the toe, and then drop down, get your heel down as low as you can, and pop back up.

15:11:06.533 --> 15:11:09.499
Alright, so it's nice and fast.

15:11:10.238 --> 15:11:15.245
and you shouldn't feel like it's a long, slow contraction like you normally would do.

15:11:15.265 --> 15:11:17.608
Now this is a higher rep exercise.

15:11:18.069 --> 15:11:30.947
If you do start to get extremely fatigued, you can always pull yourself up with the arms a little bit and kind of give yourself a little bit of assistance with your upper body because I don't want you to get so tired

15:11:30.927 --> 15:11:32.951
that you start to slow down.

15:11:32.991 --> 15:11:46.879
I want you to stay nice and rapid and bouncy because if you lose that speed and you lose that bouncy and that springiness, then we're getting away from the Achilles and we're getting more on to strictly using that calf muscle contraction.

15:11:47.199 --> 15:11:50.586
We want the perfect combination of both on this exercise.

15:11:51.007 --> 15:11:51.568
Let's do it.

15:12:17.755 --> 15:12:21.799
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

15:12:21.900 --> 15:12:23.701
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

15:12:23.742 --> 15:12:26.444
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

15:12:26.484 --> 15:12:32.992
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

15:12:33.512 --> 15:12:39.479
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

15:12:39.739 --> 15:12:43.543
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

15:12:43.523 --> 15:12:47.410
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

15:12:47.831 --> 15:12:52.179
So it's in through the nose, and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

15:12:52.239 --> 15:12:59.592
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you, when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

15:13:00.567 --> 15:13:03.290
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

15:13:03.651 --> 15:13:10.378
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

15:13:10.939 --> 15:13:16.705
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

15:13:16.725 --> 15:13:20.390
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

15:13:22.692 --> 15:13:26.997
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

15:13:29.913 --> 15:13:31.836
and that stomach will fall, okay?

15:13:32.557 --> 15:13:35.180
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

15:13:35.941 --> 15:13:44.973
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they were in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breath.

15:13:45.754 --> 15:13:53.524
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

15:13:53.785 --> 15:13:57.810
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

15:13:57.790 --> 15:14:26.683
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you could do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

15:14:26.933 --> 15:14:29.437
happens in the parasympathetic state.

15:14:29.977 --> 15:14:34.124
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

15:14:34.464 --> 15:14:48.064
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

15:14:49.934 --> 15:14:53.220
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

15:14:53.240 --> 15:14:57.507
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

15:14:57.807 --> 15:15:04.619
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

15:15:04.899 --> 15:15:08.405
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

15:15:08.685 --> 15:15:16.178
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

15:15:16.158 --> 15:15:20.509
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

15:15:20.930 --> 15:15:22.895
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

15:15:23.256 --> 15:15:25.441
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

15:15:25.722 --> 15:15:27.807
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

15:15:28.087 --> 15:15:30.293
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

15:15:30.594 --> 15:15:32.378
I'm just gonna lean forward.

15:15:32.358 --> 15:15:53.134
from the hips keeping my back nice and straight if you want to reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe you can but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back so i like to just keep my hands here to make sure that i'm not rounding the upper back and i'm just going to hold 15 seconds and then i'm going to switch over to the other side

15:15:53.468 --> 15:15:55.330
15 seconds on the other side as well.

15:15:55.450 --> 15:15:57.573
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

15:15:57.753 --> 15:15:59.195
So that is the back of the thigh.

15:15:59.415 --> 15:16:03.620
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

15:16:03.760 --> 15:16:07.444
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

15:16:07.585 --> 15:16:10.007
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

15:16:10.368 --> 15:16:12.691
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

15:16:13.031 --> 15:16:14.613
So now look at this back foot.

15:16:14.633 --> 15:16:16.035
I'm not curling the toe under.

15:16:16.435 --> 15:16:20.640
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

15:16:20.620 --> 15:16:23.946
and I'm gonna sit my hips back to that heel.

15:16:24.367 --> 15:16:29.697
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

15:16:30.017 --> 15:16:43.422
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're gonna come down to the elbows and we're just gonna hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

15:16:43.402 --> 15:16:46.567
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

15:16:46.587 --> 15:16:47.929
That's the inside of the leg.

15:16:48.410 --> 15:16:51.775
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

15:16:52.376 --> 15:16:53.057
Same thing.

15:16:53.398 --> 15:16:56.282
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

15:16:56.583 --> 15:16:59.908
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

15:16:59.888 --> 15:17:03.874
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

15:17:04.175 --> 15:17:11.806
I'm not gonna lift off and I'm just gonna lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

15:17:12.167 --> 15:17:17.655
Now a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

15:17:18.076 --> 15:17:23.584
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band

15:17:23.564 --> 15:17:28.112
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

15:17:28.513 --> 15:17:36.166
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

15:17:36.687 --> 15:17:39.472
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

15:17:39.913 --> 15:17:45.943
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

15:17:45.923 --> 15:17:48.986
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

15:17:49.046 --> 15:17:51.228
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

15:17:51.468 --> 15:17:55.111
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

15:17:55.592 --> 15:17:57.133
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

15:17:57.554 --> 15:18:00.076
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

15:18:00.156 --> 15:18:01.097
So I'm 90 there.

15:18:01.437 --> 15:18:04.220
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

15:18:04.580 --> 15:18:07.203
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

15:18:07.223 --> 15:18:11.927
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

15:18:12.267 --> 15:18:15.390
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward

15:18:15.370 --> 15:18:18.657
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

15:18:18.677 --> 15:18:23.426
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

15:18:23.466 --> 15:18:24.929
All right.

15:18:24.949 --> 15:18:30.821
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

15:18:31.562 --> 15:18:35.370
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

15:18:35.350 --> 15:18:40.677
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

15:18:41.057 --> 15:18:44.282
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

15:18:44.382 --> 15:18:47.586
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

15:18:47.966 --> 15:18:57.679
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

15:18:57.980 --> 15:19:04.467
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

15:19:04.727 --> 15:19:10.894
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

15:19:11.275 --> 15:19:15.359
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

15:19:16.540 --> 15:19:20.345
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

15:19:20.705 --> 15:19:23.929
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

15:19:23.949 --> 15:19:27.953
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

15:19:30.582 --> 15:19:33.846
So quick minimum dose advice for day three and day six.

15:19:34.146 --> 15:19:35.328
This is our core day.

15:19:35.628 --> 15:19:38.211
We're going to get a little bit of mobility, but not too much.

15:19:38.672 --> 15:19:41.716
As you can see, already extremely efficient.

15:19:42.657 --> 15:19:46.702
If you want to add core exercises from previous phases, go ahead.

15:19:47.503 --> 15:19:53.350
I've just made this one very, very short, straight to the point, since our other days are pretty tough.

15:19:53.330 --> 15:19:58.419
I don't want you to kill yourself and drain your nervous system by doing way too much core work.

15:19:59.000 --> 15:20:00.642
Cable dead bug, got to do it.

15:20:00.682 --> 15:20:02.385
If you got to do that with a band, that's fine.

15:20:02.906 --> 15:20:06.212
Elastic leg kick, do this even if you are flexible.

15:20:07.194 --> 15:20:08.816
Hollow press, very important.

15:20:09.638 --> 15:20:16.950
Kneeling hip series, this one is going to be challenging from a hip mobility standpoint, but also from a hip stability standpoint.

15:20:17.391 --> 15:20:18.713
Definitely don't skip that.

15:20:18.693 --> 15:20:21.316
Lateral step core stiffness, very important.

15:20:21.777 --> 15:20:25.981
If you feel like you need a warmup for that, go through an additional lower body warmup.

15:20:26.362 --> 15:20:31.768
Some people have creaky knees and they can't quite get into this intense little partial lunge.

15:20:32.629 --> 15:20:37.154
So if you feel like you need to warm up for that, go ahead and do an extra dynamic warmup.

15:20:37.655 --> 15:20:40.378
And then at the end of that, add whatever core you want.

15:20:40.738 --> 15:20:44.523
And of course, if you want to add a third core day, that's completely fine.

15:20:44.963 --> 15:20:45.544
Let's do it.

15:20:53.236 --> 15:20:57.162
So for our cable dead bug, I'm using a bench and a cable machine right now.

15:20:57.462 --> 15:20:58.283
You don't need the bench.

15:20:58.303 --> 15:21:20.374
You could go on the floor with the cable machine or if you don't have the cable machine or it's inconvenient in your gym, set up with a power rack or anything that you could hook a band around and you're just going to go on the floor and hold that band right here just so that you have to fire up your core, fire up your lats and stay nice and stable through that torso throughout this exercise.

15:21:20.354 --> 15:21:24.158
What I'm going to do is reach back and grab the rope.

15:21:24.178 --> 15:21:29.184
Notice I have it at a cable setting to where I can hold straight out.

15:21:29.585 --> 15:21:33.329
If that's too low, then that line of pull is going to be really awkward.

15:21:34.270 --> 15:21:35.511
So I'm holding right here.

15:21:35.571 --> 15:21:37.814
I got to engage my lats and engage my core.

15:21:38.295 --> 15:21:40.157
And then I'm going up with the feet.

15:21:40.557 --> 15:21:44.742
My back is nice and flat so I don't have an exaggerated curve.

15:21:44.722 --> 15:22:13.176
nice and flat and then I'm just going alternating all the way out back up and the other side all the way out back up all right you got to squeeze that core and keep that good neutral spine all the way through this exercise now you're not necessarily going to feel a burn of like a crunch or a sit-up but you should feel a little bit of a burn in the core more importantly this type of core strength and stability actually translates to the core

15:22:25.441 --> 15:22:34.574
For the elastic leg kicks, I want you to think about a normal, kind of like a Frankenstein kick, except we're gonna make it a little bit more rapid and a little bit more elastic.

15:22:34.594 --> 15:22:47.972
So I'm just gonna stay on the balls of the feet and I'm just gonna kick up my leg as high as I can, keeping that knee straight, and I'm just gonna switch every time so it ends up being a little bit of a skip and then a straight leg kick.

15:22:47.952 --> 15:22:48.273
Okay.

15:22:48.733 --> 15:22:50.556
Now I'm popping off the balls of the feet.

15:22:51.077 --> 15:22:53.001
I do not want your heels to touch the floor.

15:22:53.481 --> 15:22:56.166
And the reason why we're doing this, you're not going to get a huge burn.

15:22:56.206 --> 15:22:57.608
This isn't a huge workout.

15:22:57.969 --> 15:23:03.278
We're trying to get some elasticity back in the post chain, some elasticity in the hamstrings.

15:23:03.258 --> 15:23:08.750
So a lot of people sit there and stretch or even do a normal dynamic warm-up for elasticity.

15:23:09.131 --> 15:23:14.443
The thing is, for a muscle to actually increase elasticity, it has to be under one second.

15:23:14.844 --> 15:23:19.174
So if I'm going slow with that kick or if I'm holding

15:23:19.154 --> 15:23:40.437
a stretch it might increase what we call plasticity how flexible we get but not elasticity and elasticity is what actually translates to sprinting and jumping so everything has to be under one second and that's why we're going rapid with those kicks i want you to think about getting that leg up as quick as you can as high as you can

15:23:51.233 --> 15:23:53.958
So for our hollow press, this is a core exercise.

15:23:54.038 --> 15:23:55.480
It looks like I'm working my chest.

15:23:55.801 --> 15:23:57.163
I'm not actually working my chest.

15:23:57.183 --> 15:24:00.489
You will get a little pump in your pecs and your triceps and your shoulders.

15:24:00.770 --> 15:24:02.312
Most importantly, this is for the core.

15:24:02.372 --> 15:24:06.860
So whatever you would normally dumbbell bench press, we're going to go about half of that.

15:24:06.940 --> 15:24:13.432
So if normally you would for 10, you rep out like 60s, I want you to go a 30 pound dumbbell.

15:24:13.893 --> 15:24:14.153
All right.

15:24:14.133 --> 15:24:16.577
position here and then I'm just leaning back.

15:24:16.637 --> 15:24:22.686
I'm bringing my feet off the ground about six inches and now I'm bringing my shoulder off the ground about six inches.

15:24:22.726 --> 15:24:26.371
So my lower back is flat to the ground and my butt is on the ground.

15:24:26.752 --> 15:24:27.813
Nothing else is touching.

15:24:28.655 --> 15:24:30.998
A lot of people make the mistake of going up here.

15:24:31.058 --> 15:24:35.024
I need you laid down flat and then just come up a little bit.

15:24:35.825 --> 15:24:39.010
From here, I'm going to touch my elbow to the floor.

15:24:38.990 --> 15:24:41.657
And then I'm just going to press, lock out at the top.

15:24:42.178 --> 15:24:42.519
All right.

15:24:42.539 --> 15:24:49.518
So I'm challenging that anti-rotation and I'm challenging those front abs by holding in this position.

15:24:50.079 --> 15:24:50.360
All right.

15:24:50.420 --> 15:24:51.663
Keep it nice and slow.

15:24:52.185 --> 15:24:55.834
Don't let yourself fly into rotation with that dumbbell.

15:24:55.814 --> 15:25:04.289
So this is an exercise where we can load up as heavy as possible, but I do want you to start light and gradually increase as we go.

15:25:04.850 --> 15:25:11.342
For most of us, like I said, you're gonna be about 50% of what you would normally hit a dumbbell bench press with.

15:25:36.027 --> 15:25:39.491
Okay, so for my kneeling hip series, we're gonna use a band.

15:25:39.872 --> 15:25:44.177
I like to hook two bands together so that I actually have a little more room.

15:25:44.197 --> 15:25:51.605
Make sure whatever you're hooking the band to is nice and stable like a towel rack or a heavy couch or something that's not gonna be pulled along with the band.

15:25:51.625 --> 15:25:56.571
Of course, the thicker we go with that band, the more difficult this is gonna be, okay?

15:25:56.611 --> 15:25:59.294
And the further out we go, the more difficult it's gonna be.

15:25:59.314 --> 15:26:03.199
So I would start with just light tension until you get used to this drill.

15:26:03.938 --> 15:26:05.802
I use a pad so there's light on the knees.

15:26:06.222 --> 15:26:10.511
I'm going to curl the toes and I'm going to sit my hips back.

15:26:10.932 --> 15:26:14.939
First of all, right there, I'm getting some decent toe range motion, which is really important for us.

15:26:15.360 --> 15:26:17.945
Now from here, I'm just going to explode forward.

15:26:17.965 --> 15:26:19.849
So I'm going to come to full hip extension.

15:26:19.869 --> 15:26:24.057
I'm going to squeeze my glutes for two seconds as hard as I can at the top.

15:26:24.037 --> 15:26:29.066
Now from here, I'm going to bring this right foot out around and get that foot down.

15:26:29.387 --> 15:26:31.310
So I'm going to try not to lean the shoulders.

15:26:31.350 --> 15:26:35.618
Try to stay level, stable with the shoulders and with the hips.

15:26:36.319 --> 15:26:37.561
And I'm bringing it around.

15:26:38.242 --> 15:26:39.645
Get that foot flat.

15:26:39.665 --> 15:26:43.893
And then from here, I'm driving out and I'm going to squeeze the right foot down.

15:26:43.913 --> 15:26:45.235
Drive out.

15:26:45.215 --> 15:26:49.120
Get this leg parallel to the floor and squeeze this right glute.

15:26:49.220 --> 15:26:50.502
Hold for two.

15:26:50.522 --> 15:26:52.765
And we're right back down.

15:26:52.785 --> 15:26:53.866
So it's here.

15:26:53.906 --> 15:26:55.228
Extend.

15:26:55.468 --> 15:26:56.009
Squeeze.

15:26:56.249 --> 15:26:56.830
One, two.

15:26:57.791 --> 15:26:59.093
Right foot up.

15:26:59.113 --> 15:26:59.874
Drive out.

15:26:59.954 --> 15:27:00.855
Squeeze.

15:27:01.256 --> 15:27:01.977
One, two.

15:27:02.457 --> 15:27:04.540
And then we're right back down.

15:27:04.520 --> 15:27:08.445
This is challenging our hip stability in so many different ways.

15:27:08.465 --> 15:27:12.530
We're activating our glutes with that explosive extension.

15:27:12.550 --> 15:27:14.673
We're getting some toe range of motion.

15:27:14.693 --> 15:27:22.082
We're getting some lateral hip stability, those little tiny hip stabilizer muscles when we're on that half kneel and we're bringing that leg up.

15:27:22.102 --> 15:27:26.127
So there's so many different aspects of hip stability going on here.

15:27:26.367 --> 15:27:27.709
This drill is great.

15:27:27.689 --> 15:27:44.095
for building a good solid base so we can add more explosiveness to it later on and then of course you're going to do the desired reps with the right foot going forward and then we're going to do the desired reps then with the left foot going forward so we always balance out each side

15:27:56.528 --> 15:28:02.281
So for my lateral step core stiffness, what I'm gonna do is use a cable machine.

15:28:02.641 --> 15:28:05.267
I'm gonna get that cable set at around hip height.

15:28:05.628 --> 15:28:11.601
Now if you don't have a cable machine, you could just do these with a band, get it around something stable, around hip height.

15:28:12.526 --> 15:28:16.251
Either one will work, but I like to use a cable machine because we can load up a little heavier.

15:28:16.331 --> 15:28:18.774
So of course, as always, we start very light.

15:28:19.114 --> 15:28:21.577
Once we feel comfortable, we gradually go up.

15:28:21.597 --> 15:28:24.320
I like to go around 80, 90, up to 100 pounds.

15:28:24.380 --> 15:28:27.104
So I'm going to go feet pretty close together.

15:28:27.404 --> 15:28:29.987
So I'm maybe hip width, shoulder width apart here.

15:28:30.568 --> 15:28:32.751
Already I should feel like it's really pulling me in.

15:28:33.191 --> 15:28:36.475
Now I'm just going to take a little step with this left foot.

15:28:36.455 --> 15:28:45.514
and as soon as that foot hits, I'm landing with that 90 degree knee angle and then I'm keeping my core stiff and I'm pushing out of it.

15:28:46.396 --> 15:28:51.747
So many of you, what you're gonna do is you're gonna step and then you're gonna have this torso collapse.

15:28:51.727 --> 15:28:58.839
You got to keep in mind a lot of the battle on lateral movements, right, if I'm changing direction, is that core stiffness.

15:28:59.300 --> 15:29:07.234
So if I get here and I go to change directions and I deform from the core, we lose milliseconds on that change of direction.

15:29:07.634 --> 15:29:10.139
So what I want you to focus on is keeping that stiff core.

15:29:10.439 --> 15:29:16.249
As soon as that foot hits, I do not collapse at all and I can just push right back out.

15:29:17.242 --> 15:29:23.375
Alright, so it's feet together, it's here, short ground contact time, and just push up out of it.

15:29:24.698 --> 15:29:27.043
Alright, it's gotta be fast, it's gotta be rapid.

15:29:27.264 --> 15:29:34.018
We are gonna feel a burn in the leg, but we should also feel it in the core, especially in that outside oblique.

